/* ===== Microbiology set 15: more togaviruses, flaviviruses, picornaviruses, poxviruses, small DNA viruses and La Crosse (draft, Oct 30 2026) ===== */
MICRO.push(
{ id: "western-equine-encephalitis-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "Western equine encephalitis virus", also: "WEEV, western equine encephalomyelitis virus",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, icosahedral capsid with glycoprotein spikes, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, a recombinant of an eastern equine-like and a Sindbis-like ancestor",
  plain: "A mosquito-borne North American virus that once caused large summer encephalitis outbreaks in horses and people across the western plains and has become strikingly rare.",
  live: "A bird and mosquito cycle, chiefly passerine birds such as house sparrows and finches amplified by the freshwater marsh mosquito Culex tarsalis. Horses and humans are dead-end hosts that do not generate enough viremia to infect another mosquito.",
  spread: "Bite of an infected mosquito, overwhelmingly Culex tarsalis in irrigated western farmland, with Aedes melanimon and other species contributing in some settings. There is no person-to-person transmission, and laboratory aerosol exposure is a recognized occupational risk.",
  causes: "Most infections are silent or produce a brief fever with headache. A small proportion progress to encephalitis with fever, stiff neck, confusion, tremor and seizures. Infants and young children carry the highest risk of severe neurologic disease and of permanent sequelae including seizures, spasticity and developmental delay, while older adults carry the highest mortality. Classic outbreak case fatality figures sat near three to five percent.",
  mech: "The E2 glycoprotein binds cell surface receptors including protocadherin-10 on neurons, and the particle enters by clathrin-mediated endocytosis with low-pH fusion driven by E1. Replication in the cytoplasm produces a subgenomic message for the structural proteins, and nonstructural protein nsP2 shuts off host transcription, which blunts the early interferon response. Virus seeds from skin and lymphoid tissue into the blood and crosses into the central nervous system, where it infects neurons preferentially in the basal ganglia, thalamus and brainstem. Neuronal death comes from both direct cytopathic effect and the inflammatory infiltrate that follows. The age pattern of severity tracks the maturity of the blood-brain barrier and of the innate antiviral response.",
  lab: "Serology is the mainstay: IgM capture assay on serum and cerebrospinal fluid, with paired sera showing a rising titer, and plaque reduction neutralization to sort out cross-reaction with other alphaviruses. Reverse transcription PCR on cerebrospinal fluid has low yield because viremia has usually cleared by the time encephalitis appears. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, sometimes neutrophil predominant early, with modestly raised protein. Work with live virus belongs in containment because of aerosol infectivity.",
  tx: "Supportive care only. Management centers on seizure control, intracranial pressure, airway protection and the general care of a comatose patient. No antiviral has demonstrated benefit in human disease.",
  alt: "Interferon, ribavirin and immune globulin have been examined in animal models and case reports without convincing results. Rehabilitation and long-term neurologic follow-up form the substantive part of care for survivors.",
  res: "Antiviral resistance is not a clinical issue since no drug is used. The practical limitation is that even in laboratory models antiviral effect disappears once the virus has established itself in neurons, so the therapeutic window closes before most patients present.",
  prev: "Mosquito control in irrigated land, larviciding of marsh breeding sites, surveillance of sentinel chicken flocks and of equine cases, screens and repellents. An inactivated equine vaccine is licensed and widely used in horses, which also removes the amplifying signal that used to warn of human outbreaks. A formalin-inactivated human vaccine exists only as an investigational product for laboratory workers.",
  look: "West Nile virus, which now causes most arboviral encephalitis in the same geography. St Louis encephalitis virus, which shares the Culex vector. Eastern equine encephalitis virus, which is far more lethal and sits in eastern swamps. Herpes simplex encephalitis, which favors the temporal lobe and is treatable. Enteroviral meningitis in a child in late summer.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Western equine encephalitis is the alphaviral encephalitis of the western United States and Canada, spread by Culex tarsalis, with the worst outcomes in infants."],
    ["Memory trick", "Three alphaviral encephalitides sorted by map and severity: Eastern is worst and eastern, Western is milder and western, Venezuelan is mostly a flu with an occasional brain."],
    ["Classic setting", "A late summer cluster of encephalitis in an irrigated farming county of the Great Plains, preceded by sick horses, with an infant among the cases."],
    ["Step focus", "Humans and horses are dead-end hosts; birds and mosquitoes maintain the cycle, which is why equine vaccination protects horses without interrupting transmission."]],
  worm: [["The outbreak that defined it", "In the summer of 1941 an epidemic of encephalitis swept the northern plains of the United States and the Canadian prairie provinces, with more than three thousand recognized human cases and a horse epizootic that preceded it by weeks. The virus had been isolated from a California horse brain in 1930 by Karl Meyer and colleagues, and the 1941 outbreak proved the agent could reach people on a large scale. Irrigation that expanded Culex tarsalis breeding habitat was identified as the structural cause."],
    ["A virus made of two others", "Sequencing in the 1990s showed that Western equine encephalitis virus is a natural recombinant. Its nonstructural genes and capsid descend from an eastern equine encephalitis-like ancestor, while its envelope glycoproteins come from a Sindbis-like virus. The crossover happened in a coinfected cell at some point in the past, producing a new virus with new vector and host relationships. It is one of the clearest examples that alphavirus evolution proceeds by swapping modules rather than only by stepwise mutation."],
    ["Why it almost disappeared", "Human cases have collapsed from hundreds a year in the 1940s to a handful of reports since the 1990s, with no confirmed human case in the United States for many years. Proposed explanations include a genuine loss of virulence in circulating strains, widespread equine vaccination, mosquito control, air conditioning and window screens, and changes in land and water use. Whether a more virulent lineage could reemerge is unresolved, which is why sentinel surveillance in the west continues despite the quiet."],
    ["An age gradient with two ends", "Infants infected with this virus fare worse than any other group for neurologic damage, with a large proportion of surviving infants left with seizures, motor deficits or developmental delay. Mortality, in contrast, rises with age and falls heaviest on adults past sixty. The pattern suggests two different vulnerabilities: a developing nervous system that is readily infected and permanently altered, and an aging host whose capacity to survive severe brain inflammation is reduced. Middle age sits in the safest part of the curve."]] },

{ id: "venezuelan-equine-encephalitis-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "Venezuelan equine encephalitis virus", also: "VEEV, Venezuelan equine encephalomyelitis virus, peste loca",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, an antigenic complex of subtypes I to VI with epizootic subtypes IAB and IC and enzootic subtypes ID, IE and IF",
  plain: "A Latin American mosquito virus that usually causes a severe flu-like illness, occasionally encephalitis, and periodically erupts into enormous epidemics alongside horse deaths.",
  live: "Enzootic subtypes cycle quietly between rodents in humid tropical forest and Culex mosquitoes of the subgenus Melanoconion. Epizootic subtypes use horses, donkeys and mules as high-level amplifying hosts, with a wide range of mosquito vectors including Aedes and Psorophora species.",
  spread: "Mosquito bite. Unlike the eastern and western equine viruses, humans develop viremia high enough to infect mosquitoes, so people can serve as amplifying hosts. The virus is also highly infectious by aerosol, which has caused many laboratory infections and is why it was developed as a weapon by several states in the twentieth century.",
  causes: "An abrupt febrile illness in most infections: high fever, severe headache, myalgia, prostration, nausea, vomiting and sometimes diarrhea, lasting several days and often described by patients as the worst flu of their lives. Encephalitis follows in a small percentage overall but in a much larger share of infected children, with seizures, confusion and coma. Infection in pregnancy has been associated with fetal loss and congenital neurologic damage. Overall mortality is below one percent but neurologic sequelae are common among those with encephalitis.",
  mech: "Entry is mediated by E2 binding to receptors including LDLRAD3, with low-density lipoprotein receptor family proteins used by several New World alphaviruses. The virus first replicates in dendritic cells and other cells of the draining lymph node, which generates the very high viremia that distinguishes it from its eastern and western relatives. Nonstructural protein nsP2 and the capsid protein together suppress host transcription and nuclear import of transcription factors such as STAT1, so the interferon response is disarmed early. Lymphoid tissue is depleted, which accounts for the lymphopenia of the acute illness. Spread to the brain occurs through the olfactory neuroepithelium and across the cerebral vasculature, and a single amino acid change in E2 can shift an enzootic rodent virus into an epizootic equine-amplified one.",
  lab: "Virus isolation and reverse transcription PCR on acute serum are often successful because viremia is high and present during the febrile phase, which sets this virus apart from most arboviral encephalitides. IgM capture serology and plaque reduction neutralization confirm and subtype. Leukopenia with lymphopenia is typical. All manipulation of live virus requires high containment and the agent is listed as a select agent in the United States.",
  tx: "Supportive care, with attention to fluid balance in a severely febrile patient and standard management of encephalitis when it develops. No licensed antiviral exists.",
  alt: "Investigational approaches include interferon alfa, nucleoside analogs, capsid inhibitors and monoclonal antibodies against E2; none is established in human disease. Antibody therapy protects animals when given before or very soon after exposure, which is relevant mainly to laboratory accidents.",
  res: "Not a clinical concern. In research settings, escape mutants emerge readily against single monoclonal antibodies, which is why experimental antibody products combine epitopes.",
  prev: "Vector control, equine vaccination to remove the amplifying host, and avoidance of bites. The live attenuated TC-83 vaccine and the inactivated C-84 vaccine exist as investigational products for laboratory and military personnel; TC-83 is reactogenic in a substantial minority and does not protect well against all subtypes. Equine vaccines are licensed and are the principal public health tool, since epidemics in people follow epizootics in horses.",
  look: "Dengue, which shares the abrupt fever, headache and prostration in the same geography. Malaria. Leptospirosis. Influenza. Other alphaviruses, especially Mayaro and chikungunya, though those cause prominent arthritis rather than encephalitis. When encephalitis appears, rabies, herpes simplex and the other arboviral encephalitides enter the list.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Venezuelan equine encephalitis virus is the alphavirus in which humans generate high enough viremia to infect mosquitoes, so people amplify the epidemic rather than ending it."],
    ["Memory trick", "Venezuelan is the one that is mostly a flu, mostly not the brain, and is caught in the laboratory by breathing it."],
    ["Classic setting", "A large epidemic in coastal Venezuela or Colombia after heavy rain, with thousands of febrile people and dead horses in the same villages."],
    ["Step focus", "A single mutation in the E2 glycoprotein can convert an enzootic rodent strain into an epizootic equine-amplifying strain, which is the mechanism behind the periodic epidemics."]],
  worm: [["From peste loca to a weapon", "Equine encephalitis in Venezuela was described in the 1930s under local names including peste loca, the mad plague, and the virus was isolated from horse brain in 1938. Its combination of high infectivity by aerosol, a large incapacitating attack rate and low lethality made it attractive to twentieth-century biological weapons programs in both the United States and the Soviet Union. Human vaccine candidates such as TC-83 were developed primarily to protect the people building and handling the stockpiles rather than the populations at risk from mosquitoes."],
    ["The 1995 epidemic and the shape of an outbreak", "In 1995 an epidemic of subtype IC swept from Venezuela into Colombia, producing roughly a hundred thousand human cases and several hundred neurologic deaths within months. Genetic analysis showed the epizootic strain had arisen from a local enzootic subtype ID virus by a small number of envelope mutations. The sequence of events is now the textbook pattern: heavy rainfall, enormous mosquito populations, a mutation that lets the virus grow in horses, equine deaths, and then human cases in the same week."],
    ["A receptor found after eighty years", "Alphavirus receptors were long elusive. In 2020 a screen identified LDLRAD3, a little-studied member of the low-density lipoprotein receptor family, as the entry receptor for this virus, and related work identified very low-density lipoprotein receptor for Semliki Forest and Eastern equine encephalitis viruses. Removing LDLRAD3 renders mice resistant to lethal challenge. The finding gave the field a concrete target for decoy receptors and blocking antibodies after decades in which the entry step was described only in terms of pH and endosomes."],
    ["Why the epidemics stopped, and whether they will resume", "Large epizootics were recorded roughly every decade through the 1960s and 1970s, including an outbreak that moved from South America through Central America and into Texas in 1971. Mass equine vaccination, partly with the TC-83 strain, broke that rhythm, and no comparable continental wave has occurred since 1995. The enzootic subtypes remain abundant in forest rodents throughout the Americas, so the raw material for a new epizootic strain is still in place wherever equine vaccination coverage lapses."]] },

{ id: "ross-river-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "Ross River virus", also: "RRV, epidemic polyarthritis, Murray Valley rash",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, within the Semliki Forest antigenic complex of arthritogenic alphaviruses",
  plain: "Australia's commonest mosquito-borne infection, causing a rash with joint pain that can drag on for months but almost never kills.",
  live: "Macropods, chiefly kangaroos and wallabies, are the principal reservoir and amplifying hosts, with horses and possibly possums and some birds contributing. The virus persists across dry years in ways that are still debated, with vertical transmission in Aedes eggs one proposed mechanism.",
  spread: "Mosquito bite, with Culex annulirostris in inland freshwater habitats and the saltmarsh mosquitoes Aedes vigilax and Aedes camptorhynchus along the coast. Humans may reach viremia sufficient to infect mosquitoes during outbreaks, so urban transmission without macropods is possible. There is no direct person-to-person spread.",
  causes: "Epidemic polyarthritis: a symmetric peripheral polyarthralgia or frank arthritis of wrists, knees, ankles, fingers and feet, often with tenosynovitis, accompanied in roughly half of symptomatic patients by a maculopapular or vesicular rash on the trunk and limbs, fever, myalgia, headache and marked fatigue. Many infections are entirely silent. Joint pain and fatigue resolve within a few weeks in most patients but persist for three to six months in a substantial minority and occasionally for over a year. Chronic joint destruction does not occur and deaths are not attributed to it.",
  mech: "The virus enters synovial macrophages, muscle and connective tissue, and the arthritis reflects infection of and inflammation within the joint rather than a purely post-infectious immune phenomenon, since viral RNA and antigen can be recovered from synovial tissue and fluid weeks into illness. Infected macrophages release monocyte chemoattractant protein 1, interleukin 6 and tumor necrosis factor, drawing in further monocytes that are themselves permissive, which sustains a feedback loop. Complement activation through the mannose-binding lectin pathway contributes to muscle and joint damage in animal models. The virus also replicates in skeletal muscle, which accounts for the myalgia, and persistence of viral RNA in tissue macrophages is the leading explanation for symptoms that outlast viremia.",
  lab: "IgM and IgG serology on paired sera is the usual route, with IgM sometimes persisting for many months and complicating interpretation in an endemic population. A fourfold rise in IgG is more convincing. Reverse transcription PCR on serum is positive only in the first few days. Inflammatory markers are modestly raised, rheumatoid factor and antinuclear antibodies are typically negative, and synovial fluid shows a mononuclear-predominant effusion.",
  tx: "Supportive care. Simple analgesia and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for joint pain, with graded return to activity, and physiotherapy where stiffness is prominent. No antiviral therapy is available or indicated.",
  alt: "Short courses of corticosteroid have been used for severe refractory arthritis without trial evidence, and the theoretical concern is prolonged viral persistence. Hydroxychloroquine and other disease-modifying drugs have no established role. Pentosan polysulfate has been examined in small studies for persistent symptoms.",
  res: "No antiviral resistance issue arises. The practical difficulty is that persistent symptoms do not respond predictably to anything, and the absence of a treatable target is the reason management is largely explanation and reassurance.",
  prev: "Personal protection against mosquitoes, with attention to dawn and dusk activity and to saltmarsh and irrigated inland habitats, plus larval source reduction. No licensed human vaccine exists, though candidates have reached early trials; an inactivated equine vaccine has been developed in Australia. Outbreak prediction uses rainfall, tide and mosquito trapping data.",
  look: "Barmah Forest virus, which is clinically almost identical and circulates in the same areas. Chikungunya and Mayaro in travelers. Parvovirus B19 arthropathy. Acute rheumatoid arthritis, reactive arthritis and viral arthritis from rubella. Dengue, which brings more severe fever and less joint swelling. Early Lyme disease is not relevant in Australia but enters the differential in returned travelers.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Ross River virus is the arthritogenic alphavirus of Australia, with kangaroos and wallabies as reservoir and symmetric peripheral polyarthritis plus rash as the syndrome."],
    ["Memory trick", "Arthritogenic alphaviruses as CROMS: Chikungunya, Ross River, O'nyong-nyong, Mayaro, Sindbis. All joints, no brain."],
    ["Classic setting", "A camper in the Murray Valley or coastal Queensland after a wet summer, with swollen wrists and ankles, a blotchy rash and months of fatigue."],
    ["Step focus", "Viral RNA persists in synovial and muscle macrophages, which is the leading mechanism for arthralgia that continues long after the viremia clears."]],
  worm: [["A river, a mosquito and a long delay", "Doctors in Queensland described outbreaks of rash with polyarthritis from the 1920s, and the illness was long called epidemic polyarthritis. In 1959 Ralph Doherty's group isolated an alphavirus from Aedes vigilax mosquitoes trapped along the Ross River near Townsville, and the name stuck to the river rather than the disease. It took another decade of serological work and a 1979 Pacific epidemic that reached Fiji, Samoa and the Cook Islands to confirm that this virus was the agent behind the Australian syndrome."],
    ["The Pacific epidemic that proved people can amplify", "Between 1979 and 1980 the virus moved through Fiji, American Samoa, Wallis Island and the Cook Islands, infecting hundreds of thousands of people. None of these islands has macropods. The epidemic therefore demonstrated that human-mosquito-human transmission alone can sustain a large outbreak, which changed the understanding of the virus from a strictly zoonotic spillover to one capable of urban cycles. The epidemic then burned out and the virus did not establish itself in the Pacific, apparently for lack of a maintenance host between epidemics."],
    ["Why the symptoms outstay the virus", "Patients frequently report joint pain and exhaustion for six months after a brief illness, and for decades this was attributed to a post-viral syndrome of uncertain nature. Studies of synovial biopsies and of mouse models found viral RNA and antigen within joint and muscle macrophages well after the blood cleared, alongside continuing production of monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 and interleukin 6. The picture that emerged is a low-grade persistent infection in a privileged cell type rather than an immune memory that fails to switch off."],
    ["Rain, tides and prediction", "Because its two main vectors breed in such different water, inland freshwater pools and coastal saltmarsh, outbreak forecasting in Australia combines rainfall and river flow data for Culex annulirostris with tidal height records for Aedes vigilax. Public health units run mosquito trapping and sentinel chicken flocks alongside these models and issue warnings weeks before human notifications climb. It is one of the better worked examples of arboviral forecasting, and it works largely because the host ecology is so well characterized."]] },

{ id: "mayaro-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "Mayaro virus", also: "MAYV, Mayaro fever, Uruma virus",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, Semliki Forest complex, closely related to chikungunya virus",
  plain: "A South American forest virus that mimics chikungunya with fever and disabling joint pain and is probably far commoner than the case counts suggest.",
  live: "Non-human primates, particularly marmosets and howler monkeys, with rodents, marsupials and birds also implicated, in a sylvatic cycle in humid neotropical forest from Trinidad through the Amazon basin to Bolivia and Peru.",
  spread: "Bite of canopy-dwelling Haemagogus janthinomys mosquitoes, the same genus that maintains sylvatic yellow fever. Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus can transmit the virus in the laboratory, which raises concern about an urban cycle. Human infection usually follows entry into or residence beside forest.",
  causes: "An abrupt febrile illness with severe arthralgia of the ankles, wrists, knees and small joints, headache, retro-orbital pain, myalgia and a maculopapular rash appearing around the fifth day. Joint pain is frequently the dominant and most disabling feature and can persist for months in a substantial minority, closely resembling chikungunya. Hemorrhagic and neurologic presentations have been reported but are uncommon, and deaths are rare.",
  mech: "Like other arthritogenic alphaviruses it uses the E2 glycoprotein for attachment, with Mxra8 identified as a receptor for several members of this group, and enters by endocytosis with low-pH-triggered E1 fusion. Replication in fibroblasts, synovial tissue, muscle and osteoblasts produces local cytokine release, and infection of osteoblasts shifts the RANKL to osteoprotegerin ratio toward bone resorption in experimental systems. Nonstructural protein nsP2 contributes to host transcriptional shutoff and interferon antagonism. Monocyte and macrophage infiltration of the joint with interleukin 6, interferon gamma and monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 drives the arthritis, and persistence of viral RNA in joint tissue is the proposed basis of chronic symptoms.",
  lab: "IgM and IgG serology, with the important caveat of extensive cross-reaction with chikungunya and other Semliki Forest complex alphaviruses, so plaque reduction neutralization or virus-specific molecular testing is needed to separate them. Reverse transcription PCR on acute serum is positive during the first several days of fever. Leukopenia and mild thrombocytopenia are common and lead to confusion with dengue. Most laboratories in endemic regions do not test for it routinely, which is a major reason it is underdiagnosed.",
  tx: "Supportive care with analgesia, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs once dengue has been excluded, rest and fluids. There is no antiviral therapy.",
  alt: "Management of persistent arthritis follows the approach used for chronic chikungunya arthropathy: graded exercise, physiotherapy and analgesia, with disease-modifying agents considered only in refractory inflammatory cases and without trial support in this infection.",
  res: "Not applicable in the absence of antiviral drugs. The unresolved issue is diagnostic rather than therapeutic: serological cross-reaction means an unknown proportion of what is reported as chikungunya in the Americas may be Mayaro.",
  prev: "Avoidance of daytime biting in forest, long sleeves, permethrin-treated clothing and repellents for forestry, mining and agricultural workers. Mosquito control is difficult because the vector breeds in tree holes in the canopy. No licensed vaccine, though inactivated and virus-like particle candidates and a chimeric construct have shown promise in animals, aided by cross-protection studies with chikungunya.",
  look: "Chikungunya, which it resembles almost exactly and which is far better recognized in the same countries. Dengue and Zika. Oropouche fever, another underrecognized Amazonian arbovirus. Yellow fever in the same forest. Malaria. Acute viral arthritis from parvovirus B19 or rubella.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Mayaro virus is a chikungunya-like arthritogenic alphavirus of South American forests transmitted by canopy Haemagogus mosquitoes, the same genus that carries sylvatic yellow fever."],
    ["Memory trick", "Mayaro is chikungunya's Amazonian cousin: same joints, same curled posture, different continent and a monkey in the canopy."],
    ["Classic setting", "A gold miner or forestry worker returning from the Amazon with five days of fever, a rash and ankles too painful to walk on."],
    ["Step focus", "Serology cross-reacts strongly with chikungunya, so neutralization testing or PCR is what distinguishes them, and the distinction matters for outbreak attribution."]],
  worm: [["Named for a county in Trinidad", "The virus was first isolated in 1954 from the blood of five forest workers in the Mayaro county of Trinidad during an investigation of febrile illness, and the name has stayed with the place. A virus isolated independently in Bolivia and called Uruma turned out to be the same agent. Early serological surveys through the 1960s found antibody in a high proportion of rural Amazonian populations, which established decades ago that human exposure was common even though recognized clinical cases remained few."],
    ["The virus hiding inside chikungunya statistics", "Chikungunya arrived in the Americas in 2013 and spread explosively, and clinical and serological diagnosis of fever with arthritis became routine. Because antibodies against the two viruses cross-react, and because few laboratories run neutralization panels, Mayaro cases are readily absorbed into chikungunya totals. Studies that applied specific testing in Brazil, Peru, Bolivia, Venezuela and Haiti repeatedly found Mayaro infections among patients labeled with dengue or chikungunya, which suggests the recorded burden reflects testing practice more than biology."],
    ["A canopy vector and the yellow fever parallel", "Haemagogus janthinomys feeds on monkeys high in the forest canopy and descends when trees are felled or when people work beneath them. This is the same ecology that produces sylvatic yellow fever cases in woodcutters, and the overlap means both viruses move into human populations along the edge of deforestation. If Mayaro adapted efficiently to Aedes aegypti, as chikungunya did through a single envelope mutation for Aedes albopictus, the barrier between a forest-edge infection and an urban epidemic would be a short one."],
    ["Bone as a target organ", "Work on chikungunya and Mayaro has shown that these viruses infect osteoblasts and alter the balance of RANKL and osteoprotegerin, tilting bone remodeling toward resorption. This is a different mechanism from classic inflammatory arthritis and may help explain the deep bone pain patients describe and the slow recovery of joint function. It also suggests why anti-inflammatory therapy alone gives incomplete relief, and it has made bone biology an unexpected part of alphavirus research."]] },

{ id: "sindbis-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "Sindbis virus", also: "SINV, Ockelbo disease, Pogosta disease, Karelian fever",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, prototype of the Sindbis antigenic complex and the standard laboratory model alphavirus",
  plain: "A bird virus with a worldwide distribution that causes rash and joint pain in northern Europe and southern Africa and serves as the workhorse of alphavirus research.",
  live: "Migratory and resident birds, particularly passerines such as thrushes and grouse, which carry the virus along flyways between Africa and northern Europe. The virus has the widest geographic distribution of any alphavirus, with records across Africa, Europe, the Middle East, Asia and Australia.",
  spread: "Mosquito bite, with ornithophilic Culex and Culiseta species in northern Europe, principally Culex torrentium and Culiseta morsitans, and Culex univittatus in Africa. Humans are dead-end hosts. Outbreak years in Finland have followed a roughly seven-year cycle tied to grouse population dynamics.",
  causes: "A self-limited illness with a widespread itchy maculopapular or papulovesicular rash, often starting on the trunk, together with arthralgia or arthritis of the ankles, wrists, knees and small joints, low-grade fever, headache and fatigue. The rash is frequently more prominent than the fever, which distinguishes it from many arboviruses. Joint symptoms resolve within weeks in most patients but persist for a year or more in a minority. Severe disease and death are not features.",
  mech: "Entry uses the E2 glycoprotein with natural resistance-associated macrophage protein NRAMP2 identified as a receptor in insect and mammalian systems, and fusion follows acidification of the endosome. Replication is cytoplasmic with a well-characterized subgenomic promoter that made this virus the template for countless expression vectors. Disease is driven by infection of synovial and dermal tissue with a monocyte and T-cell infiltrate, interferon and interleukin 6 production, and detection of viral RNA in skin and joint samples weeks into illness. Persistent arthralgia correlates with continuing immune activation rather than with ongoing viremia. In mouse models the same virus is a classic tool for studying neuronal apoptosis and age-dependent encephalitis, which does not occur in humans.",
  lab: "IgM and IgG serology, usually by immunofluorescence or enzyme immunoassay, with a fourfold rise or IgM seroconversion confirming infection. Reverse transcription PCR on serum is useful only in the first days. Skin biopsy, if taken, shows a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate. Because the viruses in this complex cross-react, interpretation in regions where other alphaviruses circulate requires neutralization testing.",
  tx: "Supportive care only: analgesia, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and antihistamines or emollients for the rash. No antiviral is available.",
  alt: "Corticosteroids have been used in persistent arthritis in individual cases without trial evidence. Physiotherapy and graded activity are the mainstay for prolonged joint symptoms.",
  res: "Antiviral resistance has no clinical meaning here. In the laboratory, however, this is the virus in which much of what is known about alphavirus escape and attenuating mutations was first worked out.",
  prev: "Mosquito avoidance during late summer in affected regions, including repellents and protective clothing during berry picking and outdoor work, which is when northern European cases cluster. No vaccine exists and none is in development, given the mildness of the disease.",
  look: "Other arthritogenic alphaviruses, especially Ross River and chikungunya in travelers. Parvovirus B19, which also gives rash with arthropathy. Rubella. Acute reactive arthritis. Lyme disease in the same northern European geography, which also causes rash and joint pain but with a different rash and a tick exposure. Drug eruption.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Sindbis virus is the prototype alphavirus of the laboratory and causes rash with arthritis under local names: Ockelbo in Sweden, Pogosta in Finland, Karelian fever in Russia."],
    ["Memory trick", "Three countries, three names, one virus: Ockelbo, Pogosta, Karelian fever. The rash leads and the joints follow."],
    ["Classic setting", "A Finnish adult picking berries in August of an outbreak year with an itchy spotty rash and painful swollen ankles."],
    ["Step focus", "Birds are the reservoir and humans are dead-end hosts, and the bird migration route is why an Egyptian isolate and a Scandinavian disease are the same virus."]],
  worm: [["Named for a village on the Nile", "The virus was isolated in 1952 from Culex mosquitoes trapped in the village of Sindbis, north of Cairo, during arbovirus surveys in Egypt. For twenty years it was regarded as a virus of mosquitoes and birds with no clear human disease. Only in the 1980s did investigators connect it to rash and arthritis syndromes that Scandinavian and Russian clinicians had described independently under the names Ockelbo disease, Pogosta disease and Karelian fever, and show these were the same agent carried north along bird migration routes."],
    ["The laboratory workhorse", "Sindbis virus grows to enormous titers, tolerates insertion of foreign genes after its subgenomic promoter, and infects a huge range of cultured cells, which made it the model for positive-strand RNA virus replication from the 1970s onward. Infectious complementary DNA clones of Sindbis were among the first built for any RNA virus, and the replicon vectors derived from it became standard tools for protein expression, gene delivery and vaccine platforms. A great deal of general knowledge about how enveloped viruses fuse and bud was learned from this one agent."],
    ["Grouse, berries and a seven-year rhythm", "Finnish epidemiologists noticed that Pogosta disease arrived in waves roughly every seven years, in 1974, 1981, 1988, 1995 and 2002, with hundreds to thousands of cases in outbreak summers and almost none between. The cycle appears to follow the population dynamics of forest grouse, which amplify the virus, together with mosquito abundance. Cases concentrate in late summer among people harvesting berries and mushrooms, so a virus named after an Egyptian village is in practice a hazard of the Nordic forest floor."],
    ["A model of neuronal death that never happens in people", "Injected into mice, Sindbis virus produces an encephalitis whose severity depends sharply on the animal's age, and this system became one of the principal models for virus-induced neuronal apoptosis and for how antibody can clear virus from neurons without killing them. None of that corresponds to human disease, in which the nervous system is spared and the joints and skin take the damage. It is a useful reminder that a model organism's pathology belongs to the model, not necessarily to the patient."]] },

{ id: "onyong-nyong-virus", k: "vir", g: "Togaviruses", nm: "O'nyong-nyong virus", also: "ONNV, joint breaker fever, Igbo-ora virus",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Alphavirus, family Togaviridae, Semliki Forest complex, a close relative of chikungunya virus and the only alphavirus transmitted mainly by Anopheles",
  plain: "An African virus almost identical to chikungunya that caused one of the largest arboviral epidemics ever recorded and is spread by malaria mosquitoes.",
  live: "Not firmly established. Non-human primates and possibly other vertebrates are suspected reservoirs, and the virus seems to vanish between epidemics, with decades of silence followed by sudden large outbreaks. Human-mosquito-human transmission sustains epidemics once they start.",
  spread: "Bite of Anopheles gambiae and Anopheles funestus, the same mosquitoes that transmit falciparum malaria, which is unique among medically important alphaviruses. Aedes species can transmit it experimentally but appear unimportant in nature. There is no person-to-person spread.",
  causes: "Abrupt fever with severe symmetric polyarthralgia of the knees, ankles, wrists and small joints, a prominent generalized maculopapular rash, and in a large proportion of patients tender cervical and generalized lymphadenopathy, which helps separate it clinically from chikungunya. Headache, retro-orbital pain and conjunctival injection are usual. Illness lasts days to weeks, joint pain can persist for months, and mortality is essentially absent despite attack rates above half the population in epidemic villages.",
  mech: "Attachment uses the E2 glycoprotein, and Mxra8 serves as a receptor for this group of arthritogenic alphaviruses. The genome is roughly three-quarters identical to chikungunya at the amino acid level, and the two cross-protect, so prior infection with one blunts disease from the other. Vector specificity maps substantially to the ability of the virus to overcome a midgut barrier in Anopheles, with the nonstructural protein nsP3 implicated in Anopheles infectivity. In the host, infection of fibroblasts, synovium and muscle with monocyte recruitment and interleukin 6, interferon gamma and monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 release produces the arthritis, and the pronounced lymphadenopathy reflects heavy replication in lymphoid tissue.",
  lab: "Serology with IgM capture and paired IgG, bearing in mind near-complete cross-reaction with chikungunya, which has caused repeated misattribution in both directions. Reverse transcription PCR with primers that distinguish the two is the reliable route during the viremic first days, and sequencing settles outbreak attribution. Leukopenia and lymphopenia are typical, and the epidemiologic clue of Anopheles transmission is itself diagnostically useful.",
  tx: "Supportive care, with analgesia and anti-inflammatory drugs for joint pain and fluids for the febrile phase. No antiviral exists.",
  alt: "Chronic arthralgia is managed as for chikungunya, with physiotherapy, graded activity and analgesia. No disease-modifying therapy has been tested in this infection.",
  res: "Not applicable. The important practical gap is that in malaria-endemic Africa a febrile patient is commonly treated for malaria, and a negative malaria test rarely prompts alphavirus testing, so the epidemiology remains poorly mapped.",
  prev: "Anopheles control, which means that insecticide-treated bed nets and indoor residual spraying aimed at malaria also reduce transmission of this virus, although both mosquitoes bite indoors at night so coverage matters. No vaccine exists, and chikungunya vaccines may offer cross-protection, which has been proposed as a reason to consider them in African deployment decisions.",
  look: "Chikungunya, which it mimics so closely that outbreaks have been reassigned between the two retrospectively. Malaria, which is the first consideration in the same patients. Dengue. Measles and rubella in a child with rash and fever. Acute HIV seroconversion illness. Rift Valley fever.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "O'nyong-nyong virus is the alphavirus transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes, unlike the Aedes and Culex transmission of the rest, and prominent lymphadenopathy sets it apart from chikungunya."],
    ["Memory trick", "The name is Acholi for weakening of the joints. An Anopheles delivering arthritis instead of malaria is the single hook."],
    ["Classic setting", "An East African village during a large epidemic, with most households affected, swollen tender neck nodes, a blotchy rash and knees too sore to kneel."],
    ["Step focus", "It is about seventy-five percent identical to chikungunya and the two cross-protect, which is why a chikungunya vaccine is discussed as a tool against both."]],
  worm: [["The largest arboviral epidemic nobody remembers", "Beginning in 1959 near Gulu in northern Uganda, an epidemic of fever with rash and joint pain spread through Kenya, Tanzania, Malawi, Mozambique and Senegal over the following four years and is estimated to have infected more than two million people. Attack rates in some villages exceeded seventy percent. Because almost nobody died, the outbreak attracted little international attention compared with far smaller hemorrhagic fever events, and it remains one of the largest arboviral epidemics on record by case number."],
    ["A name in Acholi", "O'nyong-nyong comes from the Acholi language of northern Uganda and is usually translated as weakening of the joints, a description patients gave of being unable to straighten up or walk. The Yoruba name Igbo-ora, after a Nigerian town where a 1984 outbreak occurred, was applied to what proved to be the same virus and is sometimes translated as the disease that breaks the bones. In both cases the local name is a more accurate clinical summary than any formal label the virus has received."],
    ["Why Anopheles, and why that matters", "Every other alphavirus of medical importance relies on Aedes or Culex. This one grows efficiently in Anopheles gambiae and funestus, the principal African malaria vectors, which are night-biting, indoor-feeding and extraordinarily abundant. The consequence is that the virus has access to the most efficient mosquito populations on the continent, which helps explain epidemic attack rates above half a village. It also means malaria vector control measures are, incidentally, control measures for this virus."],
    ["Long silences and a confusing cousin", "After the 1959 to 1962 epidemic the virus essentially disappeared for over twenty years, resurfacing at Igbo-ora in Nigeria in 1984 and again in southern Uganda in 1996 and 1997. Where it persists between epidemics is unknown. Serological surveys are complicated by chikungunya antibodies that look nearly the same, and at least one outbreak originally attributed to one virus was later reassigned to the other. The epidemiology that exists therefore rests heavily on the few outbreaks where molecular testing was performed."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "st-louis-encephalitis-virus", k: "vir", g: "Flaviviruses", nm: "St Louis encephalitis virus", also: "SLEV, St Louis encephalitis",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, icosahedral, genus Orthoflavivirus, family Flaviviridae, Japanese encephalitis serocomplex",
  plain: "A North American mosquito virus that causes encephalitis chiefly in older adults and was the main cause of epidemic encephalitis in the United States before West Nile arrived.",
  live: "Birds, especially house sparrows, finches, pigeons and doves, amplified in a Culex mosquito cycle. Humans and other mammals are dead-end hosts with viremia too low and brief to infect a feeding mosquito.",
  spread: "Mosquito bite, with Culex pipiens and Culex quinquefasciatus in cities and towns, Culex tarsalis in the irrigated west and Culex nigripalpus in Florida. Urban outbreaks have been tied to stagnant water in storm drains, septic ditches and neglected pools. There is no person-to-person spread, although rare transmission through transplantation is theoretically possible as for other flaviviruses.",
  causes: "The large majority of infections are silent. Symptomatic illness runs a spectrum from a nonspecific febrile headache to aseptic meningitis to frank encephalitis with confusion, tremor, ataxia, cranial nerve signs and coma. A distinctive feature is urinary dysfunction, with dysuria and retention reported in a notable minority and attributed to involvement of the brainstem and spinal autonomic pathways. Severity rises steeply with age, and case fatality among older adults with encephalitis has reached twenty percent in outbreaks. Survivors may be left with tremor, gait disturbance, fatigue and memory problems.",
  mech: "The envelope protein binds cell surface attachment factors and the virus enters by endocytosis, fusing at low pH. The nonstructural proteins assemble a replication complex on rearranged endoplasmic reticulum membranes, and NS5 methyltransferase and NS2B-NS3 protease activity support replication while NS5 also antagonizes interferon signaling through STAT. After replication in skin and draining lymph nodes a transient viremia seeds the central nervous system, probably across cerebral endothelium. Neurons of the thalamus, substantia nigra, brainstem and anterior horn are infected, which fits the tremor, parkinsonian features and autonomic bladder signs. Much of the neuronal injury is attributable to the inflammatory response, and the age gradient tracks declining innate immunity and barrier function.",
  lab: "IgM capture serology on serum and cerebrospinal fluid, with plaque reduction neutralization to resolve cross-reaction with West Nile, dengue and other flaviviruses and to account for prior yellow fever or Japanese encephalitis vaccination. Reverse transcription PCR on cerebrospinal fluid is insensitive because viremia precedes neurologic illness. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with raised protein and normal glucose. Hyponatremia from inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion is common enough to be a recognized feature.",
  tx: "Supportive care: seizure management, airway protection, fluid and sodium management, and prevention of the complications of prolonged immobility. No antiviral has shown benefit.",
  alt: "Interferon alfa-2b was studied in a small randomized trial during a Louisiana outbreak without clear benefit. Rehabilitation and long-term neurologic follow-up are the substantive interventions for survivors.",
  res: "Not applicable in the absence of effective antiviral therapy. The practical constraint is the same as for other arboviral encephalitides: by the time neurologic symptoms bring a patient in, the virus has already seeded the brain.",
  prev: "Mosquito control focused on urban standing water, including storm drain treatment during outbreaks, plus screens, repellents and avoidance of dusk exposure. Surveillance uses sentinel chicken flocks, mosquito pools and dead bird reporting. No human vaccine exists, and development stalled once West Nile virus became the dominant concern in the same regions.",
  look: "West Nile virus, which is now far commoner, shares the vector and the bird reservoir and produces an almost identical picture. Western equine encephalitis virus. Herpes simplex encephalitis, which is treatable and favors the temporal lobe. Enteroviral meningitis. Bacterial meningitis. Autoimmune encephalitis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "St Louis encephalitis is the flaviviral encephalitis of older North American adults, spread by Culex from birds, and dysuria or urinary retention is its oddly specific clue."],
    ["Memory trick", "St Louis picks on seniors; La Crosse picks on children. Both are American summer encephalitis, opposite ends of the age curve."],
    ["Classic setting", "A late summer urban outbreak in a Mississippi valley city, with elderly patients confused and tremulous and storm drains being treated on the news."],
    ["Step focus", "It belongs to the Japanese encephalitis serocomplex alongside West Nile, which is why their serology cross-reacts and neutralization testing is needed to separate them."]],
  worm: [["The epidemic that named it", "In the summer of 1933 an outbreak of encephalitis struck St Louis and St Louis County, Missouri, with more than a thousand recognized cases and about two hundred deaths. Investigators from the United States Public Health Service isolated a virus from brain tissue and transmitted it to monkeys, establishing the agent within months, which was fast work for the era. The outbreak also prompted some of the first systematic mosquito and bird surveys for a human virus in the United States, and the methods developed then shaped American arbovirus surveillance for decades."],
    ["The 1975 wave", "The largest American epidemic of this virus occurred in 1975, with roughly two thousand cases across thirty-odd states, concentrated in the Ohio and Mississippi river valleys and in Chicago. Case fatality among older patients ran between ten and twenty percent. The outbreak drove heavy aerial insecticide spraying over urban areas and generated public controversy about pesticide exposure that influenced mosquito control policy long afterward, including the shift toward larval source reduction rather than adult spraying as the first response."],
    ["Overtaken by a relative", "Before 1999 this was the leading cause of epidemic arboviral encephalitis in the United States. West Nile virus then arrived in New York, spread to the Pacific in four years and has caused far more disease every year since. The two viruses share Culex vectors, bird reservoirs and the same age-dependent severity, and whether West Nile actively displaced its relative through cross-immunity in birds or simply out-competed it ecologically remains debated. Reported cases of the older virus have fallen to a few dozen a year."],
    ["Tremor, parkinsonism and the basal ganglia", "Survivors of severe infection sometimes carry a persistent tremor, bradykinesia and gait disturbance resembling parkinsonism, and autopsy series show inflammation and neuronal loss in the substantia nigra and thalamus. The same pattern appears after West Nile and Japanese encephalitis, and it was reported after the encephalitis lethargica epidemic of the early twentieth century. The convergence has kept alive a long-running question about whether viral injury to dopaminergic neurons can set the stage for later degenerative disease."]] },

{ id: "powassan-virus", k: "vir", g: "Flaviviruses", nm: "Powassan virus", also: "POWV, deer tick virus for lineage II",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Orthoflavivirus, family Flaviviridae, tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, with lineage I in Ixodes cookei and lineage II, deer tick virus, in Ixodes scapularis",
  plain: "The only tick-borne encephalitis virus of North America, rare but frequently devastating, and transmissible within minutes of a tick attaching.",
  live: "Small and medium-sized mammals: groundhogs, skunks and other mustelids for lineage I, and white-footed mice and other small rodents for lineage II, with deer maintaining the tick population. The virus is maintained by transstadial and some transovarial passage in the ticks themselves.",
  spread: "Bite of an Ixodes tick, with Ixodes cookei, the woodchuck tick, carrying lineage I and Ixodes scapularis, the black-legged or deer tick, carrying lineage II. Transmission can occur within fifteen minutes to a few hours of attachment because the virus sits in the tick salivary glands, which contrasts sharply with the day or two needed for Lyme disease. Rare transmission through blood transfusion and through unpasteurized milk from infected goats, as with related viruses, has been described or suspected.",
  causes: "Many infections appear to be silent or mild. Recognized disease is often severe: fever, headache and vomiting progressing over days to meningoencephalitis with confusion, seizures, cranial nerve palsies, hemiplegia, movement disorders and occasionally a poliomyelitis-like flaccid paralysis. Case fatality among reported encephalitis cases has been approximately ten to fifteen percent, and roughly half of survivors are left with lasting deficits including hemiplegia, memory impairment, headache and muscle wasting. Infants and older adults are overrepresented among severe cases.",
  mech: "The envelope protein mediates attachment and endosomal fusion, and replication proceeds on endoplasmic reticulum-derived membranes as in other flaviviruses, with NS5 antagonizing interferon-stimulated signaling and NS4B interfering with the interferon response. Tick saliva delivered with the virus contains immunomodulatory proteins that suppress local inflammation and complement, which helps establish infection at the bite. The virus is strongly neurotropic, infecting neurons of the cerebellum, basal ganglia, brainstem, thalamus and spinal anterior horn, with Purkinje cell loss and anterior horn involvement explaining the ataxia and flaccid weakness. Autopsy studies show widespread lymphocytic perivascular inflammation and microglial nodules, and the severity of outcome correlates with the extent of neuronal loss rather than with viral load at presentation.",
  lab: "IgM and IgG serology on serum and cerebrospinal fluid, with plaque reduction neutralization to confirm and to separate it from other flaviviruses, including vaccine-induced antibody from tick-borne encephalitis or yellow fever vaccination. Reverse transcription PCR on cerebrospinal fluid or blood is positive in a minority and is more useful in immunocompromised patients with prolonged viremia. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with raised protein. Magnetic resonance imaging frequently shows changes in the basal ganglia, thalamus, cerebellum and brainstem.",
  tx: "Supportive neurocritical care. Seizure control, management of raised intracranial pressure, airway protection, and prolonged rehabilitation. No antiviral therapy is established.",
  alt: "Intravenous immunoglobulin and high-dose corticosteroid have been used in individual cases with mixed and uninterpretable results. Monoclonal antibodies against the envelope protein protect animals and are an active research direction. Tick-borne encephalitis vaccine does not reliably protect against this virus despite serocomplex relatedness.",
  res: "No antiviral resistance issue arises. The real obstacle is the speed of transmission and the fact that neuroinvasion may be established before the illness is recognized, which limits any post-exposure strategy.",
  prev: "Avoidance of tick habitat, permethrin-treated clothing, repellents and daily tick checks, with the important qualification that prompt tick removal, which prevents Lyme disease, is much less reliable here because transmission is so rapid. No human vaccine is licensed. Awareness in the northeastern and upper midwestern United States and in eastern Canada and Russia, where the virus also occurs, underpins recognition.",
  look: "Other causes of arboviral encephalitis, especially West Nile virus in the same summer season. Lyme neuroborreliosis, which shares the tick and the geography and is treatable. Anaplasmosis and babesiosis from the same tick. Herpes simplex encephalitis. Enteroviral and autoimmune encephalitis. Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis in a child.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Powassan virus is the North American tick-borne flaviviral encephalitis, and transmission can occur within fifteen minutes of attachment, unlike the thirty-six hours needed for Lyme disease."],
    ["Memory trick", "Same tick, two very different clocks: Borrelia needs a day and a half, Powassan needs a quarter of an hour."],
    ["Classic setting", "A New England or Great Lakes patient in early summer with fever, then confusion, ataxia and weakness, and magnetic resonance imaging showing basal ganglia and cerebellar change."],
    ["Step focus", "It sits in the tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, and roughly half of survivors of encephalitis carry permanent neurologic deficits."]],
  worm: [["A town in Ontario, 1958", "A five-year-old boy in Powassan, Ontario, died of encephalitis in 1958, and virus isolated from his brain proved to be a new flavivirus, which took the town's name. For the next forty years the virus was a curiosity, with only about twenty human cases reported in all of North America. The ecology that was worked out then involved the woodchuck tick, a species that bites people infrequently, which neatly explained why human disease was so rare and why it stayed rare for so long."],
    ["The deer tick lineage changed the arithmetic", "In 1997 a genetically distinct variant was isolated from Ixodes scapularis in New England and named deer tick virus. That tick bites humans readily and is the same species responsible for Lyme disease, babesiosis and anaplasmosis, with infection rates in some local tick populations of a few percent. Human cases have climbed from a handful per decade to dozens per year since, concentrated in the same counties where Lyme disease is intense. The virus did not change much; the vector did."],
    ["Fifteen minutes versus thirty-six hours", "Borrelia burgdorferi lives in the tick midgut and must migrate to the salivary glands after feeding begins, which takes a day or more and gives tick checks their protective value. This virus is already present in the salivary glands of an unfed tick, so it can be injected almost immediately. The practical consequence is uncomfortable: the advice that reliably prevents Lyme disease offers much less protection here, and prevention depends on not being bitten rather than on finding the tick early."],
    ["No vaccine, despite a close relative with one", "Tick-borne encephalitis virus, a member of the same serocomplex, has effective inactivated vaccines used across Europe and Asia. Cross-neutralization between the two is partial and studies suggest that vaccine does not reliably protect against Powassan. With fewer than fifty reported cases a year in North America, there has been no commercial path to a dedicated vaccine, so research has concentrated instead on monoclonal antibodies and on candidates that might cover the whole serocomplex."]] },

{ id: "usutu-virus", k: "vir", g: "Flaviviruses", nm: "Usutu virus", also: "USUV",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Orthoflavivirus, family Flaviviridae, Japanese encephalitis serocomplex, a close relative of West Nile virus",
  plain: "An African bird virus that moved into Europe, killed large numbers of blackbirds, and occasionally causes human fever or encephalitis.",
  live: "Wild birds, with Eurasian blackbirds and house sparrows particularly susceptible in Europe and great grey owls dying in captivity, alongside bats and horses as incidental hosts. The virus overwinters in resident bird populations and in hibernating mosquitoes, and multiple lineages now circulate in Europe.",
  spread: "Bite of ornithophilic Culex mosquitoes, chiefly Culex pipiens, the same vector as West Nile virus, with which it frequently co-circulates. Humans are dead-end hosts. Transmission through blood donation is a recognized theoretical risk and asymptomatic viremic blood donors have been identified in Italy and Austria.",
  causes: "Most human infections appear to be asymptomatic, detected only through blood donor screening or seroprevalence studies. Symptomatic cases have included fever with rash and headache, and a small number of neuroinvasive cases with meningoencephalitis, mostly in immunocompromised patients, including after chemotherapy or transplantation. Idiopathic facial paralysis and a case of neuroinvasive disease in a patient with lymphoma have been reported. The avian disease is far more dramatic than the human one, with mass blackbird mortality the usual way an incursion is noticed.",
  mech: "Entry, replication and interferon antagonism follow the general flaviviral pattern, with the envelope protein mediating attachment and low-pH fusion and NS5 and NS4B interfering with interferon signaling. In susceptible birds the virus produces high viremia with necrosis of spleen, liver and brain, which is why blackbird die-offs are so visible. In humans viremia is low and transient, which is consistent with the dead-end host role and the rarity of neuroinvasion. The concentration of human neuroinvasive cases among immunosuppressed patients suggests that intact interferon and T-cell responses normally contain the virus before it reaches the central nervous system.",
  lab: "Serology cross-reacts heavily with West Nile virus, which is the central diagnostic problem, since the two share a vector, a season and a geography. Virus-specific reverse transcription PCR on blood, urine or cerebrospinal fluid, and neutralization assays run side by side against both viruses, are what distinguish them. Blood services in several European countries screen donations with nucleic acid tests that detect it. Avian carcass surveillance in blackbirds often provides the earliest signal of local circulation.",
  tx: "Supportive care. There is no specific antiviral therapy, and management of neuroinvasive disease follows the general approach to viral encephalitis.",
  alt: "Reduction of immunosuppression where feasible has been described in individual transplant and hematology cases. Intravenous immunoglobulin has been used anecdotally, as for West Nile virus, without controlled evidence.",
  res: "Not applicable. The practical issue is recognition: without specific testing, cases are likely to be reported as West Nile virus or as encephalitis of unknown cause.",
  prev: "Mosquito avoidance and control of Culex pipiens breeding in urban water, plus blood donor screening in affected regions. Surveillance of dead wild birds provides early warning. No vaccine exists for humans, and the public health response has largely been folded into existing West Nile programs.",
  look: "West Nile virus above all, which shares vector, host, season and serology. Tick-borne encephalitis in European patients with neurologic illness. Enteroviral meningitis. Other causes of fever with rash in summer. In birds, avian influenza and other causes of mass mortality.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Usutu virus is a West Nile-like flavivirus in Europe that kills blackbirds and causes rare human neuroinvasive disease, mainly in immunocompromised patients."],
    ["Memory trick", "Usutu is West Nile's quieter sibling: same Culex, same continent now, far fewer people and far more dead blackbirds."],
    ["Classic setting", "A late summer in Austria, Italy or Germany with blackbirds dropping from the trees and a transplant recipient admitted with meningoencephalitis."],
    ["Step focus", "Serological cross-reaction with West Nile virus is near total, so neutralization or specific PCR is what separates them and is why reported case numbers are unreliable."]],
  worm: [["From a South African river to Vienna", "The virus was first isolated in 1959 from Culex neavei mosquitoes near the Usutu River in Swaziland, now Eswatini, and was regarded for forty years as an African virus of no particular importance. In 2001 blackbirds began dying in large numbers around Vienna, and sequencing of their tissues identified this virus. Retrospective examination of archived material showed it had reached Tuscany as early as 1996, so its arrival in Europe preceded its recognition by at least five years."],
    ["The blackbird as sentinel", "Eurasian blackbirds are unusually susceptible, and local populations in affected areas of Austria and Germany have fallen sharply during incursion years before recovering. Because dead garden birds are conspicuous and the public reports them, blackbird mortality has become a practical early warning system for flaviviral circulation in central Europe, and ornithological networks now feed into human public health surveillance. It is one of the few settings where amateur bird reporting contributes directly to outbreak detection."],
    ["Hidden in the West Nile shadow", "The two viruses are close enough that antibody against one neutralizes the other to a degree, and most European diagnostic laboratories test for West Nile virus alone. Seroprevalence surveys using specific neutralization have found antibody in a few percent of blood donors in parts of Italy and Germany, far more than the handful of reported clinical cases. The virus is therefore probably infecting far more people than records show, with the clinical burden concentrated almost entirely in those who cannot mount a normal immune response."],
    ["A natural experiment in cross-immunity", "Because this virus and West Nile virus share mosquitoes and birds across much of Europe, researchers have asked whether prior infection with one alters the course of the other. Experimental work in birds and in mice shows partial cross-protection, and field data have been read as suggesting that heavy circulation of one may dampen the other. The question matters practically: if cross-immunity is real, then surveillance of one virus cannot be interpreted without knowing the history of the other."]] },

{ id: "kyasanur-forest-disease-virus", k: "vir", g: "Flaviviruses", nm: "Kyasanur Forest disease virus", also: "KFDV, monkey fever, monkey disease, Alkhurma virus for the related Arabian variant",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Orthoflavivirus, family Flaviviridae, tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, mammalian tick-borne group",
  plain: "A tick-borne Indian flavivirus that causes hemorrhagic fever with a late neurologic relapse and announces itself through dying monkeys in the forest.",
  live: "Small rodents, shrews, porcupines and bats maintain the virus, with black-faced langurs and bonnet macaques suffering high mortality and serving as amplifying hosts whose deaths mark local activity. Ticks maintain the virus transstadially.",
  spread: "Bite of nymphal Haemaphysalis spinigera ticks and related species, acquired by people entering forest to graze cattle, gather firewood or harvest non-timber forest products. Handling infected monkey carcasses and laboratory aerosol exposure also transmit it. There is no established person-to-person spread, although the related Alkhurma virus in Saudi Arabia has been linked to contact with camel and sheep products and raw milk.",
  causes: "An abrupt illness after three to eight days: high fever, severe headache, myalgia, prostration, conjunctival suffusion, vomiting and diarrhea, with bleeding from the gums, nose and gastrointestinal tract, hypotension, bradycardia relative to the fever, leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. Many patients then improve. In a sizeable minority a second febrile phase begins one to three weeks later with meningoencephalitis, severe headache, neck stiffness, tremor, visual disturbance and altered consciousness. Case fatality is commonly quoted between two and ten percent. Convalescence is prolonged.",
  mech: "The envelope protein mediates attachment and endosomal fusion, and replication occurs on endoplasmic reticulum membranes with NS5 and NS4B suppressing interferon responses. Tick saliva again assists establishment at the bite site. The virus replicates in macrophages and dendritic cells and reaches high titer in blood, infecting liver, spleen and bone marrow, which accounts for the leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and transaminase rise. Hemorrhage reflects thrombocytopenia, endothelial dysfunction and consumption of clotting factors rather than a single toxin. The biphasic pattern, shared with tick-borne encephalitis virus, is interpreted as an initial systemic phase followed by central nervous system invasion in those who do not clear the virus, with the inflammatory response contributing substantially to the neurologic phase.",
  lab: "Reverse transcription PCR on blood is the test of choice in the first week, when viremia is high. IgM capture serology becomes positive in the second week, with neutralization used to distinguish it from other tick-borne flaviviruses and from dengue. Complete blood count shows leukopenia and thrombocytopenia, and liver enzymes are raised. Virus isolation requires high containment, and the agent is handled in biosafety level 4 facilities in many countries because of aerosol infectivity.",
  tx: "Supportive care: fluid resuscitation, transfusion of blood products as needed, correction of electrolytes, and management of the neurologic phase as for any viral encephalitis. No antiviral is established.",
  alt: "Ribavirin has in vitro activity against some tick-borne flaviviruses but has not been shown to help in this disease. Favipiravir has shown activity in animal models and is a research direction. Supportive intensive care is what changes outcome.",
  res: "Not applicable therapeutically. The pressing operational problem is vaccine performance rather than drug resistance.",
  prev: "A formalin-inactivated vaccine produced in India has been used in the endemic districts of Karnataka since the 1960s, requiring two doses and boosters, with effectiveness estimates that have fallen well short of expectations and uptake that has been patchy. Protective clothing, tick repellents including dimethyl phthalate applied to legs, and insecticide application around monkey death sites form the rest of the program. Monkey death surveillance triggers local vaccination and tick control.",
  look: "Dengue, which is the first consideration in the same population and season. Scrub typhus and other rickettsial infection. Leptospirosis. Malaria. Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever in other parts of the region. In the neurologic phase, Japanese encephalitis and tuberculous meningitis. The related Alkhurma virus in travelers from the Arabian peninsula.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Kyasanur Forest disease is a tick-borne flaviviral hemorrhagic fever of southern India with a biphasic course, the second phase being meningoencephalitis, and monkey deaths as the sentinel event."],
    ["Memory trick", "Monkey fever: dying langurs in a Karnataka forest, a tick on the leg, bleeding first and the brain second."],
    ["Classic setting", "A cattle grazer or firewood collector in the Western Ghats of Karnataka in the dry season, after langurs were found dead nearby."],
    ["Step focus", "It belongs to the tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, which is why the course is biphasic like tick-borne encephalitis but with prominent hemorrhage in the first phase."]],
  worm: [["Discovered through dead monkeys, 1957", "In early 1957 villagers in the Kyasanur forest of Shimoga district, Karnataka, reported large numbers of dead langurs, followed by a severe febrile illness with bleeding among people who worked in the forest. Investigators from the Virus Research Centre in Pune isolated a new flavivirus from a sick monkey and from patients, and the local name monkey fever predates the formal one. The outbreak coincided with forest clearance for cattle grazing, which brought people and nymphal ticks into sustained contact."],
    ["A vaccine that works less well than hoped", "An inactivated vaccine grown in chick embryo fibroblasts has been produced in India and offered in endemic districts since the 1960s, with a primary course of two doses and annual boosters. Field effectiveness studies have found protection well below what the schedule assumes, particularly with incomplete dosing, and coverage has been uneven. Vaccine supply interruptions in the 2010s coincided with case increases. It remains one of the clearer examples of a disease-specific vaccine whose real-world performance has never matched its intention."],
    ["The disease spread as the forest shrank", "For four decades cases were confined to a few districts of Karnataka. Since the 2010s human cases and infected ticks have been identified in Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Goa and Maharashtra. The expansion has been attributed to forest fragmentation, cattle movement, changes in land use that increase the edge between forest and settlement, and better surveillance. Whichever factors dominate, the pattern matches the general rule for tick-borne zoonoses: the hazard rises where humans, livestock and wildlife share a boundary."],
    ["A relative on the Arabian peninsula", "In 1995 a hemorrhagic fever in Jeddah was traced to a flavivirus later named Alkhurma virus, which proved to be a close variant of the Indian virus with roughly ninety percent nucleotide identity. Cases have been associated with sheep and camel contact, tick bite and consumption of unpasteurized milk, and the illness combines hemorrhage with encephalitis in similar proportions. How two such closely related viruses came to occupy India and the Arabian peninsula without known intermediate foci remains an open question, with livestock trade among the proposed routes."]] },

{ id: "omsk-hemorrhagic-fever-virus", k: "vir", g: "Flaviviruses", nm: "Omsk hemorrhagic fever virus", also: "OHFV, Omsk hemorrhagic fever",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, enveloped, genus Orthoflavivirus, family Flaviviridae, tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, mammalian tick-borne group",
  plain: "A Siberian tick-borne flavivirus that causes hemorrhagic fever and is strongly tied to muskrat trapping in the western Siberian wetlands.",
  live: "Water voles, muskrats and other small rodents in the marshes and lake country of western Siberia, with Dermacentor and Ixodes ticks maintaining the virus by transstadial and transovarial passage. Muskrats, introduced to Siberia for fur in the early twentieth century, are highly susceptible and die in epizootics.",
  spread: "Tick bite, chiefly Dermacentor reticulatus and Dermacentor marginatus, and also direct contact with infected muskrats during trapping and skinning, which is thought to be the dominant route for the occupational cases. Contact with the water and lake mud of an epizootic area has been implicated, as has aerosol exposure in laboratories. Consumption of unpasteurized goat milk has been suspected, and rare secondary household transmission has been reported.",
  causes: "An abrupt illness after three to eight days with high fever, chills, severe headache, myalgia, prostration, conjunctival injection and a petechial rash, together with bleeding from the nose, gums and gastrointestinal tract, leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. A second febrile wave occurs in up to half of patients one to two weeks later, with pneumonia, nephropathy and central nervous system involvement including meningism, though frank encephalitis is less prominent than in tick-borne encephalitis. Case fatality is low, commonly quoted at one to three percent, but convalescence is long, with hearing loss, hair loss and persistent fatigue and cognitive complaints reported in survivors.",
  mech: "Replication follows the standard flaviviral scheme, with envelope-mediated entry, membrane-associated replication and interferon antagonism by NS5 and NS4B. Infection of macrophages, dendritic cells and endothelium produces high viremia, and bone marrow and lymphoid involvement accounts for the leukopenia and thrombocytopenia. Capillary injury and platelet consumption rather than a hemorrhagic toxin drive the bleeding. Compared with its close relative tick-borne encephalitis virus, this virus is relatively less neurotropic and more vasculotropic, and the genetic basis for that difference lies in a small number of envelope and nonstructural changes, which has made the pair a natural comparison for studying flaviviral tissue tropism.",
  lab: "Reverse transcription PCR on blood during the first week of fever, when viremia is substantial. IgM and IgG serology thereafter, with neutralization needed to separate it from tick-borne encephalitis virus and from prior tick-borne encephalitis vaccination, which is widespread in the same region. Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases and proteinuria are usual. Live virus work requires high containment.",
  tx: "Supportive care with fluid and blood product management, monitoring of renal function and treatment of secondary bacterial pneumonia. No specific antiviral is established.",
  alt: "Ribavirin and favipiravir have been examined in laboratory and animal systems; neither is established in human disease. Russian practice has included immunoglobulin preparations, without controlled evidence of benefit.",
  res: "Not applicable in the absence of antiviral therapy.",
  prev: "Occupational protection for trappers and fur handlers, protective clothing and gloves, tick repellents and prompt tick removal, and avoidance of handling sick or dead muskrats. The inactivated tick-borne encephalitis vaccines used across Russia provide partial cross-protection and have been proposed as a stopgap, but no dedicated licensed vaccine is in general use. Control of muskrat epizootics and surveillance in endemic districts form the rest of the program.",
  look: "Tick-borne encephalitis, which shares the vector and region but presents with encephalitis rather than hemorrhage. Hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome from hantaviruses, which is common in the same part of Russia. Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever further south. Leptospirosis. Tularemia, also a hazard of trapping. Severe influenza.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Omsk hemorrhagic fever is a tick-borne flavivirus of western Siberia associated with muskrat trapping, causing hemorrhagic fever with a biphasic course and low mortality."],
    ["Memory trick", "Omsk is the hemorrhagic twin of tick-borne encephalitis: same serocomplex, same forest, bleeding instead of brain, and a muskrat pelt in the story."],
    ["Classic setting", "A fur trapper in Omsk or Novosibirsk oblast in spring, with fever, nosebleeds, a petechial rash and a second wave of fever a fortnight later."],
    ["Step focus", "Direct contact with infected muskrats, not only tick bite, is a recognized transmission route, which is unusual among tick-borne flaviviruses."]],
  worm: [["An epidemic that followed a fur industry", "Outbreaks of hemorrhagic fever were recognized in the Omsk and Novosibirsk regions of western Siberia in the mid-1940s, and the virus was isolated in 1947 by Mikhail Chumakov's group. The timing was not coincidental: muskrats had been introduced from North America in the 1920s and 1930s to build a fur industry, and the species proved highly susceptible, dying in epizootics and transmitting infection to the trappers who handled them. A deliberate wildlife introduction had created a new human disease."],
    ["A sibling that attacks different tissue", "This virus and tick-borne encephalitis virus share the same serocomplex, overlapping geography, related tick vectors and a high degree of sequence identity, yet one causes hemorrhagic fever with modest neurologic involvement and the other causes encephalitis with little bleeding. Comparative studies of the two have become a standard route for asking which residues in the envelope and nonstructural proteins determine whether a flavivirus is vasculotropic or neurotropic, a question that also bears on dengue and on the behavior of yellow fever virus."],
    ["Why case numbers may understate it", "Several thousand cases were recorded in the decades after discovery, with activity waxing and waning alongside muskrat populations and trapping intensity. Since the 1990s reported numbers have been small. Diagnostic separation from tick-borne encephalitis requires neutralization testing that is not routinely done, and tick-borne encephalitis vaccination is widespread in Russia, which further confuses serology. Local clinicians have long argued that the recorded burden reflects the testing available rather than the amount of virus in the marshes."],
    ["Lake mud, milk and the limits of a vector label", "Calling this a tick-borne virus is accurate but incomplete. Investigations have implicated contact with water and sediment in epizootic areas, handling of carcasses, consumption of raw goat milk and occasional household transmission, in addition to tick bite. Multiple routes are a recurring feature of the mammalian tick-borne flaviviruses, and it has practical consequences: advice built only around tick avoidance would leave the occupational exposures that account for much of the recognized disease untouched."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "echovirus", k: "vir", g: "Picornaviruses", nm: "Echovirus", also: "ECHO virus, enteric cytopathic human orphan virus, Enterovirus B serotypes",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, non-enveloped, icosahedral, genus Enterovirus, species Enterovirus B, family Picornaviridae, acid stable, roughly thirty recognized serotypes",
  plain: "The commonest cause of viral meningitis in children, a family of enteroviruses named for being found before anyone knew what disease they caused.",
  live: "Humans only. The virus replicates in the pharynx and then in the gut, and is shed in stool for weeks after infection, which maintains circulation in communities with young children.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral spread above all, with respiratory droplet transmission early in infection. Summer and early autumn peaks in temperate climates, nursery and household clusters, and transmission through contaminated water including swimming pools. Vertical transmission around delivery produces severe neonatal disease, and nosocomial spread in neonatal units is well documented.",
  causes: "Most infections are asymptomatic or a nonspecific febrile illness. Recognized syndromes include aseptic meningitis, which is its signature, a nonspecific exanthem resembling rubella or measles, herpangina, pleurodynia, acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis, myocarditis and pericarditis, hepatitis and, most seriously, neonatal sepsis-like illness with hepatic necrosis, coagulopathy, myocarditis and meningoencephalitis, which carries substantial mortality. In patients with agammaglobulinemia it causes chronic meningoencephalitis and a dermatomyositis-like syndrome.",
  mech: "Different serotypes use different receptors, with decay-accelerating factor CD55 and the coxsackievirus-adenovirus receptor employed by many Enterovirus B members, and integrin and heparan sulfate interactions reported. The non-enveloped capsid is acid stable, which lets it pass the stomach intact. Internal ribosome entry site-driven translation produces a polyprotein cleaved by viral 2A and 3C proteases, and 2A also cleaves host eIF4G to shut down cap-dependent host translation while 3C cleaves transcription factors. Replication occurs on rearranged membranes, and release follows cell lysis. Spread from gut-associated lymphoid tissue through a viremia reaches meninges, heart, liver and skin. Antibody is the critical defense, which is why neonates without transplacental antibody and patients with antibody deficiency suffer severe and chronic disease.",
  lab: "Reverse transcription PCR on cerebrospinal fluid is the test of choice for meningitis and is far more sensitive than culture, with pan-enterovirus primers detecting the group but not identifying the serotype. Throat and stool PCR support the diagnosis but cannot prove causation given prolonged shedding. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, sometimes neutrophil predominant in the first day, with normal glucose and mildly raised protein. Serotyping by sequencing of the VP1 region is used in outbreak investigation.",
  tx: "Supportive care. Analgesia, antipyretics and fluids, with hospitalization for neonates and for patients needing intensive support. Established enteroviral meningitis in an otherwise healthy child resolves without antiviral therapy.",
  alt: "Intravenous immunoglobulin, which contains neutralizing antibody to common serotypes, is used in neonatal disease and in antibody-deficient patients with chronic infection, and is the mainstay in the latter group. Pocapavir and pleconaril, capsid-binding inhibitors, have been used on a compassionate basis in neonatal and immunodeficient cases with mixed results, and resistance emerges quickly on monotherapy.",
  res: "Capsid inhibitors select resistant variants rapidly because the target is a single hydrophobic pocket in VP1 and the mutational cost is low, which is a principal reason none has reached licensure. Immunoglobulin lots vary in neutralizing titer against individual serotypes, which limits its predictability.",
  prev: "Hand hygiene, careful diaper and nappy handling, exclusion of infected staff from neonatal units, and attention to pool and water hygiene. No vaccine exists or is likely, given the number of serotypes and the mildness of most infection. In antibody-deficient patients, adequate immunoglobulin replacement is the practical preventive measure.",
  look: "Other enteroviruses, including coxsackieviruses, which cause overlapping syndromes and are separated only by laboratory typing. Herpes simplex meningitis, which is important to exclude in a neonate. Bacterial meningitis, particularly in the first day when neutrophils may predominate. Mumps and lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus. In a neonate with sepsis-like illness, bacterial sepsis and inborn errors of metabolism.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Echoviruses are the leading cause of aseptic meningitis, and the cerebrospinal fluid shows lymphocytes with normal glucose and a negative Gram stain."],
    ["Memory trick", "ECHO stands for Enteric Cytopathic Human Orphan: an orphan because the virus was found first and the disease assigned later."],
    ["Classic setting", "A school-age child in August with fever, headache, photophobia and a stiff neck, well enough to be discharged in two days, or a neonate on day five with sepsis, a huge liver and bleeding."],
    ["Step focus", "Antibody is the critical defense, so neonates before transplacental antibody and patients with X-linked agammaglobulinemia get severe or chronic enteroviral disease and are given immunoglobulin."]],
  worm: [["Named for the absence of a disease", "In the early 1950s the polio laboratories were growing cytopathic agents from the stools of healthy children that were plainly enteroviruses but caused no paralysis and no identifiable illness. In 1955 a committee settled on enteric cytopathic human orphan virus, orphan because an agent without a disease was considered parentless. Within a few years aseptic meningitis, neonatal sepsis and exanthems were attributed to them, so the orphans acquired their families, but the name has outlasted its own justification by seventy years."],
    ["The enterovirus taxonomy nobody can keep straight", "Early classification split enteroviruses into polioviruses, coxsackie A, coxsackie B and echoviruses based on what they did to mice and to cell cultures. Molecular analysis showed this grouping cut across genetic relationships, so most echoviruses now sit inside the species Enterovirus B along with the coxsackie B viruses, while newer agents are simply numbered as enteroviruses. Both systems remain in use, which is why one organism can carry a classical name, a species designation and a type number at the same time."],
    ["Why a neonate is in a different category", "Maternal antibody crosses the placenta mainly in the last weeks of pregnancy, and it is serotype specific. A neonate infected around delivery with a serotype the mother has never met has essentially no humoral defense against a virus that replicates in liver, heart and brain. The result is a sepsis-like illness with hepatic necrosis and coagulopathy that is one of the few enteroviral presentations with high mortality, and the rationale for giving immunoglobulin is simply to supply the antibody the infant did not inherit."],
    ["Chronic meningoencephalitis in antibody deficiency", "Patients with X-linked agammaglobulinemia can develop a smoldering enteroviral meningoencephalitis lasting months to years, often with a dermatomyositis-like rash, muscle inflammation and progressive neurologic decline, from which virus can be recovered repeatedly. The syndrome demonstrated, more cleanly than any experiment, that control of enteroviruses depends on antibody rather than on cellular immunity. High-dose immunoglobulin replacement has made the condition much less common than it was before such therapy became routine."]] },

{ id: "enterovirus-a71", k: "vir", g: "Picornaviruses", nm: "Enterovirus A71", also: "EV-A71, EV71, enterovirus 71",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, non-enveloped, icosahedral, genus Enterovirus, species Enterovirus A, family Picornaviridae, with genogroups A, B and C and numerous subgenogroups",
  plain: "The enterovirus that causes hand-foot-and-mouth disease and, unlike the coxsackieviruses, can attack the brainstem and kill young children.",
  live: "Humans only, with young children as the main reservoir during epidemics and prolonged fecal shedding sustaining transmission. Large cyclical epidemics occur across East and Southeast Asia every two to three years.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral and respiratory droplet spread, plus direct contact with vesicle fluid and saliva, with nurseries, kindergartens and households the main settings. Fomites and contaminated surfaces contribute. Shedding from the throat lasts one to three weeks and from stool for several weeks, which is why exclusion policies have limited effect.",
  causes: "Hand-foot-and-mouth disease with oral ulcers and vesicles on palms, soles, buttocks and knees, and herpangina. Beyond that it is the enterovirus most associated with severe neurologic disease: brainstem encephalitis with myoclonic jerks, tremor, ataxia and cranial nerve palsies, aseptic meningitis, acute flaccid paralysis resembling poliomyelitis, and the feared progression to neurogenic pulmonary edema with cardiorespiratory collapse, which accounts for most deaths. Onychomadesis, with nail shedding weeks later, is a characteristic late sign. Severe disease concentrates in children under five.",
  mech: "The principal receptors are scavenger receptor class B member 2, which mediates uncoating, and P-selectin glycoprotein ligand 1 on leukocytes, with heparan sulfate and annexin II as attachment factors. The acid-stable capsid survives the stomach, replication begins in the pharynx and gut lymphoid tissue, and viremia follows. Neuroinvasion is thought to occur partly by retrograde axonal transport along peripheral and cranial nerves, which fits the strikingly focal brainstem involvement, and partly across the blood-brain barrier. Brainstem inflammation involving the medulla, pons and the nucleus tractus solitarius disrupts central autonomic control, producing a catecholamine surge that drives neurogenic pulmonary edema and left ventricular failure; this is why the terminal event is cardiopulmonary rather than purely neurologic. Viral 2A protease cleaves eIF4G and 3C cleaves host transcription and innate signaling factors, blunting the interferon response.",
  lab: "Reverse transcription PCR on throat swab, vesicle fluid, stool or cerebrospinal fluid, with VP1 sequencing to identify the type, which matters because clinical management and prognosis differ from coxsackievirus A6 or A16. Cerebrospinal fluid PCR is less often positive than in echoviral meningitis, so a negative result does not exclude brainstem encephalitis. Magnetic resonance imaging shows characteristic high signal in the dorsal pons and medulla. Hyperglycemia, leukocytosis and tachycardia are recognized warning markers for impending cardiopulmonary failure.",
  tx: "Supportive care with careful staged monitoring. Recognition of warning signs, which include persistent fever, myoclonus, lethargy, vomiting, tachycardia and hyperglycemia, prompts transfer to intensive care. Management of neurogenic pulmonary edema involves mechanical ventilation, milrinone and inotropic support, and fluid restriction, an approach developed in Taiwan that substantially reduced mortality.",
  alt: "Intravenous immunoglobulin is widely used in severe neurologic disease in Asia on the basis of observational data, and corticosteroid has been used for brainstem inflammation without trial support. Capsid inhibitors such as pleconaril and pocapavir and the protease inhibitor rupintrivir have in vitro activity, and several monoclonal antibodies and the receptor decoy approach are in development.",
  res: "Capsid-binding antivirals select resistance rapidly, as with other enteroviruses. The larger practical limitation is that the critical injury is a brainstem inflammatory process already established by the time severe signs appear, so antiviral therapy would need to be given during the exanthem phase to matter.",
  prev: "Three inactivated whole-virus vaccines against genogroup C4 were licensed in China from 2015 and have shown efficacy above ninety percent against hand-foot-and-mouth disease caused by this type in trials, with evident impact on severe cases and deaths, though protection does not extend to coxsackievirus A6 or A16. Hand hygiene, surface disinfection, nursery closure during outbreaks and exclusion of symptomatic children form the non-vaccine measures.",
  look: "Coxsackievirus A16, the classical cause of hand-foot-and-mouth disease, which rarely causes severe neurologic disease. Coxsackievirus A6, which causes a more extensive blistering rash and nail shedding. Herpetic gingivostomatitis. Varicella. Poliomyelitis in a child with flaccid paralysis. In brainstem encephalitis, listeriosis, rabies and acute disseminated encephalomyelitis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Enterovirus A71 is the hand-foot-and-mouth virus that causes brainstem encephalitis and neurogenic pulmonary edema, which is what kills children in Asian epidemics."],
    ["Memory trick", "A16 gives the rash, A71 gives the rash plus the brainstem. Seventy-one is the dangerous number."],
    ["Classic setting", "A toddler in Taiwan, Vietnam or China with oral ulcers and spotted palms, then myoclonic jerks in sleep, then pink frothy pulmonary edema within hours."],
    ["Step focus", "Myoclonic jerking, ataxia and hyperglycemia in a child with hand-foot-and-mouth disease are the recognized warning features of brainstem involvement."]],
  worm: [["California, 1969, then Asia", "The virus was first isolated in 1969 from an infant with encephalitis in California and recognized during the 1970s in outbreaks in Bulgaria and Hungary that included hundreds of cases of poliomyelitis-like paralysis and a high death toll. Those European epidemics established early that this enterovirus was neurotropic in a way the coxsackieviruses were not. It then largely left the Western literature until the 1990s, when enormous epidemics in East and Southeast Asia made it one of the most important pediatric infections in the region."],
    ["Taiwan 1998 and the invention of a protocol", "An epidemic in Taiwan in 1998 produced more than a hundred thousand reported cases of hand-foot-and-mouth disease and seventy-eight deaths, most in children under five and most from fulminant pulmonary edema within hours of neurologic signs. Clinicians there worked out a staged clinical classification and a management approach built around early recognition of autonomic instability, fluid restriction and milrinone rather than aggressive volume loading. Mortality in severe cases fell substantially, and the protocol spread across the region."],
    ["Why the lungs fail when the brainstem is inflamed", "Magnetic resonance imaging and autopsy studies localize the inflammation to the dorsal medulla and pons, including the nuclei that govern sympathetic outflow. Loss of that regulation produces a catecholamine storm with intense systemic vasoconstriction, a sudden rise in pulmonary capillary pressure and left ventricular dysfunction, flooding the alveoli with protein-rich fluid. The chest radiograph looks like heart failure and the heart is indeed failing, but the lesion is in the brainstem, which is why giving fluid worsens the picture and inodilators help."],
    ["A vaccine for one type in a disease caused by many", "Inactivated vaccines against genogroup C4 licensed in China from 2015 demonstrated high efficacy against disease caused by that type and measurable falls in severe cases and deaths. They do not protect against coxsackievirus A6 or A16, which cause much of the remaining hand-foot-and-mouth disease, so clinics continued to see the syndrome while the fatal form became rarer. It is an instructive case of a vaccine aimed deliberately at the severe end of a syndrome rather than at the syndrome itself."]] },

{ id: "human-parechovirus", k: "vir", g: "Picornaviruses", nm: "Human parechovirus", also: "HPeV, parechovirus A, formerly echovirus 22 and 23",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, non-enveloped, icosahedral, genus Parechovirus, species Parechovirus A, family Picornaviridae, with type 3 the one associated with severe neonatal disease",
  plain: "A picornavirus once filed with the echoviruses that causes mild gut and respiratory illness in most children but sepsis and brain injury in young infants.",
  live: "Humans only. Infection occurs very early in life, with most children seropositive by the age of three, and prolonged shedding in stool maintains circulation. Biennial summer and autumn epidemics of type 3 have been described in several countries.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral and respiratory routes, with household and nursery transmission the usual pattern and older siblings frequently the source for an affected infant. Shedding in stool continues for weeks. Vertical and perinatal transmission occurs and is relevant to the earliest neonatal cases.",
  causes: "In most children a mild febrile illness with diarrhea or respiratory symptoms, or no symptoms at all. Type 3 in infants under three months causes a distinctive severe syndrome: high fever, irritability described as a piercing inconsolable cry, a diffuse erythematous rash sometimes with palmar and plantar involvement, abdominal distension, tachycardia and a sepsis-like picture, with meningoencephalitis, seizures and white matter injury. Survivors of neonatal central nervous system disease have a substantial rate of cerebral palsy, epilepsy and learning difficulty. Myocarditis, hepatitis and a hemophagocytic picture are also reported.",
  mech: "Parechoviruses differ structurally from the enteroviruses: they have no VP4, their capsid lacks the canyon and hydrophobic pocket that capsid-binding antivirals target, and many types including type 1 use alpha-V integrins through an RGD motif in VP1, while type 3 lacks that motif and uses a different and still incompletely defined route. Translation is driven by an internal ribosome entry site, and the 2A protein is not a protease of the enteroviral type but an RNA-binding protein, so host translational shutoff proceeds differently. The virus replicates in gut and respiratory epithelium and lymphoid tissue, then viremia carries it to the central nervous system where type 3 produces periventricular and subcortical white matter injury with a diffusion pattern on imaging that is characteristic and distinct from the gray matter disease of enteroviruses.",
  lab: "Parechovirus-specific reverse transcription PCR is essential, because standard pan-enterovirus PCR primers do not detect it; this is the single most important practical point and the reason the agent was missed for years. Testing of cerebrospinal fluid, blood, stool and nasopharyngeal samples, with typing by VP1 sequencing. Cerebrospinal fluid is often bland, with little or no pleocytosis and normal protein even in proven meningoencephalitis, which is a notable trap. Magnetic resonance imaging shows periventricular and deep white matter signal change in severe neonatal cases.",
  tx: "Supportive care in a monitored setting, with management of seizures, fluid balance and feeding, and antibiotics continued until bacterial sepsis is excluded. No specific antiviral therapy exists.",
  alt: "Intravenous immunoglobulin has been used in severe neonatal disease on the same reasoning as for enteroviral sepsis, with case reports only. Pleconaril and related capsid inhibitors are ineffective because the drug-binding pocket present in enteroviruses is absent from the parechovirus capsid. Neurodevelopmental follow-up is an essential part of care.",
  res: "Not relevant therapeutically. The structural absence of the enteroviral capsid pocket means the existing antiviral chemistry developed for enteroviruses cannot be repurposed, which is a genuine obstacle rather than acquired resistance.",
  prev: "Hand hygiene and attention to diaper handling, with awareness that older siblings are a common source. Infection control in neonatal and pediatric units during biennial epidemic years. No vaccine exists or is in advanced development.",
  look: "Bacterial sepsis and meningitis in a young infant, which drives initial management. Enteroviral neonatal sepsis, which it closely resembles and which standard PCR will detect while missing this virus. Herpes simplex neonatal infection. Kawasaki disease, given fever and rash in an irritable infant. Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis in severe cases.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Human parechovirus type 3 causes sepsis-like illness and white matter brain injury in infants under three months, and routine enterovirus PCR does not detect it."],
    ["Memory trick", "Parechovirus is the enterovirus impostor: it was filed as echovirus 22 and 23, it needs its own PCR, and it leaves the cerebrospinal fluid looking normal."],
    ["Classic setting", "A six-week-old with fever, a red rash on palms and soles and an inconsolable high-pitched cry, with a completely unremarkable cerebrospinal fluid."],
    ["Step focus", "Its capsid lacks the hydrophobic pocket that pleconaril binds and it has no VP4, which is why it was reclassified out of the enteroviruses and why enteroviral antivirals do not work."]],
  worm: [["Two echoviruses that turned out to be something else", "Echovirus 22 and echovirus 23 were isolated in 1956 from children with summer diarrhea in Ohio and filed with the other enteric orphans. For forty years they sat uncomfortably in the group: they grew differently, their capsid proteins ran differently on gels, and their serology did not behave. Sequencing in the early 1990s showed they lacked VP4 entirely, used a different cleavage scheme and were only distantly related to the enteroviruses, and in 1999 they were moved into a genus of their own named for parent echovirus."],
    ["The virus a routine test cannot see", "Clinical laboratories detect enteroviruses with primers aimed at a conserved region of the 5 prime untranslated region, and the corresponding parechovirus sequence is different enough that those primers fail. For years infants with the severe type 3 syndrome were therefore reported as sepsis of unknown cause with negative viral studies. The problem was solved only when specific assays were added to pediatric panels during the 2000s, and the apparent emergence of the virus in the literature after 2004 largely reflects the arrival of a test rather than of a virus."],
    ["A bland tap in a sick brain", "In enteroviral meningitis the cerebrospinal fluid reliably shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, and clinicians lean on that. Infants with proven parechovirus meningoencephalitis, including those who go on to show extensive white matter injury on imaging, frequently have no cells, normal protein and normal glucose. The dissociation between a normal tap and a damaged brain has caught experienced clinicians repeatedly, and it is the main reason guidance in several countries now recommends testing cerebrospinal fluid for this virus regardless of the cell count."],
    ["White matter rather than gray", "Enteroviruses characteristically injure gray matter, which is why poliovirus takes the anterior horn and enterovirus A71 takes the brainstem nuclei. Type 3 parechovirus instead produces diffusion restriction and later gliosis in periventricular and subcortical white matter, a pattern more reminiscent of hypoxic-ischemic injury than of classical viral encephalitis. The radiologic signature is distinctive enough that neuroradiologists have proposed it as a clue to the diagnosis, and it predicts the motor and cognitive outcomes seen in follow-up."]] },

{ id: "enterovirus-d70", k: "vir", g: "Picornaviruses", nm: "Enterovirus D70", also: "EV-D70, EV70, acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis virus, Apollo 11 disease virus",
  cls: "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, non-enveloped, icosahedral, genus Enterovirus, species Enterovirus D, family Picornaviridae, a relative of enterovirus D68",
  plain: "An enterovirus that attacks the conjunctiva rather than the gut, causing explosive epidemics of bloodshot eyes and occasionally a polio-like paralysis weeks later.",
  live: "Humans only. Unusually among enteroviruses it replicates poorly in the gut and is largely an ocular and upper respiratory virus, which may be why it does not circulate endemically between epidemics in the way echoviruses do.",
  spread: "Direct and indirect contact with infected ocular secretions, through fingers, shared towels, contaminated eye drops, ophthalmic instruments and tonometers, and through swimming water. Transmission is extraordinarily efficient, with attack rates in crowded households and clinics approaching complete, and epidemics spread along shipping and air routes in days to weeks.",
  causes: "Acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis: abrupt onset over hours of foreign body sensation, pain, lacrimation, lid swelling, marked subconjunctival hemorrhage and preauricular lymphadenopathy, usually bilateral, resolving in about a week without visual loss. A small proportion of cases, roughly one in several thousand, develop an acute neurologic complication one to five weeks later: a poliomyelitis-like acute flaccid paralysis, typically asymmetric and lumbosacral, often with cranial nerve involvement and radicular pain, with incomplete recovery in many. Transient keratitis and rare uveitis occur.",
  mech: "The virus uses decay-accelerating factor CD55 as its receptor, and sialic acid-containing moieties contribute to attachment, which fits its tropism for conjunctival epithelium. The capsid is relatively acid labile compared with the classical enteroviruses, consistent with poor gut replication and with a route of entry through the eye and upper respiratory tract rather than the stomach. Replication in conjunctival epithelium produces rapid cytolysis with capillary injury and subconjunctival bleeding, which is what gives the striking clinical picture. Viral 2A protease cleaves eIF4G and 3C cleaves host factors, shutting down host synthesis. Neurologic complications are attributed to viremic spread with infection of anterior horn neurons and dorsal root ganglia, and the long delay between conjunctivitis and paralysis suggests a slower route to the cord than the local ocular infection implies.",
  lab: "Reverse transcription PCR or culture from conjunctival swab in the first few days, with VP1 sequencing to type it, which distinguishes it from coxsackievirus A24 variant, the other cause of the same epidemic syndrome, and from adenoviral conjunctivitis. Serology is of limited clinical use. In paralytic cases, cerebrospinal fluid shows a mild pleocytosis and virus is rarely recovered from it, so the diagnosis rests on the preceding conjunctivitis and on exclusion of poliovirus, which is a reportable consideration.",
  tx: "Supportive care for the eye: cool compresses, lubricants and analgesia, with avoidance of corticosteroid in the acute phase and careful exclusion of herpetic and bacterial keratitis. Paralytic disease is managed with supportive care and rehabilitation. No antiviral therapy is available.",
  alt: "Topical antibiotics have no role unless bacterial superinfection is suspected. Physiotherapy, orthotics and long-term rehabilitation determine the outcome for patients left with weakness.",
  res: "No antiviral resistance issue. The operationally important point is that the virus survives on surfaces and instruments well enough that outbreaks have been propagated through ophthalmology clinics, so control depends on disinfection and instrument hygiene rather than on any drug.",
  prev: "Hand hygiene, avoidance of shared towels and eye cosmetics, exclusion of affected individuals from schools and pools, and strict disinfection of tonometers and other ophthalmic equipment. Single-use eye drops in clinics. No vaccine exists; epidemics are controlled by hygiene and by the exhaustion of susceptible people.",
  look: "Coxsackievirus A24 variant, which causes an indistinguishable acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis and has caused many of the large recent epidemics. Adenoviral epidemic keratoconjunctivitis, which lasts longer and more often involves the cornea. Bacterial conjunctivitis. Herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis. In paralytic cases, poliomyelitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome and acute flaccid myelitis from enterovirus D68.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Enterovirus D70 causes acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis and can be followed weeks later by a poliomyelitis-like acute flaccid paralysis."],
    ["Memory trick", "Apollo 11 disease: the first epidemic spread as the astronauts landed in 1969, so bloodshot eyes and the moon landing share a date."],
    ["Classic setting", "A coastal city epidemic with thousands of people presenting within days with painful red eyes full of blood, and a handful returning a month later with a weak leg."],
    ["Step focus", "Its receptor is decay-accelerating factor CD55, and unlike most enteroviruses it is acid labile and replicates poorly in the gut, which fits the ocular route."]],
  worm: [["An epidemic named for a spacecraft", "Acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis appeared explosively in Ghana in 1969 and swept West Africa, then North Africa, Asia and eventually the Americas, affecting millions of people. Because the Ghanaian outbreak coincided with the Apollo 11 moon landing, it was popularly called Apollo 11 disease, a name that persisted in several countries. The virus was isolated in 1971 and assigned as enterovirus 70. Rumors linking the illness to the spacecraft itself circulated widely, which is an early example of outbreak misinformation traveling as fast as the outbreak."],
    ["An enterovirus that is not very enteric", "The classical enteroviruses are acid stable, pass the stomach and replicate in the gut, with disease elsewhere as a secondary event. This virus is comparatively acid labile, grows poorly in intestinal tissue and is shed inconsistently in stool, while it replicates readily in conjunctival cells at a temperature below core. It is one of the clearest demonstrations that the name of a viral genus reflects where its first members were found rather than any obligatory property of its members, a point it shares with its relative enterovirus D68 and the respiratory tract."],
    ["Paralysis weeks after the eyes clear", "During the large epidemics of the 1970s and 1980s, clinicians in India, Japan and elsewhere reported patients who recovered from conjunctivitis and then, two to five weeks later, developed acute asymmetric flaccid weakness with radicular pain and sometimes cranial nerve palsies. The incidence was low, perhaps one in several thousand infections, but the absolute numbers were substantial given epidemic size. The long latency between the ocular illness and the neurologic one has never been fully explained and remains one of the puzzles of enteroviral neuropathogenesis."],
    ["A virus spread by the eye clinic", "Because the virus persists on surfaces and on instruments and because the infectious dose through the conjunctiva is tiny, outbreaks have repeatedly been amplified in ophthalmology departments through shared tonometer tips, multi-dose eye drop bottles and unwashed hands between patients. Several published epidemics were traced to a single clinic. The experience changed ophthalmic practice, contributing to single-use drops, disposable tonometer covers and the routine of disinfecting applanation tips, measures now taken for granted for reasons that began with this virus."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "orf-virus", k: "vir", g: "Poxviruses", nm: "Orf virus", also: "contagious ecthyma virus, scabby mouth, sore mouth, contagious pustular dermatitis, ecthyma contagiosum in humans",
  cls: "Large double-stranded linear DNA virus, enveloped, ovoid brick-shaped virion, genus Parapoxvirus, family Poxviridae, replicating entirely in the cytoplasm",
  plain: "A sheep and goat poxvirus that gives shepherds, butchers and vets a single ugly nodule on the hand that heals on its own in about six weeks.",
  live: "Sheep and goats worldwide, where it causes crusted lesions around the mouth, nostrils, udder and feet. The virus is remarkably stable, surviving for months to years in dried scabs on pasture, fencing, feeding troughs and bedding, which is how flocks reinfect themselves between seasons.",
  spread: "Direct contact of broken or abraded human skin with lesions, saliva or scabs from an infected animal, or with contaminated wool, hides, fencing, shearing equipment or feed. Bottle-feeding lambs, shearing, slaughtering, hoof trimming and handling animals at religious festivals are the recognized exposures. It is not transmitted between people in any meaningful way, and rare autoinoculation to another site on the same person is the exception.",
  causes: "A solitary lesion, occasionally two or three, on a finger, hand or forearm, which evolves through recognized stages over roughly six weeks: a red papule, a target lesion with a white ring and red halo, an acute weeping nodule, a regenerative stage with black dots, a papillomatous stage and finally a dry crust that falls away. The lesion is often painless or mildly tender, measures one to three centimeters, and may be accompanied by low-grade fever, lymphangitis and regional lymphadenopathy. Erythema multiforme follows in a minority. Immunocompromised patients may develop large, progressive, fungating and recurrent lesions.",
  mech: "Entry and replication occur entirely in the cytoplasm using the virus's own transcription machinery. The genome encodes an unusual set of immunomodulators that explain both the local pathology and the self-limited course: a viral vascular endothelial growth factor homolog, VEGF-E, which drives the dermal capillary proliferation and the strikingly vascular, bleeding appearance of the lesion, a viral interleukin 10 homolog that dampens local inflammation, a chemokine-binding protein, a granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor and interleukin 2 inhibitor, and an inhibitor of interferon signaling. Because the virus provokes only weak and short-lived immunity, reinfection in both sheep and people occurs. Keratinocyte proliferation with ballooning degeneration, intracytoplasmic inclusions and a dense dermal infiltrate are the histologic features.",
  lab: "The diagnosis is usually clinical in a person with occupational animal contact and a characteristic lesion. Electron microscopy of a scab or biopsy shows an ovoid virion with a criss-cross surface pattern, distinguishing parapoxviruses from the brick-shaped orthopoxviruses. PCR on lesion material confirms the species. Histology shows epidermal hyperplasia with ballooning degeneration, eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions and marked dermal vascularity. Bacterial culture is often taken to exclude superinfection and to rule out anthrax, which is the critical alternative in an animal handler.",
  tx: "Observation with local wound care, keeping the lesion clean and covered, and analgesia. The lesion resolves spontaneously over about six weeks with little or no scarring, so intervention is usually unnecessary. Secondary bacterial infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  alt: "For large, persistent or immunocompromised cases, topical or intralesional cidofovir, topical imiquimod, cryotherapy, curettage, shave excision or electrodessication have been used in case series. Systemic antivirals are not standard. Erythema multiforme is managed symptomatically.",
  res: "No antiviral resistance issue arises in practice. The relevant biological point is the reverse: the virus resists immunity rather than drugs, since its own immunomodulators blunt the response and reinfection is possible, which is why live vaccines in sheep give only limited-duration protection.",
  prev: "Gloves and hand hygiene when handling sheep and goats, particularly when bottle-feeding lambs or treating sore mouths, covering of cuts and abrasions, and cleaning of equipment and pens. Live attenuated vaccines applied by scarification are used in flocks, with the caveat that vaccinated animals shed virulent-derived virus and the vaccine itself can infect handlers. Awareness is heightened around religious festivals involving animal slaughter, after which clusters of human cases are regularly reported.",
  look: "Cutaneous anthrax, which is the critical alternative in an animal handler and which produces a painless black eschar with marked edema. Milker's nodule from pseudocowpox, another parapoxvirus, which is essentially identical and differs only in the animal source. Cowpox, which is more necrotic and systemically unwell. Pyogenic granuloma. Sporotrichosis and atypical mycobacterial infection. Squamous cell carcinoma or keratoacanthoma in the papillomatous stage.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Orf is a parapoxvirus of sheep and goats producing a single self-limited nodule on the hand of an animal handler, resolving in about six weeks without treatment."],
    ["Memory trick", "Orf comes off a sheep's mouth onto your finger. Parapox gives nodules on hands: Orf from sheep and goats, milker's nodule from cows."],
    ["Classic setting", "A farmer who has been bottle-feeding lambs in spring, or a person who helped slaughter a sheep at a festival, with one weeping nodule on the index finger."],
    ["Step focus", "It encodes a viral vascular endothelial growth factor homolog, which accounts for the intensely vascular and readily bleeding lesion, and the first consideration to exclude is cutaneous anthrax."]],
  worm: [["An old word for an old disease", "The name orf probably derives from Old Norse hrufa, a scab or crust, reaching English through Scots and northern dialect, and shepherds in Britain used it long before anyone identified a virus. Veterinary descriptions of contagious pustular dermatitis in sheep date to the nineteenth century, and transmission to people was demonstrated experimentally in the 1930s. It is one of a handful of occupational zoonoses whose common name comes from the vocabulary of the trade rather than from a laboratory or a place."],
    ["A virus that carries a growth factor", "Sequencing in the 1990s revealed that this virus encodes a functional homolog of vascular endothelial growth factor, named VEGF-E, which binds VEGF receptor 2 and drives dermal capillary proliferation and permeability. No other poxvirus genus carries it. The gene explains the lesion's vascular, friable, strawberry-like appearance, and the protein itself became a research tool for studying receptor-specific angiogenic signaling, since it activates one receptor without the other. A sheep scab contributed a reagent to vascular biology."],
    ["Festivals, flocks and clusters", "Human cases follow the rhythms of animal husbandry and religious practice. Clusters are reported each spring among people bottle-feeding orphan lambs, among shearers, and in the days after Eid al-Adha, when large numbers of people who do not usually handle animals slaughter sheep and goats by hand. Several published case series come entirely from the week following the festival. The epidemiology is therefore a calendar as much as an ecology, and public health messaging in affected countries is timed to it."],
    ["Immunomodulators and the limits of vaccination", "The genome encodes inhibitors of interleukin 2, granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor, chemokine signaling and interferon, together with a viral interleukin 10, which together suppress the local response enough that both sheep and people can be reinfected. Live vaccines applied by scarification protect flocks only for a season or two, and vaccinated animals shed virus that can infect their handlers. The vaccine is used anyway, because the economic cost of lambs unable to suckle outweighs these drawbacks, which is a candid example of a veterinary vaccine chosen on arithmetic rather than elegance."]] },

{ id: "cowpox-virus", k: "vir", g: "Poxviruses", nm: "Cowpox virus", also: "CPXV, catpox in practice",
  cls: "Large double-stranded linear DNA virus, enveloped, brick-shaped virion, genus Orthopoxvirus, family Poxviridae, with the largest genome of the orthopoxviruses and many host-range and immunomodulatory genes intact",
  plain: "The orthopoxvirus of European wild rodents, caught almost always from a pet cat, that makes a painful necrotic skin lesion and the historical seed of vaccination.",
  live: "Wild rodents are the true reservoir, principally bank voles, field voles and wood mice across Europe and western Asia. Cattle are incidental and now rarely involved despite the name. Domestic cats become infected by hunting rodents and are the usual source for people, with rats, zoo elephants, rhinoceroses, big cats and other animals occasionally affected.",
  spread: "Direct contact of broken skin with lesions, saliva or claws of an infected animal, overwhelmingly a cat with crusted lesions on the head and paws. Rodent bites and contact with pet rats have caused clusters. Person-to-person transmission has not been convincingly documented. The virus does not spread efficiently by the respiratory route in humans.",
  causes: "A localized lesion, usually on the hand, arm or face, that begins as a papule and becomes a vesicle, then a pustule, then a deep, painful, necrotic, eschar-covered ulcer with surrounding erythema and edema, healing over six to twelve weeks and frequently leaving a scar. Painful regional lymphadenopathy, fever, malaise and myalgia are common and can be marked. In atopic dermatitis the virus can spread widely across eczematous skin, and in immunocompromised patients generalized, necrotizing and occasionally fatal infection has occurred, including ocular involvement with keratitis and visual loss.",
  mech: "Replication is entirely cytoplasmic in characteristic factories, with the virus supplying its own transcription apparatus. Among the orthopoxviruses it has retained the largest complement of host-range and immune evasion genes, including a serpin that blocks interleukin 1 beta converting enzyme, soluble receptors that bind tumor necrosis factor, interleukin 1 beta, interferon gamma and interferon alpha and beta, a chemokine binding protein, a complement control protein and an inhibitor of apoptosis. The characteristic A-type inclusion bodies seen in infected cells are made by an abundant protein that packages mature virions. Local necrosis reflects both viral cytopathic effect and the intense inflammatory response that follows once the virus can no longer suppress it; the painfulness and the depth of the ulcer distinguish it clinically from the shallower, more vascular parapoxvirus lesions.",
  lab: "PCR on lesion swab, crust or biopsy with orthopoxvirus-generic and species-specific targets, which is the standard, with sequencing to distinguish it from vaccinia, mpox and the other orthopoxviruses. Electron microscopy shows a brick-shaped virion, and histology shows ballooning degeneration with large eosinophilic cytoplasmic A-type inclusions. Serology is group reactive across the genus and cannot identify the species. Viral culture on chorioallantoic membrane produces hemorrhagic pocks, a classical feature once used for identification.",
  tx: "Supportive care with wound dressing, analgesia, which is often substantial given how painful the lesion is, and antibiotics only for bacterial superinfection. Most lesions in immunocompetent people resolve without specific antiviral therapy. Surgical debridement is generally avoided because it tends to enlarge the lesion.",
  alt: "Tecovirimat, licensed for smallpox and used in mpox, has been used in severe or progressive cowpox and in immunocompromised patients, alongside cidofovir or brincidofovir and vaccinia immune globulin in individual reports. Ocular disease is managed with ophthalmology involvement, and corticosteroid is used cautiously if at all.",
  res: "Tecovirimat resistance arises through mutations in the F13L phospholipase gene and has been documented in mpox during prolonged monotherapy, so the same vulnerability applies here. In practice, experience in this infection is too limited for resistance to be a described clinical problem.",
  prev: "Avoidance of handling cats or rodents with crusted skin lesions, gloves and hand hygiene for veterinary staff, and particular caution in people with atopic dermatitis or immunosuppression, for whom contact with an affected pet carries real risk. Smallpox vaccination protects across the genus, and the decline of routine vaccination has left the European population broadly susceptible, which is one proposed reason reported cases have increased.",
  look: "Mpox, which is now the first orthopoxvirus consideration and usually involves multiple lesions. Vaccinia infection after vaccination or laboratory exposure. Cutaneous anthrax, which gives a painless eschar with gross edema. Orf and milker's nodule, which are shallower, more vascular and much less painful. Bacterial cellulitis or an infected cat scratch, including Bartonella and Pasteurella infection. Cutaneous tularemia and sporotrichosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Cowpox is maintained in wild rodents and is caught by people almost always from a domestic cat, not from cattle, despite the name."],
    ["Memory trick", "Cowpox comes from the cat; the cow was only ever the middleman. Jenner's milkmaids started vaccination, and the Latin for cow, vacca, is why it is called vaccination at all."],
    ["Classic setting", "A teenager in rural England or Germany with a hunting cat, presenting with a single deep painful black-crusted ulcer on the hand, a tender axilla and a fever."],
    ["Step focus", "People with atopic dermatitis or immunosuppression can develop widespread or fatal infection, which is the group in which antiviral therapy and immune globulin are considered."]],
  worm: [["Jenner, the milkmaids and a name that outlived the facts", "In 1796 Edward Jenner inoculated James Phipps with material from the hand of the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes and then challenged him with smallpox, founding vaccination and giving the practice its name from vacca, the Latin for cow. The folk knowledge he drew on, that milkmaids who had caught cowpox did not take smallpox, was already current in dairy districts. Later work showed the virus Jenner propagated diverged from cowpox and became vaccinia, so the vaccine that carries the cow in its name is no longer the cow's virus."],
    ["A reservoir found two centuries late", "For most of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries the virus was assumed to be a cattle disease. Serological and virological surveys from the 1970s onward found that infection in cattle was sporadic and that antibody was widespread in wild bank voles, field voles and wood mice, which are the true maintenance hosts. Cattle, cats, zoo elephants and people are all incidental. The name is therefore a historical artifact pointing at the species least important to the virus's survival."],
    ["The cat that brings it indoors", "Case series across Europe show that the great majority of human infections follow contact with a domestic cat that has been hunting rodents and has developed crusted lesions on its face and paws. Children and young adults, who handle cats most closely and are now mostly unvaccinated against smallpox, are overrepresented. Clusters have also followed the trade in pet rats, with outbreaks in France and Germany traced to a single breeder, showing how a wildlife virus reaches cities through the pet supply chain."],
    ["The biggest genome in the genus, and what that buys", "This virus retains more host-range and immunomodulatory genes than any other orthopoxvirus, including the gene for the serpin CrmA that blocks interleukin 1 beta converting enzyme and an array of soluble cytokine receptors. Variola and vaccinia have lost many of these. The pattern fits the general idea that orthopoxvirus genomes shrink as they specialize on fewer hosts, and that the broad-host-range generalist keeps the larger toolkit. It also makes this virus a favored source of individual immune-evasion proteins for laboratory study."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "human-bocavirus", k: "vir", g: "Other DNA viruses", nm: "Human bocavirus", also: "HBoV, HBoV1 to HBoV4",
  cls: "Single-stranded linear DNA virus, non-enveloped, icosahedral, genus Bocaparvovirus, family Parvoviridae, named from bovine and canine parvovirus, with HBoV1 respiratory and HBoV2 to 4 enteric",
  plain: "A small DNA virus found in the airways of wheezing toddlers whose contribution to illness is hard to prove because it is so often there alongside something else.",
  live: "Humans, with young children as the main hosts. DNA persists in tonsillar and adenoid tissue and in the nasopharynx for months after infection, and almost all children have been infected by the age of six, so the virus is universal rather than exotic.",
  spread: "Respiratory droplet and contact spread for HBoV1, fecal-oral spread for the enteric types, with nursery and household transmission the usual pattern and a winter to spring peak in temperate climates. Prolonged shedding in respiratory secretions and stool, sometimes for months, is characteristic and is the central problem in interpreting a positive test.",
  causes: "Upper and lower respiratory tract illness in young children: coryza, cough, fever, wheezing, bronchiolitis and pneumonia, with acute wheezing episodes the presentation most consistently associated with it. Acute otitis media is common in the same episodes. Severe cases with respiratory failure and a few deaths have been reported in previously healthy children with high viral loads and no other pathogen. The enteric types are associated with gastroenteritis. Encephalitis and myocarditis have been reported rarely.",
  mech: "Replication requires dividing cells, as for all parvoviruses, and proceeds in the nucleus using host polymerase with the virus supplying NS1, a nickase and helicase that drives rolling hairpin replication of the single-stranded genome. The NP1 protein, which is unique to the bocaparvoviruses, controls alternative polyadenylation and splicing of the viral transcripts and also interferes with host DNA damage response signaling. The virus infects ciliated airway epithelium, and in differentiated airway cultures it produces epithelial disruption, loss of cilia and cell shedding without extensive lysis, which fits an illness dominated by airway obstruction and wheezing rather than by consolidation. Immune evasion is modest, and the main reason for the uncertain clinical attribution is persistence of DNA rather than any special capacity to evade defense.",
  lab: "PCR on nasopharyngeal aspirate is how it is found, usually as part of a multiplex respiratory panel, but a positive result alone does not establish causation because of coinfection and persistence. High viral load, detection of viral messenger RNA, viremia and serological evidence of acute infection with IgM or an IgG rise correlate far better with genuine disease. Serology must account for cross-reaction between the four types. Coinfection with rhinovirus, respiratory syncytial virus, adenovirus or others is found in roughly half to three quarters of positive samples.",
  tx: "Supportive care, as for other viral bronchiolitis and wheezing illness: oxygen, fluids, and airway support when needed. No antiviral is available or in development for clinical use.",
  alt: "Bronchodilators and corticosteroids are used according to the general approach to wheezing in young children rather than because of anything specific to this virus. Antibiotics have no role unless bacterial superinfection or otitis media warrants them.",
  res: "Not applicable. The important analogous problem is diagnostic rather than therapeutic: multiplex panels detect the virus so readily that it is frequently reported in children whose illness is caused by something else, which can lead to premature closure of a diagnostic workup.",
  prev: "Hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette and the general measures that reduce transmission in nurseries. No vaccine exists and none is in development, which reflects both the mildness of most disease and the difficulty of defining what fraction of illness the virus actually causes.",
  look: "Respiratory syncytial virus, which causes the same bronchiolitis picture at the same age and is far better characterized. Rhinovirus-induced wheeze. Human metapneumovirus. Parainfluenza. Adenovirus. Early asthma in a preschool child with recurrent viral wheeze. The practical question at the bedside is usually not which virus but how much support the child needs.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Human bocavirus is a parvovirus found in wheezing young children, and its causal role is uncertain because coinfection and prolonged DNA shedding are so common."],
    ["Memory trick", "Bocavirus is named from BOvine and CAnine parvovirus, its nearest relatives. A parvovirus in the airway rather than the marrow."],
    ["Classic setting", "An eighteen-month-old in winter with cough, fever and audible wheeze whose respiratory panel returns positive for this virus and for rhinovirus at the same time."],
    ["Step focus", "Like all parvoviruses it needs dividing cells and replicates in the nucleus, but it targets airway epithelium rather than erythroid precursors, so it does not cause aplastic crisis."]],
  worm: [["Found by looking for nothing in particular", "In 2005 Tobias Allander and colleagues in Stockholm applied a method they called molecular virus screening to pooled nasopharyngeal aspirates from children with respiratory illness, randomly amplifying and sequencing nucleic acid without any hypothesis about what might be there. Among the sequences was a previously unknown parvovirus. The approach, now routine as metagenomic sequencing, found an agent that had plainly been infecting nearly every child for a very long time while remaining entirely invisible to the culture and antigen methods in use."],
    ["The problem of attribution", "Finding a virus in a sick child is not the same as showing it caused the illness. This virus is detected in a substantial minority of asymptomatic children, persists in the nasopharynx and tonsils for months, and is accompanied by another respiratory pathogen in most positive samples. Researchers therefore built a hierarchy of evidence, with high viral load, viremia, detectable viral messenger RNA and acute serological responses carrying more weight than mere PCR positivity. The resulting literature is a useful case study in what a positive molecular test does and does not mean."],
    ["Named for its cousins, not its home", "The genus name is a contraction of bovine parvovirus and canine minute virus, the two previously known members of the group, so the human virus is named after animals it does not infect. Bocaparvoviruses are distinguished within the family by the NP1 protein, which no other parvovirus genus carries and which controls viral messenger RNA processing. That protein, rather than any clinical feature, is what defines the group, and it has become the focus of efforts to grow the virus efficiently in culture."],
    ["Four types, two niches", "Four human types are recognized. Type 1 is predominantly respiratory and accounts for the wheezing association, while types 2, 3 and 4 are found mainly in stool and have been linked to gastroenteritis, with type 2 notably common in surveys of children with diarrhea. The split is unusual for such closely related viruses and mirrors what happens among the enteroviruses, where tissue tropism can diverge sharply between relatives. Whether the enteric types cause diarrhea or simply pass through remains, as with the respiratory type, an open question."]] },

{ id: "torque-teno-virus", k: "vir", g: "Other DNA viruses", nm: "Torque teno virus", also: "TTV, transfusion-transmitted virus, Anelloviridae, torquetenovirus",
  cls: "Circular single-stranded negative-sense DNA virus, non-enveloped, small icosahedral capsid, genus Alphatorquevirus, family Anelloviridae, with enormous genetic diversity across many genogroups",
  plain: "A virus almost everyone carries for life without any known disease, now used as a living thermometer for how suppressed a transplant patient's immune system is.",
  live: "Humans, essentially universally. Replication is thought to occur in bone marrow, lymphocytes and liver, with lifelong persistence and continuous low-level viremia in most healthy adults, alongside a diverse population of related anelloviruses. Related viruses occur in many other mammals.",
  spread: "Multiple routes. Transfusion and transplantation transmit it efficiently, which is how it was found, and it is also transmitted vertically, through saliva and respiratory secretions, by the fecal-oral route and probably sexually. Infection is acquired in infancy and early childhood, and essentially the whole adult population is infected, so acquisition rather than exposure is never the clinical question.",
  causes: "No disease has been convincingly attributed to it despite three decades of searching, and it is best regarded as part of the normal human virome. Associations have been reported with hepatitis, respiratory disease, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, autoimmune conditions and cancers, but none has withstood scrutiny, and in each case high viral load is at least as easily explained as a consequence of altered immunity as a cause of the illness. Its clinical importance is entirely as a biomarker rather than as a pathogen.",
  mech: "The small circular single-stranded genome is replicated in the nucleus, probably by rolling circle replication using host polymerase, and encodes an ORF1 capsid and replication-associated protein together with several smaller products including one that suppresses nuclear factor kappa B signaling and a microRNA that interferes with interferon-stimulated gene expression. The genome is extraordinarily variable, with hypervariable regions in ORF1 reminiscent of the variable loops of other persistent viruses, and individuals carry swarms of related sequences rather than a single strain. Control of viral load depends on T-cell function, which is the basis of its use as a biomarker: when cellular immunity is suppressed the load rises, and when immunity recovers or rejection threatens the load falls.",
  lab: "Quantitative PCR with pan-anellovirus primers aimed at the conserved untranslated region, since primers directed at variable regions miss much of the diversity and gave misleadingly low prevalence estimates in early studies. Commercial standardized assays have been developed for transplant monitoring. Sequencing is used in virome research. There is no serologic test in clinical use, and much of the circulating antibody is bound in immune complexes, which complicated early serology.",
  tx: "No treatment is indicated, because no disease is attributed to it. The clinical use is the opposite: the viral load is measured in order to adjust other drugs.",
  alt: "Not applicable. In research settings the load has been used to guide dose adjustment of calcineurin inhibitors and mycophenolate in solid organ transplant recipients, with prospective trials under way to establish whether load-guided immunosuppression improves the balance between rejection and infection.",
  res: "Not applicable in the usual sense. The analogous technical problem is primer mismatch: the genetic diversity of the group is so great that an assay can underestimate or miss a patient's dominant lineage, which is why conserved-region assays and standardized reference materials matter for using the load as a measurement.",
  prev: "Not pursued, since there is no disease to prevent, and it is not screened for in blood donation. Interest runs the other way, toward understanding what a healthy anellovirus population looks like and whether disturbances in it signal anything.",
  look: "Nothing clinically, since it causes no recognized syndrome. Conceptually it sits alongside other markers of net immunosuppression, including absolute lymphocyte count, CD4 count, ATP release assays of T-cell function and the viral loads of cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus and BK polyomavirus, each of which also rises when cellular immunity falls.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Torque teno virus infects nearly every human, causes no known disease, and its viral load is used as a functional marker of how immunosuppressed a transplant recipient is."],
    ["Memory trick", "Torque teno comes from Latin torques, a necklace, and tenuis, thin, describing the thin circular genome. A necklace of DNA that everyone wears and nobody notices."],
    ["Classic setting", "A kidney transplant clinic measuring the load serially: a high and rising load suggesting over-immunosuppression and infection risk, a falling load suggesting under-immunosuppression and rejection risk."],
    ["Step focus", "Its control depends on T-cell function, which is why the load tracks net immunosuppression and why it was proposed as a biomarker rather than treated as a pathogen."]],
  worm: [["Found while hunting a cause of hepatitis", "In 1997 Takahiro Nishizawa and colleagues in Japan were searching for an unknown agent of post-transfusion hepatitis and recovered a novel DNA sequence from a patient whose initials were T.T. The virus was first called transfusion-transmitted virus, and the coincidence of initials with the eventual formal name, from the Latin torques for necklace and tenuis for thin, has fed a persistent muddle about where the name came from. The hepatitis connection, which motivated the search, was never substantiated."],
    ["A prevalence that depended on the primers", "Early surveys using primers aimed at the ORF1 region found the virus in a minority of people and encouraged the idea that it might be a pathogen whose presence distinguished the sick from the well. When primers were redesigned to target the conserved untranslated region, prevalence jumped to near universal. The episode is a clean illustration of how an assay's design can create an apparent epidemiology, and it reset the entire field from searching for a disease to describing a component of the normal virome."],
    ["From orphan virus to clinical instrument", "Because the load rises when T-cell function falls and falls when it recovers, groups in Vienna and elsewhere proposed measuring it as a continuous readout of net immunosuppression after solid organ transplantation, where clinicians currently balance rejection against infection largely by drug levels. Standardized quantitative assays were developed and prospective trials of load-guided immunosuppression have been conducted. A virus with no disease of its own may therefore end up being more clinically useful than many that cause one."],
    ["The most abundant unknown in the human virome", "Anelloviruses are among the most abundant and most diverse viruses in human blood, with individuals carrying complex and shifting swarms of lineages that differ by more than other viral families tolerate between species. They have no known host benefit and no known harm, and their enormous diversity suggests a very long co-evolution with primates. They are often cited as the central example of how little is known about the viruses that most humans carry all the time, and of how much of the virome is defined by absence of disease rather than presence of it."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "la-crosse-virus", k: "vir", g: "Bunyaviruses", nm: "La Crosse virus", also: "LACV, La Crosse encephalitis, California serogroup encephalitis",
  cls: "Negative-sense segmented single-stranded RNA virus with three segments, L, M and S, enveloped, genus Orthobunyavirus, family Peribunyaviridae, California serogroup",
  plain: "A woodland mosquito virus of the American midwest and Appalachia that is the commonest cause of arboviral encephalitis in children in the United States.",
  live: "Eastern chipmunks and gray squirrels are the amplifying hosts, with the treehole mosquito Aedes triseriatus as both vector and reservoir, since the virus passes transovarially into mosquito eggs that overwinter in water-filled tree holes and artificial containers. Deer and other large mammals support the mosquito population without amplifying the virus.",
  spread: "Bite of Aedes triseriatus, a daytime-biting woodland mosquito that breeds in tree holes, discarded tires, buckets and other container water close to houses. The invasive Aedes albopictus and Aedes japonicus can also transmit it, which has raised concern about spread into new areas. Humans are dead-end hosts and there is no person-to-person spread, although transmission through transplantation has been reported for related viruses.",
  causes: "Most infections are silent or produce a brief nonspecific fever. Symptomatic disease is largely a pediatric illness: fever, headache, vomiting and lethargy progressing over days to meningoencephalitis with seizures in roughly half of hospitalized children, focal neurologic signs, raised intracranial pressure and occasionally status epilepticus or a presentation mimicking a space-occupying lesion or herpes encephalitis. Case fatality is well under one percent, but around ten to fifteen percent of children are left with recurrent seizures, learning difficulties, attention problems or behavioral change, and the long-term cognitive burden is the main consequence.",
  mech: "The tripartite negative-sense genome encodes the polymerase on the L segment, the Gn and Gc glycoproteins on the M segment and the nucleocapsid plus the nonstructural protein NSs on the S segment. Gc mediates attachment and fusion and is the main determinant of neuroinvasiveness. NSs is the principal virulence factor: it suppresses host transcription and the type I interferon response and triggers apoptosis, and it has been shown to act as an inducer of cell death in neurons. The virus is strongly neuroinvasive and neurovirulent in the young, replicating in skeletal muscle first and then reaching the central nervous system across the vasculature and possibly through olfactory routes, with cortical neurons the principal target. The age restriction of severe disease is attributed to maturation of interferon responses and of the blood-brain barrier, a pattern reproduced in weanling mice.",
  lab: "IgM capture serology on serum and cerebrospinal fluid is the usual route, with plaque reduction neutralization or specific assays to separate it from other California serogroup viruses such as Jamestown Canyon virus and snowshoe hare virus. Reverse transcription PCR on cerebrospinal fluid is positive in only a minority. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a modest lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose. Electroencephalography and magnetic resonance imaging may show focal abnormality, and the frequency with which the picture mimics herpes simplex encephalitis means acyclovir is usually started empirically.",
  tx: "Supportive neurologic care: seizure control, management of raised intracranial pressure, fluid and electrolyte management, and empiric acyclovir until herpes simplex is excluded. No specific antiviral therapy is established.",
  alt: "Ribavirin was given to children in a small open study and in individual cases, with laboratory activity against the virus but no demonstrated clinical benefit and dose-limiting toxicity. Neurologic rehabilitation, antiepileptic therapy and educational support determine the long-term outcome.",
  res: "Not applicable in the absence of effective antiviral therapy.",
  prev: "Removal of container water around homes, especially discarded tires, buckets, tarpaulins and clogged gutters, which is the single most effective measure given the vector's breeding habits, together with repellents, long sleeves and screens. Daytime protection matters because the vector bites in daylight in shaded woodland. No vaccine exists, and the small annual case count has not supported development.",
  look: "Herpes simplex encephalitis, which is excluded as a priority and which it can mimic closely enough to prompt brain biopsy historically. West Nile virus, which affects older adults far more. Enteroviral meningitis in the same season. Bacterial meningitis. A brain abscess or tumor in a child with focal signs and raised pressure. Jamestown Canyon virus, a related California serogroup virus which, in contrast, affects adults.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "La Crosse virus is the leading cause of pediatric arboviral encephalitis in the United States, transmitted by the treehole mosquito Aedes triseriatus."],
    ["Memory trick", "La Crosse hits kids, St Louis hits seniors, West Nile hits everyone but hurts the old. Treeholes and tires for the first of them."],
    ["Classic setting", "A school-age child in West Virginia, Ohio, Tennessee or Wisconsin in late summer with fever, headache and a seizure, started on acyclovir while herpes testing is pending."],
    ["Step focus", "Transovarial transmission in Aedes triseriatus eggs lets the virus overwinter in tree holes, which is why the mosquito is both vector and reservoir."]],
  worm: [["A child in Minnesota and a stored specimen", "The virus was isolated in 1964 from brain tissue of a child who had died of encephalitis in La Crosse, Wisconsin, in 1960, four years after the specimen was stored. Work by Wayne Thompson and colleagues established it as a distinct member of the California serogroup, whose prototype virus had been found in California but almost never caused recognized human disease. The agent that mattered clinically therefore carries the name of the Wisconsin city rather than of the serogroup it belongs to."],
    ["The mosquito is also the reservoir", "Aedes triseriatus passes the virus into its own eggs, which survive the winter dry and frozen in tree holes and hatch infected the following spring. This transovarial route means the mosquito population carries the virus across seasons without needing a vertebrate to maintain it, and chipmunks and squirrels serve to amplify rather than to preserve it. Venereal transmission between mosquitoes has also been shown. The arrangement makes the virus unusually independent of its mammalian hosts and very difficult to eliminate from a wooded area."],
    ["Discarded tires and the geography of a disease", "Tree holes are the natural larval habitat, and a used tire is an excellent artificial one: it holds water, resists drying and sits in shade. The spread of tire dumps through Appalachia and the midwest expanded the vector's range into residential yards, and case clusters map onto peridomestic container breeding sites. Public health interventions have accordingly centered on tire removal and container emptying, which is cheaper and more durable than spraying and targets the only part of the cycle people can reach."],
    ["Mistaken for herpes, and what that cost", "Before PCR, a child with fever, seizures and focal neurologic signs raised the possibility of herpes simplex encephalitis, which at the time was diagnosed by brain biopsy. A number of children in endemic areas underwent biopsy and were found to have this infection instead. The availability of cerebrospinal fluid PCR for herpes simplex ended that practice, but the clinical overlap remains, which is why empiric acyclovir is still started in a child with this presentation while the arboviral serology is awaited."]] }
);

;
/* ===== Microbiology set 16: further parasites, intestinal protozoa, microsporidia, free-living amoebae, blood and tissue protozoa and nematodes (draft, Oct 30 2026) ===== */
MICRO.push(
{ id: "balantidium-coli", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Balantidium coli", also: "Balantioides coli, Neobalantidium coli, balantidiasis agent, the only ciliate that infects humans",
  cls: "Ciliated protozoan, phylum Ciliophora, the largest protozoan parasite of humans. Two stages only: a huge motile trophozoite 50 to 100 micrometers long, covered in rows of cilia, carrying a kidney-shaped macronucleus and a small micronucleus, with a funnel-shaped cytostome at the front; and a thick-walled spherical cyst 45 to 65 micrometers across that carries the infection out of the body.",
  plain: "A giant hairy single-celled parasite of pigs that can bore ulcers into the human colon.",
  live: "Domestic and wild pigs are the principal reservoir and usually carry it without illness. Monkeys, rats and occasionally cattle also harbor it. Human infection concentrates where people and pigs share living space and water, including parts of New Guinea, the Philippines, Bolivia, Brazil and rural Iran. There is no insect vector.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral, by swallowing cysts in water or food contaminated with pig or human feces. Pig-handling, slaughtering and the use of pig manure on vegetable plots are the recognized exposures. Cysts survive weeks in moist soil but die quickly when dried.",
  causes: "Most infections are symptomless carriage. Symptomatic balantidiasis ranges from intermittent diarrhea with cramping and weight loss to a fulminant dysentery with bloody mucoid stools, tenesmus and fever that closely resembles amebic colitis. Deep ulcers can perforate, and extraintestinal spread to the liver, lung, appendix or urogenital tract has been reported in small numbers of cases.",
  mech: "The trophozoite swims through colonic mucus on its cilia and secretes hyaluronidase, which loosens the connective tissue of the lamina propria and lets it burrow into the wall. The resulting lesion is a flask-shaped ulcer with a narrow opening and a broad base, the same architecture that Entamoeba histolytica produces, and for the same reason: lateral spread meets less resistance than vertical spread. Tissue destruction is mechanical and enzymatic rather than toxin mediated, and the parasite also engulfs host cells, bacteria and starch granules through its cytostome. Neutrophils and eosinophils accumulate at the ulcer edge, and healing is by fibrosis. Malnutrition, achlorhydria, alcoholism and immunosuppression are the recognized risk factors for invasive disease, which suggests that an intact gastric barrier and mucosal defense normally hold it at the surface.",
  lab: "Wet mount of fresh stool is the key test, because the trophozoite is so large and so briskly motile that it is hard to overlook: it rotates and glides across the field like nothing else in a stool preparation. Cysts appear in formed stool and trophozoites in liquid stool, so repeated samples raise the yield. Formalin-ether concentration and trichrome staining both work, and the macronucleus is the diagnostic structure. Colonoscopic biopsy of an ulcer edge shows trophozoites in the submucosa. Culture in suitable media is possible but little used, and serology is not available for routine work.",
  tx: "Tetracycline has long been the first choice for symptomatic infection in adults, with metronidazole as an equally accepted option and the preferred one in children and pregnancy.",
  alt: "Iodoquinol, nitazoxanide and doxycycline have all been used. Supportive rehydration matters in fulminant dysentery, and perforation becomes a surgical problem.",
  res: "No validated resistance mechanism has been described, partly because the organism is rare and no standardized susceptibility method exists. Reported treatment failures are generally attributed to reinfection from a continuing animal exposure or to advanced ulcerating disease rather than to drug resistance.",
  prev: "Separating pig waste from drinking water and vegetable plots, boiling or filtering suspect water, handwashing after animal contact, and avoiding the use of fresh pig manure on crops. No vaccine exists and none is in development.",
  look: "Entamoeba histolytica, which makes the same flask-shaped ulcer but is a tenth the size and has no cilia. Shigella and Campylobacter dysentery. Inflammatory bowel disease, which an untreated balantidial colitis has been mistaken for on biopsy. In stool preparations, ciliated protozoa from the environment and large plant cells are the usual false alarms.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Balantidium coli is the only ciliated protozoan known to cause human disease, and the pig is its reservoir."],
    ["Memory trick", "Balantidium is the Big one: biggest protozoan, bristling with cilia, from the barnyard pig."],
    ["Classic setting", "A pig farmer or abattoir worker in the Philippines or highland Bolivia with bloody diarrhea and a stool wet mount full of large spinning organisms."],
    ["Step focus", "It produces the same flask-shaped colonic ulcer as Entamoeba histolytica, and hyaluronidase is the enzyme credited with the invasion."]],
  worm: [["The parasite found twice", "Malmsten described a ciliate in the stools of two patients with dysentery in Stockholm in 1857 and called it Paramecium coli, placing it with the free-living pond ciliates that every biology student still meets. Leuckart had already found the same organism in pigs. Stein moved it to the genus Balantidium in 1862, from the Greek balantidion, a little bag, for the shape of the cyst. The genus has since been revised again, to Balantioides, on molecular grounds, so the organism now carries three names in current literature depending on how recent the source is."],
    ["Why pigs do not mind", "Pig prevalence in some herds exceeds fifty percent with no sign of illness, while human infection, though far rarer, can perforate the colon. The usual explanation is that the pig colon is the parasite's adapted niche, with a microbial and mucus environment it does not need to invade, whereas the human colon offers neither the right flora nor the right substrates, and the organism burrows in search of them. Starch appears to matter: the trophozoite consumes starch granules avidly, and the diets associated with human disease tend to be starch rich."],
    ["Big enough to see without much help", "At up to a tenth of a millimeter, Balantidium is visible as a moving speck at low power and was among the first protozoan parasites a student could reliably find alone. This has a practical consequence: the diagnosis is made by whoever looks at a fresh wet mount within the hour, and lost by whoever waits, because the trophozoite stops moving and disintegrates as the specimen cools and dries."],
    ["Rare, but the question keeps being asked", "Human balantidiasis probably numbers in the low thousands of reported cases in the whole medical literature, which makes it one of the rarest conditions that examinations still test. It survives in the curriculum because it is the sole example of a clinically important ciliate, because its reservoir and its ulcer are both memorable, and because it sits in direct contrast with Entamoeba histolytica, which is the comparison the question is usually really about."]] },

{ id: "blastocystis-hominis", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Blastocystis hominis", also: "Blastocystis species, Blastocystis sp. subtype 1 to 9 and beyond, blastocystosis",
  cls: "Stramenopile, not a true protozoan in the older sense: it belongs with the brown algae and diatoms in the SAR supergroup, is anaerobic and non-flagellated, and lacks the cell wall of its relatives. Recognized forms include a vacuolar form with a single large central vacuole, a granular form, an amoeboid form and a thick-walled cyst. Human isolates fall into many genetically distinct subtypes, numbered ST1 upward, most of which are also found in animals.",
  plain: "An extremely common vacuole-shaped organism in human stool whose capacity to cause illness has been argued over for a century.",
  live: "Humans, pigs, cattle, poultry, primates, rodents, dogs and birds, with substantial overlap of subtypes between species. Carriage is among the most common parasitic findings worldwide, with reported prevalence from a few percent in industrialized countries to more than half the population in parts of Africa, the Middle East, South America and Southeast Asia.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral, principally through water and food. Animal contact is a recognized route for several subtypes, and household clustering is common. Cysts survive in water and resist standard chlorination at usual concentrations.",
  causes: "The honest answer is that this is contested. Many carriers have no symptoms at all. Reported associations include bloating, flatulence, abdominal discomfort, loose stools, nausea, fatigue, pruritus and urticaria, and the organism turns up more often in people with irritable bowel syndrome than in controls in a number of studies, though not in all. Subtype appears to matter, with ST1, ST3, ST4 and ST7 cited more often in symptomatic series.",
  mech: "Proposed mechanisms rest on cysteine protease activity that degrades secretory IgA, a reported capacity to disrupt tight junctions and raise epithelial permeability, induction of interleukin-8 from intestinal cell lines, and apoptosis of enterocytes in culture. None of these amounts to an established pathogenic pathway in humans. A competing and increasingly discussed interpretation treats Blastocystis as a marker of a diverse, high-fiber gut microbiome rather than a cause of disease, since carriage correlates with greater bacterial richness and with lower rather than higher rates of some inflammatory conditions. The two views are not fully reconcilable, and the field has not settled.",
  lab: "Direct wet mount and permanent trichrome stain show round bodies 5 to 20 micrometers across with a large central vacuole pushing the cytoplasm and nuclei to the rim. Quantity is sometimes reported, on the unproven assumption that heavy shedding is more likely to be symptomatic. Stool culture in Jones medium is more sensitive than microscopy, and PCR with subtype sequencing is the research standard and increasingly used clinically. Finding the organism does not establish it as the cause of a patient's symptoms, which is why laboratories differ on whether to report it at all.",
  tx: "Treatment is generally reserved for persistent symptoms after other causes have been excluded. Metronidazole is the most used agent, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and nitazoxanide are the main alternatives.",
  alt: "Paromomycin, tinidazole, and combinations have all been reported. Clearance of the organism and resolution of symptoms do not reliably coincide in either direction, and a substantial proportion of treated people continue to shed it.",
  res: "In vitro work shows wide variation in metronidazole susceptibility between subtypes and even between isolates of one subtype, and apparent treatment failure is common in practice. Whether this reflects true resistance, poor luminal drug exposure, reinfection, or the possibility that the organism was never the cause is unresolved.",
  prev: "Safe water, food hygiene and handwashing, with the caveat that there is no clear evidence that eradicating carriage benefits an asymptomatic person. No vaccine and no public health program targets it.",
  look: "Cyst-like yeasts, degenerating epithelial cells, pollen grains, Entamoeba cysts and fat globules all confuse stool microscopy. When symptoms are the question, Giardia, Dientamoeba, celiac disease, bile acid diarrhea, small intestinal bacterial overgrowth and irritable bowel syndrome are the realistic competitors.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Blastocystis is one of the most frequently found organisms in human stool and its pathogenicity remains unproven, so its presence alone does not explain symptoms."],
    ["Memory trick", "Blastocystis looks like a ring with a hole: a big central vacuole with the nuclei squeezed to the edge."],
    ["Classic setting", "A routine stool panel on a person with bloating returns Blastocystis hominis, and the clinical question becomes whether anything about it needs acting on."],
    ["Step focus", "It is a stramenopile, related to algae and diatoms rather than to amoebae, which is why it sits awkwardly in the protozoa chapter."]],
  worm: [["A hundred years of disagreement", "Alexeieff named it in 1911 and considered it a yeast. Brumpt moved it to the protozoa in 1912. Through the twentieth century it drifted between fungus, protozoan and harmless passenger, and ribosomal RNA sequencing in 1996 finally placed it among the stramenopiles, beside brown seaweed and water molds. Almost nothing else in medical parasitology has had its kingdom revised so late. The pathogenicity argument has run just as long and is no closer to resolution, with review articles on both sides appearing in the same years."],
    ["The subtype problem", "Calling it Blastocystis hominis implies a human-specific species, which the genetics do not support: human isolates fall into at least nine subtypes, most shared with animals, and some subtypes differ from one another more than separate species do in other genera. Current practice is to write Blastocystis sp. plus the subtype number. This matters clinically, because studies that lumped all subtypes together were effectively comparing different organisms, which may explain why they so often disagreed."],
    ["Evidence that it may be a sign of health", "Several large microbiome cohorts have found Blastocystis carriage associated with higher bacterial diversity, more Prevotella and Ruminococcus, and a diet high in plant fiber, and with lower prevalence of Crohn disease in some datasets. On this reading the organism thrives in a well-populated, fiber-fed colon and is absent from the depleted microbiome of industrialized diets and recent antibiotics. That would make its appearance in a stool report closer to a marker of ecological richness than a finding to treat."],
    ["Why laboratories differ on reporting it", "Some laboratories report Blastocystis with a quantity, some report it with a note that significance is uncertain, and some have stopped reporting it entirely. Each policy has a defense. Reporting it can prompt courses of metronidazole in people who do not improve; suppressing it can withhold the single finding that a small number of genuinely affected patients needed. The split is a useful illustration of how diagnostic reporting decisions quietly shape treatment."]] },

{ id: "dientamoeba-fragilis", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Dientamoeba fragilis", also: "dientamoebiasis",
  cls: "Trichomonad, a flagellate that has lost its flagella, placed in the order Trichomonadida on molecular grounds despite its amoeboid appearance. The trophozoite is 5 to 15 micrometers with one or, characteristically, two nuclei whose karyosomes break into four to eight granules, and it has broad hyaline pseudopodia. A cyst stage long thought not to exist has been described in rodent models and in some human material, and a precyst form is also reported.",
  plain: "A small amoeba-shaped relative of Trichomonas that lives in the large bowel and is a recognized cause of chronic loose stools and abdominal pain.",
  live: "Humans are the main host. Pigs, gorillas and other primates carry it, and sheep, cattle, rodents and dogs have been found positive by PCR. Reported prevalence varies from under one percent to more than forty percent of stool samples depending on the method used, with molecular testing finding far more than microscopy.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral is assumed, but the route has never been settled, because the absence of a robust cyst in human stool leaves the fragile trophozoite an unlikely traveler. A long-standing hypothesis is transmission inside the eggs of Enterobius vermicularis, and the two infections do co-occur more often than chance would predict. Waterborne and food-borne clusters have also been documented.",
  causes: "Diarrhea, often intermittent and lasting weeks to months, abdominal pain, flatulence, anorexia, weight loss and fatigue, with a prominent association in children. Eosinophilia appears in a minority. Urticaria and pruritus have been reported. As with Blastocystis, asymptomatic carriage is common, and a number of investigators regard it as a commensal in most hosts.",
  mech: "Mechanisms are poorly defined. The organism lives in the lumen and crypt openings of the cecum and colon without invading, and biopsies from symptomatic patients typically show little or no inflammation, which is itself a clue that the illness is functional rather than destructive. Mucosal irritation, altered motility and local immune activation are the usual proposals. Its trichomonad ancestry means it has hydrogenosomes rather than classical mitochondria, which is relevant because that anaerobic metabolism is what the nitroimidazole drugs exploit. The demonstration that an organism with no demonstrable invasion can still track with months of symptoms is part of why it is discussed alongside irritable bowel syndrome.",
  lab: "It is missed by routine methods more often than it is found. The trophozoite disintegrates within hours of passage, so unfixed stool sent to a laboratory is usually negative; immediate fixation in polyvinyl alcohol or formalin and a permanent iron-hematoxylin or trichrome stain is the classical approach, and the fragmented karyosomes in two nuclei are the diagnostic feature. It does not survive formalin-ether concentration well and is not seen on simple wet mounts. Real-time PCR on stool is substantially more sensitive and has become the test that reveals how common the organism is. Culture in Robinson or TYSGM-9 medium is a research technique.",
  tx: "Metronidazole is the most widely used agent for symptomatic infection, and clinical response is often prompt.",
  alt: "Iodoquinol, paromomycin, tinidazole, secnidazole and doxycycline have all been reported with reasonable clearance rates. Combination approaches are used in refractory cases, and treating a coexisting Enterobius infection is often considered alongside.",
  res: "Metronidazole failure is reported, and in vitro susceptibility testing is not standardized. Because carriage is common and symptoms are nonspecific, apparent failure may equally represent a wrong attribution of the symptoms in the first place.",
  prev: "Hand hygiene, safe water and food, and household screening when an index case recurs. Treating pinworm in a household has been proposed as an indirect measure given the suspected link, though the evidence is associative.",
  look: "Entamoeba hartmanni and the small nonpathogenic amoebae, which it resembles on a poorly fixed slide. Blastocystis, which raises exactly the same question of significance. Giardia and Cryptosporidium as causes of chronic watery diarrhea. Irritable bowel syndrome, celiac disease and bile acid diarrhea in the chronic presentation.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The trophozoite classically has two nuclei with fragmented karyosomes, and it is a flagellate that has lost its flagella, related to Trichomonas rather than to the amoebae it resembles."],
    ["Memory trick", "Di for two nuclei, and fragilis because it falls apart in an unfixed specimen."],
    ["Classic setting", "A child with several months of loose stools and abdominal pain whose routine stool studies were negative until a PCR panel or an immediately fixed specimen was sent."],
    ["Step focus", "Transmission inside pinworm eggs is the classical hypothesis and the reason Enterobius co-infection is mentioned in the same breath."]],
  worm: [["Named for what it does on the slide", "Jepps and Dobell described the organism in 1918 at the Lister Institute and chose fragilis for the way the trophozoite degenerates almost as soon as the specimen leaves the body. That single property shaped seventy years of literature: the organism was regarded as rare because it was almost never seen, and it was almost never seen because the standard handling of a stool specimen destroys it. Molecular surveys since the 2000s have found it among the more common protozoa in several countries."],
    ["A flagellate in disguise", "Dobell suspected from its nuclear structure that it was related to the trichomonads rather than the amoebae, and electron microscopy and ribosomal sequencing confirmed it decades later. It retains the internal architecture of a flagellate, including hydrogenosomes and a parabasal body, while having lost the external flagella and adopted amoeboid movement. It is one of the cleanest examples in parasitology of why morphology alone is an unreliable guide to relationship."],
    ["The pinworm hypothesis", "Dobell himself proposed in 1940 that Dientamoeba travels in Enterobius eggs, after noticing the two infections kept company. Supporting evidence has accumulated slowly: amoeba-like bodies have been seen inside pinworm eggs, DNA of Dientamoeba has been amplified from Enterobius material, and epidemiological co-occurrence is reproducible. It remains unproven as the principal route, and documented waterborne outbreaks show it cannot be the only one."],
    ["The missing cyst, and then one", "For most of a century textbooks stated flatly that Dientamoeba has no cyst stage, which left its transmission unexplained. In 2012 and 2014 cyst and precyst forms were described in rodent models and subsequently sought in human material. The finding has not overturned the pinworm hypothesis so much as reopened the question, and the current position in reviews is that more than one route is probably operating."]] },

{ id: "entamoeba-dispar", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Entamoeba dispar", also: "nonpathogenic Entamoeba, the look-alike of E. histolytica",
  cls: "Amoeba of the genus Entamoeba, morphologically indistinguishable from Entamoeba histolytica. Trophozoite 10 to 60 micrometers with a single nucleus carrying a small central karyosome and fine even peripheral chromatin; mature cyst with four nuclei, 10 to 15 micrometers, with rounded-end chromatoid bars. Separation from E. histolytica rests on isoenzyme pattern, antigen detection or DNA, not on appearance.",
  plain: "The harmless twin of the amoeba that causes dysentery, identical under the microscope and responsible for most of the Entamoeba found in stool worldwide.",
  live: "Humans are the reservoir, with some carriage in nonhuman primates. It is far more common than E. histolytica: of the people reported historically as infected with E. histolytica worldwide, roughly nine in ten are now thought to have carried E. dispar instead. Carriage is highest where sanitation is poor and in travelers returning from those regions.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral, by cysts in contaminated water, food and on hands, identical in every respect to the spread of E. histolytica. Cysts resist chlorination at drinking water concentrations and survive for weeks in moist conditions.",
  causes: "No established disease. Carriers have no colitis, no liver abscess and no detectable mucosal injury, and controlled comparisons find no excess of gastrointestinal symptoms over uninfected people. Occasional reports propose minor symptoms or rare tissue lesions with E. dispar, but these are isolated and not generally accepted.",
  mech: "The interest lies in what it lacks. E. dispar possesses homologs of several E. histolytica virulence genes, including the Gal/GalNAc lectin and cysteine proteinases, but expresses them differently and at lower levels, and the amoebapores that let E. histolytica punch holes in host cells are present in less lytic forms. It fails to produce the sustained contact-dependent killing of epithelial cells and the trogocytosis, or nibbling of live host cells, that drives invasion by its sibling. The pair therefore serve as a natural experiment: two organisms with almost the same genome and completely different outcomes, which is why E. dispar is studied as the control arm for every claim about amebic virulence.",
  lab: "Light microscopy cannot separate it from E. histolytica, and reporting the two together as the Entamoeba histolytica/dispar complex is standard practice when only microscopy is available. Ingested red cells in a trophozoite favor E. histolytica but are not conclusive. Stool antigen tests using monoclonal antibodies to the Gal/GalNAc lectin distinguish the species, as does PCR, which is the reference method. Serology is positive in invasive E. histolytica infection and negative in E. dispar carriage, which makes it useful in the opposite direction.",
  tx: "No treatment is indicated for established E. dispar carriage, and most guidelines describe observation alone.",
  alt: "Where species cannot be determined and the clinical picture or the setting raises genuine concern about invasive amebiasis, some clinicians treat the complex empirically with a luminal agent such as paromomycin or diloxanide furoate, accepting that most such courses are given to carriers of the harmless species.",
  res: "Not applicable in any meaningful sense, since the organism is not treated. Luminal agents clear it at the same rates they clear E. histolytica cysts.",
  prev: "Safe water and sanitation, handwashing, and care with raw produce in endemic regions, identical to prevention of E. histolytica. No vaccine.",
  look: "Entamoeba histolytica, which is the whole clinical point. Entamoeba moshkovskii and Entamoeba bangladeshi, two more members of the complex that are also morphologically identical and also generally regarded as nonpathogenic. Entamoeba hartmanni, which is smaller. Entamoeba coli, which has eight nuclei in the mature cyst.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "E. dispar is morphologically identical to E. histolytica and causes no disease, so a microscopy report of the histolytica/dispar complex does not establish amebiasis."],
    ["Memory trick", "Dispar for disparity: the same face, a different nature."],
    ["Classic setting", "A returning traveler with a positive stool microscopy for Entamoeba and no symptoms, where antigen or PCR testing settles whether anything is wrong."],
    ["Step focus", "Antigen detection or PCR separates the species; red cells inside the trophozoite point toward E. histolytica but do not prove it."]],
  worm: [["Brumpt was right in 1925 and ignored for sixty years", "Emile Brumpt argued that the amoeba found in symptomless carriers was a separate species from the one causing dysentery, and named it Entamoeba dispar. Nobody could test the claim, because the two look the same, and the proposal was dismissed. In the late 1970s Peter Sargeaunt showed that isoenzyme patterns, or zymodemes, separated the isolates into pathogenic and nonpathogenic groups that bred true. Louis Diamond and Clark formally redescribed E. dispar in 1993. Brumpt's species name was restored almost seventy years after he proposed it."],
    ["Why the global numbers collapsed", "Textbooks once stated that 500 million people carried Entamoeba histolytica and that 10 percent of them developed disease, figures that made amebiasis look like a condition with a strangely low attack rate. Once the species could be separated, the explanation became obvious: most of those carriers had E. dispar, and the true E. histolytica infection rate was perhaps a tenth of the old figure, with a much higher proportion of those genuinely becoming ill. The epidemiology of amebiasis was rewritten without a single new case being counted."],
    ["A natural knockout experiment", "Because the two species share most of their gene content, E. dispar functions as a built-in control for amebic virulence research. When a candidate virulence factor is found in E. histolytica, the first question asked is whether E. dispar has it too. Several do, which has repeatedly forced the field away from simple presence-or-absence explanations toward differences in expression, regulation and the physical act of trogocytosis, the nibbling of living host cells that E. histolytica performs and E. dispar does not."],
    ["The practical cost of a look-alike", "In settings without antigen or molecular testing, a positive Entamoeba microscopy generally leads to a course of metronidazole plus a luminal agent. Given the ratio of the two species, the large majority of those courses treat an organism that would have done nothing, with the attendant side effects, cost and selection pressure. It is one of the clearest examples in parasitology of how a diagnostic limitation translates directly into unnecessary treatment."]] },

{ id: "entamoeba-coli", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Entamoeba coli", also: "the commensal Entamoeba, not to be confused with Escherichia coli",
  cls: "Nonpathogenic amoeba of the human large bowel. Trophozoite 15 to 50 micrometers, sluggish, with coarse and irregular peripheral nuclear chromatin and a large eccentric karyosome, and cytoplasm crowded with bacteria and debris. The mature cyst is 10 to 35 micrometers and contains up to eight nuclei, with chromatoid bodies that are splintered and sharp-ended rather than rounded.",
  plain: "A harmless amoeba that lives in almost everyone's colon in some parts of the world and matters only because finding it proves the person has swallowed something fecally contaminated.",
  live: "Humans, with the same organism or close relatives in other primates, pigs and dogs. It is among the most prevalent protozoa on earth, reaching carriage rates above fifty percent in communities without safe water and sanitation, and it is found worldwide including in high-income countries.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral through cysts in water, food and on hands. Cysts are robust and survive for weeks in a moist environment, which is what makes the organism such a durable indicator of contamination.",
  causes: "No disease. Carriage produces no diarrhea, no invasion, no abscess and no measurable symptom excess. It is a commensal that feeds on bacteria and particulate matter in the colonic lumen.",
  mech: "There is no pathogenic mechanism to describe, and that absence is the teaching point. The organism possesses no amoebapores capable of lysing host cells, no demonstrated capacity for contact-dependent killing, and no tissue-invasive behavior. It lives on the luminal side of the mucus layer and phagocytoses bacteria. Its clinical value is entirely indirect: because it is acquired by exactly the same route as Entamoeba histolytica, Giardia, Cryptosporidium, hepatitis A, typhoid and the diarrheagenic Escherichia coli, its presence in a stool sample is evidence that the fecal-oral route into this person is open, and prompts a search for the organisms that do cause disease.",
  lab: "Identified on wet mount, concentration and permanent trichrome stain. The two features that separate it from the pathogenic amoebae are nuclear and numerical: coarse clumped peripheral chromatin with a large off-center karyosome in the trophozoite, and up to eight nuclei in the mature cyst, where E. histolytica and E. dispar stop at four. Chromatoid bars, when present, have splintered ends rather than the rounded cigar shape of E. histolytica. Immature cysts with fewer nuclei are the common source of misidentification, which is why cyst size and chromatin pattern are read together.",
  tx: "No treatment is indicated, and finding it is generally reported without recommending any drug.",
  alt: "Not applicable. Where a laboratory reports it alongside symptoms, the usual course is to look for an alternative explanation rather than to treat the amoeba.",
  res: "Not applicable.",
  prev: "The same water, sanitation and hygiene measures that prevent every fecal-oral infection. Its prevalence in a community is sometimes used in field studies as a proxy measure of how well those measures are working.",
  look: "Entamoeba histolytica and Entamoeba dispar, which have four nuclei in the mature cyst and finer, more even nuclear chromatin. Entamoeba hartmanni, which is smaller. Endolimax nana and Iodamoeba butschlii, both also commensal, with their own nuclear patterns. Immature E. coli cysts with four nuclei are the classic trap.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Entamoeba coli is a nonpathogenic commensal, and the mature cyst has up to eight nuclei, which is the feature that separates it from E. histolytica and E. dispar."],
    ["Memory trick", "Coli has more nuclei and causes less trouble: eight nuclei, zero disease."],
    ["Classic setting", "A stool report in a returning traveler lists Entamoeba coli cysts, and the correct reading is that the person has had fecal exposure, not that the amoeba is the cause of their symptoms."],
    ["Step focus", "Its presence flags fecal-oral exposure, which makes a search for the genuinely pathogenic organisms worthwhile."]],
  worm: [["A name that causes trouble every year", "Entamoeba coli and Escherichia coli share a species epithet because both were named for the colon, from the Greek kolon. They are not related in any useful sense: one is a eukaryotic amoeba, the other a bacterium, and they sit in separate domains of life. Laboratory reports, chart notes and examination answers confuse them regularly enough that many microbiology texts carry an explicit warning. Writing the genus in full is the practical defense."],
    ["Counting nuclei as a diagnostic skill", "Separating Entamoeba species on a stained slide is one of the oldest fine-grained skills in clinical microscopy, and it comes down to nuclear chromatin texture, karyosome position and nucleus count in the mature cyst. It takes real training, degrades without practice, and has been steadily displaced by antigen tests and molecular panels. The skill is still taught partly because it remains the only available method in many of the places where the parasites are most common."],
    ["The commensal that measures a water supply", "Because E. coli the amoeba causes no illness but spreads exactly as the dangerous enteric pathogens do, epidemiologists have used its prevalence as a sentinel marker for the integrity of water and sanitation in a community. A village where carriage falls after a well is sunk or latrines are built is a village where the route for typhoid and amebic dysentery has also narrowed. It is a rare case of a parasite being more useful as a measurement than as a diagnosis."],
    ["Harmless is a claim that has to be defended", "Declaring an organism nonpathogenic is a stronger statement than it sounds, and the history of E. dispar shows how carefully it has to be made. For E. coli the evidence is good: carriage is enormously common, symptomatic excess is not demonstrable in controlled comparisons, and no invasive lesion has ever been convincingly attributed to it. The contrast with E. dispar, which was wrongly treated as pathogenic for seventy years because it could not be told apart from its sibling, is instructive in both directions."]] },

{ id: "sarcocystis", k: "para", g: "Intestinal protozoa", nm: "Sarcocystis species", also: "Sarcocystis hominis, Sarcocystis suihominis, Sarcocystis nesbitti, sarcocystosis, sarcosporidiosis",
  cls: "Apicomplexan protozoa with an obligate two-host cycle. In the definitive host, sexual stages in the intestinal wall produce sporocysts shed in feces. In the intermediate host, sporozoites invade endothelium, undergo schizogony, and form sarcocysts packed with bradyzoites in striated muscle. Humans act as definitive host for S. hominis, acquired from beef, and S. suihominis, from pork, and as an accidental intermediate host for S. nesbitti and related species.",
  plain: "A muscle-cyst parasite of grazing animals that humans can either carry briefly in the gut after eating undercooked meat or, far more unusually, get cysts of in their own muscles.",
  live: "The natural cycle runs between a predator and its prey. Cattle and pigs are intermediate hosts for the human-infecting species; humans and other primates are the definitive hosts. For the muscular form, snakes are the suspected definitive host of S. nesbitti, with humans as a dead-end intermediate host; macaques and other primates are the natural intermediate hosts. Sarcocystis species are globally distributed, with human intestinal infection reported from Europe, Southeast Asia and South America.",
  spread: "Intestinal sarcocystosis follows eating raw or undercooked beef or pork containing sarcocysts. Muscular sarcocystosis follows swallowing sporocysts in water or food contaminated by the feces of the definitive host, which is why the documented human outbreaks have been waterborne and linked to jungle travel rather than to meat.",
  causes: "Intestinal infection is often silent, or produces nausea, abdominal pain, bloating and watery diarrhea beginning within a day of the meal and settling over a few days to weeks; S. suihominis from pork tends to be more symptomatic than S. hominis from beef. Muscular sarcocystosis produces a biphasic illness: an early phase of fever, headache, myalgia and cough with eosinophilia, then weeks later a second phase of muscle tenderness, weakness, facial swelling and raised creatine kinase, sometimes with a transient rash.",
  mech: "In the intestinal form, sexual reproduction occurs within the lamina propria of the small bowel, and the local inflammatory response to gametogony and oocyst formation accounts for the brief enteritis; segmental eosinophilic enteritis and, rarely, obstruction have been described. In the muscular form, sporozoites released in the gut cross into the circulation, multiply in vascular endothelium, and then seed striated muscle, where they mature into sarcocysts over weeks. The early systemic illness corresponds to the vascular schizogony phase and the later myositis to cyst maturation and to the immune recognition of cyst antigen. Because humans are not the natural intermediate host, development is often incomplete, and the resulting inflammation is disproportionate to the parasite burden. Eosinophilia is prominent in both phases of the muscular form.",
  lab: "Intestinal infection is diagnosed by finding sporocysts, about 15 by 10 micrometers and usually containing four sporozoites, in stool, typically from one to several weeks after exposure; they are easily missed and stool microscopy is often repeated. Muscular infection is harder: eosinophilia and a raised creatine kinase support it, magnetic resonance imaging may show muscle edema, and muscle biopsy showing a sarcocyst is confirmatory but has a low yield because cysts are scattered. Serology and PCR on biopsy material exist in reference settings. In the outbreak setting the diagnosis has often been made epidemiologically, on a cluster of travelers from one location with the same syndrome.",
  tx: "Intestinal sarcocystosis is generally self-limited and managed supportively with fluids and symptom relief.",
  alt: "For muscular disease there is no established regimen. Albendazole has been used on the reasoning that it reaches tissue cysts, and corticosteroids have been given for the myositis. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole has also been tried. Evidence for all of these is observational and drawn largely from outbreak reports.",
  res: "No resistance is described, which reflects the absence of any standard treatment rather than reliable susceptibility.",
  prev: "Cooking or freezing beef and pork inactivates sarcocysts, and thorough cooking is the single measure that prevents the intestinal form. For the muscular form, drinking treated or boiled water in forested tropical regions is the practical measure, since sporocysts resist chlorination. No vaccine exists for human use.",
  look: "For the intestinal form, Cystoisospora belli and Cyclospora, whose oocysts can be confused on stool microscopy, and any food-borne gastroenteritis. For the muscular form, trichinellosis is the closest mimic, with the same fever, myositis, facial swelling and eosinophilia; also polymyositis, dermatomyositis, leptospirosis, dengue and acute toxoplasmosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Humans are the definitive host for S. hominis and S. suihominis, acquired from undercooked beef and pork, and an accidental intermediate host for the species that cause muscular sarcocystosis."],
    ["Memory trick", "Sarco means flesh: sarcocysts sit in flesh, and eating that flesh puts the sexual stage in your gut."],
    ["Classic setting", "A group of travelers returning from Tioman Island in Malaysia with fever, myalgia, facial swelling and striking eosinophilia some weeks after the trip."],
    ["Step focus", "The biphasic fever then myositis picture with eosinophilia overlaps closely with trichinellosis, and the exposure history is what separates them."]],
  worm: [["From a mouse in 1843", "Miescher described thread-like bodies in the striated muscle of a house mouse in 1843 and could not say what they were; for decades they were called Miescher tubules. Lankester named the genus Sarcocystis in 1882, from the Greek for flesh cyst. The two-host life cycle was not worked out until the early 1970s, when Rommel, Heydorn and Fayer showed that the cysts in prey muscle produce intestinal stages in the predator. Until then the organism's basic biology had been misunderstood for more than a century."],
    ["The Tioman Island outbreaks", "In 2011 and again in 2012, clusters of travelers returning from Tioman Island off peninsular Malaysia developed fever, myalgia and marked eosinophilia, and muscle biopsies showed sarcocysts. More than a hundred cases were eventually linked, making this the largest documented outbreak of human muscular sarcocystosis. Sarcocystis nesbitti was implicated, snakes were suspected as the definitive host on molecular grounds, and water was the likely vehicle. The episode converted a condition known from scattered case reports into a recognized traveler's illness."],
    ["Two completely different diseases from one genus", "Few parasites give a person two unrelated illnesses depending on which end of the life cycle they enter. Swallow a cyst in steak tartare and you become the definitive host, with a few days of gut upset while sexual reproduction proceeds in your intestinal wall. Swallow a sporocyst in contaminated water and you become the prey animal, with parasites multiplying in your blood vessels and settling into your muscles. The genus is the standard teaching example of how host role determines disease."],
    ["Common in livestock, quietly costly", "Sarcocystis infection of cattle and sheep is close to universal in many herds, usually without obvious illness, but heavy infection causes weight loss, abortion and the carcass condemnations known in the meat trade as eosinophilic myositis. The economic weight of the genus falls almost entirely on agriculture rather than on human medicine, which is why most of the research literature appears in veterinary journals and why human physicians meet the organism mainly through outbreak reports."]] },

{ id: "enterocytozoon-bieneusi", k: "para", g: "Microsporidia", nm: "Enterocytozoon bieneusi", also: "microsporidiosis, intestinal microsporidiosis",
  cls: "Microsporidian, an obligate intracellular spore-forming organism now classified with the fungi rather than the protozoa. It has no conventional mitochondria, retaining only reduced mitosomes, and no Golgi in the usual form. The infective spore is tiny, about 1.5 by 0.9 micrometers, with a chitinous wall and a coiled polar tubule. Stages include the proliferative meront, the sporont, the sporoblast and the mature spore, all developing in direct contact with the host cell cytoplasm.",
  plain: "A microscopic spore-forming fungus that fires a harpoon into intestinal cells and causes relentless watery diarrhea in people with very low CD4 counts.",
  live: "Humans, and a wide range of animals including pigs, cattle, dogs, cats, primates, rabbits and birds, with many genotypes shared across species and others appearing host specific. Spores persist in water, and the organism has been detected in surface water, groundwater and treated wastewater. It is the most common microsporidian found in humans.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral through water and food, with waterborne outbreaks documented. Zoonotic transmission from livestock and companion animals is well supported by genotyping. Person-to-person spread occurs, and respiratory acquisition has been proposed for some microsporidia but is not established for this species.",
  causes: "Chronic non-bloody watery diarrhea with cramping, bloating, malabsorption, weight loss and wasting, classically in people with advanced HIV and CD4 counts below 100 cells per microliter. It also causes diarrhea in solid organ and stem cell transplant recipients, in children in low-income settings, in the elderly and in travelers. Biliary involvement with sclerosing cholangitis and acalculous cholecystitis is recognized. Immunocompetent people may have a brief self-limited diarrhea or no symptoms at all.",
  mech: "The spore is the delivery device. On contact with the right conditions in the gut lumen, osmotic pressure inside the spore rises abruptly, the polar tubule everts in milliseconds and pierces the membrane of an enterocyte, and the sporoplasm is injected directly into the cytoplasm. This bypasses receptor-mediated entry entirely and is one of the fastest known mechanisms of cell invasion. Inside, the parasite develops free in the cytoplasm of villous enterocytes, mostly at the villus tips, drawing ATP from the host through nucleotide transporters because it cannot make its own. Infected cells are displaced and shed, villi blunt, crypts lengthen, and the resulting loss of absorptive surface and brush border enzymes produces malabsorptive diarrhea. Inflammation is modest; it is the loss of functioning epithelium rather than invasion or toxin that accounts for the illness, and the severity tracks the degree of immune failure rather than the parasite's own aggressiveness.",
  lab: "Spores are at the limit of light microscopy, which is why the organism went undetected for so long. Modified trichrome staining by the Weber chromotrope method stains the spore wall pink-red against a green background, and calcofluor or other chemofluorescent brighteners bind chitin and make the spores fluoresce. Species cannot be determined by light microscopy. Transmission electron microscopy was the historical reference, showing the characteristic polar tubule coil, and is what originally separated the species. PCR on stool, including multiplex panels, is now the practical method and also provides genotyping. Small bowel biopsy shows the organism in enterocytes on Giemsa or tissue Gram stain.",
  tx: "Restoring immune function is the decisive intervention: effective antiretroviral therapy with a rising CD4 count clears the infection in most people with HIV, and reducing immunosuppression has the same effect in transplant recipients. Fumagillin has activity against this species but is not licensed in many countries and carries marrow toxicity with thrombocytopenia.",
  alt: "Nitazoxanide has been used with partial and inconsistent results. Supportive care with rehydration, antimotility agents and nutritional support is a substantial part of management. Albendazole, which works well for Encephalitozoon species, is of little use here.",
  res: "The albendazole gap is structural rather than acquired: the drug binds beta-tubulin, and the beta-tubulin of Enterocytozoon bieneusi carries amino acid substitutions at the binding site that make it a poor target. This is intrinsic to the species and is the standard explanation for why the two main intestinal microsporidia respond to different drugs.",
  prev: "Safe drinking water, with the caveat that spores resist chlorination and are small enough to pass some filters; boiling is reliable. Hand hygiene after animal contact and careful food handling. For people with advanced HIV, the effective preventive measure is antiretroviral therapy that keeps the CD4 count up. No vaccine.",
  look: "Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora and Cyclospora, which cause the same chronic watery diarrhea in the same patients and are found on the same stool examination. Encephalitozoon intestinalis, which is clinically similar but albendazole responsive and more often disseminates. Mycobacterium avium complex, cytomegalovirus colitis, HIV enteropathy and giardiasis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Enterocytozoon bieneusi causes chronic watery diarrhea in advanced HIV and responds poorly to albendazole; immune reconstitution is what clears it."],
    ["Memory trick", "Entero-cytozoon lives inside the enterocyte cytoplasm, fired in through a polar tube."],
    ["Classic setting", "A person with untreated HIV and a CD4 count of 40 with months of watery diarrhea and weight loss, whose stool shows tiny pink-red spores on modified trichrome."],
    ["Step focus", "Microsporidia are now classified with the fungi, lack true mitochondria, and invade by everting a polar tubule that injects the sporoplasm directly into the host cytoplasm."]],
  worm: [["Found in a single patient in 1985", "The species was described from a Haitian man with AIDS and intractable diarrhea examined in France, and named for the patient, Bieneusi. It was the first microsporidian recognized as a common cause of human disease, and its discovery depended on electron microscopy, because the spores are near the resolution limit of a light microscope. Through the late 1980s and 1990s it became one of the defining opportunistic infections of untreated AIDS, then receded from view in high-income countries as antiretroviral therapy spread."],
    ["A fungus that gave away almost everything", "Microsporidia have among the smallest known eukaryotic genomes, in some species under three megabases, and they achieved this by discarding whole systems: oxidative phosphorylation, most amino acid synthesis, and the machinery of a conventional mitochondrion, retaining only a tiny mitosome. In exchange they import ATP straight from the host through nucleotide transporters acquired by lateral gene transfer. Their placement among the fungi, settled by molecular phylogeny in the 1990s and 2000s, overturned a century of classifying them as primitive protozoa."],
    ["The fastest harpoon in biology", "The polar tubule everts in roughly two milliseconds and can extend to a hundred times the length of the spore, reaching speeds that make it one of the fastest cellular movements known. The energy comes from a sudden rise in internal osmotic pressure as the spore takes on water, with no need for the host to cooperate at all. Because the sporoplasm is injected straight through the host membrane into the cytoplasm, the parasite skips the entire problem of receptor binding and vacuole escape that occupies most intracellular pathogens."],
    ["Two intestinal microsporidia, two drugs", "Enterocytozoon bieneusi and Encephalitozoon intestinalis cause almost the same illness and are indistinguishable on a stained stool slide, yet albendazole cures one and barely touches the other. The reason is a handful of amino acids in beta-tubulin at the benzimidazole binding site. It is an unusually clean example of why species identification can matter when morphology says the organisms are the same, and it is the practical argument for molecular testing in this group."]] },

{ id: "encephalitozoon-intestinalis", k: "para", g: "Microsporidia", nm: "Encephalitozoon intestinalis", also: "Septata intestinalis, the second intestinal microsporidian",
  cls: "Microsporidian fungus, obligate intracellular, with spores about 2.0 by 1.2 micrometers, slightly larger than those of Enterocytozoon bieneusi. It develops inside a parasitophorous vacuole divided by septa of secreted material, which is the feature that earned its original genus name Septata. It infects enterocytes, macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelium, which is the basis of its capacity to disseminate.",
  plain: "A microsporidian that causes the same chronic diarrhea as its commoner cousin, spreads beyond the gut, and responds to albendazole.",
  live: "Humans, with the same or closely related genotypes recovered from donkeys, goats, pigs, cattle, dogs and water. Spores have been detected in surface water and in irrigation water used on produce. It is considerably less common than Enterocytozoon bieneusi in most series.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral through contaminated water and food, with urine a further source once dissemination has occurred, since the organism is shed from infected renal tubular epithelium. Person-to-person and zoonotic routes are both plausible and supported by genotyping.",
  causes: "Chronic watery diarrhea with wasting in advanced HIV and in transplant recipients, clinically indistinguishable from infection with Enterocytozoon bieneusi at the bedside. Unlike that species, it disseminates: cholangitis, nephritis with spores in urine, sinusitis, bronchitis, keratoconjunctivitis, osteomyelitis and disseminated infection with involvement of liver, kidney and nasal mucosa are all described. Immunocompetent people may have self-limited diarrhea.",
  mech: "The polar tubule mechanism of entry is the same as in other microsporidia: the spore everts its tubule and injects the sporoplasm into the host cell cytoplasm. From there the pathways diverge. Where Enterocytozoon bieneusi develops free in the cytoplasm and stays confined to enterocytes, Encephalitozoon intestinalis builds a parasitophorous vacuole with internal septa and, crucially, infects macrophages and fibroblasts in the lamina propria as well as the epithelium. Infected macrophages carry it to distant sites, which is the structural reason this species disseminates and the other does not. In the gut it causes villous blunting, crypt hyperplasia and loss of absorptive surface, so the diarrhea is malabsorptive. Dissemination to renal tubular epithelium accounts for spores in urine, and biliary epithelial infection for the cholangiopathy.",
  lab: "Modified trichrome staining of stool shows pink-red spores with a belt-like stripe, and chemofluorescent brighteners show them more easily, but neither method separates the microsporidian species. Urine examination is worth including when dissemination is suspected and is positive in this species far more often than in Enterocytozoon bieneusi. Species identification rests on PCR, or historically on electron microscopy showing the septate vacuole and the number of polar tubule coils. Duodenal or jejunal biopsy with tissue Gram, Giemsa or Warthin-Starry staining shows the organism, and immunofluorescence with species-specific antibodies is available in reference laboratories.",
  tx: "Albendazole is effective for this species and generally produces clinical and microbiological response, alongside immune restoration with antiretroviral therapy in HIV or reduction of immunosuppression after transplant.",
  alt: "Fumagillin has activity. Topical fumagillin is used for the ocular form. Nitazoxanide has been reported. Supportive rehydration and nutrition remain important in advanced disease.",
  res: "Acquired resistance is not described. The clinically relevant point runs the other way: the beta-tubulin of Encephalitozoon species binds benzimidazoles well, which is why albendazole works here and not against Enterocytozoon bieneusi.",
  prev: "Boiled or treated water, hand hygiene, care with animal contact, and antiretroviral therapy to keep CD4 counts above the range where opportunistic microsporidiosis occurs. Spores resist chlorine at usual drinking water concentrations and survive for months in water at refrigerator temperature.",
  look: "Enterocytozoon bieneusi above all, since the stool slide looks the same and the treatment differs. Encephalitozoon hellem and Encephalitozoon cuniculi, the other disseminating species in the genus, more associated with keratoconjunctivitis and with systemic infection respectively. Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, cytomegalovirus colitis and Mycobacterium avium complex in the same patient population.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Encephalitozoon intestinalis disseminates and responds to albendazole, which is the practical distinction from Enterocytozoon bieneusi."],
    ["Memory trick", "Encephalitozoon travels: it gets out of the intestine, and albendazole catches it."],
    ["Classic setting", "A transplant recipient or a person with advanced HIV with chronic diarrhea who also has spores in the urine, pointing to the disseminating species."],
    ["Step focus", "Infection of macrophages and fibroblasts, not just enterocytes, is the mechanism that lets this species spread beyond the gut."]],
  worm: [["Named twice in four years", "The organism was described in 1993 as Septata intestinalis, for the septate parasitophorous vacuole that distinguished it under the electron microscope. Molecular phylogeny soon showed it belonged inside the existing genus Encephalitozoon, and it was formally transferred in 1995. Both names remain in the older literature, which is a recurring difficulty in microsporidian reading: the group was reorganized repeatedly through the 1990s and 2000s as sequence data replaced ultrastructure as the basis of classification."],
    ["The drug that divides the genus", "Benzimidazoles bind beta-tubulin, and whether they work against a given microsporidian comes down to the residues lining that pocket. Encephalitozoon species have a susceptible sequence; Enterocytozoon bieneusi does not. The practical consequence is that a clinician facing chronic diarrhea and a positive stool trichrome has a decision that morphology cannot inform, and in settings without molecular testing an empirical albendazole trial is sometimes used as both treatment and crude species test."],
    ["Why a spore in the urine matters", "Shedding in urine is unusual among the intestinal pathogens of advanced immunosuppression and is a genuinely useful discriminator. It reflects infection of renal tubular epithelium reached by infected macrophages, and it means that a urine specimen stained with a chemofluorescent brightener can point toward the disseminating species before any molecular result returns. The same finding explains sporadic reports of transmission concern around urine in household and care settings."],
    ["An organism that outlived its moment", "Encephalitozoon intestinalis was characterized at the height of the AIDS epidemic, when chronic diarrhea in people with very low CD4 counts was a daily clinical problem and microsporidia were newly visible. Effective antiretroviral therapy largely removed that population in high-income countries within a decade. The organism did not disappear; it moved into transplant medicine, pediatric populations in low-income settings and travelers, where it is reported steadily but in numbers small enough that most clinicians never meet it."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "balamuthia-mandrillaris", k: "para", g: "Free-living amoebae", nm: "Balamuthia mandrillaris", also: "Balamuthia amebic encephalitis, BAE, granulomatous amebic encephalitis agent, the leptomyxid amoeba",
  cls: "Free-living amoeba, the sole species of its genus, originally placed among the leptomyxid amoebae and now in the family Balamuthiidae. Two stages: an irregular trophozoite 12 to 60 micrometers with broad and sometimes branched pseudopodia and occasionally more than one nucleolus, and a spherical triple-walled cyst 6 to 30 micrometers. It does not grow on bacteria-seeded agar, which sets it apart from Acanthamoeba in the laboratory.",
  plain: "A soil amoeba that can seep from a skin plaque or the nose into the brain and cause a slow, almost uniformly fatal encephalitis.",
  live: "Soil and dust, including potting soil, agricultural soil and dust in arid regions, with occasional recovery from water. It was first isolated from the brain of a mandrill baboon in a San Diego zoo. There is no human-to-human transmission in ordinary life, although transmission through organ transplantation from an infected donor has been documented. Cases cluster in the southwestern United States, Peru, and parts of Asia and Australia, with a disproportionate number in people of Hispanic ancestry for reasons that are not understood.",
  spread: "Inhalation of contaminated dust with spread through the olfactory neuroepithelium, or inoculation through broken skin from soil contact. Unlike Naegleria, it is not associated with swimming or diving in warm fresh water. Organ transplantation from an infected donor has transmitted it to multiple recipients from one donor.",
  causes: "Granulomatous amebic encephalitis with a subacute to chronic course over weeks to months: headache, personality change, seizures, focal deficits, cranial nerve palsies, hemiparesis and progressive decline to coma. In Peru and other South American settings a preceding or concurrent skin lesion is characteristic, typically a painless indurated plaque on the central face or knee that precedes neurological illness by weeks to years. Sinus, lung and renal involvement are reported. Case fatality exceeds ninety percent.",
  mech: "The amoeba binds and penetrates vascular endothelium, crossing the blood-brain barrier through a combination of adhesion by galactose-binding surface proteins and secretion of metalloproteases that degrade collagen, elastin and tight junction components. It shows a marked tropism for perivascular tissue and spreads along blood vessels rather than diffusely, which produces the angiocentric, hemorrhagic and necrotizing granulomas that define the histology. In an immunocompetent host, granulomas with giant cells form around the organisms, hence the name of the syndrome; in profound immunosuppression the granulomatous response fails and the lesions are purely necrotic. Both trophozoites and cysts are found in tissue, which distinguishes it from Naegleria, where only trophozoites appear. The slow tempo relative to Naegleria infection reflects this perivascular, granuloma-forming behavior rather than any lesser destructiveness.",
  lab: "The diagnosis is made late in most cases, often at autopsy. Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein and low or normal glucose with no organisms visible, which is the usual trap, since amoebae are rarely seen in the fluid. Magnetic resonance imaging shows multiple ring-enhancing or heterogeneous lesions, often with hemorrhage, that are frequently read as tumor, tuberculoma or abscess. Brain or skin biopsy with hematoxylin and eosin shows trophozoites and cysts in a perivascular necrotizing granuloma, and immunohistochemistry or PCR at a reference laboratory confirms the species. Culture requires mammalian cell monolayers, because the organism does not feed on bacteria. Real-time PCR distinguishing Balamuthia, Acanthamoeba and Naegleria is available at a small number of reference centers.",
  tx: "There is no established regimen. Reported survivors have generally received prolonged multi-drug therapy containing miltefosine alongside several of the following: albendazole, fluconazole or voriconazole, flucytosine, pentamidine, azithromycin or clarithromycin, and sulfadiazine, often with surgical excision of accessible lesions.",
  alt: "Nitroxoline has shown activity in vitro and in isolated reports and is being examined. Corticosteroids are contentious, since they may be needed for cerebral edema while plausibly worsening the infection. Early recognition appears to matter more than any particular drug choice in the handful of survival reports.",
  res: "Formal resistance is not characterized. The near-uniform failure of treatment is attributed to late diagnosis, poor penetration of most active drugs into brain parenchyma, and the resilience of the cyst stage, which tolerates conditions that kill trophozoites.",
  prev: "No specific measure is established. Gloves and hand hygiene after soil and potting mix handling, and covering open wounds during soil contact, are reasonable in principle, though exposure is so widespread and disease so rare that no public health recommendation targets it. Donor screening is being discussed following the transplant-transmitted clusters.",
  look: "Acanthamoeba granulomatous encephalitis, which is clinically and radiologically very similar and more often associated with immunosuppression. Naegleria fowleri, which is acute, neutrophilic and tied to fresh water exposure. Tuberculoma, toxoplasmosis, neurocysticercosis, brain abscess, primary central nervous system lymphoma and glioma, which are the diagnoses most often made first. For the skin plaque, cutaneous leishmaniasis, sarcoidosis, lupus vulgaris and sporotrichosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Balamuthia causes a subacute granulomatous amebic encephalitis, and both trophozoites and cysts are found in brain tissue, unlike Naegleria."],
    ["Memory trick", "Balamuthia is the slow one from the soil, often starting as a plaque on the face; Naegleria is the fast one from the water."],
    ["Classic setting", "A farm worker or gardener in Peru or the southwestern United States with a painless plaque on the central face, then weeks later headache, seizures and ring-enhancing brain lesions."],
    ["Step focus", "Cerebrospinal fluid shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis with no visible organism, so the diagnosis usually comes from brain or skin biopsy rather than from the fluid."]],
  worm: [["Discovered in a baboon", "In 1986 a mandrill baboon at the San Diego Zoo Wild Animal Park died of meningoencephalitis, and an amoeba was isolated from its brain that matched none of the known genera. Govinda Visvesvara and colleagues characterized it and in 1993 named it Balamuthia mandrillaris, honoring the parasitologist William Balamuth and recording the host in which it was found. Human cases were then recognized retrospectively in archived tissue, which is how a newly named organism acquired a case series stretching back decades."],
    ["The Peruvian pattern", "South American case series, particularly from Peru, describe a presentation that is unusual in North America: a chronic painless skin plaque, most often on the central face or the knee, appearing months or even years before any neurological symptom. Why the disease behaves this way in one region and goes almost directly to the brain in another has not been explained, with proposals including differences in strain, in route of inoculation during agricultural work, and in host genetics. The practical value is high, because the plaque is biopsiable long before the brain is involved."],
    ["Transmitted by transplantation", "In 2009 and 2010 two clusters in the United States involved organ recipients from single donors who had died of what was retrospectively recognized as Balamuthia encephalitis. Kidney, liver and heart recipients developed encephalitis weeks after transplantation, and several died. The episodes established that the organism can be passed in a transplanted organ, forced a reconsideration of unexplained encephalitic deaths in potential donors, and remain the only documented human-to-human transmission."],
    ["Miltefosine's second career", "Miltefosine was developed as a topical breast cancer treatment, then repurposed as the first oral drug for visceral leishmaniasis. Its membrane-disrupting action also kills free-living amoebae in vitro, and it has featured in nearly every reported survival from Balamuthia and Naegleria infection since. The evidence remains a collection of case reports rather than trials, which is unavoidable for a disease this rare, and the drug has been distributed in the United States through expanded access arrangements precisely because no trial could ever be run."]] },

{ id: "sappinia", k: "para", g: "Free-living amoebae", nm: "Sappinia species", also: "Sappinia pedata, Sappinia diploidea, the fourth amoeba of human encephalitis",
  cls: "Free-living amoeba of the family Thecamoebidae, within the Amoebozoa. Its defining feature is a pair of closely apposed, flattened nuclei in the trophozoite, which touch along a flat face and divide together. The trophozoite is 40 to 80 micrometers with a thick cytoplasm and no distinct pseudopodial pattern; it forms a double-walled cyst and reproduces sexually through a cyst stage, which is unusual among the amoebae.",
  plain: "A soil amoeba usually found in animal dung that has been recorded as a cause of human brain inflammation in only a handful of cases.",
  live: "Soil, decaying vegetation and the feces of herbivores, particularly elk, buffalo and cattle; Sappinia pedata was originally isolated from elk dung. It is a free-living bacterivore with no need for an animal host. Reports of human infection are vanishingly rare, with one well-documented case and a small number of additional claimed cases.",
  spread: "Presumed inhalation of contaminated dust or soil, with entry through the nasal passages or sinuses. The single best-documented patient had a sinus infection preceding the neurological illness, which supports that route. There is no evidence of person-to-person transmission.",
  causes: "Amebic encephalitis. The index patient, a previously healthy man in Texas, developed headache, blurred vision, photophobia and vomiting with a single enhancing temporal lobe mass that was resected and shown to contain amoebae. He recovered. Later reports have described encephalitis in a few patients, with inconsistent species confirmation. No other clinical syndrome is attributed to the genus.",
  mech: "Almost nothing is established, which is an honest statement about a condition with one secure case. The lesion in the index patient was a focal necrotic and inflammatory mass with amoebic trophozoites, and the histology resembled the granulomatous amebic encephalitis of Acanthamoeba and Balamuthia without the same degree of perivascular spread. Since the organism is a normal soil bacterivore with no adaptation to mammals, infection is best understood as opportunistic invasion by an organism that happens to tolerate body temperature and to resist being killed by the cells it meets. Whether any host factor was required has not been determined; the index patient had no identified immune defect, which is itself notable.",
  lab: "No routine test exists. Diagnosis in the documented case followed surgical resection of a brain mass and histological recognition of amoebae with the characteristic paired nuclei, which is the single morphological clue the genus offers. Immunofluorescence and PCR at reference laboratories, where species-specific assays exist, provide confirmation; the index organism was initially identified as Sappinia diploidea and later reassigned to Sappinia pedata on molecular grounds. Cerebrospinal fluid findings are nonspecific and imaging shows a solitary or few enhancing lesions that are read as tumor or abscess.",
  tx: "No regimen is established. The index patient received a combination of azithromycin, pentamidine, itraconazole and flucytosine after surgical resection of the lesion and recovered, and that combination is the only reported experience.",
  alt: "By analogy with the other free-living amoebae, miltefosine, voriconazole and albendazole would be considered in a future case. Surgical excision of an accessible mass was part of the one successful outcome.",
  res: "Not characterized, and with a case total in the single digits there is no body of experience from which resistance could be inferred.",
  prev: "No specific measure. Exposure to soil and herbivore dung is universal in agricultural settings while disease is almost nonexistent, so no preventive recommendation is made.",
  look: "Acanthamoeba and Balamuthia encephalitis, which are vastly more common and share the imaging appearance. Naegleria fowleri, which is acute and fulminant. Brain tumor, tuberculoma, bacterial abscess, toxoplasmosis and neurocysticercosis, which is what a solitary enhancing lesion is nearly always due to.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Sappinia is recognized as the fourth genus of free-living amoeba reported to cause human encephalitis, alongside Naegleria, Acanthamoeba and Balamuthia, and the trophozoite has two closely apposed nuclei."],
    ["Memory trick", "Sappinia has a pair of nuclei side by side, and a single famous patient who survived."],
    ["Classic setting", "A healthy man in rural Texas with sinusitis followed by headache and a solitary temporal lobe mass that proved on resection to contain amoebae."],
    ["Step focus", "Two apposed flattened nuclei in the trophozoite is the morphological feature that names the genus."]],
  worm: [["One case, one textbook entry", "In 2001 a 38-year-old man in Texas underwent resection of a temporal lobe mass thought to be a tumor. The tissue contained amoebae with paired nuclei matching no previously reported human pathogen, and the finding was published in 2003, adding a fourth genus to the list of free-living amoebae known to infect the human brain. He recovered fully, which is itself remarkable in a group of infections with mortality above ninety percent. A single patient is the entire clinical evidence base for most of what is written about the genus."],
    ["Elk dung and the comparative method", "Sappinia pedata was described from elk feces and is a routine soil organism with no interest in vertebrates. Its appearance in a human brain illustrates a theme running through this group: Naegleria lives in warm water, Acanthamoeba in soil and tap water, Balamuthia in dust, and all of them make a living eating bacteria. None has any need for a human host, and none benefits from being inside one. Amebic encephalitis is an accident of thermotolerance and of resistance to phagocytes, not an adaptation."],
    ["Reassigned after publication", "The original report identified the organism as Sappinia diploidea, the better-known species of the genus. Molecular analysis of the preserved isolate later showed it to be Sappinia pedata, and the case literature was corrected. This sort of retrospective reassignment is common in the free-living amoebae, where morphological species boundaries were drawn long before sequencing and where a given clinical case may be the only human isolate of its kind ever examined."],
    ["Why rarity is hard to interpret", "A genus with one secure case may be genuinely almost incapable of causing human disease, or it may be causing cases that are never recognized because nobody looks for paired nuclei in a resected brain lesion. The free-living amoebae have a history of the second explanation: Balamuthia cases were identified retrospectively in archived tissue once the organism had a name and an antibody. Whether Sappinia follows the same path depends on whether anyone has reason to test for it."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "leishmania-tropica", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Leishmania tropica", also: "Old World anthroponotic cutaneous leishmaniasis, oriental sore, dry urban sore, Aleppo boil, Baghdad boil",
  cls: "Kinetoplastid protozoan, family Trypanosomatidae, subgenus Leishmania. Two stages: the flagellated promastigote in the sandfly gut and in culture, and the aflagellate intracellular amastigote, 2 to 5 micrometers with a nucleus and a rod-shaped kinetoplast, inside host mononuclear phagocytes.",
  plain: "The sandfly-borne parasite behind the slowly crusting dry sore of Middle Eastern and Central Asian cities, which heals over a year and leaves a scar.",
  live: "Humans are the principal reservoir in the classical anthroponotic urban cycle, which is what distinguishes it from Leishmania major. Dogs, rock hyraxes and rodents act as reservoirs in some foci, so a zoonotic cycle exists alongside the human one. Transmission is by female phlebotomine sandflies, chiefly Phlebotomus sergenti. Endemic across the Middle East, Afghanistan, Pakistan, Iran, Turkey, Central Asia, and parts of North and East Africa, with urban outbreaks in Kabul, Aleppo and Damascus.",
  spread: "Bite of an infected sandfly, a small silent insect that feeds at dusk and through the night and breeds in cracked walls, rubble and animal shelters. Direct inoculation through shared needles and congenital transmission are reported but negligible epidemiologically. Crowding, rubble and interrupted vector control after conflict drive the urban outbreaks.",
  causes: "Cutaneous leishmaniasis with a characteristic dry lesion: a papule at the bite site weeks to months after exposure, slowly enlarging into a nodule and then an ulcer with a raised indurated edge and an adherent crust, typically on the face or an exposed limb, usually painless, taking up to a year or more to heal and leaving a depressed scar. Leishmaniasis recidivans, a chronic relapsing plaque with satellite papules at the edge of an old scar, is a recognized complication. Viscerotropic disease without classical kala-azar, with fever, malaise and splenomegaly, was described in Gulf War veterans.",
  mech: "Promastigotes deposited in the dermis are taken up by neutrophils and then by macrophages, where the parasite survives inside the phagolysosome by remodeling it: lipophosphoglycan on the surface scavenges oxygen radicals and delays phagosome maturation, surface protease GP63 cleaves complement and host signaling proteins, and the parasite inhibits interleukin-12 production so that the macrophage fails to summon a strong type 1 response. Amastigotes divide inside the vacuole until the cell ruptures. The lesion itself is largely immune: a vigorous but imperfectly controlled cell-mediated response produces the granulomatous induration and the ulceration, which is why the sore is dry and slow and why healing coincides with the development of strong delayed hypersensitivity. The same immunology accounts for leishmaniasis recidivans, where an excessive response holds the parasite at very low numbers while generating chronic inflammation at the scar edge.",
  lab: "Slit-skin smear or dermal scraping from the indurated edge of the lesion, stained with Giemsa, shows amastigotes inside macrophages with their nucleus and kinetoplast; yield falls as lesions age. Culture in Novy-MacNeal-Nicolle medium grows promastigotes. PCR on lesion material is the most sensitive method and, with sequencing or restriction analysis, identifies the species, which matters because treatment differs. Histology shows a granulomatous dermal infiltrate with few organisms in chronic lesions. The leishmanin skin test is positive in cutaneous disease and has been used in surveys. Serology is of little use in cutaneous leishmaniasis.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on lesion number, size, site and species. Localized disease is often treated with intralesional antimonial injection, thermotherapy or cryotherapy, and systemic pentavalent antimony is used for multiple, large, facial or disabling lesions.",
  alt: "Oral miltefosine, fluconazole and itraconazole have been used with variable results against this species. Liposomal amphotericin B is an option for refractory or disfiguring disease. Paromomycin ointment is used for suitable lesions. Spontaneous healing eventually occurs in most untreated cases, which is why the decision to treat weighs scarring, site and duration rather than mortality.",
  res: "Antimonial unresponsiveness is reported for this species and varies by focus; reduced drug uptake through the aquaglyceroporin AQP1 and increased thiol-dependent detoxification by trypanothione are the described mechanisms. Miltefosine resistance has been mapped to transporter mutations in other Leishmania species and is a theoretical concern here. Response rates differ enough between species and regions that identifying the species informs the choice of drug.",
  prev: "Insecticide-treated bed nets with a fine mesh, since sandflies pass through standard mosquito netting, repellents, long sleeves at dusk, indoor residual spraying, and clearing rubble and cracked masonry that shelter the flies. Prompt treatment of human cases has a direct effect in the anthroponotic cycle because the human case is the reservoir. No licensed human vaccine, although leishmanization, deliberate inoculation to induce immunity, was practiced historically.",
  look: "Leishmania major, which causes a wetter, faster, more exudative sore with the same geography. Leishmania infantum cutaneous disease. Cutaneous tuberculosis and lupus vulgaris, sporotrichosis, mycetoma, leprosy, basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma, pyoderma and insect bite reactions with secondary infection. In the recidivans form, lupus vulgaris is the closest mimic.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Leishmania tropica causes the dry urban sore in an anthroponotic cycle where humans are the reservoir, in contrast to the zoonotic rural cycle of Leishmania major."],
    ["Memory trick", "Tropica is the dry town sore: slow, crusted, urban, and spread person to fly to person."],
    ["Classic setting", "A soldier or aid worker back from Kabul or Aleppo with a painless crusted ulcer on the cheek that has been slowly enlarging for four months."],
    ["Step focus", "Amastigotes with nucleus and kinetoplast inside dermal macrophages on a Giemsa-stained slit-skin smear is the diagnostic image."]],
  worm: [["The Aleppo boil and the Baghdad boil", "Cutaneous leishmaniasis has carried the name of whichever city was describing it: Aleppo boil, Baghdad boil, Delhi boil, Jericho boil, salek in Iran, bouton d'Orient in French. Alexander Russell described the Aleppo lesion in detail in 1756, a century and a half before anyone saw the parasite. James Homer Wright identified organisms in a lesion from an Armenian girl in Boston in 1903, in the same year that Leishman and Donovan described the visceral parasite in India, which is why 1903 appears twice in the history of the genus."],
    ["Leishmanization, a vaccine that worked and was abandoned", "Because a healed cutaneous lesion confers lasting immunity, communities across the Middle East and Central Asia practiced deliberate inoculation, often on a covered part of the body, so that the inevitable sore would not land on the face. In the twentieth century this was formalized: the Soviet Union and Iran inoculated large numbers of people with live parasites, and Israel used it in soldiers. It worked, with protection rates that modern candidate vaccines have not matched. It was stopped because a live inoculum produced lesions that sometimes failed to heal, occasionally caused immunosuppression, and could not be standardized or licensed."],
    ["Leishmaniasis recidivans and the cost of a good immune response", "In a small proportion of L. tropica infections, the healed scar develops a creeping edge of fresh papules that persists for years and resists treatment. The parasite burden in these lesions is minimal and the histology is densely granulomatous with a strong delayed hypersensitivity response, which is the opposite of the picture in diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis, where the immune response has failed and the skin is full of organisms. The two conditions are the poles of the same immunological spectrum, in close parallel to tuberculoid and lepromatous leprosy."],
    ["War and the sandfly", "Urban cutaneous leishmaniasis tracks conflict with unusual fidelity. Collapsed buildings provide breeding and resting sites, vector control programs stop, people sleep outdoors or in damaged housing, and the human reservoir goes untreated. Kabul recorded tens of thousands of cases in the late 1990s and early 2000s, and Syria's epidemic expanded sharply after 2011 to the point where the Aleppo boil regained its old name in the medical literature. The organism needs no new biology to produce an epidemic; it needs rubble."]] },

{ id: "leishmania-major", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Leishmania major", also: "Old World zoonotic cutaneous leishmaniasis, wet rural sore, moist oriental sore",
  cls: "Kinetoplastid protozoan, subgenus Leishmania. Promastigote in the sandfly and in culture, amastigote within macrophages of the host. It is the best studied Leishmania species in the laboratory and the standard model organism for the type 1 and type 2 helper T cell paradigm.",
  plain: "The sandfly parasite of desert rodents that gives humans a wet, rapidly ulcerating sore which crusts and heals within a few months.",
  live: "Desert and semidesert rodents are the reservoir, above all the great gerbil Rhombomys opimus in Central Asia and the fat sand rat Psammomys obesus in North Africa and the Middle East, with other gerbils and jirds elsewhere. Humans are incidental hosts in a zoonotic cycle. The vector is Phlebotomus papatasi and related species, which breed in rodent burrows. Endemic across North Africa, the Sahel, the Middle East, Iran, Central Asia and northwest India.",
  spread: "Sandfly bite, typically acquired in rural or peri-desert settings near rodent colonies. Transmission is seasonal, following the sandfly season, and occupational exposure during agriculture, military deployment, construction and oil field work is characteristic. Burrow-dwelling vectors mean that human cases cluster where development disturbs rodent habitat.",
  causes: "Cutaneous leishmaniasis with a shorter incubation than Leishmania tropica, often two to eight weeks, and a wetter lesion: a papule that becomes a nodule and then a moist exudative ulcer with a raised border, often multiple, commonly on the limbs, frequently with regional lymphadenopathy and sometimes with subcutaneous nodules along lymphatics. Most lesions heal spontaneously in three to six months, leaving a scar. Mucosal spread is rare, in contrast to the New World species.",
  mech: "Promastigotes injected with sandfly saliva are taken up by neutrophils first, which the parasite uses as a temporary shelter, and are then transferred to macrophages when the neutrophils are cleared, a sequence described as a Trojan horse strategy. Inside the macrophage, lipophosphoglycan and the surface protease GP63 inhibit oxidative killing and subvert host signaling, and the parasite suppresses interleukin-12 so that differentiation toward a protective response is delayed. Sandfly saliva contributes directly, since its vasodilatory and immunomodulatory peptides enhance early parasite establishment. Control depends on interferon gamma from type 1 helper T cells activating macrophage nitric oxide synthase, and the brisk development of that response is what makes most L. major lesions ulcerate hard and then heal. Where the response fails, the lesion persists or disseminates; where it overshoots, the ulcer is large and destructive. This species, in inbred mouse strains with opposite outcomes, is where the entire type 1 and type 2 helper T cell framework was first worked out.",
  lab: "Giemsa-stained smear or scraping from the lesion edge shows intracellular amastigotes; the yield is better in young, actively inflamed lesions than in old ones. Culture in Novy-MacNeal-Nicolle medium, and PCR on a lesion swab, aspirate or biopsy, which is the most sensitive approach and allows species assignment by sequencing, restriction fragment analysis or species-specific probes. Histology shows a dense dermal infiltrate of macrophages containing organisms early and a granulomatous reaction with few organisms later. Species identification matters for prognosis as well as treatment, since this species rarely involves mucosa.",
  tx: "Because most lesions self-heal, management is often local: cryotherapy, thermotherapy, intralesional antimony, topical paromomycin, or observation of small lesions in cosmetically unimportant sites. Systemic pentavalent antimony is used for multiple, large, facial or functionally significant lesions.",
  alt: "Fluconazole has been reported effective against this species in randomized trials in Saudi Arabia, which is unusual among the Leishmania. Miltefosine, liposomal amphotericin B and ketoconazole are further options. Combination of local heat with systemic therapy is used in resistant lesions.",
  res: "Antimonial failure occurs but is less prominent than in the anthroponotic species and in South Asian visceral disease, where decades of monotherapy selected resistance. The described mechanisms, reduced aquaglyceroporin-mediated uptake and increased trypanothione-dependent detoxification, are the same across the genus. Because the reservoir is a rodent rather than a human, drug pressure on the transmitted population is much lower, which is the structural reason resistance has stayed limited in zoonotic foci.",
  prev: "Repellents, fine-mesh nets and protective clothing at dusk, insecticide-treated clothing in deployed populations, and control of rodent reservoirs by deep ploughing or poisoning of burrows, which has been used at scale in Central Asia and Iran. Treating human cases does not reduce transmission here, because humans are not the reservoir, which is a direct contrast with Leishmania tropica. No licensed vaccine, though this species dominates vaccine research.",
  look: "Leishmania tropica, which is drier, slower and urban. Leishmania aethiopica. Bacterial ecthyma and impetigo, a furuncle, an infected insect bite, sporotrichosis, mycobacterial skin infection, and tropical ulcer. Multiple lesions with lymphatic nodules can mimic sporotrichosis closely.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Leishmania major causes the moist rural sore in a zoonotic cycle with desert rodents as the reservoir, and it usually heals spontaneously within months."],
    ["Memory trick", "Major is moist: fast, wet, multiple sores from the rodent burrows of the desert."],
    ["Classic setting", "A soldier deployed to Iraq or a geologist working in the Moroccan desert with two or three wet ulcers on the forearms six weeks after arriving."],
    ["Step focus", "The type 1 versus type 2 helper T cell paradigm was established in mouse models of this species: interferon gamma and nitric oxide control the infection, an interleukin-4 dominated response permits it."]],
  worm: [["The organism that taught immunology its first dichotomy", "In the 1980s Robert Coffman, Timothy Mosmann and others proposed that helper T cells differentiate into two functional sets, and L. major in mice became the proving ground. C57BL/6 mice mount an interferon gamma response, contain the parasite and heal; BALB/c mice mount an interleukin-4 response and develop progressive disease. Two inbred strains, one parasite, opposite outcomes. The model has been refined many times since, with regulatory T cells and type 17 responses added, but the original experiments still underpin how immunologists describe the choice a naive T cell makes."],
    ["Great gerbils and the Silk Road", "Rhombomys opimus digs vast communal burrow systems across the Central Asian deserts, and those burrows house both the rodents and the sandflies, giving the parasite a stable cycle entirely independent of humans. Archaeological and historical evidence suggests the disease has followed the Silk Road and the irrigation schemes along it for millennia. Twentieth-century agricultural expansion into the deserts, including large Soviet irrigation projects, repeatedly put human settlements next to intact burrow systems and produced epidemics without any change in the parasite."],
    ["Sandfly saliva as a co-factor and a vaccine target", "A sandfly does not inject parasites into a neutral site; it injects them in saliva containing vasodilators, anticoagulants and immunomodulators that measurably increase the size of the resulting lesion in animal models. The corollary attracted attention: animals pre-exposed to uninfected sandfly bites, and so immune to salivary proteins, resist subsequent infection. Vaccine candidates based on salivary antigens such as PpSP15 have progressed on this reasoning, which is an unusual strategy of vaccinating against the vector's spit rather than the pathogen."],
    ["Fluconazole, an exception worth remembering", "Most azoles disappoint against Leishmania, which is why the genus is still treated largely with antimony, amphotericin and miltefosine. A randomized trial in Saudi Arabia published in 2002 found oral fluconazole substantially improved healing of L. major lesions compared with placebo, and later work supported higher doses. The result has not generalized well to other species, and it is a reminder that in this genus the species name, not the clinical appearance, is what predicts a drug response."]] },

{ id: "leishmania-mexicana", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Leishmania mexicana", also: "Leishmania (Leishmania) mexicana, chiclero ulcer, chiclero ear, bay sore, New World cutaneous leishmaniasis",
  cls: "Kinetoplastid protozoan, subgenus Leishmania, within the Leishmania mexicana complex alongside L. amazonensis and L. venezuelensis. Promastigote in the sandfly, amastigote in host macrophages, with this complex notable for forming large parasitophorous vacuoles containing many amastigotes.",
  plain: "A forest sandfly parasite of Central America that classically ulcerates the ear of someone working in the jungle and, in rare hosts, spreads across the skin without ulcerating at all.",
  live: "Forest rodents are the reservoir, including the big-eared climbing rat Ototylomys phyllotis, spiny pocket mice and other sylvatic rodents. The vector is Lutzomyia olmeca olmeca and related New World sandflies, which bite in the forest canopy and understory during the day as well as at dusk. Endemic in southern Mexico, Belize, Guatemala, Honduras and parts of Central America, with the related complex extending into South America, and sporadic autochthonous cases reported in Texas and Oklahoma.",
  spread: "Bite of a forest-dwelling Lutzomyia sandfly during occupational or recreational exposure in the forest. Historically this meant the chicleros, the workers who tapped chicle gum from sapodilla trees in the Yucatan and slept in the forest during the rainy season. Modern exposures are forestry, archaeology, ecotourism, military training and field biology.",
  causes: "Localized cutaneous leishmaniasis: a nodule then a painless well-demarcated ulcer with a raised rolled border, often single, on exposed skin. The classical presentation is on the pinna, where the lesion is slow to heal and can destroy cartilage, giving the chiclero ulcer of the Yucatan. Most non-ear lesions heal spontaneously within a few months. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis, with widespread nonulcerating nodules resembling lepromatous leprosy, occurs in a small number of patients with a selective anergy to leishmanial antigen, and L. mexicana complex species are a leading cause. Mucosal disease is uncommon with this species, which separates it from the Viannia subgenus.",
  mech: "Amastigotes of this complex multiply inside unusually large communal parasitophorous vacuoles, which is a morphological hallmark and reflects a different strategy from the single-parasite vacuoles of other species. Survival inside the macrophage relies on lipophosphoglycan, the GP63 protease and cysteine proteinases that interfere with antigen presentation and with the signaling needed for a protective type 1 response. The outcome in a given person is determined mainly by immunology: an effective cell-mediated response with interferon gamma produces a single ulcer that heals and leaves lasting immunity, whereas selective anergy to leishmanial antigen allows uncontrolled multiplication in skin macrophages over the whole body surface without ulceration, which is diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis. The ear is a problem not because the parasite prefers it but because the pinna is thin, poorly vascularized cartilage with little subcutaneous tissue, so inflammation there destroys structure and heals badly.",
  lab: "Giemsa smear or biopsy from the lesion border shows amastigotes, and in this complex they are often numerous and clustered in large vacuoles. Culture in Novy-MacNeal-Nicolle medium grows readily for this species. PCR with species identification is the preferred method, and distinguishing the subgenus Leishmania from the subgenus Viannia has direct clinical weight, because Viannia species carry mucosal risk and are generally treated systemically. The Montenegro leishmanin skin test is positive in localized disease and characteristically negative in diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis, which is a diagnostic point worth knowing.",
  tx: "Small localized lesions away from the ear, face and joints are often treated locally with thermotherapy, cryotherapy, intralesional antimony or topical paromomycin, and some are observed. Lesions on the ear, face or over joints, and multiple lesions, are generally treated systemically with pentavalent antimony or an alternative.",
  alt: "Ketoconazole has reported activity against this species. Miltefosine and liposomal amphotericin B are used for refractory disease. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis responds poorly to everything, relapses after each course, and has been managed with prolonged antimony, amphotericin, miltefosine and immunomodulation with interferon gamma or immunotherapy, with limited durable success.",
  res: "Clinically significant antimonial unresponsiveness occurs, and the mechanisms described for the genus apply: reduced uptake through aquaglyceroporin, raised trypanothione-mediated thiol defense, and increased drug efflux. The poor response in diffuse cutaneous disease is better explained by host anergy than by parasite resistance, since isolates from those patients are often drug susceptible in vitro.",
  prev: "Avoiding forest exposure at peak biting times, long sleeves and trousers, permethrin-treated clothing, repellents and fine-mesh hammock nets. Reservoir control is impractical in a sylvatic cycle, and treating human cases has no effect on transmission because humans are incidental. No vaccine.",
  look: "Leishmania braziliensis and the other Viannia species, which matter because of mucosal risk and are distinguished molecularly rather than clinically. Sporotrichosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, chromoblastomycosis, cutaneous tuberculosis, atypical mycobacteria, squamous cell carcinoma and tropical ulcer. In diffuse cutaneous disease, lepromatous leprosy is the closest mimic, both clinically and immunologically.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Leishmania mexicana causes the chiclero ulcer of the ear and is a leading cause of diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis, in which the leishmanin skin test is negative."],
    ["Memory trick", "Mexicana goes for the ear of the chiclero in the Yucatan forest."],
    ["Classic setting", "A forestry worker or archaeologist in the Yucatan or Belize with a painless rolled-edge ulcer on the pinna that has persisted for months."],
    ["Step focus", "Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis reflects selective anergy to leishmanial antigen, giving widespread nonulcerating nodules, a negative skin test and poor response to treatment."]],
  worm: [["The chewing gum trade and an ulcerated ear", "Chicle, the latex of the sapodilla tree, was the base of commercial chewing gum until synthetic substitutes displaced it. Tapping it meant months in the Yucatan forest in the rainy season, sleeping where the sandflies fed, and the resulting ear ulcer became so characteristic of the trade that it entered the medical literature as ulcera de los chicleros. The occupation has largely gone; the name survives in textbooks, which is why a lesion is still described by reference to a job most clinicians have never heard of."],
    ["Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis and the leprosy parallel", "A handful of patients infected with the L. mexicana complex develop nodules over much of the skin, teeming with amastigotes, with no ulceration, no granuloma and a negative leishmanin skin test. The parallel with lepromatous leprosy is exact in structure: a specific failure of cell-mediated response to one organism, with normal responses to everything else, producing a high parasite burden and poor treatment response. The two diseases are routinely taught together for this reason, and both sit at the anergic pole of an immunological spectrum whose other end is a sparse, intensely granulomatous, hard-to-find-the-organism lesion."],
    ["Big vacuoles and a different kind of housekeeping", "Most Leishmania amastigotes sit one to a tight vacuole. The mexicana complex builds large communal vacuoles holding many parasites, visible at light microscopy and a useful clue on histology. The arrangement has drawn research interest because it implies different control of vacuole fusion and nutrient acquisition, and because cysteine proteinase B of this complex, which is abundant and implicated in degrading host antigen-presentation machinery, is partly what makes it a favored laboratory model for how the parasite shapes the response against itself."],
    ["Leishmaniasis in Texas", "Locally acquired cutaneous leishmaniasis has been reported in Texas and Oklahoma since the 1900s, with the L. mexicana complex and native woodrats implicated, transmitted by North American Lutzomyia sandflies. Case numbers are small but the geographic range of both vector and reservoir appears to be moving north. It is a standing counterexample to the assumption that leishmaniasis in a North American patient implies travel, and the diagnosis has been delayed in several reported cases for exactly that reason."]] },

{ id: "trypanosoma-rangeli", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Trypanosoma rangeli", also: "the nonpathogenic American trypanosome, the Chagas confounder",
  cls: "Kinetoplastid protozoan, family Trypanosomatidae, subgenus Herpetosoma. The bloodstream trypomastigote is long and slender, 26 to 36 micrometers, noticeably longer than that of Trypanosoma cruzi, with a small subterminal kinetoplast. Development in the insect vector involves epimastigotes in the gut that invade the hemocoel and reach the salivary glands, producing metacyclic trypomastigotes there.",
  plain: "A South American trypanosome that infects humans harmlessly but confuses the blood tests used to diagnose Chagas disease.",
  live: "A wide range of wild and domestic mammals, including opossums, anteaters, sloths, monkeys, dogs, cats and bats, with humans as incidental hosts. Vectors are triatomine bugs, principally Rhodnius prolixus and other Rhodnius species. Distributed from Central America through much of South America, with geographic overlap with Trypanosoma cruzi across most of its range.",
  spread: "Inoculation by the bite of an infected triatomine bug, since this species reaches the salivary glands and is transmitted anteriorly in saliva. This is a genuine biological distinction from Trypanosoma cruzi, which is shed in the bug's feces and enters through the bite wound or mucosa when the host scratches. Both routes can occur with T. rangeli, but salivary transmission is characteristic.",
  causes: "No established human disease. Infections are detected incidentally or in surveys, and parasitemia is transient and low. There is no cardiomyopathy, no megaesophagus and no megacolon, and no convincing report of any clinical syndrome. The clinical importance is entirely diagnostic: it cross-reacts with Trypanosoma cruzi serology and can be mistaken for it on a blood film, which risks a false diagnosis of Chagas disease with its implications for prognosis, blood donation and treatment.",
  mech: "The relevant mechanism is the absence of one. Trypanosoma rangeli does not establish intracellular amastigote nests in cardiac or smooth muscle, which is the step on which the entire pathology of Chagas disease depends, and it lacks the trans-sialidase activity and surface molecule repertoire that T. cruzi uses to invade non-phagocytic cells and to persist for decades. Parasitemia is cleared by the immune response within weeks. The species is, however, pathogenic to its vector: in Rhodnius it crosses the gut wall into the hemocoel, infects the hemolymph and salivary glands, and impairs hemolymph coagulation, immune function and feeding, which reduces bug survival and fecundity. It is one of the clearer examples of a parasite that harms its insect host and not its mammalian one.",
  lab: "On a stained blood film the trypomastigote is longer and more slender than that of T. cruzi, with a smaller kinetoplast, but overlap is substantial and morphology alone is unreliable in practice. Hemoculture and xenodiagnosis grow it. Serological cross-reaction with T. cruzi antigen is extensive because the two share many antigens, which is the root of the problem: conventional Chagas serology cannot separate them, and in regions where both circulate this inflates apparent Chagas seroprevalence. PCR with species-specific primers, or restriction analysis of amplified kinetoplast or ribosomal sequences, provides the definitive distinction, and detection of the parasite in the vector's salivary glands is itself diagnostic of the species.",
  tx: "No treatment is indicated, since no disease is attributed to it.",
  alt: "Not applicable. The practical action when it is identified is to withdraw a presumptive Chagas diagnosis and to avoid committing a patient to benznidazole or nifurtimox, both of which are poorly tolerated, on the basis of a cross-reacting antibody test.",
  res: "Not applicable.",
  prev: "The same vector control, housing improvement and triatomine elimination programs that target Trypanosoma cruzi incidentally reduce it. No measure is directed at it specifically.",
  look: "Trypanosoma cruzi above all, which is the entire clinical issue. The African trypanosomes, which are morphologically similar on a film but epidemiologically unmistakable. Other nonpathogenic mammalian trypanosomes such as Trypanosoma lewisi, and Trypanosoma minasense in primates, which raise the same cross-reactivity question in survey work.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Trypanosoma rangeli is nonpathogenic in humans but cross-reacts with Trypanosoma cruzi serology, so it can produce a false diagnosis of Chagas disease."],
    ["Memory trick", "Rangeli travels in the bug's saliva and causes no range of disease; cruzi travels in the bug's feces and causes all of it."],
    ["Classic setting", "A survey in Colombia or Venezuela finds a high rate of Chagas seropositivity in a community with little cardiac disease, and species-specific PCR shows much of it is Trypanosoma rangeli."],
    ["Step focus", "Salivary, anterior transmission distinguishes T. rangeli from the fecal, posterior transmission of T. cruzi, and the absence of intracellular amastigote nests explains the absence of disease."]],
  worm: [["Named from a bug in Venezuela", "Enrique Tejera described the organism in 1920 from the gut of Rhodnius prolixus collected in Venezuela and named it for Rafael Rangel, the Venezuelan parasitologist. For decades it was treated as a curiosity of insect parasitology. Its medical relevance emerged only as serological surveys for Chagas disease spread across Latin America and investigators began to notice that seroprevalence in some areas did not match the burden of cardiac disease at all."],
    ["A parasite that is hard on its vector", "The usual expectation is that a vector-borne parasite spares the insect it depends on. Trypanosoma rangeli does not: it crosses the gut wall into the hemocoel of Rhodnius, interferes with hemolymph clotting and with the insect's cellular immune response, and reduces survival, molting success and feeding efficiency. Studies have even examined it as a candidate biological control agent for triatomine populations. It is a working reminder that benign to the mammal and benign to the insect are independent questions."],
    ["Why serology cannot tell them apart", "Trypanosoma rangeli and Trypanosoma cruzi share a large proportion of their antigens, and the whole-parasite or crude-lysate antigens used in conventional Chagas serology bind antibodies to both. Attempts to find a discriminating recombinant antigen have had partial success, and the practical answer has been molecular: species-specific PCR, or sequencing of kinetoplast or ribosomal regions. Where both parasites circulate, published Chagas seroprevalence figures from before molecular confirmation are generally read with that caveat attached."],
    ["The value of a harmless relative", "Because the two species share a vector, a geographic range and much of their antigenic repertoire while differing completely in outcome, T. rangeli has become a comparative tool for asking what makes T. cruzi pathogenic. The differences that stand out are the capacity to invade non-phagocytic cells, the trans-sialidase family, and the ability to persist intracellularly for decades. Each is a candidate explanation for Chagas cardiomyopathy, and each can be tested against a close relative that lacks it."]] },

{ id: "babesia-divergens", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Babesia divergens", also: "European babesiosis agent, bovine babesiosis agent, redwater fever agent in cattle",
  cls: "Apicomplexan piroplasm, order Piroplasmida, family Babesiidae. Intraerythrocytic stages are small pleomorphic trophozoites 1 to 3 micrometers, classically paired and divergent at an obtuse angle, which is the origin of the species name, together with ring forms and occasional tetrads. Sexual stages and sporogony occur in the tick, and there is no exoerythrocytic stage in the vertebrate, which is a key distinction from malaria.",
  plain: "A cattle parasite carried by European sheep ticks that causes a fast, massive destruction of red cells in people who have lost their spleen.",
  live: "Cattle are the reservoir and natural host, with the tick Ixodes ricinus as vector; wild ruminants and rodents are also involved in maintaining the cycle. Human infection is reported across Europe, in France, Ireland, the United Kingdom, Spain, Portugal, Croatia, Scandinavia and Russia, and Babesia divergens-like organisms have been reported from the United States. Human cases are rare and concentrated in rural and agricultural settings.",
  spread: "Bite of an Ixodes ricinus tick, usually the nymph, acquired in pasture, woodland edge and farmland. Transfusion-transmitted babesiosis is documented for the genus and is a recognized blood safety concern, chiefly for Babesia microti but a principle that applies here. Vertical transmission is reported rarely.",
  causes: "Human infection is overwhelmingly in people who have had a splenectomy, and in that setting it is a fulminant illness: abrupt high fever with rigors, severe intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinuria giving dark red or brown urine, jaundice, profound anemia, acute kidney injury, disseminated intravascular coagulation, pulmonary edema and shock, progressing over a few days. Mortality in the historical European series approached forty percent. People with intact spleens appear largely to clear it, and asymptomatic seropositivity is documented in exposed rural populations.",
  mech: "Sporozoites injected by the tick enter red cells directly, with no liver or lymphocyte stage, and divide by binary fission to produce the paired divergent forms. Rupture of infected cells releases merozoites that invade further red cells, and the cycle amplifies rapidly; parasitemias above thirty percent have been recorded. The destruction is mechanical and direct: hemolysis is intravascular, free hemoglobin is filtered and precipitates in tubules, and the combination of hemoglobinuria, hypotension and cytokine release produces acute kidney injury. The splenic dependence is the defining feature of the biology. The spleen removes parasitized and deformed red cells and supports the antibody response, and without it parasitemia is not restrained, which is why asplenia converts a near-silent zoonosis into a disease that can kill in days. Unlike falciparum malaria, Babesia does not sequester in capillaries through surface knobs, so the pathology is hemolytic rather than obstructive.",
  lab: "Giemsa-stained thin blood film shows small intraerythrocytic forms, often multiple per cell, with paired pyriform bodies lying at a divergent angle and sometimes a Maltese cross tetrad; there is no pigment, no Schuffner dots and no gametocyte, which are the main features separating babesiosis from malaria on a film. Parasitemia is frequently very high in asplenic patients. PCR confirms the genus and species, and 18S ribosomal sequencing is the reference method; serology by indirect immunofluorescence is useful for exposure but lags in acute disease. Hemolytic markers are deranged: low hemoglobin and haptoglobin, raised lactate dehydrogenase and unconjugated bilirubin, hemoglobinuria, and a reticulocytosis once the marrow responds.",
  tx: "Severe infection with this species is treated with intravenous clindamycin together with quinine, which is the regimen on which the European survival experience was built, with red cell exchange transfusion in high parasitemia or severe hemolysis.",
  alt: "Atovaquone with azithromycin is the standard combination for Babesia microti and has been used here, though reported experience is smaller. Supportive care is substantial: transfusion, renal replacement therapy and intensive care support. Exchange transfusion is the intervention that reduces parasite load fastest.",
  res: "Atovaquone resistance through cytochrome b mutations is documented in Babesia microti in immunocompromised patients on prolonged therapy, and azithromycin resistance has been reported in the same setting. For B. divergens, case numbers are too small for resistance patterns to be established, and failure generally reflects the speed of the illness rather than the drug.",
  prev: "Tick avoidance in pasture and woodland, permethrin-treated clothing, repellents and daily skin checks with prompt removal. People without a spleen are counseled about tick exposure and about seeking care urgently for fever, as they are for other encapsulated and intraerythrocytic infections. There is a veterinary vaccine tradition for bovine babesiosis but no human vaccine.",
  look: "Plasmodium falciparum, which also shows rings at high parasitemia but has pigment, banana gametocytes and a travel history. Babesia microti, which causes a milder and more protracted illness in the northeastern United States and often infects people with spleens. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, clostridial sepsis, hemolytic uremic syndrome and overwhelming post-splenectomy sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Babesia divergens causes fulminant hemolysis almost exclusively in people who have had a splenectomy, and the European first-line regimen is clindamycin with quinine."],
    ["Memory trick", "Divergens diverges in pairs on the film and diverges from microti by needing no spleen in the patient."],
    ["Classic setting", "A farmer in Ireland or Brittany who had a splenectomy years earlier, now with high fever, dark urine, jaundice and a blood film showing intraerythrocytic parasites in a quarter of the red cells."],
    ["Step focus", "Babesia has no exoerythrocytic liver stage, no malarial pigment and no gametocytes on the film, and may show a Maltese cross tetrad."]],
  worm: [["Named for the angle of a dividing parasite", "Victor Babes described intraerythrocytic organisms in cattle with hemoglobinuria in Romania in 1888, giving the genus its name. The species epithet divergens records a morphological detail visible on a stained film: the paired pear-shaped parasites sit nose to nose at an obtuse angle rather than in parallel. It remains one of the few organisms whose name tells you exactly what to look for down the microscope, which is why the detail persists in every description of the species."],
    ["Texas fever and the first proof of arthropod transmission", "The piroplasms hold a foundational place in medicine because of a cattle disease. In 1893 Theobald Smith and Frederick Kilborne showed that Texas cattle fever, caused by Babesia bigemina, was transmitted by the tick Rhipicephalus annulatus, and that the tick passed it to its own offspring. This was the first demonstration that an arthropod transmits a disease agent, published four years before Ross implicated the mosquito in malaria. Veterinary parasitology established the principle on which the whole of vector-borne human medicine rests."],
    ["The spleen as a visible organ of defense", "Few infections demonstrate splenic function as plainly. With a spleen, B. divergens exposure usually produces no illness and sometimes only antibodies; without one, the same exposure can produce a thirty percent parasitemia and death within days. The spleen is filtering deformed and parasitized red cells and mounting the antibody response that limits reinvasion, and its absence removes both. The same logic is why asplenic patients are vaccinated against encapsulated bacteria and why babesiosis belongs in the counseling given after splenectomy."],
    ["Two babesias, two diseases", "Babesia microti in the northeastern United States is a rodent parasite carried by Ixodes scapularis, infects people with intact spleens, produces a protracted flu-like illness with hemolysis, and is treated with atovaquone and azithromycin. Babesia divergens in Europe is a cattle parasite carried by Ixodes ricinus, infects almost only the asplenic, moves in days, and is treated with clindamycin and quinine. The genus is a case study in how reservoir and host immunity, rather than the parasite's own machinery, set the shape of a disease."]] },

{ id: "plasmodium-simium", k: "para", g: "Blood and tissue protozoa", nm: "Plasmodium simium", also: "zoonotic simian malaria of the Atlantic Forest, howler monkey malaria",
  cls: "Apicomplexan protozoan of the genus Plasmodium, a parasite of New World monkeys that is genetically almost identical to Plasmodium vivax and is now regarded as a vivax lineage that transferred into platyrrhine monkeys. Stages follow the vivax pattern: sporozoite, hepatic schizont, ring and amoeboid trophozoite, erythrocytic schizont, merozoite and rounded gametocyte, with sporogony in the mosquito.",
  plain: "A monkey malaria of the Brazilian Atlantic coastal forest that spills over into people and looks like vivax malaria on the blood film.",
  live: "Brown howler monkeys, Alouatta guariba clamitans, and brown capuchins, Sapajus nigritus, are the natural hosts, with the parasite maintained in the canopy of the Atlantic Forest of southeastern Brazil. The vector is the bromeliad-breeding mosquito Anopheles, subgenus Kerteszia, chiefly Anopheles cruzii, which breeds in water held in the leaf axils of epiphytic bromeliads and feeds at both canopy and ground level.",
  spread: "Bite of an infected Anopheles cruzii. Human cases are acquired during work, residence or recreation in or at the edge of the forest, outside the recognized Amazonian malaria transmission area. Whether sustained human-to-human transmission occurs, as opposed to repeated zoonotic spillover from monkeys, has not been settled.",
  causes: "Uncomplicated malaria with fever, chills, headache, myalgia and thrombocytopenia, generally mild, with low parasitemia. Severe disease has not been a feature of the reported outbreaks. Because cases occur in a region classified as malaria-free, they are frequently misdiagnosed initially as dengue, influenza or another febrile illness, and diagnosis is often delayed.",
  mech: "The mechanism follows Plasmodium vivax closely, which is unsurprising given the genetic relationship. Merozoites preferentially invade reticulocytes, which caps parasitemia at a low level and accounts for the mild clinical course. Fever follows synchronous schizont rupture and the release of parasite material that triggers cytokine production. Whether this parasite forms hypnozoites in the human liver, and therefore whether it can relapse, is an open question of real practical importance, since it determines whether a radical cure is appropriate; the vivax relationship makes it plausible. There is no sequestration mechanism comparable to the PfEMP1 knobs of Plasmodium falciparum, so neither cerebral nor placental pathology is expected. Its main biological interest is as an example of a Plasmodium lineage crossing between host orders twice: from humans or their ancestors into New World monkeys after European contact, and now back again.",
  lab: "Giemsa thick and thin films show enlarged red cells with Schuffner dots, amoeboid trophozoites and rounded gametocytes, indistinguishable from Plasmodium vivax by microscopy. Rapid diagnostic tests based on parasite lactate dehydrogenase may be positive and do not identify the species. Species assignment requires molecular work, and routine vivax-specific PCR primers amplify it as well, so detection depends on sequencing or on assays designed to separate the two, including mitochondrial and apicoplast markers and a distinguishing deletion in the 18S ribosomal region. Without such testing, cases are recorded as vivax malaria, which is how the outbreaks were initially classified.",
  tx: "Reported cases have responded to the standard treatment used for Plasmodium vivax, chloroquine for the blood stage in a chloroquine-sensitive setting, which Brazil remains for vivax.",
  alt: "Artemisinin-based combinations where chloroquine is unsuitable. Primaquine has been given in some cases on the reasoning that a vivax-like parasite may form hypnozoites, with G6PD status taken into account; whether this is necessary remains undetermined.",
  res: "No resistance has been characterized, which reflects the small number of recognized cases and the absence of any sustained drug pressure on a parasite population that spends most of its existence in monkeys.",
  prev: "Repellents and protective clothing in and near the Atlantic Forest, avoiding dawn and dusk exposure at forest edges, and awareness among clinicians in a region officially free of malaria that a febrile patient with forest exposure may have it. Reservoir control is not feasible, since the hosts are protected primates in a conservation area. The existence of a monkey reservoir has implications for malaria elimination targets, because a zoonotic source cannot be removed by treating human cases.",
  look: "Plasmodium vivax, which is the whole diagnostic difficulty, and Plasmodium malariae and Plasmodium brasilianum, another New World monkey parasite indistinguishable from P. malariae and also zoonotic. Dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever, leptospirosis, rickettsial infection and influenza in a febrile patient from this region. Plasmodium knowlesi in Southeast Asia is the direct conceptual parallel.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Plasmodium simium is a zoonotic monkey malaria of the Brazilian Atlantic Forest that is genetically and microscopically almost identical to Plasmodium vivax."],
    ["Memory trick", "Simium is simian vivax: howler monkeys in the canopy, bromeliad mosquitoes, and a film you would call vivax."],
    ["Classic setting", "A hiker or forest worker in the hills near Rio de Janeiro, a region declared malaria-free, with fever and a blood film showing vivax-like parasites."],
    ["Step focus", "Zoonotic malarias such as P. simium in Brazil and P. knowlesi in Southeast Asia mean that an animal reservoir can sustain transmission after human cases are eliminated."]],
  worm: [["A parasite that crossed twice", "Fonseca described Plasmodium simium in Brazilian howler monkeys in 1951. Genomic work since has shown that it is not an ancient monkey parasite at all but a Plasmodium vivax lineage that entered New World primates relatively recently, most plausibly from humans arriving during and after European colonization, since New World monkeys and Old World parasites had no earlier contact. The 2015 to 2016 human cases in the Atlantic Forest therefore represent the parasite returning to a host its ancestors left a few centuries ago."],
    ["Mosquitoes that breed in plants", "Anopheles of the subgenus Kerteszia lay their eggs in the water held by bromeliads growing on tree trunks and branches, which is a breeding habitat with no standing pools to drain and no margins to spray. Bromeliad malaria was a recognized problem along the Brazilian coast in the mid twentieth century, and control attempts included removing epiphytes from forests. The vector's habitat is one reason the Atlantic Forest cycle has proved so durable: the larval sites are distributed through the canopy of a protected ecosystem."],
    ["Mistaken for vivax by design", "Because the two parasites are so close, the standard molecular assays used in Brazil to confirm vivax malaria also amplify P. simium, and microscopy cannot separate them. The 2015 and 2016 outbreaks near Rio de Janeiro were initially reported as vivax cases, and the zoonotic origin emerged only when investigators sequenced mitochondrial and ribosomal regions and found the monkey lineage. The episode showed that a country's malaria surveillance can be working exactly as designed and still miss a change in what is being transmitted."],
    ["What zoonotic malaria means for elimination", "Malaria elimination strategies assume the human reservoir can be emptied. Plasmodium knowlesi in Malaysian Borneo and Plasmodium simium in southeastern Brazil both break that assumption, because macaques and howler monkeys will keep the parasite circulating whatever happens to human cases. In Sabah, human knowlesi cases rose as the other human malarias were driven down. Whether to count such cases toward malaria elimination targets is a live policy question, and one with no satisfactory answer."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "angiostrongylus-cantonensis", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Angiostrongylus cantonensis", also: "rat lungworm, Parastrongylus cantonensis, eosinophilic meningitis agent, neuroangiostrongyliasis",
  cls: "Metastrongyloid nematode of the superfamily Metastrongyloidea. Adults, 20 to 25 millimeters in the female and about 20 in the male, live in the pulmonary arteries of rats. The cycle runs rat to mollusk and back: first-stage larvae are passed in rat feces, develop to infective third-stage larvae in snails and slugs, and in humans those larvae migrate to the central nervous system and die there without maturing.",
  plain: "A worm of rat lungs whose larvae, swallowed in a snail or on unwashed salad, crawl into the human brain and set off a severe headache with eosinophils in the spinal fluid.",
  live: "Rats, principally Rattus rattus and Rattus norvegicus, are the definitive hosts. Snails and slugs, above all the giant African land snail Lissachatina fulica and the semi-slug Parmarion martensi, are intermediate hosts. Freshwater prawns, land crabs, frogs and monitor lizards act as paratenic hosts that carry viable larvae without developing them. Originally recognized in Southeast Asia and the Pacific, it is now established in Hawaii, Australia, the Caribbean, Brazil and the southeastern United States, with the range expanding along with its snail hosts.",
  spread: "Swallowing third-stage larvae: in raw or undercooked snails, slugs, prawns, crabs or frogs; in vegetables and salad contaminated by slug trails or by a small slug hidden in the leaves; and in unfiltered water or raw juice. Transmission by handling without swallowing is not established. Children are described swallowing snails out of curiosity, which accounts for a share of pediatric cases.",
  causes: "Eosinophilic meningitis is the characteristic illness: severe headache, often the worst the patient has had, with neck stiffness, vomiting, photophobia, paresthesias and hyperesthesia of the skin, and sometimes cranial nerve palsies, particularly the sixth and seventh. Fever is often absent or mild, which is a useful feature. More severe neuroangiostrongyliasis produces radiculitis, myelitis, encephalitis, coma and death, more often in young children and in heavy infections. Ocular invasion with a larva visible in the vitreous or anterior chamber is described. Most cases resolve over weeks, though headache and paresthesia can persist for months.",
  mech: "Larvae swallowed by a human penetrate the gut, reach the bloodstream and migrate to the central nervous system within one to two weeks, which is the natural route in a rat; in the rat they then move back to the pulmonary arteries and mature. In a human they cannot complete this and die in the meninges, brain parenchyma, spinal cord or eye. The damage is of two kinds. Mechanical tracking by a migrating larva produces linear necrotic tracks and hemorrhage, visible on imaging and at autopsy. More importantly, the death of the larva releases antigen and provokes an intense eosinophilic and granulomatous inflammatory response that is responsible for most of the clinical illness, which is why symptoms can worsen as larvae die and why treatment that kills larvae without controlling inflammation can make a patient worse. Raised intracranial pressure from inflamed meninges accounts for the headache, and repeated therapeutic lumbar puncture relieves it.",
  lab: "Cerebrospinal fluid shows a pleocytosis with eosinophils above ten percent of white cells, which is the defining laboratory finding, with raised protein, normal or mildly low glucose and often raised opening pressure; the fluid may look faintly turbid. Peripheral eosinophilia is common but not universal. Larvae are seen in the fluid in only a small minority of cases, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis. Real-time PCR on cerebrospinal fluid is now available in reference laboratories and has improved confirmation substantially, and serology by immunoblot exists in some centers. Magnetic resonance imaging may show linear tracks, nodular enhancement, leptomeningeal enhancement or nothing at all. Diagnosis is often made on the combination of exposure history, eosinophilic pleocytosis and the exclusion of other causes.",
  tx: "Management centers on reducing inflammation and intracranial pressure: corticosteroids shorten the duration of headache in randomized work, and repeated therapeutic lumbar puncture relieves pressure directly. Analgesia is a substantial part of care.",
  alt: "Albendazole has been used, generally together with corticosteroids rather than alone, on the reasoning that killing larvae without covering the inflammatory response risks worsening symptoms; the evidence for benefit is mixed and the combination has become common practice in Hawaii and parts of Asia. Ocular disease is managed by surgical removal of the larva where feasible.",
  res: "No anthelmintic resistance is described. The debate in this infection concerns whether killing the parasite helps at all, given that the illness is driven by the inflammatory response to a dying larva, rather than whether the drug works.",
  prev: "Washing and inspecting leafy vegetables carefully, since a small slug in a lettuce is a documented vehicle, cooking snails, prawns, crabs and frogs thoroughly, covering water storage, controlling rats and snails around homes and gardens, and supervising young children around snails and slugs. Freezing kills larvae. No vaccine.",
  look: "Gnathostoma spinigerum, which also causes eosinophilic meningitis but more often with hemorrhage and radicular pain and a different exposure history. Neurocysticercosis, Baylisascaris procyonis, Toxocara, Trichinella and Schistosoma in the central nervous system, coccidioidal meningitis, tuberculous meningitis, lymphoma, drug reactions and the eosinophilic pleocytosis that can follow a ventricular shunt or intrathecal contrast. Viral and bacterial meningitis are the first diagnoses considered before the eosinophil count is noticed.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Angiostrongylus cantonensis is the commonest cause of eosinophilic meningitis worldwide, acquired by swallowing larvae in snails, slugs or contaminated vegetables."],
    ["Memory trick", "Rat lungworm takes a wrong turn: in a rat it goes brain then lung, in a person it goes brain then dies, and the eosinophils arrive."],
    ["Classic setting", "A traveler in Thailand or a resident of Hawaii with an excruciating headache, skin hypersensitivity and a spinal fluid eosinophil count of twenty percent, two weeks after eating raw snail salad."],
    ["Step focus", "Eosinophils above ten percent in the cerebrospinal fluid with a compatible exposure is the diagnostic combination; the larva itself is rarely seen."]],
  worm: [["From a rat in Canton to a global parasite", "Chen described the worm in the pulmonary arteries of rats in Canton in 1935. Nomura and Lin recovered it from the cerebrospinal fluid of a patient in Taiwan in 1945, establishing the human disease. Its spread since has been one of the clearest examples of a parasite traveling with an introduced host: the giant African land snail was deliberately moved around the Pacific and the Americas as food, as a pet and by accident, and the worm followed, reaching Hawaii, Australia, Brazil and Florida over a few decades."],
    ["The semi-slug that changed Hawaii", "Hawaiian cases increased markedly after the arrival of Parmarion martensi, a semi-slug from Southeast Asia, which carries much higher larval burdens than other local mollusks, climbs into vegetation and so ends up in produce more often. Investigations on the Big Island linked clusters of severe pediatric cases to this species. It is a case in which the arrival of one new intermediate host, rather than any change in the worm, altered the epidemiology of a disease."],
    ["Treating the inflammation rather than the worm", "For years the question in eosinophilic meningitis was whether to give albendazole, with the worry that a dying larva would release antigen into the meninges and worsen the illness. A randomized trial in Thailand showed that prednisolone reduced the duration of headache, and subsequent practice settled on corticosteroids as the core of treatment with albendazole added in many centers. The underlying lesson is general to helminth infections of the central nervous system: when the pathology is immunological, killing the parasite is not automatically the goal."],
    ["A disease of raw food traditions and of salad", "Outbreaks have followed raw snail dishes in Thailand, raw prawn and frog preparations in China, raw vegetable juice in Taiwan and salad in Jamaica, where a group of visiting students was affected in 2000. The two routes are distinct in a way that matters for prevention: deliberately eating an intermediate host, which education can address, and accidentally swallowing a hidden slug on a lettuce leaf, which requires washing and inspection rather than dietary change."]] },

{ id: "gnathostoma-spinigerum", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Gnathostoma spinigerum", also: "gnathostomiasis agent, Yangtze edema, consular disease, tua chid in Thai usage",
  cls: "Spirurid nematode. The adult, 11 to 54 millimeters, lives in a gastric tumor-like mass in the wall of the stomach of dogs and cats. Its head bears a globular bulb covered with rows of recurved hooklets, which is the structural feature that gives the genus its name and accounts for much of the damage it does. The cycle involves copepods as first intermediate hosts and fish, frogs, snakes, eels and birds as second intermediate and paratenic hosts, where the advanced third-stage larva waits.",
  plain: "A hook-headed worm larva from raw freshwater fish that wanders through human skin, leaving migratory swellings, and sometimes bores into the spinal cord or brain.",
  live: "Dogs, cats and wild felids are the definitive hosts. Freshwater fish, swamp eels, frogs, snakes, chickens and ducks carry the infective larva. Copepods of the genus Cyclops are the first intermediate host. Endemic in Thailand, Laos, Vietnam, Myanmar, Japan, China, and increasingly recognized in Mexico, Ecuador and Peru, with Gnathostoma binucleatum the main species in the Americas.",
  spread: "Eating raw or lightly pickled freshwater fish, eel, frog, snake or poultry containing third-stage larvae. The Thai dish somfak, made with fermented raw fish, and Mexican ceviche are the classical vehicles. Drinking water containing infected copepods is a documented route, as is percutaneous penetration while handling infected meat, and transplacental transmission has been reported.",
  causes: "An initial phase within a day or two of ingestion of fever, malaise, epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting and marked eosinophilia as larvae penetrate the gut wall. The characteristic later illness is cutaneous gnathostomiasis: intermittent migratory swellings, firm, nonpitting, sometimes itchy or painful, lasting a week or so and recurring at a different site weeks later, often on the trunk, face or limbs, continuing over months to years. Creeping eruption and subcutaneous abscesses occur. Visceral forms involve lung, with cough, pleural effusion and pneumothorax, liver, gut, eye with intraocular larva and loss of vision, ear, and most seriously the central nervous system: eosinophilic radiculomyeloencephalitis with severe radicular pain, sudden paraplegia, subarachnoid hemorrhage and a high mortality.",
  mech: "The larva does not mature in a human and instead migrates, and its mechanism is overwhelmingly mechanical. The hooklet-covered cephalic bulb and the cuticular spines let it tunnel through tissue planes, and it secretes proteases, a hyaluronidase and acetylcholinesterase that assist passage. In soft tissue this produces the characteristic intermittent migratory swelling, which traces a single worm moving and then resting. In the central nervous system the larva enters along a nerve root or a vessel and bores through the cord and brain, producing hemorrhagic tracks, and this is what distinguishes the neurological presentation from that of Angiostrongylus: the illness begins with radicular pain in the distribution of the root the worm entered, and bloody or xanthochromic cerebrospinal fluid is common because the worm tears vessels as it travels. Eosinophilic inflammation accompanies the tracks and contributes, but the destructive element is the worm's armored head rather than the immune response to it.",
  lab: "Eosinophilia is usual and often marked. Definitive diagnosis is by recovering and identifying the larva from a lesion, which happens in a minority of cases, and the hooklet rows on the head bulb are what identify it. Serology by immunoblot detecting antibody to a 24 kilodalton antigen is the practical confirmatory test and is used widely in Thailand and Japan. In neurological disease the cerebrospinal fluid shows eosinophilic pleocytosis and is frequently bloody or xanthochromic, and magnetic resonance imaging shows hemorrhagic tracks and nodular enhancement. Biopsy of a migratory swelling often shows eosinophilic inflammation with a track but no worm, since the larva has usually moved on.",
  tx: "Albendazole over a course of two to three weeks is the usual systemic treatment for cutaneous disease, with a recognized phenomenon of the larva emerging into the skin during treatment, where it can be removed.",
  alt: "Ivermectin is an alternative with comparable reported cure rates and the convenience of a short course, and repeated courses are sometimes given for recurrence. Surgical removal of a visible or palpable larva is both diagnostic and curative for that worm. In central nervous system disease, corticosteroids are added to control inflammation and edema, and the role of anthelmintics is debated for the same reason as in angiostrongyliasis.",
  res: "No anthelmintic resistance is described. Treatment failure is understood as a failure to reach a larva in an inaccessible site, or as the presence of additional larvae from the same meal, since a single infected fish may carry many.",
  prev: "Cooking freshwater fish, eel, frog, snake and poultry thoroughly; freezing is also effective. Avoiding raw and fermented fish preparations in endemic regions, treating or boiling drinking water, and using gloves when preparing fish. No vaccine, and the reservoir in dogs, cats and wild fish makes environmental control impractical.",
  look: "Cutaneous larva migrans from animal hookworm, which creeps continuously rather than appearing and disappearing. Loa loa and Dracunculus, sparganosis, cysticercosis, fascioliasis, strongyloidiasis with larva currens, angiedema, cellulitis and urticaria for the swellings. For the neurological form, Angiostrongylus cantonensis, subarachnoid hemorrhage of any cause, transverse myelitis, spinal tumor and Guillain-Barre syndrome.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Gnathostomiasis follows eating raw freshwater fish and causes intermittent migratory subcutaneous swellings with eosinophilia; the central nervous system form produces radicular pain and bloody cerebrospinal fluid."],
    ["Memory trick", "Gnatho means jaw: a hooked head that gnaws its way through tissue, swelling here this week and there the next."],
    ["Classic setting", "A traveler who ate ceviche in Mexico or fermented raw fish in Thailand, with a firm migrating swelling on the abdomen that comes and goes every few weeks and an eosinophil count of fifteen percent."],
    ["Step focus", "Bloody or xanthochromic cerebrospinal fluid with eosinophils separates neurognathostomiasis from Angiostrongylus eosinophilic meningitis, where the fluid is not usually bloody."]],
  worm: [["A worm from a London zoo tiger", "Richard Owen described the genus in 1836 from a gastric mass in a young tiger that had died at the Zoological Society of London. Human infection was recognized in Thailand in the early twentieth century, and the migratory swelling it causes acquired local names long before the cause was known. In Japan the disease was called Yangtze edema, after the region where travelers acquired it, and the term consular disease appears in older literature because European consular staff in Asia were among the first documented Western patients."],
    ["Why the swelling comes and goes", "Most creeping skin lesions are continuous tracks, because the larva keeps moving. Gnathostoma produces something different: a firm swelling that lasts a week or two, resolves completely, and reappears somewhere else weeks later. The pattern reflects a single large larva that migrates, settles in a plane of tissue, provokes local inflammation, then moves on. Recognizing an intermittent rather than continuous lesion is the clinical step that separates this diagnosis from cutaneous larva migrans, and the history over months is more informative than the appearance of any one swelling."],
    ["The hooks do the damage", "The cephalic bulb carries four to eight transverse rows of recurved hooklets, and the body bears cuticular spines. This is a worm built to tunnel, and in the central nervous system the consequence is direct: hemorrhagic tracks through cord and brain, radicular pain as it enters along a nerve root, and blood in the cerebrospinal fluid. Reported mortality in neurological gnathostomiasis has been high, in some series above twenty percent, and survivors frequently have permanent deficits. It is one of the few helminth infections where the mechanical injury clearly outweighs the immunological one."],
    ["Emerging in the Americas", "Gnathostomiasis was long considered an Asian disease. Mexican cases began appearing in the medical literature in the 1970s and increased sharply, and Gnathostoma binucleatum was established as the species responsible, acquired from ceviche made with freshwater fish from coastal lagoons. Cases followed in Ecuador and Peru. The consequence for practice elsewhere is that a migratory swelling in a traveler returning from Latin America now belongs in the same differential as one from Southeast Asia, which was not true a generation ago."]] },

{ id: "baylisascaris-procyonis", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Baylisascaris procyonis", also: "raccoon roundworm, raccoon ascarid, neural larva migrans agent",
  cls: "Ascarid nematode, family Ascarididae, closely related to Ascaris and Toxocara. Adults, 9 to 22 centimeters, live in the small intestine of raccoons. Eggs passed in raccoon feces embryonate in soil over two to four weeks and remain viable for years. In humans and other accidental hosts the larva hatches, migrates through tissue and does not mature, with an unusual tendency to grow large and to enter the central nervous system.",
  plain: "A raccoon intestinal worm whose eggs, swallowed from contaminated soil, send larvae into a child's brain and eyes with devastating consequences.",
  live: "Raccoons, Procyon lotor, are the definitive host, with prevalence above seventy percent in some North American populations and high rates in urban and suburban raccoons. Rodents, rabbits and birds act as intermediate hosts and are eaten by raccoons, completing the cycle. Humans are accidental hosts, as are dogs, which can occasionally harbor adult worms, and a wide range of zoo and wild animals in which fatal neurological disease is well documented. The parasite is established across much of North America and has been reported in Europe and Japan following raccoon introduction.",
  spread: "Swallowing embryonated eggs from soil, sandboxes, decks, woodpiles, attics or anywhere raccoons repeatedly defecate, which they do at communal latrine sites. Most human cases are in very young children with pica or developmental disability, and in people with heavy environmental exposure. Eggs are sticky, resist disinfectants and survive years in soil, so contaminated sites remain hazardous long after raccoons have left.",
  causes: "Neural larva migrans is the feared presentation: in a toddler, a few weeks of irritability or lethargy followed by rapidly progressive eosinophilic meningoencephalitis with seizures, ataxia, coma, cortical blindness and severe permanent neurological damage or death. Ocular larva migrans produces diffuse unilateral subacute neuroretinitis with visual loss, and in adults this is the more common recognized form. Visceral larva migrans with eosinophilia, hepatomegaly and pulmonary infiltrates occurs. Asymptomatic seropositivity is documented, which indicates that light exposure is far commoner than clinical disease.",
  mech: "The feature that makes this organism more dangerous than its close relative Toxocara is larval behavior. Baylisascaris larvae continue to grow substantially after hatching, reaching up to two millimeters in length, and they show a marked tropism for the central nervous system, entering the brain and spinal cord in a much larger proportion of accidental hosts. A growing, actively migrating larva in brain tissue produces mechanical destruction along its path, and the surrounding eosinophilic granulomatous response adds extensive secondary injury with edema and demyelination. The combination explains the severity: a toddler's brain sustains multiple destructive tracks and a violent inflammatory response at once. Larvae may persist and continue migrating for months, which is why the illness progresses rather than resolving and why single short courses of treatment are insufficient. In the eye, a single larva in the subretinal space can destroy central vision.",
  lab: "Peripheral eosinophilia and eosinophilic pleocytosis in the cerebrospinal fluid are the usual findings, with raised protein. Larvae are almost never recovered from the fluid. Serology by Western blot against recombinant Baylisascaris antigens is available at a small number of reference laboratories and is the practical confirmatory test, although cross-reaction with Toxocara is a limitation addressed by using recombinant antigens. Magnetic resonance imaging shows periventricular and deep white matter lesions and enhancement. Ocular diagnosis is clinical, by direct visualization of a motile larva in the retina on funduscopy, which is one of the few settings in parasitology where the worm is seen alive in the patient. Definitive diagnosis has often been made at autopsy or on brain biopsy.",
  tx: "There is no regimen of established efficacy. Reported practice combines albendazole, which penetrates the central nervous system reasonably well, with corticosteroids to control the inflammatory response, started as early as possible.",
  alt: "Ivermectin has been used. For ocular disease, laser photocoagulation of a visualized larva destroys it directly and is the established intervention, with corticosteroids for the accompanying inflammation. Prophylactic albendazole after a documented high-risk ingestion has been recommended in some case discussions on the reasoning that treatment before larvae reach the brain is the only realistic chance of preventing injury.",
  res: "Not described, and not the limiting factor. The obstacle is that by the time neurological symptoms appear, destructive tracks already exist in the brain, and killing the larvae does not repair them.",
  prev: "Discouraging raccoons from attics, decks, chimneys and gardens, securing garbage and not feeding them, identifying and treating raccoon latrine sites, since eggs resist disinfection and heat or removal of the contaminated material is what works, supervising young children in areas with raccoon activity, and handwashing after soil or sandbox play. Covering sandboxes when not in use is a specific and effective measure. No vaccine.",
  look: "Toxocara canis and cati, which cause the same syndromes far more commonly but far more mildly, with neurological involvement rare. Angiostrongylus cantonensis and Gnathostoma in eosinophilic meningitis. Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, viral encephalitis, brain tumor, and metabolic and mitochondrial disease in a deteriorating toddler. For the ocular form, toxoplasmic retinochoroiditis, toxocaral granuloma and retinoblastoma.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Baylisascaris procyonis is the raccoon roundworm, and its larvae invade the central nervous system far more readily than those of Toxocara, causing fatal eosinophilic meningoencephalitis in toddlers."],
    ["Memory trick", "Procyon is the raccoon: the one ascarid larva that grows big and goes for the brain."],
    ["Classic setting", "A two-year-old with pica who plays in a sandbox near a raccoon latrine, presenting with weeks of irritability then seizures, eosinophilic cerebrospinal fluid and white matter lesions."],
    ["Step focus", "Direct visualization of a motile larva in the retina on funduscopy is diagnostic of ocular larva migrans and allows treatment by laser photocoagulation."]],
  worm: [["A veterinary problem that became a human one", "Baylisascaris procyonis was long known to wildlife veterinarians as the cause of fatal neurological disease in woodchucks, rabbits, squirrels, zoo primates and emus; the literature on captive animal deaths considerably predates the human literature. The first confirmed human case of neural larva migrans was reported in 1984 in a child in Pennsylvania. Fewer than thirty confirmed human cases have been published, which makes it one of the rarest parasitic diseases of North America and one of the most severe."],
    ["Eggs that outlast the raccoon", "Ascarid eggs are among the toughest biological structures in the environment: the shell resists formalin, bleach, freezing and desiccation, and the embryonated egg remains infective in soil for years. Raccoons also use communal latrines, returning to the same log, roof or deck corner, so contamination concentrates in exactly the places children play. Decontamination is generally by heat, with flaming or removal of the contaminated material, because chemical disinfection does not work. The practical implication is that removing the raccoons does not make the site safe."],
    ["Why this larva and not Toxocara", "Both are ascarid larvae in the wrong host, and both cause visceral and ocular larva migrans, but the outcomes differ enormously. Baylisascaris larvae keep growing after hatching and show a far stronger tendency to enter and persist in the central nervous system. A larger, more mobile larva in a child's brain, migrating for months, causes injury of a different order. The comparison is the standard illustration that the severity of a zoonotic helminth infection depends on larval behavior and size rather than on the number swallowed."],
    ["The one worm a clinician can watch", "In ocular larva migrans the larva is visible through the ophthalmoscope, moving in the subretinal space, and treatment is to aim a laser at it. There are very few situations in human medicine where a clinician sees the parasite alive, identifies it by its motion, and destroys it under direct vision. That this is possible in the eye and impossible in the brain, where the same larva does vastly more harm unseen, is a fair summary of why the ocular form has a better prognosis."]] },

{ id: "capillaria-philippinensis", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Capillaria philippinensis", also: "Paracapillaria philippinensis, Aonchotheca philippinensis, intestinal capillariasis, mystery disease of Luzon",
  cls: "Trichinelloid nematode, related to Trichuris and Trichinella. Adults are small and hair-thin, the female 2.5 to 4.3 millimeters and the male 1.5 to 3.9 millimeters, and they burrow into the mucosa of the small intestine. The female is unusual in producing both thick-shelled eggs that pass in feces and thin-shelled eggs that hatch in situ, which permits internal autoinfection and allows the worm burden to multiply inside one host.",
  plain: "A hair-thin intestinal worm from raw freshwater fish that multiplies inside its own host until the gut leaks protein and the patient wastes away.",
  live: "Fish-eating birds are considered the natural definitive host, and freshwater and brackish fish are the intermediate hosts; gobies and other small fish have been implicated. Humans are a host in which the parasite reproduces fully and becomes the source of its own amplification. Endemic foci are in the Philippines, particularly northern Luzon, and in Thailand, with cases reported from Japan, Taiwan, Korea, Indonesia, India, Iran, Egypt and Colombia.",
  spread: "Eating whole raw or undercooked small freshwater or brackish fish containing infective larvae. The traditional practice of swallowing small live fish whole, or eating them lightly salted, is the documented route in the Philippine and Thai foci. Human-to-human transmission is not described, but eggs passed into water can reach fish, so a human case can seed the local cycle.",
  causes: "A protracted and potentially fatal enteropathy. Early symptoms are vague abdominal pain, borborygmi and intermittent diarrhea, and over weeks to months the illness becomes a severe chronic diarrhea with voluminous stools, malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy with hypoalbuminemia and marked edema, electrolyte loss with hypokalemia, muscle wasting, weakness and cachexia. Untreated, mortality in the early Philippine outbreaks was high, with death from cardiac failure, electrolyte disturbance or secondary infection. Eosinophilia is often absent or mild, which is a distinctive and misleading feature.",
  mech: "The mechanism is autoinfection, and it is what makes a tiny worm lethal. Larvae mature in the mucosa of the jejunum and ileum, and adult females produce two kinds of egg. Thick-shelled eggs leave in the feces to continue the external cycle. Thin-shelled eggs hatch within the intestinal lumen, and the larvae reinvade the same host's mucosa, so each generation multiplies the burden without any further exposure. Worm numbers in fatal cases have been counted in the tens of thousands. The worms burrow through the epithelium and into the lamina propria, flattening villi, breaching the epithelial barrier and provoking a dense inflammatory infiltrate. The result is a combination of reduced absorptive surface and direct leakage of plasma protein into the lumen, which produces the hypoalbuminemia, edema and electrolyte depletion that dominate the clinical picture. Lymphatic and vascular invasion with dissemination of larvae to other organs has been described in heavy infections.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows the characteristic eggs: peanut or barrel shaped, 36 to 45 micrometers, with a striated shell and flattened, less protruding bipolar plugs, which is the feature that separates them from Trichuris trichiura eggs. Larvae and adult worms also appear in stool in heavy infections. Egg output can be intermittent, so repeated examinations raise the yield. Endoscopic biopsy of the small bowel shows worms in all stages within the mucosa. Laboratory findings reflect the enteropathy: low albumin and total protein, hypokalemia, low calcium, anemia and a low or normal eosinophil count. Barium studies historically showed a coarse mucosal pattern.",
  tx: "Albendazole over a prolonged course, typically ten days or longer, is the treatment used in current practice, together with correction of fluid, electrolyte and protein deficits, which is a major part of care in advanced disease.",
  alt: "Mebendazole over a longer course, of three weeks or more, was the original effective treatment and remains an option. Thiabendazole was used historically. Relapse after a short course is well recognized because of the autoinfective cycle, which is why courses are extended and follow-up stool examinations are performed.",
  res: "No anthelmintic resistance is established. Apparent failure is generally attributed to too short a course against an autoinfecting population, in direct parallel to the reasoning behind prolonged treatment of Strongyloides hyperinfection.",
  prev: "Cooking freshwater and brackish fish thoroughly and abandoning the practice of swallowing small fish whole or raw, which is the single effective measure in the endemic foci. Sanitation that keeps human feces out of fish ponds and estuaries interrupts the cycle from the other direction. Health education in the affected villages of Luzon was what ended the original epidemic. No vaccine.",
  look: "Trichuris trichiura, whose eggs are similar but have prominent protruding polar plugs and a smooth shell. Strongyloides hyperinfection, which is the closest conceptual mimic because it also autoinfects and also causes a protein-losing enteropathy. Celiac disease, tropical sprue, Whipple disease, intestinal lymphangiectasia, inflammatory bowel disease, giardiasis and HIV enteropathy in a patient with chronic diarrhea and hypoalbuminemia.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Capillaria philippinensis autoinfects: thin-shelled eggs hatch in the gut lumen and the larvae reinvade the same host, so the worm burden multiplies internally and the infection can be fatal."],
    ["Memory trick", "Capillaria is hair-thin and multiplies at home: raw fish in, protein out, and the patient swells with edema."],
    ["Classic setting", "A man from northern Luzon or northeastern Thailand who eats raw small fish, with months of watery diarrhea, generalized edema, hypoalbuminemia and a normal eosinophil count."],
    ["Step focus", "The eggs resemble those of Trichuris trichiura but have flattened, less protruding bipolar plugs and a striated shell."]],
  worm: [["An epidemic with no known cause", "In 1962 a schoolteacher in Pudoc West, a village in Ilocos Norte in northern Luzon, died of a wasting diarrheal illness, and over the following years hundreds of villagers fell ill and more than a hundred died. The condition was called the mystery disease. Chitwood, Velasquez and Salazar identified a previously undescribed nematode in 1968, and the parasite was named Capillaria philippinensis for the country in which it was found. It remains among the few helminth infections that produced a lethal epidemic in a defined community."],
    ["Two kinds of egg, one dangerous consequence", "The female's capacity to produce both thick-shelled eggs for export and thin-shelled eggs that hatch where they are laid is the whole of this parasite's clinical character. A single infective meal can lead, through successive internal generations, to tens of thousands of worms. Autoinfection is rare among helminths, found otherwise chiefly in Strongyloides stercoralis, Hymenolepis nana and Enterobius, and in each case it is the feature that turns a self-limited infection into one that can escalate without further exposure."],
    ["Epidemiology solved by watching what people ate", "Investigators traced the Luzon outbreak to the local habit of eating small whole raw fish from the brackish ponds and streams around the villages, often as a drinking accompaniment, and experimental infection of monkeys and gerbils with larvae from those fish reproduced the disease. The fish-eating bird reservoir was inferred later. The investigation is a standard example of field epidemiology in parasitology: the life cycle was reconstructed from a dietary history and animal experiments before any molecular tool existed."],
    ["Why a short course is not enough", "Early treatment attempts with standard courses of benzimidazoles produced improvement followed by relapse, because larvae already in the mucosa continued to mature and the autoinfective cycle restarted. Current practice uses extended courses, of ten days or more of albendazole or three weeks or more of mebendazole, with follow-up stool examination. The same reasoning governs treatment of Strongyloides hyperinfection, and the parallel is worth holding, because in both cases the error is to treat a multiplying population as though it were a fixed one."]] },

{ id: "brugia-malayi", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Brugia malayi", also: "Malayan filariasis, Brugian lymphatic filariasis, with Brugia timori as the related Indonesian species",
  cls: "Filarial nematode, family Onchocercidae. Adults are slender white threads, the female about 5 centimeters and the male about 2.5, living coiled in lymphatic vessels and nodes. Females release sheathed microfilariae into the blood, about 200 to 280 micrometers long, with nuclei extending to the very tip of the tail, typically two terminal nuclei set apart, which is the morphological key. Development to the infective third-stage larva occurs in the mosquito. The worm harbors the endosymbiotic bacterium Wolbachia, which it depends on for fertility and survival.",
  plain: "A thread-like worm that blocks the lymphatic channels of the leg and causes swelling and recurrent fevers, carried by swamp mosquitoes in Southeast Asia.",
  live: "Humans are the main host of the periodic form. The subperiodic form in Malaysia and Indonesia also infects leaf monkeys and other primates and domestic cats, giving it a genuine animal reservoir, which is a significant difference from Wuchereria bancrofti and a complication for elimination. Vectors are Mansonia species, which breed in swamps where their larvae attach to aquatic plants, and also Anopheles and Aedes in some settings. Endemic in parts of India, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, the Philippines, Vietnam, southern China and South Korea historically, concentrated in rural swampy areas.",
  spread: "Bite of an infected mosquito depositing third-stage larvae on the skin, which enter through the bite puncture. Repeated and prolonged exposure over years is generally needed before the heavy burdens that cause lymphatic disease accumulate, which is why the illness is one of long residence rather than brief travel.",
  causes: "Most infected people have microfilaremia without symptoms. Acute episodes of adenolymphangitis present with fever, pain and a red tender streak spreading down a limb from an inflamed node, lasting days and recurring. Chronic disease follows years of infection: lymphedema of the leg, progressing in some to elephantiasis with thickened verrucous skin, and lymphadenopathy. Compared with Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi affects the leg below the knee more characteristically, and genital involvement with hydrocele and scrotal elephantiasis is much less common. Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia, with nocturnal wheeze, cough, restrictive lung function and very high eosinophil counts, is an immunological reaction to microfilariae in the lung and occurs with both species.",
  mech: "Adult worms living in lymphatic vessels dilate them and damage the valves, and the lymphatic dysfunction begins long before any swelling is visible, detectable by lymphoscintigraphy in symptomless microfilaremic people. Three processes then combine. The presence and the death of adult worms provoke inflammatory infiltration and fibrosis around the vessel, progressively obstructing lymph flow. Release of Wolbachia from dying worms is a potent inflammatory stimulus through innate immune receptors and contributes substantially to the acute attacks. Finally, impaired lymphatic drainage permits recurrent bacterial and fungal infection of the skin of the limb, and each episode of cellulitis causes further scarring, so that much of the progression from mild swelling to elephantiasis is driven by secondary infection rather than by the worm. This is why skin care and hygiene reduce progression even when no parasite remains. Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia is a separate mechanism: a hypersensitivity response in which microfilariae are trapped and destroyed in the lung, with high IgE and no microfilariae in the blood.",
  lab: "Microfilariae are detected on a Giemsa-stained thick blood film or by membrane filtration of blood, collected to match the periodicity of the local strain, which is nocturnal in most areas and nocturnally subperiodic in Malaysia and Indonesia. Species identification rests on the sheath, which is present in both Brugia and Wuchereria, and on tail nuclei: Brugia malayi has nuclei extending into the tail tip with two terminal nuclei distinctly separated, whereas Wuchereria bancrofti has a tail free of nuclei at the tip. Antigen tests widely used for Wuchereria bancrofti do not detect Brugia, so Brugia-specific antibody tests such as the BmR1-based rapid test are used instead, a practical point that affects elimination mapping. Ultrasound can show motile adult worms in lymphatics, and PCR is available. Eosinophilia and raised IgE are usual.",
  tx: "Programmatic mass drug administration and individual treatment in most endemic settings use diethylcarbamazine with albendazole, which clears microfilariae and has partial activity against adults. Where onchocerciasis is co-endemic, diethylcarbamazine is avoided and ivermectin-based regimens are used instead.",
  alt: "Doxycycline over several weeks targets the Wolbachia endosymbiont and is the only approach that reliably sterilizes and kills adult worms, with the practical limitations of a long course and unsuitability in pregnancy and young children. For established lymphedema, limb elevation, meticulous skin hygiene, treatment of entry lesions, compression and exercise are the interventions that improve function, and surgery is used for selected cases. These measures work independently of whether any parasite remains.",
  res: "Resistance is not established in human filariae, although reduced microfilarial clearance after repeated ivermectin exposure has been reported in Onchocerca and monitoring is in place. The practical constraint on treatment is not resistance but the limited macrofilaricidal activity of the standard drugs, which is why doxycycline and the Wolbachia strategy attracted so much attention.",
  prev: "Mass drug administration under the Global Programme to Eliminate Lymphatic Filariasis, repeated annually for several years to drive microfilarial prevalence below transmission thresholds, together with vector control. Mansonia control is harder than for other vectors because the larvae attach to aquatic vegetation rather than breathing at the surface, so larviciding is ineffective and clearing vegetation from swamps is the practical measure. Bed nets help where Anopheles is the vector. The animal reservoir of the subperiodic form means that human treatment alone may not interrupt transmission in those areas.",
  look: "Wuchereria bancrofti, which causes the same syndrome more extensively and with genital involvement, and which is distinguished on microfilarial tail morphology and by antigen testing. Brugia timori, with a longer microfilaria and a distinctive geography in southeastern Indonesia. Lymphedema from Konyak or podoconiosis, which is non-filarial and caused by silicate absorption from volcanic soil, cellulitis, deep vein thrombosis, congestive heart failure, lymphoma and malignant lymphatic obstruction. For tropical pulmonary eosinophilia, asthma, Loffler syndrome and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Brugia malayi microfilariae are sheathed with nuclei extending to the tail tip and two separated terminal nuclei, whereas Wuchereria bancrofti has a nucleus-free tail tip."],
    ["Memory trick", "Brugia has nuclei all the way to the tip of the tail, bites below the knee, and travels with Mansonia from the swamp."],
    ["Classic setting", "A man from rural Kerala or Sulawesi with recurrent fever and a tender red streak down the calf, then years later a thickened swollen leg below the knee."],
    ["Step focus", "Wolbachia is an obligate bacterial endosymbiont of filarial worms, which makes doxycycline an effective macrofilaricide and makes bacterial products a driver of the inflammatory attacks."]],
  worm: [["Named for the man who separated it", "Lichtenstein recognized in the 1920s that the filariasis of Sumatra differed from bancroftian filariasis, and Brug described the microfilaria in 1927; the genus Brugia was later named in his honor. The distinction mattered because the Malayan form has a different vector, a different clinical distribution favoring the lower leg, and in some areas an animal reservoir. It is one of several occasions in parasitology where a careful look at the tail of a microfilaria rewrote the epidemiology of a region."],
    ["The bacterium inside the worm", "Filarial worms carry Wolbachia, an alphaproteobacterium passed down the maternal line, and they cannot reproduce or survive long without it. The discovery turned a drug problem on its head: no good macrofilaricide existed, but an ordinary antibiotic directed at the bacterium sterilizes and eventually kills the adult worm. Doxycycline courses of four to six weeks produce effects that no single-dose antifilarial achieves. The same biology also explains why dying worms cause such intense inflammation, since bacterial products are released alongside worm antigen."],
    ["Elephantiasis is partly a bacterial disease", "The progression from mild lymphedema to grotesque elephantiasis was long assumed to be the worm's doing. Work in India, Brazil and Africa from the 1990s showed that repeated bacterial and fungal infection of a limb with impaired lymphatic drainage is the main engine of progression, and that simple hygiene, washing, nail care, treating interdigital fungal infection and attending to small wounds, substantially reduces acute attacks and halts worsening. This made morbidity management a core part of filariasis programs, independent of drug distribution, and it is effective in people whose worms are long dead."],
    ["Mosquitoes that do not come to the surface", "Mansonia larvae pierce the submerged stems and roots of water plants such as water hyacinth and Pistia and draw air from them, so they never need to visit the water surface. Surface larvicides and oils, the mainstay of mosquito control, simply do not reach them. Control of Brugian filariasis has therefore depended on clearing aquatic vegetation from swamps and ponds, a labor-intensive measure, which is part of why this form of lymphatic filariasis persisted in swampy rural areas after other forms had been pushed back."]] },

{ id: "mansonella-perstans", k: "para", g: "Nematodes (roundworms)", nm: "Mansonella perstans", also: "Dipetalonema perstans, Acanthocheilonema perstans, perstans filariasis",
  cls: "Filarial nematode, family Onchocercidae. Adults are small for a filaria, the female about 7 centimeters and the male about 4.5, and they live in the serous cavities, the peritoneal and pleural spaces, the pericardium and the retroperitoneal and mesenteric tissue rather than in lymphatics or skin. Microfilariae are unsheathed, about 200 micrometers long, with a blunt rounded tail and no periodicity, so they circulate in the blood at all hours. Most isolates carry the Wolbachia endosymbiont.",
  plain: "A small filarial worm of the body cavities, extremely common across central Africa, that circulates in the blood day and night and causes little or nothing that can be clearly pinned on it.",
  live: "Humans are the principal host, with gorillas and chimpanzees also infected. The vectors are tiny biting midges of the genus Culicoides, chiefly Culicoides austeni and Culicoides grahamii, which breed in mud and decaying vegetation. It is widely distributed across sub-Saharan Africa from Senegal to Uganda and down to Zimbabwe, with foci in the Caribbean and northeastern South America, and prevalence in some central African communities exceeds fifty percent, making it one of the most common filarial infections of all.",
  spread: "Bite of an infected Culicoides midge, an insect small enough to pass through ordinary mosquito netting, which bites during the day as well as at dusk. Transmission is intense in forested and riverine areas, and infection is typically acquired in childhood and persists for years.",
  causes: "Attribution is genuinely uncertain. Many infected people have no symptoms at all. Reported associations include transient subcutaneous swellings resembling Calabar swellings, pruritus, arthralgia, abdominal and chest pain, pericarditis and pleuritis, hepatomegaly, fatigue and headache, often with marked eosinophilia. Recurrent angiedema and conjunctival nodules are described. The main clinical significance in practice is twofold: its microfilariae are found incidentally on blood films taken for other reasons, and its presence confounds the diagnosis and the mass treatment programs aimed at Loa loa, Onchocerca and Wuchereria.",
  mech: "There is no well-characterized pathogenic mechanism, which is the honest position. Adults in the serous cavities provoke a local eosinophilic and fibrotic reaction that has been found at surgery and autopsy, and this is the plausible basis for the reports of pericarditis, pleuritis and peritoneal involvement. Microfilariae circulating continuously in enormous numbers drive a strong type 2 immune response with high eosinophil counts and raised IgE, and this immune activation is the more likely explanation for the pruritus, swellings and arthralgia than any direct tissue injury. A separate and important consequence is immunological: chronic mansonellosis modulates the host response in ways that have been shown to blunt responses to vaccines including BCG, and to alter the immune response to coincident malaria, tuberculosis and HIV. This immunomodulation, rather than any lesion the worm produces, may be its main contribution to human health.",
  lab: "Microfilariae are found on a Giemsa thick blood film or by membrane filtration, taken at any time of day since there is no periodicity, which is itself a diagnostic feature when compared with the nocturnal Wuchereria and Brugia and the diurnal Loa loa. Identification rests on the absence of a sheath, the small size, the blunt rounded tail and the nuclei extending to the tail tip. It is often an incidental finding on a film requested for malaria. No antigen test is in routine use, serology cross-reacts across filarial species, and PCR distinguishes it where available. Eosinophilia and raised IgE are usual and may be the finding that prompts the search.",
  tx: "No regimen is both reliable and simple. Doxycycline over several weeks, directed at the Wolbachia endosymbiont, has produced sustained clearance of microfilariae in trials and is the most effective reported approach.",
  alt: "Diethylcarbamazine has limited and inconsistent activity against this species. Ivermectin alone is largely ineffective against it, which is a notable exception within the filariae. Mebendazole and albendazole have been used, and combinations of ivermectin with albendazole have shown partial effect. Treatment is often not given at all when the infection is incidental and the patient is well.",
  res: "Rather than acquired resistance, the issue is intrinsic unresponsiveness: this species does not respond to ivermectin or to diethylcarbamazine in the way the other human filariae do, for reasons that are not fully explained but may relate to the location of adults in serous cavities and to differences in the glutamate-gated chloride channels that ivermectin targets.",
  prev: "No program targets it. Culicoides midges pass through standard bed nets, bite in daylight and breed in habitats that are difficult to modify, so vector control is impractical. Repellents and fine-mesh screening give partial protection. Mass drug administration campaigns against other filariae have reduced it only marginally, which is part of why it has persisted at high prevalence while onchocerciasis and lymphatic filariasis have been pushed back.",
  look: "Loa loa, whose microfilaria is sheathed and diurnal and whose swellings are the classical Calabar swellings; co-infection is common in central Africa. Mansonella ozzardi, found in Latin America and the Caribbean, with a blunt tail and no sheath but distinguishable on detail, and Mansonella streptocerca, whose microfilariae are in the skin with a hooked tail. Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia, both sheathed and periodic. Onchocerca volvulus, whose microfilariae are in skin rather than blood.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Mansonella perstans microfilariae are unsheathed with a blunt tail and show no periodicity, so a blood film taken at any time of day will find them."],
    ["Memory trick", "Perstans persists: no sheath, no periodicity, no clear disease, and no response to ivermectin."],
    ["Classic setting", "A blood film taken to look for malaria in a patient from Cameroon shows unsheathed microfilariae, and the patient has marked eosinophilia and no definite symptoms."],
    ["Step focus", "Its adults live in serous cavities rather than lymphatics or skin, and doxycycline aimed at Wolbachia is the approach with the best reported clearance."]],
  worm: [["Discovered by Manson in a sleeping sickness patient", "Patrick Manson described the microfilaria in 1891 in blood from a patient in West Africa, and the genus was later named for him. The parasite was found repeatedly through the colonial period in African blood films, nearly always as an incidental finding alongside the search for something else, and that is largely how it is still encountered. More than a century later the fundamental question about it, whether and how it makes people ill, has not been settled."],
    ["Common, neglected, and probably consequential", "Prevalence above fifty percent in parts of central Africa makes this one of the most widespread human parasites, and it receives almost no programmatic attention because it lacks an unambiguous disease. The recent shift in thinking concerns immunology: chronic filarial infection shifts the immune response toward type 2 and regulatory patterns, and studies have found impaired responses to BCG vaccination and altered responses to malaria and tuberculosis in infected people. A parasite that changes how its host responds to other infections and to vaccines may matter a great deal while causing no symptoms of its own."],
    ["The filaria that ignores ivermectin", "Ivermectin transformed the treatment of onchocerciasis and contributes to lymphatic filariasis programs, and it is nearly inert against Mansonella perstans. The result is that in central African communities receiving repeated mass ivermectin, perstans prevalence barely moves while onchocerciasis falls. Doxycycline aimed at Wolbachia works, but a six-week antibiotic course cannot be delivered in a mass campaign. The organism is a standing example of how the practicalities of drug delivery, not just drug activity, decide which parasites get eliminated."],
    ["A confounder in the Loa loa problem", "Mass ivermectin distribution in central Africa is constrained by Loa loa, since people with very high Loa microfilarial loads can develop serious encephalopathy after treatment, so programs screen blood films before treating. Mansonella perstans circulates in the same populations, appears on the same films, and is told apart from Loa loa only by sheath and periodicity. Misidentification in either direction has consequences: treating someone wrongly assessed as low risk, or withholding treatment unnecessarily. A morphological detail on a thick film carries real operational weight."]] }
);

;
/* ===== Microbiology set 17: further cestodes, trematodes and ectoparasites (draft, Oct 30 2026) ===== */
MICRO.push(
{ id: "echinococcus-multilocularis", k: "para", g: "Cestodes (tapeworms)", nm: "Echinococcus multilocularis", also: "fox tapeworm, agent of alveolar echinococcosis, alveolar hydatid disease",
  cls: "Cyclophyllidean cestode, adult only 1.2 to 4.5 mm with two to five proglottids living in the small intestine of foxes and other canids, larval metacestode forming a multivesicular infiltrating mass in the liver of rodents and, accidentally, of humans",
  plain: "A tiny fox tapeworm whose larval stage grows through the human liver like a slow cancer rather than forming a single cyst.",
  live: "Red and arctic foxes are the principal definitive hosts, with domestic dogs, raccoon dogs, coyotes and cats also carrying adult worms, and the intermediate hosts are arvicoline rodents, chiefly voles, lemmings and muskrats. The parasite occupies a broad northern hemisphere band: central and eastern Europe, where the range has expanded westward with urban fox populations, Turkey, Russia, Central Asia, western and northern China, Japan on Hokkaido, and parts of Alaska and north-central North America. Human infection remains uncommon, with a few thousand cases a year worldwide and the heaviest burden on the Tibetan plateau and in Xinjiang.",
  spread: "Fecal-oral ingestion of eggs passed by a definitive host, through contaminated wild berries, fallen fruit, garden vegetables, water, soil or dust, or through close handling of foxes, fox pelts or infected dogs. Dogs that hunt rodents are a particularly relevant domestic source because they are kept close to people. There is no person-to-person spread and no risk from eating rodents or from the liver lesions of another human.",
  causes: "Alveolar echinococcosis, a chronic infiltrating disease of the liver with an incubation period usually of five to fifteen years. Early lesions are silent and found incidentally. Established disease produces right upper quadrant pain, hepatomegaly, obstructive jaundice from biliary invasion, cholangitis, portal hypertension, a palpable hard mass and weight loss. The lesion extends directly into diaphragm, lung, vena cava and retroperitoneum, and metastatic foci reach lung, brain and bone. Untreated, mortality historically exceeded ninety percent over ten to fifteen years, which is why the disease is compared to a slow-growing hepatic malignancy.",
  mech: "Eggs hatch in the intestine and the oncosphere crosses the mucosa and reaches the liver through the portal circulation, where it develops into a metacestode that is not a single fluid-filled bladder but a spongy aggregate of small vesicles embedded in dense fibrous and necrotic tissue. Growth is exogenous and budding rather than concentric, so the lesion has no limiting host capsule and extends along tissue planes and into vessels and bile ducts. Host immunity determines outcome more than parasite biology: a Th1-dominated granulomatous response restricts and sometimes aborts the lesion, while a Th2 and regulatory profile with high interleukin-10 permits unchecked proliferation, and profound immunosuppression accelerates everything. Central necrosis and calcification develop as the mass outgrows its blood supply, and small protoscolex-bearing vesicles can seed distant organs. The clinical picture therefore resembles a tumor because the growth pattern genuinely is infiltrative.",
  lab: "Ultrasound shows an irregular hyperechoic mass with indistinct margins, central necrotic cavities and scattered calcification, classified by the World Health Organization PNM system that parallels tumor staging. Computed tomography defines the calcification and vascular involvement, magnetic resonance imaging characterizes the microvesicular pattern and biliary anatomy, and positron emission tomography with fluorodeoxyglucose maps metabolically active parasite tissue at the lesion rim and is used to follow treatment. Serology with recombinant Em2plus, Em18 or EgAgB antigens is sensitive and reasonably species-discriminating, and falling Em18 antibody correlates with inactivity. Histology shows the characteristic small vesicles with a periodic acid-Schiff positive laminated layer, often without visible protoscolices. Polymerase chain reaction on tissue confirms the species. Eosinophilia is modest or absent, and liver function tests reflect biliary obstruction rather than the parasite itself.",
  tx: "Radical surgical resection with clear margins, as for a liver tumor, offers the only cure, and resectability is assessed in the same way as for hepatic malignancy. Albendazole, which is parasitostatic rather than parasiticidal here, is given for at least two years after complete resection and indefinitely when resection is incomplete or impossible, and long-term albendazole has turned a near-uniformly fatal disease into a chronic controllable one. Biliary obstruction is relieved by stenting or drainage, and liver transplantation has been used in far advanced disease, with the caveat that posttransplant immunosuppression permits regrowth of residual parasite, so albendazole continues afterward.",
  alt: "Mebendazole where albendazole is not tolerated or absorbed poorly. Interventional radiology for abscessed cavities and biliary disease. Amphotericin B has been used as salvage in a handful of patients intolerant of benzimidazoles. Nitazoxanide proved disappointing in clinical use despite in vitro promise, and newer candidates including mefloquine and repurposed kinase inhibitors remain investigational.",
  res: "Acquired drug resistance has not been documented. Failure of albendazole reflects its static action, variable and food-dependent absorption of the active sulfoxide metabolite, poor penetration into the necrotic core of the lesion, and the fact that stopping treatment allows regrowth, so relapse after discontinuation is the rule in unresected disease rather than evidence of resistance. Plasma metabolite monitoring is used in specialist centres because underexposure is a commoner cause of failure than parasite biology.",
  prev: "Hand washing after handling dogs, soil or wild game, washing of garden produce and wild berries, and regular praziquantel treatment of dogs in endemic areas, since dogs are the household-level source. Anthelmintic baiting of fox populations has reduced egg contamination in several European and Japanese programmes but needs sustained effort to hold the gain. Avoiding contact with fox carcasses and pelts, and care during trapping and skinning, protects occupationally exposed people. Freezing at deep temperatures destroys eggs while refrigeration does not. There is no human vaccine, and the EG95 vaccine protects sheep against the related Echinococcus granulosus rather than against this species.",
  look: "Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma head the differential and are the diagnoses most often made first. Hepatic metastases, cystic echinococcosis from Echinococcus granulosus, which forms a unilocular cyst with a clear wall and daughter cysts, pyogenic and amoebic abscess, tuberculous liver disease, and the very rare polycystic echinococcosis from Echinococcus vogeli in Central and South America. The distinction from cystic hydatid matters because that disease is drained and this one is resected.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Alveolar echinococcosis grows by exogenous budding without a host capsule and behaves like a liver cancer, unlike the single well-walled cyst of Echinococcus granulosus."],
    ["Memory trick", "Multilocularis has many little locules: a spongy infiltrating mass. Granulosus makes one grand balloon. Fox and vole for multilocularis, dog and sheep for granulosus."],
    ["Classic setting", "A farmer or dog owner from central Europe, Hokkaido or the Tibetan plateau with years of vague right upper quadrant discomfort, jaundice and an irregular calcified hepatic mass taken at first for cholangiocarcinoma."],
    ["Step focus", "Humans are an accidental and poorly suited intermediate host, and the lesion rarely produces mature protoscolices, which is why histology can show vesicles without hooklets."]],
  worm: [["The disease that was mistaken for cancer for a century", "Nineteenth-century pathologists in Bavaria and Switzerland described a destructive hepatic lesion they called alveolar colloid cancer, and argued for decades about whether it was neoplastic or parasitic. Rudolf Virchow examined specimens and leaned toward a parasite; others pointed to the infiltrative growth as proof of malignancy. The dispute was settled only when the full fox and vole life cycle was worked out in the 1950s and 1960s. The confusion was not carelessness. The lesion really does grow by budding into surrounding tissue, metastasize and recur after incomplete excision."],
    ["Foxes moved into town", "For most of the twentieth century this was a disease of trappers and remote alpine valleys. From the 1980s red fox populations in Europe rose sharply after rabies was controlled by oral vaccination, and the foxes colonized suburbs and city parks in Germany, Switzerland and France. Surveys began finding infected foxes in urban green space, eggs in garden soil, and the parasite spreading into the Netherlands, Belgium, Denmark and Scandinavia. Human cases have risen accordingly. It is one of the clearest instances of a wildlife conservation success reshaping a human infection map."],
    ["Albendazole changed the prognosis without curing anyone", "Before benzimidazoles, unresectable alveolar echinococcosis killed almost everyone within fifteen years. Albendazole does not kill the metacestode reliably, it holds it still. Long-term cohorts from Besancon and Zurich showed ten-year survival rising from roughly ten percent to around eighty, with most patients on continuous treatment for life and relapsing within months if it stops. The drug converted a fatal infection into a managed chronic disease, which is a useful reminder that parasitostatic therapy can be transformative without ever achieving cure."],
    ["Why the vesicles are often empty", "In voles the metacestode fills with protoscolices, the infective stage that a fox eats. In humans the lesion grows and infiltrates but frequently produces no protoscolices at all, so histology shows the laminated layer and small vesicles with nothing inside them. Humans are an evolutionary dead end for this parasite, and the mismatch is part of the pathology: the larva that cannot complete its development instead persists, proliferates and destroys the organ it is stuck in."]] },

{ id: "taenia-asiatica", k: "para", g: "Cestodes (tapeworms)", nm: "Taenia asiatica", also: "Asian Taenia, Asian taeniasis, formerly Taenia saginata asiatica",
  cls: "Cyclophyllidean cestode, adult 4 to 8 m, scolex unarmed in the adult but with a rudimentary armed rostellum in the larva, gravid proglottids with posterior protuberances, humans the only definitive host and pigs the usual intermediate host, with the cysticercus developing in liver rather than muscle",
  plain: "A tapeworm of East and Southeast Asia that looks like the beef tapeworm, comes from eating raw pig liver, and was recognized as a separate species only in the 1990s.",
  live: "Humans are the sole definitive host. Domestic pigs are the principal intermediate host, with wild boar, cattle, goats and monkeys able to carry cysticerci experimentally or naturally. The distribution is Asian and tied to culinary practice: Taiwan, where it was first delineated among Indigenous communities, South Korea, Japan, China, Indonesia, particularly Bali and Sumatra, Thailand, Vietnam, the Philippines and Nepal. Prevalence in some villages where raw pig viscera is eaten at communal feasts has been reported above twenty percent.",
  spread: "Eating raw or lightly cooked pig liver, and to a lesser extent other viscera, containing the larval cysticercus. The liver tropism of the larva is the reason the exposure differs from the muscle-based exposure of the pork and beef tapeworms. Eggs passed in human feces reach pigs through open defecation and free-ranging husbandry. Human cysticercosis from swallowing the eggs of this species has not been convincingly demonstrated, although the question is not fully closed because the larva retains vestigial hooks.",
  causes: "Intestinal taeniasis, which is usually mild: vague abdominal discomfort, nausea, hunger pangs, mild weight loss, anal pruritus and, most characteristically, the spontaneous passage of motile proglottids, which the patient notices and which is often the presenting complaint. Heavy or multiple worm burdens occasionally cause appendicitis, cholangitis or intestinal obstruction when proglottids migrate. The important clinical consequence is diagnostic rather than symptomatic, because misidentification as Taenia solium prompts unnecessary neuroimaging and alarm, while misidentification as Taenia saginata misses the pork liver exposure that sustains transmission.",
  mech: "The cysticercus eaten in pig liver is freed by digestion, the scolex everts and attaches to the upper small intestine by four suckers, and the worm adds proglottids to a strobila that may exceed eight metres, maturing in about two to three months and living for years. Nutrition is entirely by absorption across the tegument, which also bears a glycocalyx and releases protease inhibitors that protect it from host digestive enzymes and blunt local immune recognition, so inflammation is minimal and symptoms are mechanical rather than inflammatory. Gravid proglottids detach, are passed, and release eggs that are immediately infective to pigs. In the pig the oncosphere crosses the gut wall and travels through the portal system to the liver, where it matures into a cysticercus within weeks, a tissue preference that distinguishes it sharply from its relatives and that was one of the first clues that it was a distinct species.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows Taenia eggs that cannot be distinguished between species, so species assignment rests on the proglottid or scolex, or on molecular testing. Gravid proglottids of this species have characteristic posterior protuberances and a particular pattern of uterine branching, and the larva has a rudimentary armed rostellum, but morphology overlaps enough with Taenia saginata that confident separation generally requires polymerase chain reaction and sequencing of cytochrome c oxidase subunit 1 or the internal transcribed spacer regions, or restriction fragment analysis. Coproantigen enzyme immunoassay detects taeniasis without identifying the species. Serology is of little use for intestinal infection. Eosinophilia is mild or absent.",
  tx: "A single dose of praziquantel clears the adult worm, and niclosamide, which is not absorbed from the gut, is an equally effective alternative. Cure is assessed by the absence of proglottids and eggs over the following weeks, and recovery of the scolex supports complete expulsion. Treatment of the carrier also removes the source of eggs for pigs, which is the public health rationale for finding cases in villages where raw liver is eaten.",
  alt: "Albendazole over several days has been used. Nitazoxanide has activity against intestinal cestodes. Where coexisting Taenia solium cannot be excluded, niclosamide is sometimes favoured over a cysticidal drug on the reasoning that a non-absorbed agent will not act on undiagnosed tissue cysts.",
  res: "No meaningful drug resistance has been described. Apparent failure usually reflects an incompletely expelled worm regenerating from a retained scolex, reinfection from continued consumption of raw liver within the same household or community, or the presence of a second worm. Praziquantel efficacy against cestodes remains high across all reported settings.",
  prev: "Thorough cooking of pig liver and viscera, since the culinary practice is the entire transmission route, and adequate freezing where raw consumption is culturally important. Confinement of pigs away from human feces, latrine construction and use, and inspection of pig liver at slaughter interrupt the cycle. Treating human carriers in affected communities reduces the egg load reaching pigs. No vaccine exists for humans, although the TSOL18 class of oncosphere antigen vaccines developed for Taenia solium in pigs has prompted interest in analogous approaches.",
  look: "Taenia saginata, which it most closely resembles and from which it was separated, and Taenia solium, which it is distinguished from because that species causes human cysticercosis. Diphyllobothrium species, whose eggs are operculate and whose proglottids are wider than long. Hymenolepis species, which are much smaller with distinctive eggs. Passage of proglottids is sometimes confused by patients with pinworms, vegetable fibre or mucus.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The larva of Taenia asiatica localizes to pig liver rather than muscle, so infection follows eating raw pig viscera, and human cysticercosis has not been established for this species."],
    ["Memory trick", "Asiatica is the Asian one that comes from liver. Solium from pork muscle with seizures, saginata from beef muscle and safe, asiatica from pig liver and Asian."],
    ["Classic setting", "An adult in Taiwan, Korea or Bali who attended a feast featuring raw pig liver and now passes flat motile white segments in the stool."],
    ["Step focus", "Taenia eggs are identical across species, so species identification depends on the proglottid, the scolex or molecular testing, which is why a reported Taenia egg alone does not answer the cysticercosis question."]],
  worm: [["A species hidden inside another for decades", "Through the 1970s and 1980s Taiwanese investigators, led by Fan Pei-chen, kept finding a puzzle among Indigenous communities: taeniasis that looked like beef tapeworm infection in people who ate almost no beef, acquired instead from raw pig liver at ceremonial meals. Feeding experiments showed the larva developed in pig liver rather than cattle muscle, the proglottids had distinctive posterior protuberances, and the larva carried vestigial hooks. The organism was described as a subspecies of Taenia saginata in 1992 and elevated to full species as Taenia asiatica in 1993, after genetic work confirmed the separation."],
    ["Why liver instead of muscle", "The three human Taenia species differ in where the oncosphere settles, and that single difference drives everything else. Taenia solium and Taenia saginata lodge in striated muscle, so infection follows eating undercooked meat. Taenia asiatica lodges in the liver and other viscera, so the exposure is raw liver, a dish prepared and shared in particular communities. A parasite's distribution is therefore set as much by cuisine as by climate, and the Asian tapeworm persists precisely where a specific traditional food survives."],
    ["The cysticercosis question that will not quite close", "Human cysticercosis is the reason species identification matters, and the consensus is that Taenia asiatica does not cause it. The caveat is that the larva retains a rudimentary armed rostellum, aligning it developmentally closer to Taenia solium than its adult morphology suggests, and a handful of reports have raised the possibility of tissue larvae in humans without convincing confirmation. Most authorities treat the risk as absent or negligible, while noting that absence of evidence in a parasite studied for three decades in a small number of countries is weaker reassurance than it sounds."],
    ["A hybrid in the wild", "Molecular surveys in Thailand, China and Myanmar have repeatedly found tapeworms whose nuclear and mitochondrial markers do not agree, which is the signature of hybridization between Taenia asiatica and Taenia saginata. Hybrid and introgressed worms appear to be reasonably common where both species circulate, and some are parthenogenetic. This complicates the tidy picture of three separate human Taenia species, raises practical questions about which molecular target a laboratory relies on, and leaves open whether hybrid worms differ in their larval tissue preference."]] },

{ id: "hymenolepis-diminuta", k: "para", g: "Cestodes (tapeworms)", nm: "Hymenolepis diminuta", also: "rat tapeworm, Rodentolepis diminuta, agent of hymenolepiasis diminuta",
  cls: "Cyclophyllidean cestode, adult 20 to 60 cm with an unarmed club-shaped scolex bearing four suckers and no hooks, eggs round to slightly oval with a thick striated shell, no polar filaments, requiring an arthropod intermediate host",
  plain: "A rat tapeworm that occasionally infects children who swallow a grain-beetle or flea, and causes little more than mild abdominal upset.",
  live: "Rats and mice are the natural definitive hosts worldwide, and the parasite is one of the commonest cestodes of urban rodents. The obligate intermediate hosts are arthropods that eat rodent feces, chiefly grain and flour beetles of the genus Tenebrio and Tribolium, fleas, cockroaches, earwigs and various moth larvae. Human infection is sporadic and reported from every inhabited region, concentrated in children in settings with rodent infestation, stored grain and poor housing.",
  spread: "Accidental swallowing of an infected arthropod, most often in contaminated flour, cereal, dried fruit or pre-cooked grain, or by a toddler picking up and eating an insect. Eggs passed in rodent or human feces are eaten by the arthropod, in which the cysticercoid develops over one to two weeks. Direct egg-to-human transmission does not occur because the intermediate host is obligatory, and there is no autoinfection, which separates this species sharply from Hymenolepis nana and explains why infections are usually light and self-limited.",
  causes: "Most infections are asymptomatic and discovered on stool examination. Symptomatic cases, usually children with more than a few worms, report intermittent periumbilical or diffuse abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea, anorexia, irritability, restless sleep, anal and nasal itching and occasionally mild weight loss. Modest peripheral eosinophilia and raised IgE are described. Heavy burdens are rare in humans. There is no tissue or larval stage in people, so no extraintestinal disease, and spontaneous clearance within weeks to months is common because the human intestine is an imperfect environment for the worm.",
  mech: "A swallowed arthropod is digested, the cysticercoid is released, the scolex everts and attaches to the mucosa of the mid small intestine with four unarmed suckers, and the worm matures in about three weeks, absorbing nutrients across its tegument. Pathology is slight and mostly mechanical and immunological: local villous blunting, increased mucus production, mild mucosal eosinophil and mast cell infiltration, and a Th2-skewed response with IgE and goblet cell hyperplasia, which in rodent models is what eventually expels the worm. Because there is no internal autoinfection and no tissue migration, worm numbers stay low and the mucosal barrier is not breached, which is why systemic illness does not develop. The same mild, strongly Th2-polarizing and non-invasive behavior is what made the species attractive to investigators interested in deliberate helminth exposure.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy is the diagnosis and shows round to slightly oval eggs 60 to 80 micrometres across, larger than those of Hymenolepis nana, with a thick radially striated outer shell, a six-hooked oncosphere and, importantly, no polar filaments in the space between the oncosphere and the shell, which is the single discriminating feature from Hymenolepis nana. Formalin-ethyl acetate concentration improves yield since egg output is low. Proglottids disintegrate in the bowel and are seldom seen intact, and an expelled scolex is unarmed, unlike the armed rostellum of Hymenolepis nana. Molecular assays and multiplex gastrointestinal panels distinguish the species where available. Mild eosinophilia may accompany infection and is not diagnostic.",
  tx: "A single dose of praziquantel clears the infection and is the standard choice. Follow-up stool examination some weeks later confirms cure, and because there is no autoinfection a single course generally suffices, unlike Hymenolepis nana, where repeat dosing is routine. Many light infections in older children and adults resolve without any treatment.",
  alt: "Niclosamide over several days. Nitazoxanide has activity against intestinal cestodes and is an option where praziquantel is unavailable. Albendazole is considerably less reliable against cestodes than against nematodes and is not a preferred agent here.",
  res: "No resistance has been documented in this species. Treatment failure is essentially always reinfection from a continuing arthropod source in the home, grain store or kitchen, which is why attention turns to rodent and insect control rather than to a second drug.",
  prev: "Rodent control, proper storage and sealing of grain, flour and cereal, discarding insect-infested dry goods, and household insect management, since the arthropod intermediate host is the only route in. Hand washing and supervision of small children reduce the chance of an insect being eaten. There is no vaccine, and none is needed given the self-limited nature of the infection.",
  look: "Hymenolepis nana, which is far more common in humans, has smaller eggs with polar filaments, an armed rostellum and a capacity for internal autoinfection that allows heavy burdens. Dipylidium caninum, also arthropod-borne and also a disease of small children, passes egg packets and cucumber-seed proglottids. Taenia species have much larger worms and different eggs. Nonspecific functional abdominal pain in a child is the practical differential, and an incidental egg finding does not by itself explain chronic symptoms.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Hymenolepis diminuta requires an arthropod intermediate host and cannot autoinfect, so human infections stay light, whereas Hymenolepis nana needs no intermediate host and can autoinfect to heavy burdens."],
    ["Memory trick", "Diminuta has the bigger egg, which is the trap. Diminuta eggs are larger and have NO polar filaments; nana eggs are smaller and DO have them. Diminuta needs a beetle; nana needs nobody."],
    ["Classic setting", "A toddler with intermittent abdominal pain and an incidental finding of large thick-shelled cestode eggs on a stool examination, in a home with a flour-beetle infestation or rodents."],
    ["Step focus", "Polar filaments present equals nana; absent equals diminuta. The unarmed scolex also separates diminuta from the armed rostellum of nana."]],
  worm: [["The laboratory's favourite tapeworm", "Because it completes its cycle between a rat and a flour beetle, both easily kept in cages and jars, Hymenolepis diminuta became the standard model cestode of twentieth-century parasitology. Thousands of papers on cestode physiology, tegumental absorption, carbohydrate metabolism, the crowding effect on worm size and the immunology of helminth expulsion rest on this species. Much of what is taught about how tapeworms feed and how a Th2 response clears them was worked out in rats carrying a worm that is only a footnote in human medicine."],
    ["A parasite proposed as a therapy", "Its mildness and its strong Th2 and regulatory signature made it a candidate for deliberate helminth therapy in autoimmune and allergic disease, alongside pig whipworm and human hookworm. Small trials and a substantial self-experimentation literature explored it in inflammatory bowel disease, multiple sclerosis and allergy on the hygiene-hypothesis reasoning that the immune system evolved expecting worms. Results have been inconsistent and mostly disappointing, and regulatory authorities have moved against commercial supply, but the idea itself reshaped how immunologists think about helminth-driven regulatory networks."],
    ["Why the eggs are the exam question", "The two Hymenolepis species share a genus, a habitat and a clinical banality, so the only thing that reliably separates them in practice is the egg, and the discriminator is counterintuitive. The smaller species, Hymenolepis nana, carries delicate polar filaments in the space between oncosphere and shell, while the larger-egged Hymenolepis diminuta has none. Generations of microscopy teaching have therefore reduced two organisms to a single visual cue, and the clinically important difference, autoinfection, follows from the identification rather than from anything visible in the stool."],
    ["The crowding effect", "Put one Hymenolepis diminuta in a rat and it grows long and heavy. Put a hundred in and each worm is a fraction of the size, with total biomass almost unchanged. This crowding effect, described in detail in the 1960s, showed that cestode growth is regulated by competition for a shared resource, probably luminal carbohydrate, rather than by an intrinsic size programme. It became one of the standard illustrations of density-dependent regulation in parasite populations, and it is a reason worm counts alone predict disease poorly in many helminth infections."]] },

{ id: "dipylidium-caninum", k: "para", g: "Cestodes (tapeworms)", nm: "Dipylidium caninum", also: "double-pored dog tapeworm, cucumber tapeworm, flea tapeworm, agent of dipylidiasis",
  cls: "Cyclophyllidean cestode, adult 10 to 70 cm with a rhomboid scolex bearing a retractable rostellum armed with several rows of thorn-shaped hooks, proglottids with bilateral genital pores and the shape of a cucumber seed or rice grain, eggs passed in packets of five to thirty",
  plain: "The tapeworm of dogs and cats, caught by swallowing an infected flea, which turns up in toddlers as wriggling cucumber-seed segments in the diaper.",
  live: "Dogs and cats worldwide, in which it is one of the commonest tapeworms, with wild canids and felids also infected. The obligate intermediate hosts are the flea species that live on those animals, chiefly Ctenocephalides felis and Ctenocephalides canis, with Pulex irritans and the dog chewing louse Trichodectes canis also competent. Human cases are reported from every continent and are overwhelmingly in infants and toddlers, with a few hundred cases in the literature, almost certainly a large underestimate given how often the diagnosis is made and not reported.",
  spread: "Swallowing an infected flea, which happens when a crawling child puts a hand, a toy or a pet's fur in the mouth, or when a dog licking a child's face transfers a flea. Flea larvae eat eggs or egg packets in the environment, and the cysticercoid matures inside the adult flea, so only the adult flea is infective. There is no egg-to-human route, no autoinfection and no person-to-person spread, and the presence of human infection is therefore a direct marker of household flea infestation rather than of hygiene in the usual sense.",
  causes: "Most infections cause little: mild abdominal discomfort, anal pruritus, diarrhea, irritability and restless sleep. The near-universal presenting feature is a parent finding motile white segments, described as resembling cucumber seeds, grains of rice or maggots, on the child, in the diaper or in bedding, where they may continue to move and contract for a time. Occasional reports describe poor appetite, mild weight loss and modest eosinophilia. Severe disease, obstruction and extraintestinal spread essentially do not occur, so the clinical weight of the infection lies in family alarm and in the diagnostic confusion with pinworm, maggots and Taenia infection.",
  mech: "The flea is swallowed and digested, releasing the cysticercoid, and the armed rostellum everts to anchor the scolex in the small intestinal mucosa while the four suckers hold position. The worm matures in about three to four weeks and adds proglottids, each hermaphroditic and unusually equipped with genital pores on both lateral margins, which is the feature the genus is named for. Gravid proglottids detach singly or in short chains, retain active muscular motility, and crawl out through the anus or are passed in stool, releasing packets of eggs as they dry and rupture. Pathology is confined to the attachment site and consists of minor mucosal irritation and a mild local eosinophilic and mast cell response, with no invasion, no larval stage in humans and no toxin, which is why the clinical picture is mechanical and behavioral rather than inflammatory.",
  lab: "The diagnosis is usually made on the proglottid, which is the specimen a parent brings in: motile, white, barrel to cucumber-seed shaped, about 10 by 3 millimetres, with a genital pore on each side, and when cleared and examined it shows the characteristic bilateral genitalia. Routine stool microscopy frequently misses the infection because eggs are released inside packets rather than freely, and finding a packet of five to thirty six-hooked eggs within a common membrane is diagnostic. A desiccated proglottid may be mistaken for a rice grain or a maggot, and softening it in saline restores recognizable structure. Species identification is morphological, with polymerase chain reaction available in reference settings, and recent molecular work suggests distinct canine and feline genotypes. Eosinophilia is mild and inconstant.",
  tx: "A single dose of praziquantel is the standard treatment and is reliably effective. Simultaneous treatment of the household dogs and cats, together with environmental flea control, prevents immediate reinfection, and without that step a second human case in the same household is a common sequel. Reassurance is a substantial part of management, since the infection is benign and the frightening part is the sight of the moving segments.",
  alt: "Niclosamide. Nitazoxanide has been used. Veterinary management commonly uses praziquantel or epsiprantel for the animals, with an insect growth regulator or adulticide for the fleas, and treatment of the pets alone, without environmental flea treatment, is a frequent reason reinfection continues.",
  res: "No drug resistance has been reported in this species. Recurrence reflects an untreated flea population and untreated pets rather than parasite biology, and persistent cases in households are an entomological problem.",
  prev: "Flea control on dogs and cats and in the home, which removes the only route of transmission, routine anthelmintic treatment of pets, hand washing after handling animals, and discouraging face-licking and the mouthing of pet bedding and toys by infants. Because transmission needs an adult flea to be swallowed, effective flea management essentially eliminates human risk. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Enterobius vermicularis, the pinworm, which is the commonest alternative explanation for a parent reporting worms in a child and which produces thin white threads rather than flat segments. Taenia species, whose proglottids are larger, have a single lateral genital pore and come from meat rather than fleas. Hymenolepis species, which are small and arthropod or directly transmitted. Fly larvae and maggots from a soiled diaper, undigested food such as rice or sesame, and vegetable fibre account for a large share of specimens brought in by worried parents.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Human dipylidiasis follows swallowing an infected flea, occurs almost exclusively in infants and toddlers, and is diagnosed by the cucumber-seed proglottid with two genital pores or by egg packets rather than by free eggs."],
    ["Memory trick", "DIpylidium has DI, meaning two, for two genital pores. Cucumber seed shape, flea-borne, and a crawling baby. Egg packets, not single eggs."],
    ["Classic setting", "A one-year-old in a household with a flea-infested dog, whose mother brings in a small white motile segment found in the diaper that she first thought was a maggot."],
    ["Step focus", "Routine stool ova and parasite examination is often negative because the eggs stay inside packets, so the proglottid is the diagnostic specimen."]],
  worm: [["Named for a doubled anatomy", "Dipylidium comes from the Greek for two entrances, a reference to the genital pore on each lateral margin of every proglottid, an arrangement unusual among tapeworms and the quickest way to identify the segment under a hand lens. Linnaeus described it in the eighteenth century as a worm of dogs. The flea as intermediate host was established in the 1860s by Melnikov, who found cysticercoids in dog fleas, making it one of the earliest fully worked out cestode life cycles and an early demonstration that an insect could be an obligate link in a worm's development."],
    ["A diagnosis made by a parent, not a laboratory", "Few parasitic infections are so consistently brought to attention by the patient's family rather than by testing. The gravid proglottid stays motile for a time after it is passed, so what a parent sees is a small white object actively contracting in a diaper. Stool microscopy, the test a clinician is most likely to order, is frequently negative because the eggs remain packaged. The practical lesson is that the specimen worth examining is the object in the tissue the parent brought, and that discarding it in favour of a stool collection often loses the diagnosis."],
    ["Why it is a disease of toddlers and almost nobody else", "Infection requires swallowing a whole adult flea. That is an event confined to people who put their hands and the world in their mouths and who spend their time at floor level where fleas and pets are. Adults are infected only occasionally, usually with a history of a pet licking the face or of handling animals and then eating. The age distribution is therefore not about immunity at all but about behavior, which makes this one of the clearest examples of a parasite whose epidemiology is set by human developmental stage."],
    ["Two tapeworms wearing one name", "Molecular studies in the 2010s and 2020s on worms from dogs and cats in Japan, Europe and the Americas found two consistently distinct genotypes with host preference, differing enough in mitochondrial and nuclear sequence that several authors argue they represent separate species, with proposals to resurrect older names for the feline lineage. Human cases have been attributed to both. The taxonomy is unsettled, and it illustrates how often a familiar, well-described parasite turns out to be a complex once sequencing is applied to a sample large enough to see the structure."]] },

{ id: "spirometra-sparganosis", k: "para", g: "Cestodes (tapeworms)", nm: "Spirometra species", also: "sparganosis, Spirometra erinaceieuropaei, Spirometra mansoni, Spirometra proliferum, Sparganum, agent of human sparganosis",
  cls: "Pseudophyllidean cestode related to Diphyllobothrium, with a three-host cycle through a copepod and a vertebrate second intermediate host, adult worms living in dogs and cats, and the human disease caused by the migrating plerocercoid larva known as a sparganum, a ribbon-like white larva centimetres long",
  plain: "A tapeworm larva from frogs, snakes or untreated water that never grows up in people and instead crawls slowly through the tissues, sometimes into the eye or the brain.",
  live: "Dogs, cats and wild carnivores carry the adult worm and shed eggs in feces. Freshwater copepods are the first intermediate host, and frogs, snakes, fish, birds and small mammals are second intermediate hosts carrying the plerocercoid. The disease is concentrated in East and Southeast Asia, particularly China, Korea, Japan, Thailand and Vietnam, with cases across South America, Africa, Europe and North America. Humans are an accidental host in which the larva cannot mature, so there is no onward transmission.",
  spread: "Three routes, all leading to the same larva in the tissues. Drinking untreated water containing infected copepods, which is the route that begins with the procercoid. Eating raw or undercooked frog, snake, fish, chicken or wild pig containing plerocercoids. And the poultice route, applying raw frog or snake flesh to the eye, a wound or a skin lesion as a traditional remedy, from which the larva migrates directly into the host tissue, which accounts for a large share of ocular cases in parts of Asia.",
  causes: "A slowly migrating, painless or mildly tender subcutaneous nodule that changes position over weeks and months, often on the trunk, abdominal wall, groin, breast or limb, sometimes with surrounding urticaria, pruritus and local edema, and sometimes mistaken for a tumor or a lipoma. Ocular sparganosis causes periorbital swelling, lacrimation, ptosis, a palpable orbital mass, proptosis and visual loss. Cerebral sparganosis is the most serious form and presents with seizures, headache, hemiparesis and progressive focal deficits over months to years, closely imitating a brain tumor. Visceral disease involves lung, pleura, intestine, retroperitoneum, spinal cord, scrotum and breast. The rare proliferative form, attributed to Sparganum proliferum, produces disseminated budding larvae and is usually fatal.",
  mech: "A swallowed procercoid or plerocercoid penetrates the intestinal wall and migrates through soft tissue, and because the human host offers no site in which the pseudophyllidean larva can mature into an adult worm, it simply continues to move, remaining alive and growing slowly for years and sometimes more than a decade. Damage is mechanical and inflammatory: the larva leaves a tract of necrosis behind it, and the host mounts an intense eosinophilic granulomatous response around the tunnel, with fibrosis, Charcot-Leyden crystals and eventually a cavity containing the worm. In the brain this produces migrating areas of inflammation, edema, hemorrhage and gliosis, which is why serial imaging shows lesions that move and change, an almost unique radiological behavior. Larval secretions, including cysteine proteases and an immunoglobulin-degrading activity, blunt local immunity and help the larva persist. The proliferative form differs fundamentally in that the larva buds and multiplies in tissue.",
  lab: "Definitive diagnosis is the recovery and identification of the larva at excision, which is a white, flat, ribbon-like, actively motile worm a few centimetres long with a longitudinal groove and no scolex with suckers. Histology shows a sinuous tract with eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation and the characteristic parenchyma with calcareous corpuscles. Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay against sparganum antigens on serum, and on cerebrospinal fluid in cerebral disease, is sensitive and widely used in Korea and China and supports a presumptive diagnosis. Imaging in cerebral disease shows irregular enhancing lesions, the tunnel sign, serpiginous enhancement, white matter degeneration, ipsilateral ventricular dilatation and, on repeated studies, migration of the lesion, which is the most suggestive single finding. Peripheral and cerebrospinal fluid eosinophilia is usual. Polymerase chain reaction on tissue identifies the species.",
  tx: "Surgical removal of the intact larva is the definitive treatment and is curative for accessible subcutaneous, ocular and single cerebral lesions. Praziquantel at high and prolonged dosing has been used where surgery is not feasible, with limited and inconsistent efficacy, since the drug penetrates tissue larvae poorly, and albendazole has similarly modest results. Corticosteroids are used to control the inflammatory response in cerebral and ocular disease, particularly around the time of intervention. Antiseizure medication is used for cerebral disease, and ocular cases are managed with ophthalmic surgical expertise because an incompletely removed larva can migrate further.",
  alt: "Repeated or extended praziquantel and albendazole courses in inoperable disease. Stereotactic and endoscopic approaches for deep cerebral lesions. Local injection of ethanol around subcutaneous larvae has been described. The proliferative form has no effective therapy.",
  res: "Drug resistance is not the issue. The problem is pharmacological access: praziquantel is highly effective against adult cestodes in the gut lumen but reaches a larva encased in a granulomatous tract at concentrations that are often sublethal, and a surviving larva simply resumes migration. Treatment failure after apparently complete excision usually means a second larva, since multiple larvae from one exposure are common.",
  prev: "Drinking boiled, filtered or treated water in endemic areas, since copepods are removed by simple filtration, thorough cooking of frog, snake, fish, poultry and wild game, and avoidance of the traditional practice of applying raw amphibian or reptile flesh to the eye or to wounds, which is a well-documented route. Hand hygiene after handling frogs and snakes, and treatment of dogs and cats, reduce environmental egg load. There is no vaccine and no chemoprophylaxis.",
  look: "Subcutaneous lesions mimic lipoma, epidermoid cyst, soft tissue sarcoma, breast carcinoma, cysticercosis, gnathostomiasis, which also produces migratory swellings and comes from raw fish in overlapping regions, paragonimiasis, dirofilariasis and loiasis. Cerebral disease mimics glioma, metastasis, tuberculoma, neurocysticercosis, cerebral abscess and vasculitis, and in practice is frequently operated on as a suspected tumor. Ocular disease mimics orbital cellulitis, dacryoadenitis, orbital pseudotumor and ocular cysticercosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Human sparganosis is caused by a plerocercoid larva that cannot mature in people, so it migrates through tissue for years and the treatment is surgical removal rather than a drug."],
    ["Memory trick", "Sparganum is a wandering ribbon. Three ways in: drink the copepod, eat the frog or snake, or put the frog on your eye. In the brain, the lesion that moves between scans."],
    ["Classic setting", "A patient from East or Southeast Asia with a migrating painless nodule on the abdominal wall, or a young adult with years of seizures and a serpiginous enhancing lesion that has changed position on serial magnetic resonance imaging."],
    ["Step focus", "Migration of a cerebral lesion on serial imaging, with eosinophilia and positive sparganum serology, is the pattern that separates this from tumor and from neurocysticercosis."]],
  worm: [["The frog poultice", "In parts of China, Korea, Vietnam and Thailand, raw frog or snake flesh has long been applied to inflamed eyes, sprains and open wounds as a cooling remedy. The plerocercoid in that flesh does not wait to be eaten: it leaves the dead amphibian and enters the living human tissue pressed against it. This accounts for a substantial fraction of ocular and periorbital sparganosis in the published series, and it is one of very few parasitic infections acquired transcutaneously from a medicinal practice rather than from food, water or a vector."],
    ["A larva sequenced from a man's brain", "In 2014 a team in Britain reported a patient with four years of worsening neurological symptoms and a lesion that had moved several centimetres across his brain on successive scans over that period. Surgeons removed a living worm, and the group sequenced its genome directly from the specimen, the first genome of Spirometra erinaceieuropaei. The sequence revealed a genome around ten times larger than that of other tapeworms, with expanded families of proteases, and it identified candidate drug targets. A single accidental larva therefore became the reference genome for its species."],
    ["Why the lesion moves", "Almost no other intracranial pathology relocates. A tumor grows, an abscess expands, a granuloma consolidates, but a sparganum crawls, leaving a trail of inflammation behind it and provoking new inflammation ahead. Serial imaging therefore shows the enhancing focus in a different place, sometimes a different lobe, months later, with white matter degeneration and ventricular dilatation on the affected side. Radiologists in endemic regions treat migration plus eosinophilia plus positive serology as close to diagnostic, and it is the single most useful sign in a disease that otherwise looks like glioma."],
    ["The proliferating sparganum nobody has placed", "Sparganum proliferum is the name given to larvae that bud and multiply in human tissue, producing hundreds of irregular branching worms through skin, muscle and viscera, with a course that has been fatal in nearly every reported case. Fewer than a few dozen cases exist in the literature, from Japan, Taiwan, Venezuela, Paraguay and the United States. Molecular work indicates it is a distinct lineage rather than an aberrant form of the common species, but no adult worm has ever been found and its natural life cycle remains unknown, which leaves a lethal human parasite with no identified reservoir."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "schistosoma-japonicum", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Schistosoma japonicum", also: "Oriental blood fluke, Katayama fever agent, agent of Asian intestinal schistosomiasis",
  cls: "Digenean blood fluke, separate sexes with the slender female held in the gynecophoral canal of the male, adults in the superior mesenteric venous plexus, eggs small and rounded with a small lateral knob or spine, intermediate host the amphibious snail Oncomelania",
  plain: "The Asian blood fluke, a zoonosis of water buffalo and other mammals that lays far more eggs than its African relatives and scars the liver severely.",
  live: "Humans plus an unusually wide range of reservoir mammals, which is the defining epidemiological feature: water buffalo, cattle, pigs, dogs, goats, horses and more than forty rodent and other species, with water buffalo historically the dominant contributor of eggs to the environment in China. The snail intermediate host is Oncomelania hupensis, amphibious rather than fully aquatic, which lives in marshland, irrigation ditches and the margins of lakes. Transmission persists in parts of China, chiefly the lake and marshland regions of the Yangtze basin, the Philippines and small foci in Indonesia on Sulawesi, with Japan having eliminated the disease.",
  spread: "Cercariae released from the snail penetrate intact skin during contact with fresh water, in rice paddies, ditches, lakes and flooded fields, so occupational exposure in farming and fishing is the main pattern. Eggs reach water in human or animal feces and hatch into miracidia that infect the snail. Because the amphibious snail lives at the water margin and the reservoir hosts are livestock, exposure is not confined to swimming and wading by people, and animal contamination maintains transmission even where human treatment coverage is high.",
  causes: "Cercarial dermatitis at the site of penetration. Acute schistosomiasis, called Katayama fever and most severe and most frequently recognized with this species, appears three to eight weeks after exposure with fever, rigors, urticaria, cough, wheeze, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy and marked eosinophilia. Chronic intestinal disease produces abdominal pain, bloody mucoid diarrhea, colonic polyposis, protein-losing enteropathy and growth failure in children. Hepatosplenic disease is the characteristic outcome: periportal Symmers pipestem fibrosis, portal hypertension, splenomegaly, variceal bleeding and ascites with preserved hepatocellular function until late. Ectopic eggs cause pulmonary hypertension from pulmonary arteriolar disease, and cerebral schistosomiasis with seizures and focal deficits, which is more common with this species than with the others.",
  mech: "Cercariae penetrate skin, transform into schistosomula, migrate through lung to the liver, mature and pair, and the adult pair migrates against portal flow into the small radicles of the superior mesenteric vein, where the female lays eggs. The decisive quantitative fact is fecundity: a female produces several thousand eggs a day, roughly ten times the output of Schistosoma mansoni, and they are laid in clumps, so the egg burden delivered to tissue is far heavier. Eggs penetrate toward the gut lumen or are swept to the liver, where each lodged egg provokes a granuloma around antigens secreted through its shell, and the confluence of many granulomas along portal tracts produces the dense periportal fibrosis of Symmers. Adults themselves provoke little response because they adsorb host antigens, consume host immunoglobulin and continuously remodel their tegument, which is how they survive for a decade or more in the bloodstream. Immune-complex glomerular disease and ectopic egg deposition in lung and central nervous system complete the picture.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy with Kato-Katz thick smear shows the small rounded egg, 70 to 100 by 55 to 65 micrometres, with a minute lateral knob that is easily missed, and egg output per worm is high but excretion is intermittent, so repeated samples and concentration improve yield. Rectal snip histology detects eggs when stool is negative. Serology with soluble egg antigen or adult worm antigen is sensitive and widely used in low-intensity settings in China but cannot distinguish current from past infection, and circulating cathodic and anodic antigen assays indicate active infection. Polymerase chain reaction on stool or serum is increasingly used for low-burden surveillance. Ultrasound grades the network pattern of periportal fibrosis. Eosinophilia, raised IgE and anemia are usual, and liver function tests are often near normal despite advanced fibrosis.",
  tx: "Praziquantel, given as a split or single-day course, is the treatment for all schistosome species and is effective against adult worms while having little action on immature migrating schistosomula, which is why retreatment some weeks later is used after a defined heavy exposure. Acute Katayama fever is managed with corticosteroids to control the hypersensitivity response, with praziquantel given or repeated once the acute phase settles, since killing worms during the acute illness can worsen symptoms. Established portal hypertension is managed as portal hypertension of any cause, with endoscopic variceal therapy and beta-blockade, and antiparasitic treatment prevents progression without reversing established fibrosis.",
  alt: "Artemisinin derivatives, artemether and artesunate, have activity against immature schistosomula that praziquantel lacks and have been used in China for prophylaxis and in combination, with the reservation that widespread use in malarious areas risks selecting artemisinin resistance in Plasmodium. Oxamniquine is active only against Schistosoma mansoni and has no role here. Mefloquine has shown antischistosomal activity in trials.",
  res: "Praziquantel has been used on an enormous scale for four decades and confirmed clinical resistance remains unproven, although reduced susceptibility has been produced in the laboratory, and reduced cure rates in some field settings are more readily explained by heavy burdens, reinfection, the drug's inactivity against immature stages and variable bioavailability. The dependence of the entire control effort on a single drug is widely regarded as the principal strategic vulnerability in schistosomiasis control.",
  prev: "Avoiding contact with fresh water in endemic marshland, protective footwear and clothing where contact is unavoidable, and prompt vigorous towelling, which removes some cercariae before penetration. Mass drug administration with praziquantel reduces morbidity and transmission but cannot interrupt a cycle fed by livestock, so Chinese control has combined human and bovine treatment, replacement of water buffalo with machinery, snail control with niclosamide and environmental modification of marshland, sanitation and health education. That integrated approach, sustained over decades, moved China from tens of millions infected to the verge of elimination, and Japan achieved elimination by the 1970s largely through environmental change. No vaccine is licensed, and bovine vaccine candidates have been pursued precisely because of the zoonotic reservoir.",
  look: "Schistosoma mansoni, which causes the same intestinal and hepatosplenic disease with a large lateral-spined egg and no significant animal reservoir, and Schistosoma mekongi, which is closely related, has a similar egg and occupies the Mekong basin. Katayama fever mimics typhoid, malaria, acute HIV, viral hepatitis, visceral leishmaniasis and drug reaction. Hepatosplenic disease mimics cirrhosis of any cause, and the preserved hepatocellular function is the clue. Cerebral disease mimics neurocysticercosis, tuberculoma and tumor.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Schistosoma japonicum has a small round egg with a tiny lateral knob, lays about ten times as many eggs as Schistosoma mansoni, and has a broad mammalian reservoir including water buffalo, which is why human treatment alone cannot interrupt transmission."],
    ["Memory trick", "Japonicum is the Japanese one with the Jiggly small round egg and a tiny knob. Mansoni has a big lateral spine, haematobium a terminal spine. Japonicum is the zoonosis, so treat the buffalo too."],
    ["Classic setting", "A rice farmer from the Yangtze lake region or a Philippine province with hepatosplenomegaly, varices and a nodular periportal pattern on ultrasound, or a traveller with fever, urticaria and eosinophilia a month after rafting."],
    ["Step focus", "Katayama fever is a serum-sickness-like hypersensitivity to migrating schistosomula and egg antigens, so it occurs before eggs appear in stool and serology may still be negative."]],
  worm: [["Named for a Japanese valley and solved by a Japanese team", "The disease was described in the Katayama district of Hiroshima prefecture, where farmers suffered an acute febrile illness after flooding the rice fields. Fujiro Katsurada identified the parasite in 1904 from an autopsy and named it Schistosoma japonicum, and Keinosuke Miyairi and Masatsugu Suzuki demonstrated in 1913 that the miracidium entered a small amphibious snail, establishing the snail stage of any schistosome for the first time. Japan then eliminated the disease over the following sixty years by concreting irrigation channels, draining marsh and removing the snail habitat, without a drug campaign."],
    ["The invasion that was called off", "In 1949 and 1950 the People's Liberation Army massed troops in the Yangtze marshes to train for an amphibious assault on Taiwan. Tens of thousands of soldiers developed acute schistosomiasis from wading in snail-infested water, and the operation was postponed. Mao then made schistosomiasis control a national campaign, with mass snail-crushing mobilizations, poems written about the disease and decades of sustained effort. The military episode is frequently cited as the moment a parasite altered the course of a planned invasion."],
    ["Why fecundity matters more than worm count", "Schistosoma mansoni females shed a few hundred eggs daily; Schistosoma japonicum females shed several thousand, in clumps rather than singly. Since pathology is driven almost entirely by granulomas forming around trapped eggs, the same number of worm pairs delivers a far heavier fibrotic load. This is the mechanistic reason Asian schistosomiasis produces more severe hepatosplenic disease, more frequent cerebral involvement and a more dramatic acute syndrome, and it is an instance where a single reproductive parameter explains a clinical difference between sibling species."],
    ["The buffalo problem", "Mass praziquantel treatment can drive human prevalence very low and yet transmission continues, because water buffalo standing in the same water shed enormous numbers of eggs. Modelling and field trials in the Yangtze showed bovines contributing the majority of the environmental egg load in some settings. Chinese control therefore treated cattle and buffalo with praziquantel, fenced animals from water, subsidized mechanical tractors to replace buffalo in paddy cultivation and pursued a bovine vaccine. It is the standard illustration of why a zoonotic reservoir changes a control strategy from a medical programme into an agricultural one."]] },

{ id: "schistosoma-mekongi", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Schistosoma mekongi", also: "Mekong schistosome, agent of Mekong schistosomiasis",
  cls: "Digenean blood fluke of the japonicum group, separate sexes with the female carried in the male gynecophoral canal, adults in the mesenteric venous plexus, egg small and rounded with a minute lateral knob, intermediate host the small aquatic snail Neotricula aperta",
  plain: "A close Asian relative of the Japanese blood fluke confined to the Mekong river, carried by a tiny snail that lives on rocks in the current.",
  live: "Humans and dogs are the significant hosts, with pigs and some rodents also infected, and the dog reservoir is an important practical obstacle to elimination. The intermediate host is Neotricula aperta, a snail only a couple of millimetres long that attaches to rocks and submerged vegetation in fast-flowing sections of the river, which concentrates transmission in particular rapids and channels. The distribution is extremely narrow: the Mekong basin in southern Laos, chiefly Khong and Mounlapamok districts of Champasak province, and northeastern Cambodia in Kratie and Stung Treng provinces, with an at-risk population of a few hundred thousand.",
  spread: "Skin penetration by cercariae during water contact, with the striking feature of marked seasonality: the snail releases cercariae chiefly in the low-water dry season, from roughly January to May, when the river drops, the rocks and channels are exposed and villagers, especially children, swim, bathe, fish and play in shallow water. Eggs enter the river in human and dog feces where sanitation is absent and riverside defecation is routine. The geographic and seasonal concentration means exposure is tightly tied to particular villages and particular months.",
  causes: "Cercarial dermatitis, then in some an acute syndrome resembling Katayama fever with fever, urticaria, cough and eosinophilia, although acute illness is reported less often than with Schistosoma japonicum. Chronic infection produces abdominal pain, chronic or bloody diarrhea, anorexia, hepatomegaly with left lobe predominance, splenomegaly, anemia, growth retardation and impaired cognitive performance in children. The severe end point is periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension, splenomegaly, esophageal varices and fatal hematemesis, which was common in the heavily infected villages before control began and gave the disease its local reputation as a cause of children dying of swollen bellies. Ectopic egg disease is uncommon but reported.",
  mech: "The sequence follows the schistosome pattern: skin penetration, transformation to schistosomula, migration through lung and liver, pairing, and migration to the mesenteric venules where egg laying begins about five weeks after exposure. Fecundity is intermediate, lower than Schistosoma japonicum but with eggs laid in small clusters, and the pathology is again entirely egg driven, with granuloma formation around antigens diffusing through the eggshell, a Th2 and interleukin-13 dominated fibrotic response, and progressive collagen deposition along portal tracts producing the pipestem pattern. Eggs that reach the gut wall cause mucosal ulceration, polyp formation and chronic blood loss. Adult worms evade immunity by tegumental turnover and antigen adsorption and survive for years. Reinfection in a hyperendemic village means the fibrotic burden accumulates through childhood, which is why severe portal hypertension appeared in adolescents.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy with Kato-Katz shows an egg very similar to that of Schistosoma japonicum but slightly smaller, roughly 50 to 65 by 40 to 55 micrometres, round with a small lateral knob, and reliable species assignment rests on geography rather than morphology. Repeated samples are needed because output is low and intermittent in treated populations. Serology by enzyme immunoassay and the circulating cathodic antigen urine assay are more sensitive than microscopy at low intensity and have been used in Lao and Cambodian surveys, where microscopy badly underestimated prevalence. Polymerase chain reaction on stool detects light infections. Ultrasound shows the periportal fibrosis network and splenomegaly. Eosinophilia, anemia and raised IgE are usual.",
  tx: "Praziquantel, given as a split dose on a single day, is the treatment, and cure rates are high. Treatment of children at school age captures the group with the heaviest burdens. Acute schistosomiasis is managed with corticosteroid cover and delayed or repeated praziquantel, and established portal hypertension is managed as portal hypertension of any cause, since antiparasitic treatment halts progression rather than reversing fibrosis.",
  alt: "Artemisinin derivatives have activity against immature stages and have been studied in combination with praziquantel. Oxamniquine has no activity against this species. No second drug is in routine programme use, which leaves control dependent on praziquantel alone.",
  res: "No confirmed praziquantel resistance has been described in this species. Residual egg positivity after mass treatment reflects reinfection in a highly focal and seasonal transmission setting, a dog reservoir shedding eggs into the same water, and praziquantel inactivity against migrating schistosomula, rather than demonstrated reduced drug susceptibility.",
  prev: "Mass drug administration with praziquantel in the endemic districts, sustained annually, which reduced prevalence from more than fifty percent in some villages in the 1980s to low single figures, together with latrine construction and promotion, provision of safe water for bathing and washing, health education about dry season water contact, and treatment of dogs. Snail control is difficult because Neotricula aperta lives in fast water on rocks where molluscicide application is impractical. The two governments and the World Health Organization have declared elimination as a public health problem the goal, and the remaining obstacles are the dog reservoir, persistent riverside defecation and the risk of resurgence if annual treatment lapses, which has happened before in this focus. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Schistosoma japonicum, which it closely resembles in egg morphology and clinical behavior and from which it is separated chiefly by geography and snail host, and Schistosoma mansoni, whose egg bears a prominent lateral spine. Opisthorchis viverrini, which is endemic in the same river basin, also causes hepatobiliary disease and is frequently a coinfection. Liver disease from hepatitis B, alcohol and aflatoxin-related cirrhosis, and thalassemia, which is common in the region and also presents with splenomegaly and anemia.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Schistosoma mekongi is restricted to the Mekong basin in southern Laos and northern Cambodia, has a japonicum-like small round egg with a lateral knob, and uses the tiny rock-dwelling snail Neotricula aperta."],
    ["Memory trick", "Mekongi lives in the Mekong. Small egg like japonicum, small snail, small map. Transmission in the dry season, when the river falls and children swim on the rocks."],
    ["Classic setting", "A Lao or Cambodian child from a Mekong island village with a distended abdomen, hepatosplenomegaly and anemia, who swims daily in the river during the dry season."],
    ["Step focus", "It is a member of the japonicum group, so eggs are small and round with a lateral knob rather than spined, and the separation from Schistosoma japonicum rests on geography."]],
  worm: [["A species found in a hospital in Paris", "In 1957 a Lao boy from the Mekong town of Khong was treated in Paris with hepatosplenic disease and schistosome eggs that resembled those of Schistosoma japonicum, a species not known to occur anywhere near Laos. The observation prompted surveys along the river through the 1960s, the snail host was identified in the 1970s, and the organism was formally described as a separate species, Schistosoma mekongi, in 1978. It is a reminder that a single exported case can reveal a focus of disease affecting a whole region."],
    ["The smallest snail in schistosomiasis", "Neotricula aperta is about two millimetres long and clings to rocks and submerged wood in fast-flowing channels, a habitat utterly unlike the still ponds and ditches of Biomphalaria and Bulinus. That ecology dictates everything about the disease: transmission concentrates where rapids and rocky shallows occur, it peaks in the dry season when the water level exposes those habitats, it affects the people who swim and fish there, and molluscicide, which works in a ditch, is futile in a river in flood. Snail biology rather than human behavior draws the map."],
    ["Mass treatment, success, and the long tail", "Khong district in the 1980s had villages with more than half the population infected and adolescents dying of variceal bleeding. Annual praziquantel campaigns, begun in the late 1980s with Medecins Sans Frontieres and later carried by the national programmes, drove egg prevalence below five percent within a few years and essentially abolished severe morbidity, one of the clearest public health victories in the region. Yet when treatment rounds lapsed, prevalence climbed back, and sensitive antigen and molecular surveys later showed infection persisting at levels microscopy had declared almost absent."],
    ["Dogs on the riverbank", "Elimination here is held up less by biology than by village dogs. Surveys in the Lao and Cambodian foci have found substantial infection rates in dogs, which defecate at the water margin precisely where children bathe. Human mass treatment therefore removes most but not all of the egg supply. Treating dogs, confining them or resolving the sanitation problem for both species is the remaining step, and it places a parasite elimination programme in the awkward position of needing veterinary coverage in some of the poorest riverside communities in Southeast Asia."]] },

{ id: "schistosoma-intercalatum", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Schistosoma intercalatum", also: "Schistosoma guineensis, rectal bilharziasis of Central Africa, the Lower Guinea and Zaire strains",
  cls: "Digenean blood fluke of the haematobium group, separate sexes with the female in the male gynecophoral canal, adults in the inferior mesenteric and rectal venous plexus, egg large with a terminal spine and acid-fast on Ziehl-Neelsen staining, intermediate hosts Bulinus africanus and Bulinus forskalii group snails",
  plain: "A Central African blood fluke that lives in the rectal veins and causes bloody stool, with an egg that looks like the bladder fluke but is passed in feces.",
  live: "Humans are the principal host, with sheep, goats, rodents and some other mammals carrying light infections of limited epidemiological weight. The snail hosts are Bulinus species, with the Lower Guinea form using Bulinus forskalii and the Zaire form using Bulinus africanus, a difference that underlies the splitting of the taxon. The distribution is small and patchy in the rainforest belt of Central and West Africa: Cameroon, Gabon, Equatorial Guinea, Sao Tome and Principe, Chad, the Central African Republic, Nigeria and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Current taxonomy treats the Lower Guinea form as Schistosoma guineensis and reserves Schistosoma intercalatum for the Zaire form.",
  spread: "Cercarial penetration of skin during contact with slow-moving or standing fresh water, in streams, pools, forest creeks and water collection and bathing sites. Eggs reach water in feces, so transmission depends on the absence of latrines near water bodies. The foci are small and sharply localized, often a single valley or island, because the Bulinus snail habitats are limited, and the distribution is being reshaped by hybridization with Schistosoma haematobium where the two species meet.",
  causes: "The mildest human schistosomiasis of the major species. Cercarial dermatitis, occasionally an acute Katayama-like syndrome. Chronic disease centres on the distal bowel: lower abdominal pain, tenesmus, diarrhea with blood and mucus, rectal polyps and occasionally rectal prolapse in children, closely resembling bacillary or amoebic dysentery. Hepatosplenomegaly occurs but severe periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension are distinctly uncommon compared with Schistosoma mansoni and Schistosoma japonicum, and genital and urinary involvement are seen when hybrids with Schistosoma haematobium are present. Anemia, eosinophilia and growth effects in heavily infected children complete the picture.",
  mech: "The life cycle follows the schistosome plan, with adult pairs settling preferentially in the inferior mesenteric and rectal venous plexus rather than the superior mesenteric or vesical plexus, which explains the rectal localization of disease. Eggs trapped in the rectal and colonic wall provoke eosinophilic granulomas, mucosal ulceration, polyp formation and chronic blood loss, while relatively few eggs are swept to the liver, which is the proposed reason hepatic fibrosis is mild. Fecundity is lower than that of the other species, and worm burdens in surveys tend to be modest, so the total egg load delivered to tissue is smaller. Adults evade immunity by the usual tegumental mechanisms. The species is notable for interspecific mating: where it coexists with Schistosoma haematobium, heterologous pairing produces viable hybrids whose offspring show intermediate egg morphology, broader snail compatibility and apparently greater fitness, and the hybrids have progressively displaced pure populations in parts of Cameroon.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows a large terminal-spined egg, 140 to 240 by 50 to 85 micrometres, which looks like the egg of Schistosoma haematobium but is found in feces rather than urine, and the eggshell is acid-fast on Ziehl-Neelsen staining, a classic distinguishing feature from Schistosoma haematobium, whose shell is not. Terminal-spined eggs in stool with a slight terminal spine deflection, together with geography, make the diagnosis. Rectal snip biopsy is sensitive. Hybrids produce intermediate egg shapes and may be passed in both urine and stool, which confounds morphological identification, so molecular typing with mitochondrial and nuclear markers has become necessary in the hybrid zones. Serology does not separate species. Eosinophilia is usual and ultrasound rarely shows significant hepatic change.",
  tx: "Praziquantel as a single-day course, with cure rates that are high, and this species is regarded as among the most praziquantel-responsive of the schistosomes. Treatment resolves the dysenteric symptoms and the rectal polyps regress. School-based mass treatment captures the heaviest burdens, as in the other species.",
  alt: "Artemisinin derivatives have activity against immature stages. Oxamniquine is inactive against members of the haematobium group. Metrifonate, historically used against Schistosoma haematobium, has fallen out of use and is not an option here.",
  res: "No resistance has been reported, and the small size of the endemic foci means drug pressure has been limited compared with the major species. The practical complication is not resistance but identity: as hybrids with Schistosoma haematobium spread, the organism being treated and monitored in Cameroon and neighbouring countries is increasingly not the pure species, and programme data recorded against species names become difficult to interpret.",
  prev: "Latrine provision to keep feces out of the small forest streams that transmit it, safe water supply for bathing and washing to remove the need for contact, snail control where habitats are small and defined, which is more feasible here than for river-dwelling species, mass praziquantel treatment and health education. The limited geography makes local elimination realistic, and transmission on Sao Tome and in parts of Cameroon has fallen markedly. The spread of hybrid schistosomes complicates control because hybrids use a wider range of snails and infect both bowel and urinary tract. No vaccine is available.",
  look: "Schistosoma haematobium, whose egg is also terminal-spined but is passed in urine and is not acid-fast, and with which this species hybridizes. Schistosoma mansoni, with a large lateral-spined egg in stool, which coexists in parts of the range. Amoebic colitis, bacillary dysentery, Shigella and Campylobacter infection, inflammatory bowel disease, Trichuris trichiura with rectal prolapse in children, and colorectal polyps or carcinoma in adults with rectal bleeding.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Schistosoma intercalatum has a terminal-spined egg like Schistosoma haematobium but passes it in feces, the shell is acid-fast on Ziehl-Neelsen staining, and the disease is rectal rather than urinary."],
    ["Memory trick", "InterCALATUM is intermediate: a haematobium-shaped egg in a mansoni-style stool sample. Terminal spine plus acid-fast plus feces equals intercalatum."],
    ["Classic setting", "A child from a forest village in Cameroon, Gabon or Sao Tome with chronic bloody mucoid diarrhea, tenesmus and terminal-spined eggs in stool."],
    ["Step focus", "It is the mildest of the major human schistosomes, with rectal disease and little hepatic fibrosis, and it hybridizes with Schistosoma haematobium, which shifts egg morphology and organ involvement."]],
  worm: [["An egg in the wrong place", "The species was described by Fisher in 1934 from the Belgian Congo, and its oddity was immediately apparent: a terminal-spined egg, the signature of the urinary bladder fluke, appearing in stool from patients with rectal bleeding. The name intercalatum, meaning inserted between, records exactly that intermediate position between the haematobium and mansoni groups. The acid-fast staining of the eggshell, noticed later, gave laboratories a simple way to separate it from Schistosoma haematobium, and remains one of very few instances where Ziehl-Neelsen is used on a parasite egg."],
    ["Split into two species by its snails", "Two forms had long been recognized, one in Lower Guinea using Bulinus forskalii snails and one in the Congo basin using Bulinus africanus, differing also in egg dimensions and in experimental cross-breeding behavior. In 2003 the Lower Guinea form was raised to a separate species, Schistosoma guineensis, leaving Schistosoma intercalatum for the Zaire form. Most of the published clinical and epidemiological literature, including the Cameroon and Sao Tome work, concerns what is now called Schistosoma guineensis, which makes reading the older literature a careful exercise."],
    ["Swamped by a hybrid", "In Loum, Cameroon, surveys through the 1970s and 1980s documented something unusual: Schistosoma haematobium arrived in a focus of Schistosoma intercalatum, the two species mated, and the hybrid offspring, with intermediate eggs, wider snail compatibility and apparently greater fitness, progressively replaced the pure populations. Within two decades the original species was difficult to find there. It is one of the best-documented cases of introgressive hybridization displacing a parasite species in the wild, and it has become a reference point for the broader concern about schistosome hybrids across Africa."],
    ["Why the liver is spared", "Schistosoma mansoni and Schistosoma japonicum sit in the superior mesenteric drainage, so eggs swept backward reach the liver in numbers and the end result is pipestem fibrosis and portal hypertension. This species settles in the inferior mesenteric and rectal plexus, with lower fecundity, so eggs lodge in the rectal wall, cause dysentery and polyps, and comparatively few arrive in the portal tracts. The clinical mildness of the disease therefore follows from a difference of a few centimetres in where the adult worms choose to live."]] },

{ id: "paragonimus-westermani", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Paragonimus westermani", also: "oriental lung fluke, agent of paragonimiasis, pulmonary distomiasis",
  cls: "Digenean lung fluke, hermaphroditic, plump reddish-brown adult shaped like a coffee bean about 8 to 16 mm long, living in pairs in cystic cavities in the lung, egg golden-brown and operculate, two intermediate hosts, a freshwater snail and then a crab or crayfish",
  plain: "A coffee-bean-shaped fluke caught from raw freshwater crab that burrows into the lung and causes a chronic cough with rusty sputum, closely imitating tuberculosis.",
  live: "Humans plus a wide range of carnivorous mammals, chiefly dogs, cats, tigers, leopards, pigs and wild boar, which makes it a zoonosis with abundant animal reservoirs. The first intermediate hosts are small freshwater snails of the Semisulcospira and related genera, and the second are freshwater crabs and crayfish. Paragonimus westermani is distributed across East and Southeast Asia, Korea, Japan, China, the Philippines, Taiwan, Thailand, Vietnam, India and Sri Lanka, while related species cause the same disease elsewhere: Paragonimus kellicotti in North America from crayfish, Paragonimus mexicanus in Latin America and Paragonimus africanus and uterobilateralis in West Africa.",
  spread: "Eating raw, pickled, drunken, salted or undercooked freshwater crab or crayfish containing metacercariae, including dishes such as raw crab marinated in soy sauce or wine, which are regional delicacies. Paratenic transmission occurs from eating raw or undercooked wild boar meat containing immature worms. Contamination of hands, utensils and chopping boards during the preparation of raw crab also transmits, and drinking water containing crushed crab tissue has been implicated. The metacercaria is not killed by alcohol marinade, vinegar or brief salting, which is why culinary tradition rather than poverty drives the epidemiology.",
  causes: "An abdominal phase in the first weeks, with abdominal pain, diarrhea and urticaria as larvae cross the gut wall and traverse the peritoneum, sometimes with fever and marked eosinophilia. The pulmonary phase, developing over weeks to months, produces chronic cough, hemoptysis with characteristic rusty-brown or blood-flecked sputum containing eggs, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, night sweats, low-grade fever and weight loss, a syndrome essentially indistinguishable from pulmonary tuberculosis. Pleural effusion, pneumothorax and empyema occur, with eosinophilic pleural fluid. Ectopic paragonimiasis is important: migratory subcutaneous nodules, especially with Paragonimus skrjabini, abdominal masses, and cerebral paragonimiasis, which presents with seizures, headache, visual loss and hemiparesis and historically accounted for a notable proportion of acquired epilepsy in parts of Korea and China.",
  mech: "Metacercariae excyst in the duodenum, penetrate the intestinal wall, wander in the peritoneal cavity, cross the diaphragm and pleura and enter the lung parenchyma, where two worms usually settle together and provoke a fibrous capsule, forming a cyst that communicates with a bronchiole. Within the cyst the pair feeds on blood and tissue, releases eggs into the cavity, and the eggs drain into the airway and are coughed up or swallowed. The pathology is a combination of the migratory tract damage, the inflammatory reaction to the worms and their secretions, which includes cysteine proteases that digest tissue and cleave immunoglobulin, and the granulomatous response to eggs deposited in surrounding parenchyma and pleura. Blood and necrotic debris in the cyst give the sputum its rusty color. Worms survive many years, and larvae that fail to find the lung continue wandering, which is how cerebral and subcutaneous disease arises, usually in heavy infections and more often in children.",
  lab: "Sputum microscopy for the operculate golden-brown egg, about 80 to 120 by 45 to 70 micrometres, with a flattened opercular shoulder and a thickened abopercular end, is the core test, and because patients swallow sputum, stool examination also detects eggs, particularly in children. Egg output is intermittent, so repeated samples and sodium hydroxide digestion of sputum improve yield. Chest radiography and computed tomography show ring shadows, nodules with a necrotic centre, a burrow or worm-migration track, cavitation, pleural thickening and effusion, patterns that overlap heavily with tuberculosis, and the cystic lesion with an adjacent linear track is the most suggestive. Enzyme immunoassay and immunoblot serology against adult worm antigen is sensitive and is the practical diagnostic route in nonendemic settings and in cerebral disease, where eggs are absent from sputum. Peripheral and pleural fluid eosinophilia is prominent. Magnetic resonance imaging in cerebral disease shows conglomerated ring-enhancing lesions with hemorrhage, sometimes described as a soap bubble pattern, and chronic calcification.",
  tx: "Praziquantel over two to three days is the standard treatment and cure rates exceed ninety percent. Triclabendazole, given as one or two doses, is equally effective and better tolerated, and is the preferred agent in many settings. Cerebral disease is treated with the same drugs together with corticosteroids and antiseizure medication, since dying worms provoke inflammation in a closed compartment, and surgery is reserved for mass lesions, hydrocephalus and large pleural collections. Pleural empyema and trapped lung may need drainage or decortication.",
  alt: "Bithionol, the historical treatment, given over several weeks, with significant gastrointestinal toxicity. Niclofolan has been used. Where the diagnosis is strongly suspected and testing is unavailable, a therapeutic trial has been used as a practical alternative to prolonged antituberculous therapy.",
  res: "Resistance is not described. The common clinical failure is misdiagnosis: a patient treated for smear-negative tuberculosis for months while the fluke continues, which is well documented across Asia and in migrant and refugee populations elsewhere. Incomplete response also reflects dense fibrous encapsulation of long-standing cysts and the limited penetration of drug into a calcified cerebral lesion.",
  prev: "Thorough cooking of freshwater crab and crayfish, since neither alcohol, vinegar, soy marinade nor brief salting kills the metacercaria, separation of utensils and surfaces used for raw crab, and care with wild boar meat. Health education aimed at specific dishes is more effective than general advice, because the exposure is a named food in a named context. Snail and crab control is impractical. Treating dogs and cats reduces local egg contamination. There is no vaccine and no chemoprophylaxis.",
  look: "Pulmonary tuberculosis is the central differential and the usual initial diagnosis, with lung abscess, bronchiectasis, lung cancer, aspergilloma, melioidosis, nocardiosis, Loeffler syndrome and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. Hemoptysis with eosinophilia narrows it considerably. Cerebral disease mimics tuberculoma, neurocysticercosis, cerebral abscess, arteriovenous malformation and tumor. Migratory subcutaneous nodules mimic sparganosis, gnathostomiasis, cutaneous larva migrans and loiasis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Paragonimus is acquired from raw or pickled freshwater crab or crayfish and presents as chronic cough with rusty-brown sputum containing operculate eggs, closely mimicking tuberculosis, with eosinophilia as the distinguishing clue."],
    ["Memory trick", "Crab in, lung out. A coffee-bean fluke in a lung cyst, rusty sputum, operculate egg. Tuberculosis plus eosinophilia equals think lung fluke."],
    ["Classic setting", "A patient from Korea, China or Southeast Asia with months of cough, blood-streaked rusty sputum, a cavitary chest film, repeatedly negative sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli and a high eosinophil count, or in North America a Missouri fisherman who ate raw crayfish on a canoe trip."],
    ["Step focus", "Cerebral paragonimiasis produces no eggs in sputum, so serology plus imaging showing conglomerate ring lesions with hemorrhage carries the diagnosis."]],
  worm: [["Found in a tiger at a zoo", "In 1878 Coenraad Westerman, curator of the Amsterdam zoo, found flukes in the lungs of a Bengal tiger that had died there, and the parasite was named for him. Human infection was identified almost simultaneously in Taiwan and in Japan, and by the 1880s Japanese investigators had connected the human disease with raw crab consumption. Kenji Kobayashi and others worked out the full snail and crab cycle in the first decades of the twentieth century. The parasite therefore reached medicine through veterinary pathology, which is fitting for an organism whose natural hosts are large carnivores."],
    ["The great tuberculosis masquerade", "Chronic cough, hemoptysis, night sweats, weight loss and a cavitary upper lobe lesion describe both diseases, and in the regions where the fluke is endemic tuberculosis is common, so the wrong diagnosis is the default. Series from Korea, China and Japan found substantial numbers of patients carrying a tuberculosis diagnosis for months or years, taking four-drug therapy without improvement, before sputum was examined for eggs. Two findings break the tie: marked eosinophilia, which tuberculosis does not cause, and repeatedly negative acid-fast smears in someone who looks consistently unwell."],
    ["Crayfish in Missouri", "Paragonimus kellicotti is native to North America and cycles through crayfish in Midwestern rivers. Clusters of cases have been reported from Missouri among canoeists and campers who ate raw or undercooked crayfish on the water, often on a dare or as part of a drinking game. Patients presented with fever, eosinophilia, pleural effusion and chest pain and were investigated for lymphoma and tuberculosis before the exposure history emerged. It is a useful corrective to the assumption that lung flukes are an exclusively Asian diagnosis."],
    ["Epilepsy from a wandering larva", "Before the 1970s cerebral paragonimiasis was a recognized cause of childhood epilepsy and hemiplegia in parts of Korea and China, accounting for an appreciable share of seizure clinic attendance in some series. Larvae that fail to reach the lung migrate along the carotid sheath and jugular foramen into the cranium, where they bleed, tunnel and calcify, leaving conglomerate ring lesions and old hemorrhage on imaging decades later. As raw crab consumption declined with public health campaigns, so did the neurological disease, leaving a cohort of older adults with calcified lesions and long-standing epilepsy as the residue."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "opisthorchis-viverrini", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Opisthorchis viverrini", also: "Southeast Asian liver fluke, Mekong liver fluke, agent of opisthorchiasis",
  cls: "Digenean liver fluke, hermaphroditic, flat lancet-shaped adult 5 to 10 mm living in the intrahepatic bile ducts, egg small, operculate and yellow-brown with a shouldered operculum and a small abopercular knob, two intermediate hosts, a Bithynia snail and then a cyprinid freshwater fish",
  plain: "A small liver fluke from raw fermented river fish that lives in the bile ducts for decades and is a proven cause of bile duct cancer.",
  live: "Humans are the main host, with dogs, cats and fish-eating mammals as reservoirs of modest epidemiological weight relative to the enormous human burden. The first intermediate host is the small operculate snail Bithynia, which lives in shallow seasonally flooded wetlands, and the second is cyprinid fish of genera such as Puntius, Hampala and Cyclocheilichthys, the small scaly fish of ponds and paddy. The distribution is the Lower Mekong basin: northeastern Thailand, where prevalence in some provinces has exceeded a third of the adult population, Laos, Cambodia and central and southern Vietnam, with roughly ten million people infected. Opisthorchis felineus occupies a parallel niche in Siberia and eastern Europe.",
  spread: "Eating raw, undercooked, fermented or lightly salted freshwater cyprinid fish containing metacercariae, in dishes such as koi pla, a raw minced fish salad, and short-fermented preparations in which the fermentation period is too brief to kill the larvae. Eggs pass in feces and reach ponds and wetlands where sanitation is absent. The cycle is sustained by a culinary tradition of eating raw fish combined with open defecation into the same water bodies that supply the fish, and family and village customs mean the exposure begins in childhood and continues for life.",
  causes: "Light infections are silent. Moderate and heavy infections cause right upper quadrant and epigastric discomfort, flatulence, fatigue, anorexia, hepatomegaly and a sense of fullness after meals. Chronic infection produces cholangitis, cholelithiasis and intrahepatic duct stones, periductal fibrosis, biliary obstruction, recurrent pyogenic cholangitis, liver abscess and gallbladder dysfunction. The outstanding consequence is cholangiocarcinoma, cancer of the bile duct epithelium, for which this fluke is classified as a Group 1 human carcinogen, and northeastern Thailand has the highest recorded incidence of that cancer anywhere in the world. Cholangiocarcinoma presents late with painless jaundice, weight loss and a hepatic mass and is usually unresectable at diagnosis.",
  mech: "Metacercariae excyst in the duodenum, the juvenile fluke ascends the ampulla of Vater into the intrahepatic bile ducts, chiefly in the left lobe, matures in about a month and lives for ten to twenty years or more, feeding on biliary epithelium and secretions. Carcinogenesis is driven by three converging processes. Mechanical irritation from the fluke's suckers and movement causes chronic epithelial injury and compensatory hyperplasia. The fluke's excretory and secretory products, including a granulin-like growth factor and thioredoxin peroxidase, directly stimulate cholangiocyte proliferation and suppress apoptosis. And the chronic inflammatory infiltrate generates nitric oxide and reactive oxygen and nitrogen species that produce oxidative DNA damage and nitrosamine formation, which is amplified by dietary nitrosamines from the same fermented foods. The result is decades of proliferation under mutagenic pressure, with periductal fibrosis, adenomatous hyperplasia, dysplasia and ultimately adenocarcinoma, and the risk persists after the fluke itself is cleared.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy with formalin-ethyl acetate concentration or Kato-Katz shows the small operculate egg, 25 to 35 by 12 to 20 micrometres, which is essentially indistinguishable from the eggs of Clonorchis sinensis and the minute intestinal flukes such as Heterophyes and Metagonimus, so geography and molecular testing resolve the species. Egg counts correlate with worm burden and with cancer risk. Formalin-ethyl acetate concentration technique and the FLOTAC and mini-FLOTAC methods improve sensitivity at low intensity. Urine and serum antigen assays and polymerase chain reaction are used in research and in elimination-phase surveillance. Ultrasound shows periductal fibrosis as increased echogenicity around portal branches, gallbladder enlargement and sludge, and is used for population screening for advanced biliary disease. Eosinophilia is mild. Raised alkaline phosphatase and a rising CA 19-9 in a long-infected person raise concern for malignancy.",
  tx: "Praziquantel, as a single-day divided course, clears the fluke with cure rates above ninety percent. Established periductal fibrosis improves partially after treatment in younger patients with lighter disease but advanced fibrosis and dysplasia do not reverse, and the elevated cancer risk is reduced rather than abolished. Cholangiocarcinoma is managed surgically where resectable, with chemotherapy otherwise, and outcomes remain poor. Recurrent pyogenic cholangitis needs antibiotics and biliary drainage.",
  alt: "Albendazole over several days has moderate efficacy. Tribendimidine has shown efficacy comparable to praziquantel in trials in Laos and is an emerging option. Mebendazole has been used historically with inferior results.",
  res: "No established praziquantel resistance. The dominant problem is immediate reinfection: in a village where raw fish is eaten weekly, egg positivity returns within months of mass treatment, so repeated annual rounds have been given for decades in parts of Thailand without interrupting transmission. There is also a counterintuitive concern that treatment alone, by relieving symptoms while the dietary habit continues, may do little for cancer incidence, which is why current programmes pair treatment with behavior change.",
  prev: "Thorough cooking of freshwater fish, since freezing requirements are stringent and brief fermentation, salting, souring with lime and alcohol marinades do not kill metacercariae, together with latrine construction and use to keep eggs out of ponds, and treatment of dogs and cats. Mass praziquantel campaigns in northeastern Thailand from the 1980s reduced prevalence substantially but plateaued, and the current Thai Lawa model integrates treatment, intensive community and school education about koi pla, ecosystem and snail monitoring, and ultrasound screening for early biliary disease, achieving sustained reductions where single-component programmes failed. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Clonorchis sinensis, which causes the same disease with a nearly identical egg in China, Korea and Vietnam and is also a Group 1 carcinogen, and Opisthorchis felineus in Russia and Siberia. Fasciola hepatica, whose egg is much larger and whose disease involves parenchymal migration and a different drug. The minute intestinal flukes share egg morphology but not the biliary disease. Gallstone disease, primary sclerosing cholangitis, recurrent pyogenic cholangitis from other causes, and hepatocellular carcinoma are the clinical differentials in advanced disease.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Opisthorchis viverrini is a Group 1 carcinogen causing cholangiocarcinoma, is acquired from raw or fermented cyprinid freshwater fish, and is treated with praziquantel."],
    ["Memory trick", "Raw fish to bile duct to bile duct cancer. Opisthorchis and Clonorchis both make tiny operculate eggs and both cause cholangiocarcinoma. Fasciola comes from watercress and has a big egg."],
    ["Classic setting", "A man in his fifties from Khon Kaen or Udon Thani province in northeastern Thailand who has eaten koi pla since childhood, presenting with painless jaundice, weight loss and an unresectable hilar bile duct tumor."],
    ["Step focus", "The egg cannot be distinguished from Clonorchis or from the minute intestinal flukes by microscopy, so the species is assigned by geography and confirmed molecularly."]],
  worm: [["A fluke named from a civet", "The parasite was described in 1911 by Poirier from a Malayan civet of the genus Viverra, which supplied the species name, and human infection in Thailand was recognized in the 1950s and 1960s as surveys in the northeast began counting eggs in stool and found a third or more of villagers positive. Chamlong Harinasuta and colleagues at Mahidol University established both the scale of infection and its link to biliary disease, and the Thai northeastern provinces became the most intensively studied parasite focus in Southeast Asia."],
    ["The highest rate of a cancer anywhere", "Khon Kaen province in northeastern Thailand records the highest incidence of cholangiocarcinoma in the world, by a wide margin, in both men and women. The epidemiological case against the fluke was built from the geographic overlap, the dose-response relationship between egg counts and cancer risk, hamster experiments in which fluke infection plus dietary nitrosamine produced bile duct tumors while either alone produced few, and a plausible molecular mechanism. The International Agency for Research on Cancer classified it as a definite human carcinogen in 1994 and reaffirmed that in 2011."],
    ["A worm that secretes a growth factor", "Among the proteins the fluke releases into bile is a granulin-like molecule that binds cholangiocyte receptors and drives proliferation and wound healing, which serves the parasite by repairing the epithelium it feeds on. In cell and animal models that single protein induces proliferative changes resembling early neoplasia, and the fluke also secretes thioredoxin peroxidase that suppresses host cell apoptosis. The parasite is therefore not merely an irritant: it actively instructs the tissue to divide and not to die, over a period of decades, which is a close approximation of a tumor promoter."],
    ["Koi pla and the limits of mass treatment", "Thailand distributed praziquantel on a very large scale from the 1980s and watched prevalence fall and then stop falling, because treated villagers ate raw fish salad again the following week. Cholangiocarcinoma rates did not drop in proportion. The Lawa Lake model, developed from the late 2000s, added intensive community engagement, school curricula, cooking demonstrations, snail and fish monitoring and liver ultrasound screening, and produced reductions that held. The lesson repeatedly drawn is that a food-borne parasite sustained by a valued traditional dish is a cultural problem that a drug can manage but not solve."]] },

{ id: "fasciolopsis-buski", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Fasciolopsis buski", also: "giant intestinal fluke, agent of fasciolopsiasis",
  cls: "Digenean intestinal fluke and the largest fluke of humans, hermaphroditic, fleshy oval adult 20 to 75 mm long and up to 20 mm wide attached to the duodenal and jejunal mucosa, egg large, operculate and yellow-brown and almost identical to that of Fasciola hepatica, intermediate host a planorbid snail with metacercariae encysted on aquatic plants",
  plain: "The largest fluke to infect people, swallowed on the skin of water chestnuts and other raw aquatic plants, which clamps onto the small bowel and causes diarrhea and swelling.",
  live: "Humans and pigs, with pigs the major animal reservoir in the areas where they are fed aquatic plants or allowed to forage in ponds, and dogs and rabbits occasionally infected. The snail intermediate hosts are planorbid snails of the genera Segmentina and Hippeutis in ponds, ditches and flooded fields. The distribution is East, South and Southeast Asia: central and southern China, Taiwan, Thailand, Laos, Vietnam, Cambodia, Bangladesh, India, Assam and Bihar in particular, Nepal, Myanmar, Indonesia and Malaysia, with an estimated ten million or more people infected, heavily skewed toward children.",
  spread: "Swallowing metacercariae encysted on the surface of raw freshwater plants, classically water caltrop and water chestnut, whose hulls are peeled with the teeth, and also water bamboo, lotus root, water spinach, water hyacinth and watercress. The habit of peeling the nut shell with the teeth and spitting it out delivers the metacercariae directly. Drinking untreated pond water containing free metacercariae also transmits. Pig feces and human feces used as pond fertilizer, a long-standing practice in parts of China and India, seed the cycle.",
  causes: "Light infection is asymptomatic. Moderate burdens cause epigastric and abdominal pain, often two hours after eating, nausea, foul diarrhea alternating with constipation, excessive appetite, flatulence and the passage of undigested food. Heavy burdens, which in children can reach hundreds or thousands of worms, cause a serious syndrome: profuse diarrhea, malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy, hypoalbuminemia with facial and lower limb edema and ascites, anemia, marked abdominal distension, growth retardation, intestinal obstruction and acute ileus. Toxic and allergic manifestations including urticaria and generalized edema are attributed to absorbed worm metabolites. Fatal outcomes in heavily infected malnourished children are recorded, usually from intestinal obstruction or cachexia.",
  mech: "Metacercariae excyst in the duodenum and the young fluke attaches to the mucosa with oral and ventral suckers, maturing in about three months and living roughly a year. Pathology is produced in three ways. Mechanical attachment and the sheer size of the worms cause mucosal ulceration, erosion, hemorrhage and abscess formation at each attachment site, and a heavy burden physically coats the proximal small bowel, occupying absorptive surface and occasionally obstructing the lumen or the ileocecal valve. Chronic inflammation with mucus hypersecretion and villous damage impairs absorption, producing a malabsorptive diarrhea and protein loss. And absorbed worm metabolites appear to drive the systemic allergic and edematous features, which resolve rapidly after treatment in a way that nutritional repletion alone would not explain. There is no tissue migration phase and no hepatic invasion, which separates this fluke sharply from Fasciola.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows a large operculate egg, 130 to 150 by 60 to 90 micrometres, which is practically indistinguishable from that of Fasciola hepatica and also resembles Echinostoma and Gastrodiscoides eggs, so the differentiation rests on clinical picture, exposure and, decisively, on recovering an adult worm after treatment, which is unmistakable given its size. Eggs are abundant in moderate and heavy infections, since each worm sheds many thousands daily, so a single direct smear usually suffices. Formalin-ethyl acetate concentration is used in light infection. Molecular methods distinguish the species where available. Eosinophilia is usually present and may be marked, with hypoalbuminemia, anemia and megaloblastic features from malabsorption in severe cases.",
  tx: "Praziquantel as a single-day divided course is highly effective and is the treatment of choice, and expelled worms are often passed visibly. Nutritional rehabilitation, correction of anemia and management of hypoalbuminemia accompany treatment in heavily infected malnourished children, and the edema resolves over days to weeks. Intestinal obstruction may need surgical attention.",
  alt: "Triclabendazole, which is the drug for Fasciola, also has activity here. Niclosamide has been used. Albendazole and mebendazole have limited efficacy. Where the species cannot be distinguished from Fasciola on egg morphology, the choice between praziquantel and triclabendazole matters, since Fasciola responds poorly to praziquantel.",
  res: "No resistance is described. Recurrence reflects continued consumption of raw aquatic plants from the same contaminated ponds, and in hyperendemic villages in Assam and central China seasonal reinfection after the harvest of water caltrop has been documented repeatedly.",
  prev: "Peeling and cooking or scalding aquatic plants rather than peeling them with the teeth, boiling or treating pond water, and keeping human and pig feces out of ponds used to grow aquatic vegetables, which addresses the practice of night soil fertilization at the root of transmission. Confining pigs and treating them reduces the reservoir. Mass praziquantel treatment in school-age children in hyperendemic areas reduces burden and the severe childhood syndrome. Immersion of plants in boiling water for a short period destroys metacercariae, and drying does as well. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Fasciola hepatica, whose egg is nearly identical but whose disease is hepatobiliary with a migratory phase and which responds to triclabendazole rather than praziquantel, making the distinction practically important. Echinostoma and Gastrodiscoides hominis, other intestinal flukes of the region with similar eggs. Giardiasis, tropical sprue, celiac disease and other causes of malabsorptive diarrhea with edema. Protein-energy malnutrition with kwashiorkor, which the heavy childhood infection closely imitates and often accompanies.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Fasciolopsis buski is the largest human fluke, is acquired by eating raw aquatic plants such as water chestnut and water caltrop, stays in the intestine without liver involvement, and is treated with praziquantel."],
    ["Memory trick", "Buski is big and in the bowel; Fasciola is in the liver. Both have the same big operculate egg, so the plant and the organ tell them apart. Water chestnut peeled with the teeth."],
    ["Classic setting", "A child in Assam or central China with abdominal distension, chronic foul diarrhea, facial and leg edema and a low albumin, during the season when water caltrop is harvested and eaten raw."],
    ["Step focus", "The egg is essentially identical to that of Fasciola hepatica, so the differentiation is clinical, and it matters because Fasciola requires triclabendazole while this fluke responds to praziquantel."]],
  worm: [["Found at an autopsy in London", "George Busk, a British naval surgeon and anatomist, found several large flukes in the duodenum of a Lascar sailor who died in London in 1843, and described them without knowing what they were. The genus and species names commemorate him. The life cycle through planorbid snails and encystment on aquatic plants was worked out by Nakagawa in Taiwan and by Barlow in China in the 1920s, with Claude Barlow famously infecting himself in order to study the course of the disease and the output of eggs over time."],
    ["Water caltrop and the teeth", "Transmission hinges on an everyday action. Water caltrop and water chestnut are harvested from ponds, sold fresh, and the tough hull is traditionally split with the incisors and spat out before the kernel is eaten. Metacercariae encyst on exactly that surface. The nut itself is harmless; the peeling is the exposure. Public health messaging in Chinese and Indian endemic areas therefore focused less on avoiding the food than on changing how it is opened, which is an unusually narrow and achievable behavioral target."],
    ["A fluke that produces kwashiorkor", "A child with hundreds of large flukes carpeting the duodenum loses protein through ulcerated mucosa, absorbs poorly across damaged villi and develops hypoalbuminemia with facial puffiness, pitting edema and ascites, a picture clinically indistinguishable from protein-energy malnutrition. In Chinese and Indian series such children were often treated for malnutrition alone. The distinguishing observation is that a single course of an anthelmintic produces rapid resolution of the edema, which nutritional therapy alone does not, and that detail has rescued a good many diagnoses."],
    ["Night soil, ponds and vegetables in one system", "In integrated pond agriculture, human and pig excreta fertilize water in which aquatic vegetables are grown and fish are raised, an efficient and ancient closed loop that also happens to complete this fluke's life cycle. Eggs enter the water, snails amplify, metacercariae settle on the crop, and the crop is eaten raw. Control therefore collided with a productive farming system rather than with poor hygiene as such, and the decline of fasciolopsiasis in much of China followed changes in fertilizer practice and sanitation more than any drug campaign."]] },

{ id: "heterophyes-heterophyes", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Heterophyes heterophyes", also: "Von Siebold fluke, minute intestinal fluke, agent of heterophyiasis",
  cls: "Digenean minute intestinal fluke, hermaphroditic, pear-shaped adult only 1 to 1.7 mm long with a ventrogenital sucker beside the ventral sucker, burrowing between villi of the small intestine, egg tiny and operculate and very like that of Clonorchis, two intermediate hosts, a brackish-water Pirenella snail and then a mullet or tilapia",
  plain: "A fluke barely a millimetre long, from raw or salted brackish-water fish, whose eggs can slip into the bloodstream and lodge in the heart or brain.",
  live: "Humans, dogs, cats, foxes and fish-eating birds, with the animal reservoir substantial. The first intermediate host is the brackish-water snail Pirenella conica, and the second is euryhaline fish, chiefly mullet of the genus Mugil and also tilapia, which tolerate the brackish lagoons where the snail lives. The distribution follows those lagoons: the Nile delta and Egyptian coastal lakes, where it is classically described, Israel, Turkey, Tunisia, Sudan, Iran, and foci in Korea, Japan, China, Taiwan and the Philippines, with related heterophyid species widely distributed.",
  spread: "Eating raw, lightly salted, pickled or sun-dried brackish-water fish containing metacercariae, with the Egyptian preparations of salted mullet known as fesikh and sardine or mullet cured for short periods being the classic vehicles, since light salting does not kill the larvae. Eggs pass in the feces of humans, dogs, cats and birds into the lagoons. Because fish-eating birds are effective hosts, the cycle persists independently of human infection, which limits what human treatment can achieve.",
  causes: "Light infection is asymptomatic. Moderate to heavy infection causes colicky abdominal pain, mucous diarrhea, nausea, excessive appetite or anorexia and mild weight loss, with a malabsorptive component when hundreds or thousands of worms are present, since the fluke burrows between the villi and causes mucosal inflammation and shallow ulceration. The distinctive and serious manifestation is ectopic egg embolization: eggs released by worms deeply embedded in the mucosa enter mesenteric lymphatics and venules and are carried to the heart, where they lodge in the myocardium and valves and provoke granulomatous myocarditis, conduction disturbance and heart failure, and to the brain and spinal cord, where they cause focal neurological deficits and seizures. This was a recognized cause of cardiac disease in the Philippines and in parts of the eastern Mediterranean.",
  mech: "Metacercariae excyst in the small intestine and the minute adult attaches and burrows into the intervillous space and sometimes into the lamina propria, feeding on mucus and epithelial debris, maturing within about two weeks and living some months. Local pathology consists of villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, mucosal inflammation with eosinophils and plasma cells, shallow ulceration and increased mucus, which accounts for the diarrhea and the malabsorption in heavy burdens. The mechanism that distinguishes the heterophyids is anatomical: because the worm sits deep in the mucosa rather than in the lumen, a proportion of eggs is deposited into tissue rather than into the bowel contents, and those eggs reach lymphatics and venules and embolize. In heart muscle and valve tissue each lodged egg generates a granuloma with surrounding fibrosis, and enough of them produce myocarditis and valvular thickening, while in the central nervous system they produce focal granulomas, hemorrhage and seizures. The parasite is small and the disease modest, but the embolic route makes an otherwise trivial intestinal infection occasionally lethal.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows a tiny operculate egg, 25 to 30 by 15 to 17 micrometres, essentially indistinguishable from the eggs of Metagonimus, Clonorchis and Opisthorchis, which is the central diagnostic difficulty, since the clinical consequences of the biliary flukes and the minute intestinal flukes differ. Recovering an adult worm after treatment, by purgation and sieving of stool, permits definitive identification on the basis of the ventrogenital sucker. Egg output per worm is low, so concentration techniques and repeated samples are needed in light infection. Polymerase chain reaction and sequencing are the reliable modern discriminators. Eosinophilia is usually mild. In suspected ectopic disease, echocardiography, cardiac imaging and endomyocardial or cerebral histology showing operculate eggs in granulomas make the diagnosis, often retrospectively.",
  tx: "Praziquantel as a single dose or a single-day divided course clears intestinal infection reliably. Treatment does not remove eggs already embolized to the heart or brain, so established cardiac or neurological disease is managed supportively, with heart failure therapy, antiseizure medication and corticosteroids for inflammation as clinically appropriate.",
  alt: "Niclosamide has been used. Tribendimidine has activity against intestinal flukes. Albendazole and mebendazole are unreliable here.",
  res: "No resistance is described. Reinfection after treatment is common wherever salted or raw mullet remains part of the diet, and the avian and canine reservoirs mean the environmental egg load is not reduced by treating people.",
  prev: "Thorough cooking of brackish-water fish, since light salting, pickling, drying and marinating do not reliably kill metacercariae, and adequate freezing or prolonged proper salting where raw or cured preparations are traditional. Sanitation reduces the human contribution to lagoon contamination, although birds and dogs sustain the cycle. Health education aimed at the specific cured fish preparations is the practical intervention, and outbreaks in Egypt have repeatedly followed the festival consumption of fesikh. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Metagonimus yokogawai, which is clinically and microscopically almost identical and is distinguished by the position of the ventral sucker and by geography, and the other heterophyids including Haplorchis species. Clonorchis sinensis and Opisthorchis viverrini, whose eggs look the same but which cause biliary disease and bile duct cancer, which is the distinction that matters most clinically. Giardia, other causes of chronic mucous diarrhea, and in the ectopic form, viral myocarditis, rheumatic valve disease, eosinophilic endomyocardial disease and neurocysticercosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Heterophyes heterophyes is a minute intestinal fluke from raw or salted brackish-water fish whose eggs can embolize to the heart and brain and cause granulomatous myocarditis and neurological disease."],
    ["Memory trick", "Heterophyes is heterogeneous: a tiny bowel fluke with an extra genital sucker whose eggs wander to the heart. Salted mullet from the Nile delta."],
    ["Classic setting", "A patient in the Nile delta with mucous diarrhea and abdominal pain weeks after a festival meal of fesikh, salted mullet, with tiny operculate eggs in stool."],
    ["Step focus", "The egg cannot be distinguished from those of Clonorchis, Opisthorchis or Metagonimus, so an adult worm recovered after treatment or a molecular test is required for species identification."]],
  worm: [["Described from a boy in Cairo", "Theodor von Siebold found the fluke in 1852 in the intestine of a boy who died in Cairo, and it was named heterophyes, meaning of different nature, for the extra genital sucker that set it apart from other small flukes. The Nile delta, with its brackish coastal lakes, the Pirenella snail and a long tradition of eating salted mullet, turned out to be an almost perfect setting for it, and Egyptian parasitology has followed the organism ever since, with surveys in delta villages finding prevalence in the tens of percent at some periods."],
    ["Eggs in the heart", "In the Philippines in the 1960s, investigators examining hearts at autopsy in patients who had died of unexplained heart failure found operculate fluke eggs embedded in granulomas in the myocardium and valves, in people who had eaten raw brackish-water fish. The parasite responsible was a heterophyid. The finding explained a proportion of otherwise idiopathic cardiac disease in those communities and established the principle that a fluke of trivial intestinal consequence can be lethal by the route its eggs take rather than by anything the adult worm does."],
    ["Fesikh and the festival", "Egyptian cases cluster around Sham el-Nessim, the spring festival at which salted fish is traditionally eaten, and outbreaks of both heterophyiasis and of botulism from inadequately cured fesikh have been reported following it. The curing process, when properly prolonged, is adequate; the problem is short-cut commercial preparation. Public health efforts have focused on the curing time and on the supply chain rather than on the custom itself, which is centuries old and not negotiable."],
    ["The tyranny of a small operculate egg", "Heterophyes, Metagonimus, Haplorchis, Clonorchis and Opisthorchis all produce an operculate egg between twenty-five and thirty-five micrometres long, and a microscopist looking at stool cannot tell them apart. Yet one group lives in the bile ducts and causes cholangiocarcinoma while the other lives in the bowel and occasionally embolizes to the heart. Epidemiological surveys across Asia that reported small operculate eggs as Clonorchis for decades are now understood to have conflated several species, and molecular surveys have substantially redrawn the maps."]] },

{ id: "metagonimus-yokogawai", k: "para", g: "Trematodes (flukes)", nm: "Metagonimus yokogawai", also: "Yokogawa fluke, smallest human fluke, minute intestinal fluke, agent of metagonimiasis",
  cls: "Digenean minute intestinal fluke and the smallest fluke infecting humans, hermaphroditic, pear-shaped adult 1 to 2.5 mm long with the ventral sucker displaced to the right of the midline, living between the villi of the jejunum, egg tiny and operculate, two intermediate hosts, a Semisulcospira snail and then a sweetfish or other freshwater fish",
  plain: "The smallest fluke that infects people, caught from raw sweetfish or trout, which lodges between the intestinal villi and causes watery diarrhea.",
  live: "Humans, dogs, cats, pigs and fish-eating birds including pelicans and gulls, with the animal reservoir important. The first intermediate host is the freshwater snail Semisulcospira, the same genus that transmits the lung fluke, and the second is freshwater fish, principally the sweetfish Plecoglossus altivelis, known in Japan as ayu, along with dace, trout, carp and perch. The distribution is East Asia and beyond: Japan, Korea, Taiwan, China, far eastern Russia and Siberia, with foci in the Balkans, Spain and Israel, and related Metagonimus species recognized across the region.",
  spread: "Eating raw or undercooked freshwater fish containing metacercariae encysted in the scales, fins, gills and muscle, in dishes of raw sliced sweetfish and in salted or pickled preparations that do not kill the larvae. The habit of eating whole small fish raw, and of handling fish and then food, delivers the infection. Eggs pass in the feces of humans and of fish-eating mammals and birds into rivers and streams, and the avian reservoir sustains the cycle independently of human infection.",
  causes: "Light infections, which are the majority, are asymptomatic. Moderate and heavy burdens cause intermittent watery or mucous diarrhea, colicky epigastric and periumbilical pain, nausea, lassitude, anorexia and mild weight loss, with symptoms often persisting for weeks and recurring. Malabsorption appears with very heavy burdens of thousands of worms, since the flukes occupy the intervillous spaces of the upper small bowel and cause villous atrophy. Ectopic egg embolization to heart and brain, well recognized for the related heterophyids, is reported for this species as well but is distinctly uncommon. The infection is generally mild and self-limited, and its clinical importance lies substantially in being mistaken for other causes of chronic diarrhea and in the misidentification of its eggs as those of the carcinogenic biliary flukes.",
  mech: "Metacercariae excyst in the small intestine and the minute adult burrows into the intervillous space and sometimes into the mucosa of the jejunum, feeding on mucus and cellular debris, maturing in about two weeks and surviving some months to a year. The resulting pathology is local and mechanical and inflammatory: blunted and fused villi, crypt elongation, mucosal infiltration with eosinophils, lymphocytes and plasma cells, increased mucus secretion, superficial erosion and, where the burden is very heavy, a measurable reduction in absorptive surface producing a malabsorptive diarrhea. Because the worm sits within the mucosa, a small proportion of eggs can enter lymphatics and venules and embolize, which is the mechanism of the rare cardiac and cerebral disease, although this happens far less often than with Heterophyes. There is no tissue migration, no biliary invasion and no systemic toxic phase, so the clinical picture stays confined to the gut in nearly every case.",
  lab: "Stool microscopy shows a tiny operculate egg, 26 to 32 by 15 to 17 micrometres, which is practically identical to the eggs of Heterophyes, Clonorchis and Opisthorchis, and this is the recurring diagnostic problem. Egg output per worm is low, so formalin-ethyl acetate concentration and repeated specimens improve detection. Definitive identification rests on recovering adult worms after purgation following treatment, where the ventral sucker displaced to the right of the midline, and the shape and position of the seminal receptacle, separate this genus from Heterophyes, which has a ventrogenital sucker. Molecular identification and sequencing resolve the species and have revealed a complex of several Metagonimus species in Korea and Japan previously reported as one. Eosinophilia is mild or absent and routine blood tests are typically unremarkable.",
  tx: "Praziquantel as a single dose or single-day divided course is highly effective and is the standard treatment, with cure confirmed by negative stool examination some weeks later. Symptoms resolve promptly, and heavy infections with malabsorption recover as the mucosa regenerates.",
  alt: "Niclosamide and tribendimidine have been used. Albendazole and mebendazole are not reliable. Because the egg is indistinguishable from those of the biliary flukes, which also respond to praziquantel, the same drug covers the diagnostic uncertainty, which is a practical convenience.",
  res: "No resistance is reported. Reinfection follows continued raw fish consumption, and in Korean and Japanese riverside communities where sweetfish is a seasonal delicacy, repeated infection year after year is the norm rather than treatment failure.",
  prev: "Thorough cooking of freshwater fish, which is the only reliable measure, since pickling, salting and vinegar marinades do not kill metacercariae, together with adequate freezing where raw preparations are traditional. Sanitation reduces the human egg contribution, although the bird and mammal reservoirs sustain transmission. Health education targeted at the specific dishes, particularly raw sweetfish in season, is the practical intervention. There is no vaccine and no prophylactic drug.",
  look: "Heterophyes heterophyes and the other heterophyid flukes, from which it is distinguished by the position of the ventral sucker and by the fish and snail hosts. Clonorchis sinensis and Opisthorchis viverrini, which share the egg appearance but cause biliary disease and bile duct cancer, making the distinction clinically consequential. Giardia, Cyclospora, irritable bowel syndrome, lactose intolerance and other causes of chronic watery diarrhea with mild symptoms, which is the differential most patients are investigated for before a stool examination finds eggs.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Metagonimus yokogawai is the smallest fluke infecting humans, comes from raw freshwater fish such as sweetfish, and is identified by the ventral sucker displaced to the right of the midline."],
    ["Memory trick", "Metagonimus is the mini one with a sucker off to the side. Heterophyes has the extra ventrogenital sucker. Both are tiny bowel flukes from raw fish with identical eggs."],
    ["Classic setting", "A Japanese or Korean patient with weeks of intermittent watery diarrhea and abdominal cramps after eating raw sweetfish in the summer season, with tiny operculate eggs in stool."],
    ["Step focus", "Eggs of the minute intestinal flukes and of the biliary flukes are indistinguishable by microscopy, so adult worm recovery after treatment or molecular testing assigns the species."]],
  worm: [["Named for the man who found it in a snail", "Sadamu Yokogawa, working in Taiwan in 1911 and 1912, found the fluke and then worked out that the metacercariae encysted in the scales and tissues of the sweetfish and that the first host was a Semisulcospira snail. The organism carries his name, and his work established the pattern for Japanese and Taiwanese food-borne trematode research in the following decades, in which the fish, the snail and the dish were traced together rather than separately."],
    ["The smallest fluke, and the problem of scale", "At one to two and a half millimetres, an adult Metagonimus is smaller than a sesame seed and is easily lost in stool sediment or overlooked at autopsy. The entire clinical and taxonomic literature therefore depended on purgation techniques and careful sieving to recover intact worms, a laborious procedure that few laboratories perform now. Species identification has consequently shifted almost entirely to molecular methods, and with it has come the recognition that what was reported as a single species for a century is a complex of several."],
    ["Sweetfish in season", "Ayu, the sweetfish, is a prized seasonal delicacy in Japan, eaten grilled but also sliced raw, and infection rates in riverside communities rose and fell with the fishing season. Surveys in the mid-twentieth century found high prevalence along particular rivers, and campaigns focused specifically on the raw preparation rather than on the fish. Prevalence fell markedly with changes in diet and with improved sanitation, and metagonimiasis is now largely a sporadic diagnosis in Japan and Korea rather than a community problem."],
    ["An egg that redrew epidemiological maps", "For decades, surveys across East and Southeast Asia reported small operculate eggs in stool as Clonorchis sinensis, because Clonorchis was the known important fluke of the region and the eggs look the same. Molecular surveys from the 1990s onward demonstrated that a substantial proportion of those positives were in fact minute intestinal flukes, Metagonimus, Heterophyes or Haplorchis species, with entirely different clinical implications. The correction mattered because Clonorchis carries a bile duct cancer risk and the intestinal flukes do not, so population cancer risk estimates built on microscopy alone required revision."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "cimex-lectularius", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Cimex lectularius", also: "bed bug, common bedbug, Cimex hemipterus for the tropical bedbug, cimicosis",
  cls: "Wingless hematophagous insect of the family Cimicidae, adult reddish-brown and flattened, about 4 to 7 mm and apple-seed shaped, five nymphal instars each requiring a blood meal, a temporary ectoparasite that feeds on the host and lives off it in cracks and furniture",
  plain: "A flat, wingless, blood-feeding insect that hides in mattress seams by day, bites in rows at night, and causes itching and sleeplessness rather than disease.",
  live: "Humans are the preferred host of Cimex lectularius, with bats and poultry as ancestral and occasional alternatives, and Cimex hemipterus occupying tropical regions. The insect itself lives not on the host but in the environment: mattress seams and tufts, bed frames and headboards, box springs, upholstered furniture, skirting boards, behind wallpaper and picture frames, and inside electrical fittings. It survives many months without feeding at room temperature and longer when cool, which is why vacant rooms remain infested. Distribution is global and urban, with a marked worldwide resurgence since the late 1990s.",
  spread: "Passive transport. The insects and their eggs travel in luggage, clothing, second-hand furniture, mattresses, books and bedding, and move between adjoining apartments through wall voids, conduits and shared corridors. There is no person-to-person transmission in the infectious sense and no association with cleanliness, since the insect needs only a host and a crack to hide in. Hotels, hostels, shelters, trains, cinemas, dormitories and residential care facilities are the classic amplification settings, and the global increase in travel is a major driver of the resurgence.",
  causes: "Pruritic bites, typically appearing on exposed skin as erythematous papules, wheals or small vesicles with a central punctum, classically arranged in a line or cluster of three, which is often described as breakfast, lunch and dinner, and reflecting several probing attempts rather than several insects. The reaction depends on sensitization, so first exposures may produce nothing while repeated exposures produce increasingly large and delayed wheals, and a minority develop bullous lesions or a diffuse urticarial reaction. Secondary bacterial infection from scratching, including impetigo and cellulitis, is the commonest complication. Heavy long-standing infestation has caused iron deficiency anemia in infants and in debilitated adults. The dominant morbidity is psychological: insomnia, anxiety, hypervigilance, social withdrawal, stigma and, in some reports, symptoms meeting criteria for post-traumatic stress, together with the financial cost of eradication and discarded furniture. Anaphylaxis to bed bug bites is reported but rare.",
  mech: "The insect emerges at night, is drawn by carbon dioxide, warmth and host odour, and feeds for three to ten minutes using a stylet bundle that cuts the skin and probes for a capillary, injecting saliva containing anticoagulants, a nitrophorin vasodilator, platelet aggregation inhibitors including an apyrase, and proteins with anesthetic effect that keep the host from waking. The entire clinical picture is an immune response to those salivary proteins: an immediate type I wheal in sensitized people and a delayed type IV papular reaction, which is why the lesions appear hours to days after the bite and why the interval shortens with repeated exposure. The insect carries numerous pathogens in its gut, and more than forty have been detected, yet it has never been established as a competent biological vector of any human infection in natural conditions, with the partial and debated exception of experimental Trypanosoma cruzi transmission. The reason appears to be a combination of feeding behavior, the absence of defecation during feeding and gut barriers to pathogen development.",
  lab: "The diagnosis rests on finding the insect, its exuviae, eggs or dark fecal spotting, since bite morphology is nonspecific and no laboratory test confirms a bed bug reaction. Inspection of mattress seams, bed frame joints, headboard crevices and nearby furniture with a torch, sometimes with canine scent detection or interceptor traps under bed legs, establishes the infestation. The insect is identified morphologically: flattened, wingless, with vestigial wing pads, a broad pronotum and, in Cimex lectularius, a wider body than the tropical species. Skin biopsy, when performed for atypical lesions, shows a superficial and deep perivascular lymphocytic and eosinophilic infiltrate with dermal edema and sometimes eosinophilic spongiosis, which is suggestive but not specific. Full blood count may show iron deficiency in severe prolonged infestation.",
  tx: "Treatment of the person is symptomatic, since the insect is not on the body: topical corticosteroids and oral antihistamines for pruritus, emollients, and antibiotics for secondary bacterial infection. Eradication is directed at the environment and is the definitive intervention, combining physical measures such as high-temperature laundering, tumble drying, steam, whole-room heat treatment, freezing, vacuuming, mattress encasement and disposal of infested items, with professional insecticide application using combinations of pyrethroids, neonicotinoids, desiccant dusts such as silica gel and diatomaceous earth, and insect growth regulators. Treatment of adjoining dwellings is often necessary, and documentation and explanation substantially reduce the distress.",
  alt: "Chlorfenapyr and the fungal biopesticide Beauveria bassiana have been used where pyrethroid resistance is high. Oral ivermectin taken by the host has shown some insecticidal effect on feeding bugs in small studies and is not an established control measure. Cognitive behavioral approaches and reassurance address the psychological burden, which is often the most disabling element.",
  res: "Insecticide resistance is the central reason for the global resurgence and is extensive. Pyrethroid resistance is widespread and mediated by knockdown resistance mutations in the voltage-gated sodium channel, upregulated cytochrome P450 and esterase detoxification, and thickened cuticle reducing penetration. Resistance to carbamates, organophosphates and neonicotinoids is documented, and cross-resistance is common. The withdrawal of DDT and of organochlorines, both for toxicity and because of resistance, coincided with the resurgence, and current practice relies on rotating modes of action and on non-chemical measures precisely because no single insecticide holds.",
  prev: "Inspection of mattress seams and headboards on arrival in accommodation, keeping luggage off beds and floors, laundering and hot-drying clothing after travel, caution with second-hand furniture and mattresses, use of mattress encasements and bed-leg interceptors, and prompt reporting in multi-unit housing so that adjoining dwellings are treated together. Heat is the most reliable kill method available to a household. There is no vaccine, no repellent of established efficacy against this insect and no chemoprophylaxis, and the measures are entirely environmental.",
  look: "Flea bites, which cluster on the lower legs and ankles, mosquito bites, which are random and solitary, scabies, which itches worst at night and burrows in the finger webs with lesions at characteristic sites, papular urticaria, dermatitis herpetiformis, bullous pemphigoid, drug eruption, folliculitis, and delusional parasitosis, which presents with itch, specimens and no insects. Bird mite and rodent mite bites from nests in roof spaces produce a near-identical eruption and are a classic missed cause.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Bed bugs cause pruritic bites, often in a linear group of three, and intense psychological distress, but they have never been shown to transmit any human pathogen in natural conditions."],
    ["Memory trick", "Breakfast, lunch and dinner in a row. They live in the bed, not on the person, so treat the room and not the patient. Apple seed shape, wingless, nocturnal."],
    ["Classic setting", "A traveller or apartment dweller with weeks of itchy papules in rows on the arms, neck and shoulders appearing overnight, sleeping poorly, with dark spotting found along the mattress seam."],
    ["Step focus", "The reaction is an immune response to salivary proteins, so first exposure may be silent and later bites produce larger, faster wheals, which explains why one member of a household is covered and another has nothing."]],
  worm: [["Out of the caves with us", "Cimex lectularius appears to have begun as a parasite of bats, and genetic work suggests the lineage that feeds on humans diverged when people shared caves with bats in the Middle East, then followed them into dwellings. Bed bugs are mentioned in classical Greek and Roman texts, recommended variously as medicine and complained of as a plague, and they were nearly universal in European and North American housing by the nineteenth century. Infestation rates in cities before the Second World War were extraordinary, with surveys in British cities finding a third or more of homes affected."],
    ["Eliminated by DDT, returned without it", "After 1945, DDT and other organochlorines all but eradicated bed bugs from high-income countries within a decade, so thoroughly that two generations of physicians never saw a case and the insect disappeared from teaching. From the late 1990s it returned, rapidly and globally, driven by insecticide resistance, the shift to targeted baits for cockroaches and ants that no longer incidentally killed bed bugs, increased international travel and the second-hand furniture trade. The resurgence is a standard case study in how pest control success can be undone by the very chemistry that produced it."],
    ["Traumatic insemination", "Bed bug reproduction is violent and consequential. The male does not use the female's genital tract but pierces her abdominal wall with a sharpened paramere and injects sperm directly into the body cavity, a process called traumatic insemination that wounds the female, shortens her life and has driven the evolution of a specialized organ, the spermalege, that limits the damage. The behavior matters practically: a single mated female transported in luggage can found an infestation, and repeated harassment of females drives dispersal within a building."],
    ["The harm that is not infectious", "More than forty human pathogens have been detected in bed bugs, from hepatitis B DNA to Trypanosoma cruzi, and none has been shown to be transmitted by them in natural settings. Yet studies of affected residents in shelters and public housing document rates of insomnia, anxiety and depressive symptoms comparable to other serious housing stressors, with social isolation, discarded possessions and financial loss. It is a useful counterexample to the assumption that an arthropod matters chiefly as a vector; this one causes substantial measurable morbidity while transmitting nothing."]] },

{ id: "tunga-penetrans", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Tunga penetrans", also: "chigoe flea, sand flea, jigger, bicho de pe, pique, agent of tungiasis",
  cls: "Hematophagous flea of the family Tungidae, the smallest known flea at about 1 mm, in which the fertilized female burrows permanently into the epidermis and hypertrophies to the size of a pea, a permanent endoparasitic ectoparasite, unlike the free-living male",
  plain: "A tiny sand flea whose pregnant female burrows into the skin of the foot, swells to the size of a pea, and leaves a painful nodule with a black dot at its centre.",
  live: "A wide range of mammals serve as hosts, including humans, dogs, cats, pigs, rats, goats and cattle, with dogs, pigs and rats sustaining transmission in affected communities. The flea develops in warm, dry, shaded sandy soil, so it lives in the dirt floors and surroundings of houses, in sandy yards, animal pens, beaches and unpaved streets. Originally South American, it spread to sub-Saharan Africa in the nineteenth century and is now endemic across Latin America, the Caribbean, sub-Saharan Africa, Madagascar, India and Pakistan, with prevalence in affected communities frequently above twenty percent and in some surveys exceeding half of children and elderly residents.",
  spread: "Contact between bare skin and infested soil or sand, which is why the feet account for the great majority of lesions, with the periungual skin of the toes, the soles, heels and interdigital spaces most affected, and hands, elbows, buttocks and perineum involved in people who sit or crawl on the ground. The flea does not move between people. Transmission is intensely linked to going barefoot, earthen floors, and the presence of dogs and pigs in the household compound, and it is markedly seasonal, peaking in the dry season when soil conditions favour larval development.",
  causes: "Tungiasis. The embedded female produces a white papule with a central black dot, the exposed posterior part of the flea, which enlarges over one to two weeks into a painful pruritic nodule up to a centimetre across with a surrounding ring of hyperkeratosis, often described as producing an intense itching and a sensation of pressure or burning. Clusters and plaques of dozens or hundreds of lesions occur in heavy infestation, with fissuring, deformity of the nail and periungual tissue, loss of nails, difficulty walking, and in severe chronic cases permanent deformity, autoamputation of digits and disabling impairment of gait. Bacterial superinfection is the main danger: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes and a range of Gram-negative and anaerobic organisms produce abscess, cellulitis, lymphangitis, osteomyelitis, gangrene and tetanus, and tetanus following tungiasis is well documented in unimmunized people. Chronic disease carries stigma and school absence in children.",
  mech: "The fertilized female penetrates the epidermis head first, leaving only the terminal abdominal segments with the anus, genital opening and respiratory spiracles exposed at the surface as the black dot. She reaches the upper dermis, remains with her mouthparts in contact with a dermal capillary and feeds continuously on blood, and over one to two weeks undergoes neosomy, a dramatic hypertrophy in which the abdomen swells two thousand fold into a sphere, pushing the surrounding epidermis into a hyperkeratotic ring. Eggs are expelled through the exposed opening onto the ground over several weeks, several hundred in total, and the flea then dies in situ and is slowly extruded, leaving a crater that heals with fibrosis. The pathology is a combination of mechanical distension and pressure on dermal structures, which accounts for the pain, an intense inflammatory and eosinophilic response around the parasite, and the breach of the epidermal barrier maintained for weeks, which provides a direct portal for bacteria, and this bacterial sequel rather than the flea itself causes most of the serious morbidity.",
  lab: "The diagnosis is clinical and visual: a white or yellowish papule or nodule with a central black punctum on the foot of a person exposed to sandy soil, often with several lesions at different stages. Dermoscopy is useful and shows the central pore with a brownish ring and sometimes the pulsating exposed segments and visible eggs, which distinguishes it from a wart or a foreign body granuloma. Lesions are staged by the Fortaleza classification into five stages from penetration through hypertrophy and egg expulsion to the residual crater. Excised tissue shows the flea in a cavity in the epidermis and upper dermis with its exoskeleton, eggs and digestive tract visible, surrounded by hyperkeratosis and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate with eosinophils. Radiographs are indicated where osteomyelitis is suspected, and wound culture guides antibiotic choice in superinfection.",
  tx: "Surgical or mechanical extraction of the flea under sterile conditions with a sterile needle or curette, followed by wound care, is the mainstay, and the cavity is irrigated and dressed. Topical dimeticone, a two-component silicone oil applied to the lesions, has become the best-supported medical treatment, suffocating the flea with a high rate of lesion inactivation within a week, and avoids the risks of digging at lesions with unsterile instruments, which is the common community practice and a major source of bacterial complication. Secondary bacterial infection is treated with appropriate antibiotics, tetanus immunization status is reviewed in every case, and analgesia and wound care accompany both approaches.",
  alt: "Occlusive petrolatum or paraffin to suffocate the flea, topical ivermectin, metrifonate and thiabendazole have been used with variable results, and oral ivermectin has not shown convincing efficacy in trials. Cryotherapy and electrodesiccation have been described. Coconut-oil-based repellent lotions applied to the feet reduce new lesions substantially in community trials and function as prevention rather than treatment.",
  res: "Drug resistance is not a recognized issue, because the effective treatments are mechanical and physical rather than pharmacological. The relevant resistance problem is bacterial: the wounds are frequently colonized by Staphylococcus aureus and Gram-negative organisms with acquired resistance, and in communities with easy access to unsupervised antibiotics the superinfecting flora is often difficult to treat.",
  prev: "Wearing closed shoes, which is the single most effective measure and the one most constrained by poverty, cementing or sealing earthen floors, keeping dogs, pigs and rats away from living areas and treating animal reservoirs, and regular application of a repellent such as the coconut-oil-based formulation that has been tested in Brazilian and African communities and reduced new lesions by around ninety percent with twice-daily use. Tetanus immunization protects against the most lethal complication. Community-level approaches that combine footwear provision, animal treatment and environmental improvement have reduced prevalence where sustained. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Plantar wart, which lacks the central black dot of the exposed flea and does not change over days, foreign body granuloma and embedded thorn or splinter, myiasis, which has a larva rather than a flea and a different time course, cutaneous larva migrans, scabies, pyogenic granuloma, paronychia, subungual melanoma in a single pigmented lesion, dracunculiasis and a simple abscess or ingrown toenail. Multiple lesions with black dots on the toes of a barefoot child in an endemic area are seldom anything else.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Tungiasis is caused by the pregnant female chigoe flea burrowing into the skin of the foot, producing a nodule with a central black dot, and the major morbidity comes from bacterial superinfection including tetanus."],
    ["Memory trick", "Tunga penetrans penetrates and then swells. White nodule, black dot, barefoot child, periungual toe. Treat it by taking it out or smothering it with dimeticone, not with a tablet."],
    ["Classic setting", "A barefoot child in a sandy Brazilian favela or a Kenyan village in the dry season with several painful itchy nodules around the toenails, each with a dark central point, and a household dog and pig in the yard."],
    ["Step focus", "Neosomy, the two thousand fold hypertrophy of the embedded female, is what makes the lesion grow over a week or two, and it is also why the Fortaleza staging follows the parasite's own development."]],
  worm: [["Carried back on the Columbian exchange, and then exported", "The chigoe flea is American in origin, described in sixteenth-century Spanish and Portuguese accounts of the New World, where it tormented sailors and colonists. It crossed to Africa in 1872 aboard the British ship Thomas Mitchell, which unloaded sand ballast at Ambriz in Angola, and spread across the continent within three decades, reaching the Indian Ocean coast and Madagascar. It is one of the clearest documented instances of an ectoparasite's intercontinental introduction being traced to a single vessel and cargo."],
    ["The flea that becomes a sphere", "Neosomy, the term for a radical change in body form after the adult stage, is rare among insects and extreme in this species. A one-millimetre flea burrows in and over ten days expands its abdomen to roughly a centimetre in diameter, a volume increase of about two thousand fold, with the cuticle stretching and the internal organs reorganizing around the enlarging ovaries. Only the posterior tip stays at the surface, which is both the respiratory opening and the egg exit, and which appears clinically as the black dot everyone is taught to look for."],
    ["Dimeticone instead of a needle", "For centuries the treatment has been to dig the flea out, usually with whatever sharp object is at hand, which is effective and also the principal route by which the wounds become infected, sometimes with tetanus or osteomyelitis as the result. Trials in Brazil, Kenya and Uganda in the 2010s showed that a dimeticone silicone oil of specific viscosity, applied twice over two days, inactivated a large majority of lesions within seven days with no instrumentation. It is a notably elegant solution: a physical suffocant with no pharmacology, no resistance potential and no need for sterile technique."],
    ["A neglected disease of the poorest households", "Tungiasis maps precisely onto poverty within communities, not just between them. Surveys in Brazilian favelas and East African villages find that the children and elderly people with the heaviest infestations live in houses with earthen floors, have no shoes, and share compounds with dogs and pigs, and the same individuals carry the stigma, miss school and lose mobility. The disease is painful, disabling, disfiguring, cheap to prevent with footwear, and received almost no research attention until the 1990s, which is why it is a standard example in discussions of what makes a disease neglected."]] },

{ id: "dermatobia-hominis", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Dermatobia hominis", also: "human botfly, torsalo, berne, ura, agent of furuncular myiasis",
  cls: "Larva of a neotropical oestrid fly, the adult a large hairy bluish fly resembling a bumblebee that does not feed, the larva a barrel-shaped maggot with rings of backward-pointing spines living subcutaneously for five to ten weeks, transmitted by phoresy on a biting insect",
  plain: "A South American fly whose eggs ride on mosquitoes and whose larva grows inside a boil in the skin with a breathing hole at the top.",
  live: "A wide range of mammals and some birds, including cattle, which bear the main economic burden, dogs, sheep, pigs, monkeys and humans. The adult fly lives in humid lowland forest and forest edge and does not feed at all, living only days to weeks. The distribution is neotropical: southern Mexico through Central America to Paraguay and northern Argentina, with Brazil, Costa Rica, Belize, Panama and Colombia prominent, and the great majority of cases seen outside the region are in returning travellers, which makes it one of the commonest imported tropical skin conditions in travel clinics.",
  spread: "The female fly does not approach the host at all. She captures a mosquito, a stable fly, a tick or another biting arthropod in flight and glues her eggs to its abdomen, a behavior called phoresy. When that vector later feeds on a mammal, the warmth of the skin triggers the eggs to hatch within seconds and the larvae penetrate the skin through the bite puncture, a hair follicle or intact skin. There is no person-to-person spread, and infestation reflects a single exposure event in a forested area, often recalled by the traveller as a mosquito bite that never settled down.",
  causes: "Furuncular myiasis. A pruritic papule appears at the penetration site within days and enlarges over one to three weeks into a firm, tender, erythematous nodule one to three centimetres across with a central punctum through which serosanguineous fluid discharges and through which the larva's posterior spiracles are intermittently visible. Patients characteristically describe intermittent sharp lancinating pain, a crawling or moving sensation and pain that is worse at night, which reflects the larva's movement. Lesions are usually single or few, on exposed areas: scalp, face, arms, legs, back and occasionally scrotum and breast. Regional lymphadenopathy and mild constitutional symptoms occur. Complications include secondary bacterial infection, abscess, cellulitis and rarely, with scalp lesions in infants, penetration of the fontanelle or skull and intracranial extension, and ophthalmomyiasis when the larva enters periocular tissue.",
  mech: "The hatched larva burrows into the dermis and subcutaneous tissue and orients itself with its posterior spiracles at the skin surface to breathe, which is why the lesion has a central pore and why occluding that pore is the basis of several treatments. The barrel-shaped body bears concentric rows of backward-directed cuticular spines and hooks that anchor it firmly, so traction alone frequently tears the larva and leaves fragments behind. The larva feeds on tissue fluid and exudate rather than consuming tissue extensively, and secretes proteolytic enzymes and antibacterial compounds that keep the surrounding cavity remarkably free of pyogenic infection in many cases. It grows through three instars over five to ten weeks, and the host mounts an intense eosinophilic and neutrophilic inflammatory response producing the furuncular wall. When mature, the larva emerges spontaneously, drops to the ground and pupates in the soil, so an untreated lesion is self-limiting, which is a genuine clinical option though rarely an acceptable one.",
  lab: "Diagnosis is clinical, resting on a furuncle with a central pore that discharges and does not respond to antibiotics, in a person who has visited a neotropical forest, together with the symptom of movement or lancinating pain. Dermoscopy shows the central pore and the larva's posterior end with its dark spiracles and sometimes its movement. Ultrasound demonstrates a hypoechoic cavity containing an echogenic mobile structure and is useful in confirming the diagnosis, assessing depth and counting larvae. Definitive diagnosis and speciation are made on the extracted larva, which is barrel-shaped with characteristic rows of spines and a distinctive spiracular pattern. Histology shows a cavity in the dermis and subcutis containing larval cuticle and spines surrounded by a dense mixed infiltrate with abundant eosinophils. Blood eosinophilia is mild or absent, which distinguishes it from the migratory helminths.",
  tx: "Removal of the intact larva is the treatment. Occlusion of the central pore with petrolatum, beeswax, paraffin, nail varnish, chewing gum or a strip of bacon causes the larva to migrate outward in search of air over minutes to hours, after which it can be grasped and withdrawn, which avoids an incision. Injection of local anesthetic into the base of the lesion, which both anesthetizes and raises pressure, often expels the larva. Where occlusion fails, a small incision with gentle extraction under local anesthesia removes it, and lateral pressure rather than traction reduces the risk of rupture. The cavity is irrigated and dressed, and secondary bacterial infection is treated with antibiotics. Oral or topical ivermectin has been used to kill the larva before removal, particularly for multiple or awkwardly placed lesions.",
  alt: "Oral ivermectin alone, which kills the larva and allows it to be extruded or resorbed, with the caveat that a dead larva left in place can provoke a marked foreign body and inflammatory reaction. Topical ivermectin under occlusion. Surgical excision of the whole lesion is reserved for complicated or periocular disease. Leaving the larva to complete development and emerge is biologically valid and occasionally chosen in remote settings.",
  res: "Not applicable in the usual sense, since treatment is mechanical. The recurring practical problem is incomplete removal: the backward-pointing spines anchor the larva so firmly that pulling tears it, leaving cuticular fragments that provoke a persistent foreign body granuloma and a non-healing nodule, which is the commonest reason a treated lesion fails to settle.",
  prev: "Personal protection against the biting insects that carry the eggs, with insect repellent, permethrin-treated clothing, long sleeves and trousers and bed nets in neotropical forest areas, since the botfly itself never touches the host and the whole exposure runs through a mosquito or fly bite. Drying laundry indoors or ironing it, which matters more for the African tumbu fly, is of marginal relevance here. Veterinary control in cattle, where the economic burden lies, uses macrocyclic lactones. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Bacterial furuncle or abscess, which responds to antibiotics and drainage and lacks the central pore and the sensation of movement, infected insect bite, foreign body reaction, epidermoid cyst, cutaneous leishmaniasis, which is a common competing diagnosis in the same regions, tungiasis, cutaneous larva migrans, which is linear and migratory rather than furuncular, cutaneous anthrax, sporotrichosis, and myiasis from other flies, particularly Cordylobia anthropophaga, the African tumbu fly, which produces an identical lesion but is acquired from eggs laid on soil or drying laundry rather than carried by a vector.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Dermatobia hominis eggs are carried to the host by a biting insect, a behavior called phoresy, and the larva produces a boil with a central breathing pore, treated by occluding the pore or extracting the larva intact."],
    ["Memory trick", "The botfly hitchhikes: it glues its eggs to a mosquito. A boil that breathes and moves. Plug the hole and it comes up for air."],
    ["Classic setting", "A traveller returning from the Amazon or Costa Rica with a tender boil on the scalp or arm that has not responded to two courses of antibiotics, discharges serous fluid and gives episodes of sharp pain and a crawling sensation at night."],
    ["Step focus", "Phoresy distinguishes this fly from Cordylobia anthropophaga, the tumbu fly, which lays eggs on soil and laundry, and the two produce clinically identical furuncular myiasis on different continents."]],
  worm: [["A fly that outsources its delivery", "Most parasitic flies lay eggs on or near the host. Dermatobia hominis captures a mosquito or stable fly in mid-air, holds it, glues a batch of eggs to its abdomen and releases it, then plays no further part. The eggs sit dormant until the warmth of a mammal triggers them to hatch within seconds, at the exact moment the vector is feeding and the skin is punctured. The adult fly never feeds in its short life. It is one of the most elaborate delivery mechanisms in medical entomology and it means the parasite's distribution is set by the vector's range rather than its own."],
    ["Bacon, petrolatum and nail varnish", "Because the larva must breathe through the central pore, every traditional and modern treatment exploits the same vulnerability: block the hole and the larva has to come up. Reported occlusive agents include petrolatum, beeswax, paraffin, chewing gum, nail polish, adhesive tape and, in a widely cited case series from the 1990s, a strip of raw bacon fat taped over the lesion, which the larva reliably crawled into within hours. The method is pleasingly simple, avoids an incision and reduces the chance of tearing the larva, which is the main complication of surgical extraction."],
    ["Why pulling makes it worse", "The larva is anchored by concentric rings of backward-pointing cuticular spines, functionally a biological barb. Traction from the surface drives those spines into the surrounding tissue and the larva separates, usually leaving the anterior portion behind. The retained cuticle and spines then provoke a foreign body granuloma and a non-healing nodule that may need excision months later. This single piece of anatomy is the reason the recommended approaches raise the larva out from below or encourage it to emerge, rather than grasping and pulling."],
    ["Cattle, not people, carry the cost", "Human cases are memorable and well photographed, but the economic weight of Dermatobia hominis falls on Latin American cattle, where larvae perforate hides, reduce weight gain and milk yield and downgrade leather, with losses estimated in the hundreds of millions of dollars annually. Veterinary control with macrocyclic lactones and the search for a vaccine are driven by that burden. The human disease, by contrast, is a handful of travellers a year in any given clinic, which is a familiar imbalance in zoonotic parasitology."]] },

{ id: "cochliomyia-hominivorax", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Cochliomyia hominivorax", also: "New World screwworm, primary screwworm fly, gusano barrenador, agent of obligate traumatic myiasis",
  cls: "Larva of a calliphorid blowfly, adult a metallic blue-green fly with three dark dorsal thoracic stripes and orange head, the larva an obligate parasite that feeds on living tissue, named for the screw-like rings of spines that anchor it, with Chrysomya bezziana as the Old World equivalent",
  plain: "A blowfly whose larvae feed only on living flesh, burrowing deeper into a wound like a screw and destroying tissue as they go.",
  live: "All warm-blooded animals, with cattle, sheep, goats, horses, dogs and wildlife the principal hosts and humans infested opportunistically. The adult fly lives in warm humid climates and does not survive hard frost, which defines its range and made eradication feasible. The historical distribution covered the southern United States, Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean and South America to northern Argentina. Eradication campaigns cleared the United States by 1966, Mexico and Central America progressively to Panama by 2001, and the Darien gap in Panama is maintained as a permanent barrier, so the fly now persists in South America, Cuba, Haiti, Jamaica and parts of the Caribbean.",
  spread: "The gravid female is attracted to the odour of a wound, a mucosal orifice or a discharge on a living animal and lays two to three hundred eggs at its margin, often in a shingled mass, and a wound may receive eggs from several flies. Larvae hatch within a day and immediately burrow into the living tissue. Human infestation follows neglected wounds, ulcers, surgical sites, umbilical stumps in neonates, and natural orifices including nose, ears, mouth, eyes, vagina and anus, particularly in people who are unconscious, intoxicated, debilitated, homeless, elderly, or living with uncontrolled wounds in rural endemic areas. There is no person-to-person transmission.",
  causes: "Obligate traumatic myiasis, which differs fundamentally from the facultative myiasis of ordinary blowflies because the larvae consume living tissue rather than necrotic debris. A wound becomes rapidly enlarging, foul-smelling and serosanguineous, with visible larvae whose dark posterior ends protrude and retract, intense pain, surrounding cellulitis and progressive destruction. Nasal and nasopharyngeal infestation causes epistaxis, facial pain, obstruction and, with extension, destruction of the nasal septum, turbinates, palate and orbit, and penetration of the cribriform plate with meningitis, cerebral abscess and death. Aural myiasis causes otalgia, discharge and a sensation of movement, with tympanic perforation and intracranial extension. Ophthalmomyiasis destroys orbital contents. Secondary bacterial infection, sepsis and tetanus follow, and untreated extensive infestation in a neglected patient is fatal.",
  mech: "Larvae hatch at the wound edge and burrow head down into living tissue, feeding with mouth hooks on tissue fluid and on flesh, and the body is ringed with bands of spines that act like the thread of a screw, so the larva advances and resists extraction, which is the origin of the name. They secrete proteolytic enzymes that liquefy tissue, and the mass of larvae, which may number in the hundreds, generates heat and a characteristic odour that attracts further gravid females, so an untreated wound is reinfested repeatedly and enlarges rapidly. Deep burrowing follows tissue planes and reaches bone, sinus, orbit and cranial cavity. The host response is an intense neutrophilic and eosinophilic inflammatory reaction with tissue necrosis. Larvae feed for five to seven days, drop to the ground and pupate in soil. The decisive biological feature is obligate parasitism of living tissue: unlike facultative blowfly larvae, which debride necrotic material and were historically used therapeutically, these larvae require and destroy viable flesh.",
  lab: "Diagnosis rests on finding and identifying the larvae, which requires species identification because the clinical implications differ sharply between obligate and facultative myiasis. Third instar larvae of this species show the characteristic pigmented dorsal tracheal trunks visible through the cuticle, the arrangement of the posterior spiracles and the complete rings of spines, and entomological consultation or molecular identification confirms the species. Imaging with computed tomography is used in nasal, aural and orbital disease to define extension, bone destruction and intracranial involvement. Wound culture guides antibiotic therapy for the near-universal secondary bacterial infection. In many countries the New World screwworm is a notifiable condition in animals and suspected cases in people prompt immediate veterinary and public health notification because of the eradication programmes.",
  tx: "Complete removal of all larvae, which is the definitive step, combined with thorough surgical debridement of destroyed tissue and wound irrigation. Larvae are induced to emerge by occlusion or by instillation of agents that provoke them to move toward the surface, including ether, chloroform, turpentine historically, and more commonly now topical ivermectin solution or a topical larvicide, after which they are removed individually with forceps, and the wound is explored because retained larvae continue to burrow. Oral or topical ivermectin kills larvae and has substantially simplified management, particularly in nasal and aural disease where mechanical access is difficult. Broad-spectrum antibiotics treat the secondary infection, tetanus immunization is reviewed, and reconstructive surgery addresses the resulting defects. Notification of the agricultural authority occurs in eradication-zone countries.",
  alt: "Nitrofurazone dressings, chloroform in vegetable oil and other historical larvicidal instillations. Endoscopic removal for nasal and sinus disease. Hyperbaric oxygen has been described anecdotally. In animals, organophosphate and macrocyclic lactone treatments are standard, and coumaphos wound dressings are used in eradication programmes.",
  res: "Insecticide resistance in field populations has been documented for organophosphates in South America and is a concern for the veterinary treatments on which containment depends. For human treatment the issue does not arise, since management is surgical and larvicidal rather than dependent on systemic drug susceptibility, and ivermectin remains effective.",
  prev: "Prompt cleaning and covering of wounds, care of umbilical stumps, management of chronic ulcers, and protection of debilitated and unconscious people from fly access with screening and dressings, which is the practical individual measure. The broader prevention is the sterile insect technique eradication programme, which has removed the fly from North and Central America and maintains a permanent barrier at the Darien gap in Panama with continuous release of sterile males, surveillance of livestock and quarantine of animal movement. Incursions, such as the Florida Keys outbreak in deer in 2016 and the northward spread through Central America and into Mexico recorded from 2023 onward, are met with emergency sterile fly release. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Facultative myiasis from Lucilia, Calliphora, Phormia and Sarcophaga species, in which larvae feed on necrotic tissue, the wound is not progressively destroyed and the clinical urgency is far lower, and the distinction is made on larval identification. Chrysomya bezziana, the Old World screwworm, which is biologically equivalent and occurs in Africa, Asia and the Middle East. Furuncular myiasis from Dermatobia or Cordylobia, which is a discrete nodule rather than a destructive wound. Necrotizing soft tissue infection, cutaneous anthrax, mucocutaneous leishmaniasis, rhinoscleroma, extranodal natural killer T-cell lymphoma of the nasal type and invasive fungal sinusitis in the destructive nasal presentations.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Cochliomyia hominivorax larvae are obligate parasites of living tissue, burrowing deeper into the wound like a screw and destroying it, which distinguishes them from facultative blowfly larvae that consume only necrotic tissue."],
    ["Memory trick", "Hominivorax means man-devourer, and the screwworm screws itself in. Obligate equals living flesh; facultative equals dead flesh and maggot debridement therapy."],
    ["Classic setting", "A debilitated or homeless person in rural South America with a neglected leg ulcer that has enlarged rapidly over days, smells foul and contains dozens of larvae whose dark ends move at the surface, or an infant with nasal infestation and septal destruction."],
    ["Step focus", "Identification of the larva matters clinically, because obligate screwworm myiasis is a destructive surgical emergency while facultative myiasis in a necrotic wound is comparatively benign."]],
  worm: [["The insect that was eradicated by sterilizing it", "In the 1930s Edward Knipling and Raymond Bushland reasoned that since the female screwworm mates once, releasing overwhelming numbers of sterilized males would collapse the population. Sterilization by gamma irradiation of pupae was developed in the 1950s, tested on Curacao, which was cleared in a few months, and then applied to Florida, the southern United States, Mexico and Central America. The programme cleared the United States by 1966 and reached Panama by 2001, at a cost repaid many times over in livestock savings, and the sterile insect technique became the template for pest eradication worldwide."],
    ["A barrier across the Darien", "Eradication from North and Central America is not permanent in the sense of being over. The fly remains established in South America, and reinvasion is prevented by a permanent biological barrier at the narrowest point of the isthmus in Panama, where a plant in Pacora rears and releases tens of millions of sterile flies weekly and inspectors monitor livestock. The programme has run continuously for more than two decades. From 2023 the barrier came under pressure as cases moved north through Central America and into Mexico, prompting emergency expansion of sterile fly production, which shows how an eradication achieved by continuous effort can be lost by interruption."],
    ["Maggots that heal and maggots that kill", "Sterile Lucilia sericata larvae are used therapeutically to debride chronic wounds, because they consume necrotic tissue, secrete antibacterial compounds and leave viable tissue alone. Cochliomyia hominivorax larvae look superficially similar and do the opposite: they require living flesh and burrow into it. The entire clinical significance of larval identification in a maggot-infested wound sits on that distinction, and it is the reason entomological identification, which seems like a formality, changes management from reassurance to urgent debridement."],
    ["The Florida Keys deer", "In 2016 the screwworm reappeared in the United States for the first time in fifty years, in the endangered Key deer of the Florida Keys, where males were found with wounds swarming with larvae. The response combined sterile fly release, treatment of individual deer with doramectin at feeding stations, carcass removal and movement restrictions, and the outbreak was declared over in 2017 after the loss of around a tenth of the deer population. It was a demonstration both of how quickly an eradicated pest can re-establish and of how effective a prepared response can be."]] },

{ id: "demodex-folliculorum", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Demodex folliculorum", also: "follicle mite, eyelash mite, Demodex brevis, demodicosis, demodicidosis",
  cls: "Obligate commensal mite of the human pilosebaceous unit, elongated and cigar-shaped with eight stubby legs clustered anteriorly, Demodex folliculorum about 0.3 to 0.4 mm living in the hair follicle and Demodex brevis shorter and living deeper in the sebaceous gland, with a life cycle of about two weeks entirely on the host",
  plain: "A tiny cigar-shaped mite that lives head-down in almost everyone's facial hair follicles and only causes trouble when its numbers rise.",
  live: "Humans exclusively for these two species, which are found on essentially all adults, with density rising through life so that the mites are near-universal by middle age. They concentrate where sebaceous glands are dense: the nasolabial folds, nose, cheeks, forehead, chin, external ear canal, eyelashes and eyebrows, and also on the chest and scalp. Demodex folliculorum occupies the upper follicle in groups, feeding on follicular epithelium and sebum, and Demodex brevis sits singly deeper in the sebaceous duct. Related species cause mange in dogs and other mammals and do not establish on people.",
  spread: "Direct contact, chiefly facial contact between mother and infant in early life and between close contacts thereafter, with transfer of mites on sebum and hair. Colonization begins in infancy and builds over decades. Because carriage is essentially universal, transmission is not clinically meaningful in the way it is for a pathogen, and the question in practice is not whether a person has the mite but whether the density and the host response have shifted from commensal to pathological.",
  causes: "Nothing at all in most people. Overgrowth, defined in practice by a density above about five mites per square centimetre on standardized skin surface biopsy, is associated with a set of inflammatory facial conditions collectively called demodicosis: pityriasis folliculorum, a diffuse fine scaling and roughness with follicular prominence and a sensation of dryness and burning, papulopustular rosacea, with inflammatory papules and pustules on a background of erythema, granulomatous rosacea, and perioral and periorificial dermatitis. Demodex blepharitis produces cylindrical dandruff collaring the eyelash base, lid margin itching worst in the morning, lash loss, madarosis, meibomian gland dysfunction, chalazion and recurrent styes, and is increasingly recognized as a specific and treatable cause of chronic blepharitis. Demodex folliculitis and a florid pustular eruption occur in immunosuppression, including HIV infection, leukemia, transplantation and during topical or systemic corticosteroid use, which is also a classic trigger for steroid-induced rosacea.",
  mech: "The mite lives head-down in the follicle, feeding on follicular epithelial cells and sebum with its stylet-like mouthparts, has no anus and accumulates waste within its body, which is released into the follicle when the mite dies. Pathology arises along several converging routes. Mechanical blockage of the follicle and sebaceous duct by mite bodies and the keratin and debris they generate produces follicular plugging, the cylindrical dandruff of blepharitis and the roughness of pityriasis folliculorum. Mite chitin, proteins and the contents released at death act as an innate immune stimulus, activating toll-like receptor 2 on keratinocytes, driving cathelicidin and kallikrein 5 expression and the production of LL-37, which is the same pathway implicated in rosacea independently of the mite. The mite also carries bacteria, notably Bacillus oleronius, whose proteins provoke a strong inflammatory and lymphocyte proliferative response in patients with rosacea, so part of the reaction may be directed at the mite's microbiome rather than the mite. Finally the mite can breach the follicular wall, allowing contents into the dermis and provoking a granulomatous response. Whether high mite density causes rosacea or results from the altered sebaceous and immune environment of rosacea skin is not settled, and both are probably true in a feedback loop.",
  lab: "Standardized skin surface biopsy, in which cyanoacrylate glue on a slide is pressed to the skin and lifted to remove the follicular contents of a defined area, allows mites to be counted per square centimetre and is the reference method. Simpler approaches include expression of follicular contents onto a slide with mineral oil, adhesive tape stripping, and examination of epilated eyelashes under light microscopy, where mites are seen protruding from the follicular sheath and cylindrical dandruff is visible at the lash base. Dermoscopy shows Demodex tails, fine whitish filaments emerging from follicular openings, and follicular plugs, and in vivo reflectance confocal microscopy visualizes mites without sampling. Skin biopsy of inflammatory lesions shows mites within follicles surrounded by perifollicular lymphohistiocytic and sometimes granulomatous inflammation. Because carriage is universal, finding a mite is not diagnostic and the density and the clinical context carry the interpretation.",
  tx: "Treatment is aimed at reducing mite density in people with clinical disease rather than at eradication. Topical ivermectin cream, applied once daily, is the best-supported treatment for papulopustular rosacea with demodicosis and reduces both mite counts and inflammatory lesions. Topical permethrin, benzyl benzoate, crotamiton, sulfur preparations and metronidazole are used. Oral ivermectin, sometimes combined with metronidazole, is used for extensive or refractory disease and for immunosuppressed patients with florid folliculitis. For Demodex blepharitis, lid hygiene with warm compresses and lid margin cleaning, tea tree oil preparations containing terpinen-4-ol, and the topical lotilaner ophthalmic solution licensed specifically for this indication are used. Withdrawal of topical corticosteroids on the face, where they have been driving mite proliferation, is a central part of management.",
  alt: "Oral metronidazole, doxycycline at anti-inflammatory dosing for the rosacea component, azelaic acid, selenium sulfide and precipitated sulfur. Tea tree oil and its active component terpinen-4-ol for lid disease. Intense pulsed light and thermal lid treatments for meibomian dysfunction accompanying blepharitis.",
  res: "Acquired resistance has not been characterized in these mites, and treatment response is generally good. Relapse is frequent and reflects the fact that the mite is a permanent commensal: reducing density does not remove it, and the underlying sebaceous and immune environment that permitted overgrowth persists, so maintenance treatment is often needed. Apparent failure also reflects diagnosis of demodicosis in patients whose facial inflammation has another cause.",
  prev: "Avoidance of prolonged topical corticosteroid and calcineurin inhibitor use on the face, which is the single most consistent iatrogenic driver of mite proliferation, routine facial and lid hygiene, and management of the underlying rosacea and seborrheic tendency. Because colonization is universal and begins in infancy, prevention of carriage is neither possible nor a goal, and the aim is prevention of overgrowth. Avoidance of shared eye cosmetics and of oil-based cleansers that leave residue in the lash line is used in blepharitis management. There is no vaccine.",
  look: "Rosacea without significant demodicosis, acne vulgaris, seborrheic dermatitis, perioral dermatitis, steroid-induced acneiform eruption, contact dermatitis, lupus erythematosus and dermatomyositis in facial erythema, pityriasis versicolor, Malassezia folliculitis, eosinophilic folliculitis in HIV infection, and bacterial folliculitis. In the eye, staphylococcal and seborrheic blepharitis, meibomian gland dysfunction from other causes, allergic and atopic lid disease, and sebaceous carcinoma masquerading as chronic unilateral blepharitis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Demodex mites are universal commensals of the human pilosebaceous unit, so finding them is normal, and disease is defined by increased density together with clinical inflammation, classically rosacea, blepharitis with cylindrical dandruff, and folliculitis after topical corticosteroid use or in immunosuppression."],
    ["Memory trick", "Everybody has Demodex. Folliculorum in the follicle, brevis is brief and sits deeper in the gland. Cylindrical dandruff on the lash means mites. Steroids on the face feed them."],
    ["Classic setting", "A middle-aged patient with months of papulopustular facial eruption after prolonged use of a topical corticosteroid cream, or chronic morning lid itching with waxy collars around the eyelashes that has not responded to lid hygiene alone."],
    ["Step focus", "The toll-like receptor 2 and cathelicidin pathway links mite antigens to the innate inflammation of rosacea, which is why causation between high mite density and rosacea remains genuinely ambiguous."]],
  worm: [["Found in earwax, in 1842", "Jakob Henle and Gustav Simon independently described mites in human skin in 1841 and 1842, Simon finding them in acne comedones and Henle in cerumen, and Demodex folliculorum became one of the first human ectoparasites described microscopically. For over a century it was regarded as a harmless curiosity of normal skin, which is largely accurate. The question of when a universal commensal becomes a pathogen has been argued continuously since, and it is a cleaner example of that general problem than almost any bacterium."],
    ["A mite with no anus", "Demodex has no excretory opening, so metabolic waste accumulates inside the body throughout its two-week life and is released into the follicle when the mite dies and disintegrates. This unusual anatomy has been proposed as a reason mite death, rather than mite presence, may trigger inflammation, and it fits the clinical observation that patients sometimes flare early in treatment. Genome sequencing published in 2022 showed an extremely reduced gene set consistent with an organism far down the path toward obligate symbiosis, with some authors arguing it is becoming an internal symbiont rather than a parasite."],
    ["Rosacea, cause or consequence", "Mite densities are consistently higher in rosacea skin than in controls, treatment that reduces mites improves rosacea, and mite antigens activate the same cathelicidin pathway known to drive rosacea inflammation, all of which argues for a causal role. Against that, rosacea skin has altered sebum, impaired barrier function and dysregulated innate immunity, any of which would allow mites to proliferate as a consequence. The honest position after decades of work is that the relationship is bidirectional, which is why topical ivermectin works well and yet nobody calls rosacea an infestation."],
    ["An eye drop for a mite", "Demodex blepharitis was for years treated with lid scrubs and tea tree oil of variable concentration and tolerability, with the active component terpinen-4-ol identified as the effective agent. In 2023 lotilaner ophthalmic solution, an isoxazoline that blocks mite GABA-gated chloride channels and was developed from veterinary flea and tick products, was licensed specifically for Demodex blepharitis after trials showing high rates of lash collarette clearance. It is a neat instance of veterinary parasiticide chemistry arriving in human ophthalmology for an organism that everyone already carries."]] }
);

MICRO.push(
{ id: "pulex-irritans", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Pulex irritans", also: "human flea, common flea, house flea",
  cls: "Wingless laterally flattened hematophagous insect of the order Siphonaptera, about 2 to 4 mm, lacking both genal and pronotal combs, with a complete metamorphosis through egg, larva, pupa and adult, and a free-living larva that feeds on organic debris rather than blood",
  plain: "The flea named for people, which actually prefers pigs and dogs, bites in clusters around the ankles and waist, and has carried plague.",
  live: "Despite the name, this flea is a generalist that infests pigs, dogs, foxes, badgers, rodents and humans, and in many regions pigs and dogs are the principal hosts while human infestation follows from living alongside them. Adults spend time both on the host and in bedding, floor cracks, animal pens and soil, and the larvae develop off the host in dust, bedding material and the floor of dwellings and byres, feeding on organic debris including adult flea feces. The distribution is worldwide, with heaviest burdens in rural housing with earthen floors and close animal contact, and notable persistence in the Andes, parts of Africa, the Middle East, central Asia and southern Europe.",
  spread: "Adults jump onto a host to feed and move between hosts and the environment, so infestation is acquired from contaminated bedding, floors, animal pens and close contact with infested animals or people. Eggs fall from the host into the environment, larvae develop in floor dust and bedding over one to several weeks, and pupae can remain dormant for months until vibration, warmth or carbon dioxide signals a host, which is why a vacant building can produce a sudden mass emergence. Human-to-human transmission is not a meaningful concept; the environment is the reservoir.",
  causes: "Pruritic bites, appearing as small erythematous papules with a central punctum, typically in groups and lines around the ankles, lower legs, waistband and anywhere clothing is tight, and the reaction is a sensitization phenomenon that escalates with repeated exposure. Papular urticaria, particularly in children, with intensely itchy excoriated papules and secondary impetiginization, is the commonest presentation in endemic housing. Flea bite hypersensitivity produces bullous lesions in some people. Secondary bacterial infection from scratching is frequent. The important consequences are as a vector: this species is a competent vector of Yersinia pestis and has been implicated as the principal vector in human-to-human plague transmission in several Andean and African foci, and it transmits Rickettsia typhi and Rickettsia felis, carries Bartonella species, and is an intermediate host for the tapeworms Dipylidium caninum and Hymenolepis diminuta.",
  mech: "The adult flea pierces the skin with stylet mouthparts and feeds for minutes, injecting saliva containing anticoagulants, vasodilators and apyrase that inhibit platelet aggregation, and the bite reaction is an immunological response to those salivary antigens, immediate and wheal-like in sensitized people and delayed and papular in others, which explains the escalating reactions over weeks and why household members differ so markedly. Pathogen transmission works by two routes. Blockage of the proventriculus by a Yersinia pestis biofilm, the mechanism classically described for the rat flea, causes regurgitation of bacteria into the bite, and early-phase transmission in the first days after an infectious meal occurs without blockage and is now thought to account for much of the epidemic spread. Rickettsia typhi and Rickettsia felis are shed in flea feces and enter through the scratched bite rather than by inoculation, which is why the disease follows scratching rather than biting. Cestode transmission requires the flea itself to be swallowed.",
  lab: "Identification is entomological. The flea is recovered by combing the host or by collecting from bedding and floor sweepings, and Pulex irritans is distinguished from the cat and dog fleas by the absence of both the genal comb below the eye and the pronotal comb behind the head, with a single row of bristles and the shape of the head and the ocular bristle position completing the identification. Bite reactions have no specific laboratory signature, and biopsy shows a wedge-shaped perivascular lymphocytic and eosinophilic infiltrate. When vector-borne disease is suspected, diagnosis turns on the pathogen: culture, polymerase chain reaction and antigen testing for Yersinia pestis on aspirate, blood or sputum, and serology or molecular testing for murine and flea-borne spotted fever rickettsioses. Flea pools from a plague focus are tested by polymerase chain reaction in outbreak investigation.",
  tx: "The bite reaction is managed symptomatically with topical corticosteroids, oral antihistamines and treatment of secondary bacterial infection, since the flea does not live in the skin. Control of the infestation is the definitive measure and is environmental and veterinary: insecticide treatment of floors, bedding and animal pens, treatment of dogs and pigs with appropriate veterinary products, laundering and hot-drying of bedding, vacuuming, and where possible the sealing of earthen floors. Where plague or rickettsial infection is suspected, the specific antimicrobial treatment of that disease takes priority.",
  alt: "Insect growth regulators such as methoprene and pyriproxyfen applied to the environment, desiccant dusts, and systemic veterinary isoxazolines and macrocyclic lactones for the animal hosts. Permethrin-treated bedding and clothing. In plague foci, mass insecticide dusting of dwellings and rodent burrows is used alongside rodent control, and doing rodent control without flea control is recognized as hazardous because displaced fleas then seek human hosts.",
  res: "Insecticide resistance in flea populations is documented for organophosphates, carbamates and pyrethroids in various regions, with mechanisms including knockdown resistance sodium channel mutations and enhanced detoxification, and it has complicated plague vector control in some African foci. Resistance in this species is less intensively monitored than in the cat flea, which carries the commercial veterinary research interest.",
  prev: "Treatment of dogs, pigs and other household animals, separation of animal sleeping areas from human ones, improvement of flooring, regular laundering and sun-drying of bedding, insecticide application in dwellings in plague foci, and rodent control conducted together with flea control rather than before it. Personal protection with repellents and permethrin-treated clothing is used in outbreak investigation and fieldwork. There is no vaccine against the flea, and plague vaccines are of limited availability and are used occupationally rather than as population protection.",
  look: "Ctenocephalides felis and Ctenocephalides canis, the cat and dog fleas, which are far more commonly responsible for flea bites in urban settings and which bear genal and pronotal combs, and Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea of classical plague transmission. Bed bug bites, which occur on exposed skin and in rows and come from the bed rather than the floor. Mosquito bites, scabies, papular urticaria of any cause, folliculitis, dermatitis herpetiformis and delusional parasitosis.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Pulex irritans lacks both the genal and the pronotal comb, which identifies it, and despite the name human flea it commonly infests pigs and dogs and has been implicated as a vector of human-to-human plague transmission."],
    ["Memory trick", "Pulex irritans has no combs: no genal comb, no pronotal comb, nothing to comb its hair with. Bites around the ankles and waistband. Ctenocephalides has both combs."],
    ["Classic setting", "A child in rural Andean or African housing with earthen floors and pigs in the compound, with grouped excoriated itchy papules around the ankles and waist, or a plague focus investigation in which this species predominates in dwellings."],
    ["Step focus", "Fleas are the intermediate host for Dipylidium caninum and Hymenolepis diminuta, so a swallowed flea transmits a tapeworm, while a flea bite transmits plague and the flea's feces transmit murine typhus."]],
  worm: [["The flea that is named for the wrong host", "Linnaeus named it Pulex irritans, the irritating flea, on the assumption that it was the human flea, and the name stuck. Surveys across its range repeatedly find it most abundant on pigs, dogs, foxes and badgers, with human infestation largely a consequence of sharing space with those animals. It is a reasonable illustration of how early taxonomy encoded human-centred assumptions, and of why vector studies now begin with host-use surveys rather than with the species name."],
    ["A rival candidate for the Black Death", "The standard account of the medieval plague pandemics assigns transmission to Xenopsylla cheopis on black rats. Historians and entomologists have long objected that the speed, seasonality and household clustering of medieval mortality fit poorly with a rat-driven epizootic, and some have argued for human ectoparasites, Pulex irritans and the body louse, transmitting directly between people. Modelling studies in the 2010s found human ectoparasite transmission fitted European mortality curves better than rat-flea models in most cities tested. The question remains contested, and recent genomic work on medieval remains has not settled the vector."],
    ["Fleas in modern plague foci", "In Madagascar, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Tanzania and the Andes, entomological surveys in dwellings during human plague outbreaks have repeatedly found Pulex irritans in large numbers inside houses and on patients. Its presence correlates with household clustering of cases in a way that rat flea abundance sometimes does not. The practical consequence is that control programmes dust houses and bedding rather than relying solely on rodent and burrow treatment, and that killing rats without first killing fleas is understood to increase human risk as hungry fleas disperse."],
    ["The pupa that waits", "A flea pupa can remain in its cocoon for months, metabolically quiet, until vibration, warmth, carbon dioxide or pressure signals that a host has arrived, at which point adults emerge within seconds. This explains the familiar phenomenon of a family entering a house that has stood empty and being covered in fleas within minutes, and it explains why a single insecticide treatment often fails: the adults are killed, the protected pupae are not, and a new cohort emerges days later, which is why control combines an adulticide with an insect growth regulator."]] },

{ id: "xenopsylla-cheopis", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Xenopsylla cheopis", also: "oriental rat flea, tropical rat flea, the plague flea",
  cls: "Wingless laterally flattened hematophagous flea of the family Pulicidae, about 2 to 3 mm, lacking genal and pronotal combs but distinguished by a vertical mesopleural rod and the shape of the spermatheca, a parasite chiefly of the rat Rattus rattus and Rattus norvegicus, with free-living larvae in nest debris",
  plain: "The rat flea that transmits plague and murine typhus, which bites people when its rat host dies and nothing else is available.",
  live: "Rats are the natural hosts, principally the black rat Rattus rattus and the brown rat Rattus norvegicus, along with other commensal and wild rodents, and the flea lives on the host and in its nest, where the larvae develop in accumulated debris and adult flea feces. Distribution is tropical and subtropical and now effectively worldwide through shipping, including port cities in temperate zones. Sylvatic plague reservoirs in ground squirrels, prairie dogs, marmots and gerbils are maintained by other flea species, with this flea the classic bridge into the commensal rat and thence to people in urban epidemics.",
  spread: "The flea feeds on rats and transfers to humans when the rodent host dies, which is the central epidemiological fact: a plague epizootic kills the rats, their fleas leave the cooling carcass, and in the absence of a rat they bite whoever is nearby. Fleas also move between rats in nests, in grain stores and along shipping routes, and rats carried in cargo have moved both rat and flea between continents, which is how plague reached ports worldwide in the third pandemic. Human infestation is incidental and the flea does not establish a human population.",
  causes: "Bites cause pruritic papules, usually on the lower legs, which are a minor matter. The significance of this species is entirely as a vector. It transmits Yersinia pestis, causing bubonic plague with a painful regional bubo, fever and prostration, progressing in some to septicemic plague with disseminated intravascular coagulation and gangrene, and to secondary pneumonic plague, which then transmits person to person by droplet without any further insect involvement. It transmits Rickettsia typhi, the agent of murine or endemic typhus, a febrile illness with headache, myalgia and a transient macular rash, generally milder than epidemic typhus but occasionally severe with pneumonia, meningoencephalitis and renal failure, and it carries Rickettsia felis. It serves as an intermediate host for Hymenolepis diminuta and Hymenolepis nana when swallowed.",
  mech: "Plague transmission occurs by two routes. In the classical blocked-flea mechanism, Yersinia pestis forms a biofilm in the flea proventriculus mediated by the hemin storage locus, obstructing the gut so that incoming blood is regurgitated into the bite wound together with bacteria, and the starving flea bites repeatedly and persistently, which makes it an efficient and desperate vector. Blockage takes one to two weeks to develop and the blocked flea dies within days. Early-phase transmission, recognized more recently, occurs within one to four days of an infectious meal without blockage, is less efficient per bite but involves far more fleas over a far shorter interval, and is now considered to drive the explosive early spread of an epizootic. Rickettsia typhi, by contrast, multiplies in the flea midgut and is shed in flea feces, which remain infectious in dust for long periods and enter through the scratched bite, through mucous membranes or by inhalation, so murine typhus is contracted from flea dirt rather than from the bite itself, and the flea is not harmed and transmits for life, including transovarially.",
  lab: "Identification of the flea is morphological, with the absence of genal and pronotal combs, the presence of a vertical mesopleural rod dividing the mesopleuron, and the shape of the spermatheca distinguishing it from Pulex irritans and from the Ctenocephalides species. Flea pools collected from rodents and burrows in outbreak investigation are tested by polymerase chain reaction for Yersinia pestis and Rickettsia typhi, and flea index, the mean number of fleas per rodent, is a classical surveillance measure used to assess epidemic risk. In patients, plague is diagnosed by Gram and Wayson or Giemsa stain of bubo aspirate showing bipolar safety-pin bacilli, culture, rapid F1 antigen dipstick and polymerase chain reaction, and murine typhus by paired serology for Rickettsia typhi with immunofluorescence, molecular testing on blood and the usual accompanying findings of thrombocytopenia, mildly raised transaminases and hyponatremia.",
  tx: "There is no treatment of the flea in a patient. The flea bite is managed symptomatically, and the clinical priority is the transmitted infection. Plague is treated with an aminoglycoside such as gentamicin or streptomycin, or with a fluoroquinolone, or doxycycline, started on clinical suspicion without waiting for confirmation, with isolation precautions where pneumonic disease is possible and post-exposure prophylaxis for contacts. Murine typhus responds to doxycycline, with a marked and rapid clinical response that is itself diagnostically supportive. Vector control is the public health response and uses insecticide dusting of rodent burrows, dwellings and runways, combined with rodent control sequenced after flea control.",
  alt: "Chloramphenicol for plague meningitis. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole has been used for plague prophylaxis historically and is inferior. For murine typhus, azithromycin and chloramphenicol are alternatives in pregnancy and childhood, and a fluoroquinolone has been used though with less supportive evidence. Vector control alternatives include insect growth regulators, systemic insecticides delivered to rodents in bait, and in some programmes insecticide-treated bait boxes.",
  res: "Insecticide resistance in this species is documented in plague foci, including DDT and pyrethroid resistance in Madagascar and organophosphate resistance elsewhere, and it is a genuine constraint on outbreak response. Antimicrobial resistance in Yersinia pestis is rare but plasmid-mediated multidrug resistant isolates were recovered in Madagascar in the 1990s, which was a significant finding given the small number of drugs used and the speed at which plague kills.",
  prev: "Rodent control in and around dwellings, grain stores and ports, with flea control performed first or simultaneously so that fleas leaving dying rats do not turn to people, insecticide dusting of burrows and dwellings in active foci, rat-proofing of buildings and ships, and international port sanitation regulations, which were the original reason for international health regulation. Personal protection with repellent and permethrin-treated clothing for field and outbreak workers, and avoidance of handling dead rodents and of camping near rodent colonies in sylvatic plague areas. Plague vaccines based on killed whole cell or F1-V subunit preparations exist with limited availability and are used occupationally. There is no vaccine against the flea.",
  look: "Pulex irritans, which lacks the mesopleural rod and is more associated with pigs, dogs and people, Ctenocephalides felis and canis, which bear genal and pronotal combs and account for most urban flea bites, and Nosopsyllus fasciatus, the northern rat flea, which is a less efficient plague vector. For the diseases, plague mimics other causes of acute lymphadenitis with fever including tularemia, which also presents with an ulcer and node, staphylococcal and streptococcal adenitis, cat scratch disease and anthrax, and murine typhus mimics dengue, leptospirosis, typhoid, scrub typhus and acute undifferentiated fever generally.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Xenopsylla cheopis transmits Yersinia pestis when the biofilm-blocked flea regurgitates bacteria into the bite, and it transmits Rickettsia typhi in its feces, which enter through the scratched bite rather than by the bite itself."],
    ["Memory trick", "Rat dies, flea flies, human dies. Plague goes in through the bite; murine typhus goes in through the scratch, because typhus is in the flea dirt. No combs but a mesopleural rod."],
    ["Classic setting", "A patient in a Madagascan highland village or a sylvatic focus in the American southwest with sudden fever and an exquisitely tender inguinal bubo days after a rat die-off in the house, or a febrile patient in a port city with headache, rash and a flea-infested rodent population nearby."],
    ["Step focus", "Pneumonic plague, once established secondarily, transmits person to person by respiratory droplets with no flea involved, which is why isolation and contact prophylaxis matter and why plague is a potential agent of deliberate release."]],
  worm: [["Three years from bacterium to flea", "Alexandre Yersin isolated the plague bacillus in Hong Kong in 1894, and the vector took longer and cost more. Paul-Louis Simond, working in Karachi in 1898, showed that a healthy rat separated from a sick rat by a barrier that only fleas could cross became infected and died, and argued that the flea was the link. His conclusion was dismissed for several years until confirmed by the Indian Plague Commission between 1905 and 1908, whose exhaustive experiments established the rat-flea-human chain and the role of this species. The delay in accepting it cost lives during the third pandemic."],
    ["The blocked flea, and why it bites so insistently", "Yersinia pestis forms a biofilm in the flea's proventriculus that physically obstructs the passage of blood into the midgut. The flea is therefore starving, feeds repeatedly and with increasing urgency, and each attempt forces regurgitation of bacteria into the wound. The mechanism was described by Bacot and Martin in 1914 and became the textbook account. It also has an odd implication: the bacterium kills its vector within days, so the efficient transmission stage is also a terminal one, which is why early-phase transmission from unblocked fleas was proposed and then demonstrated as the driver of epizootic speed."],
    ["A flea index as an epidemic forecast", "Plague surveillance for most of the twentieth century rested on trapping rats, combing them and counting fleas, generating a flea index. An index above about one in commensal rats was treated as a threshold of epidemic risk, because enough fleas were present for an epizootic to spill into people. The measure is crude and has been criticized, but it was operational, cheap and actionable with the technology of the time, and flea index surveys in ports and in Indian and Madagascan towns guided dusting campaigns for decades."],
    ["Why killing the rats first is dangerous", "The lesson learned repeatedly in plague control is sequence. Poisoning rats in an infested dwelling kills the host and liberates its fleas, which then seek the nearest warm-blooded animal, which is a person. Outbreaks have been precipitated by well-intentioned rodent campaigns conducted without insecticide. Modern guidance therefore dusts for fleas first or simultaneously, and it is one of the clearest practical examples in vector-borne disease of an intervention that is actively harmful when the order of operations is wrong."]] },

{ id: "trombicula-chiggers", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Trombicula species", also: "chiggers, harvest mites, red bugs, berry bugs, scrub itch mites, Leptotrombidium for the scrub typhus vectors, trombiculiasis",
  cls: "Larval stage of trombiculid mites, the six-legged larva being the only parasitic stage at about 0.2 mm and bright red or orange, with free-living predatory nymphs and adults that have eight legs, feeding on dissolved host tissue rather than blood and detaching after a few days",
  plain: "The six-legged larval mite that attaches at the sock line or waistband, dissolves a little skin to feed, and leaves a fiercely itchy welt.",
  live: "Larvae feed on a wide range of vertebrates including rodents, birds, reptiles and mammals, with humans incidental hosts, and the nymphs and adults are free-living soil predators that feed on arthropod eggs and small invertebrates. The habitat is tall grass, scrub, brush, forest edge, berry patches, leaf litter and the margins of fields, and the mites cluster in patches so that exposure is highly localized, often described as walking through a particular spot. Distribution is worldwide, with Eutrombicula alfreddugesi prominent in the Americas, Neotrombicula autumnalis in Europe, and Leptotrombidium species in the Asia-Pacific tsutsugamushi triangle, where they transmit scrub typhus.",
  spread: "The larva climbs vegetation, waits for a passing host, attaches to skin and feeds for one to several days before dropping off. Attachment favours sites where clothing is tight or skin is thin: ankles, sock line, behind the knee, groin, waistband, axilla and bra line. There is no person-to-person transmission and no establishment on the host, and the mite does not burrow into the skin or lay eggs in it, which are the two most persistent misconceptions about it.",
  causes: "Trombiculiasis. Intensely pruritic erythematous papules, papulovesicles or wheals appear within hours at the attachment sites, clustered and concentrated at the sock line and waistband, with itching that peaks at one to two days and persists for one to two weeks and is frequently described as more intense than any other arthropod bite. Papular urticaria, excoriation, lichenification and secondary bacterial infection follow from scratching. Summer penile syndrome, with penile swelling, itch and dysuria in boys, is attributed to chigger bites in that site. In the Asia-Pacific the critical consequence is vector-borne: Leptotrombidium larvae transmit Orientia tsutsugamushi, causing scrub typhus with fever, headache, myalgia, regional lymphadenopathy, a macular rash and in about half of cases an eschar at the bite site, which may progress to pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis, acute kidney injury, myocarditis and multiorgan failure with appreciable mortality if untreated.",
  mech: "The larva attaches with its chelicerae, pierces the stratum corneum, and secretes saliva containing proteolytic and hydrolytic enzymes that liquefy host epidermal cells, forming a feeding tube or stylostome, a hardened hyaline channel of host and parasite material through which the mite sucks dissolved tissue. It does not take blood and does not burrow. The clinical reaction is a hypersensitivity response to those salivary proteins and to the stylostome left behind, which is why the itch begins after the mite has fed, why it outlasts the attachment by many days, why it worsens on repeat exposure, and why removing the mite does not abort the reaction. For scrub typhus, Orientia tsutsugamushi is maintained transovarially in Leptotrombidium populations, so the larva is infectious at its first and only feed without having to acquire the organism from a host, and the bacterium is inoculated with saliva, multiplies at the site to produce the eschar, then disseminates through endothelial cells causing a systemic vasculitis.",
  lab: "Diagnosis of the bite reaction is clinical, based on the distribution at clothing constriction sites and the history of exposure to grass or scrub, and no test confirms it. The larva, if still attached, is visible as a minute red or orange dot and can be identified microscopically by its six legs, which separates a larval trombiculid from any eight-legged mite. Dermoscopy may show the attached larva. Biopsy shows the stylostome extending into the epidermis with a surrounding spongiosis and a perivascular lymphocytic and eosinophilic infiltrate, and the finding of a stylostome is characteristic. For scrub typhus, diagnosis is by indirect immunofluorescence serology on paired sera, which remains the reference, polymerase chain reaction on eschar swab, eschar crust or blood, which is most sensitive early, and rapid immunochromatographic tests, with the eschar being the single most useful clinical finding and often overlooked in skin folds.",
  tx: "The bite is managed symptomatically: washing with soap and water to remove any remaining larvae, topical corticosteroids of mid to high potency, oral antihistamines, cool compresses, and antibiotics for secondary bacterial infection. Antiparasitic drugs have no role, since the mite has usually detached by the time the patient presents and the symptoms are immunological. The traditional practice of applying nail varnish to suffocate a burrowed mite is based on a false premise, since the mite neither burrows nor remains. Scrub typhus is treated with doxycycline, which produces a rapid defervescence, with azithromycin as the alternative in pregnancy, childhood and suspected doxycycline-resistant foci, and rifampicin has been used in areas with poor response.",
  alt: "Topical anesthetics and antipruritics including pramoxine, menthol and calamine. A short course of oral corticosteroids for severe widespread reactions. For scrub typhus, chloramphenicol historically and azithromycin or rifampicin where response to doxycycline is poor.",
  res: "Not applicable to the mite, which is not treated pharmacologically in the patient. For scrub typhus, reduced response to doxycycline has been reported from northern Thailand and some other foci, with azithromycin and rifampicin used in those settings, although the molecular basis is not established and the clinical significance remains debated.",
  prev: "Permethrin-treated clothing and gear, which is the most effective measure and is the basis of military protection in endemic Asia, together with DEET or picaridin repellent on skin, particularly around ankles, waist and wrists, tucking trousers into socks, avoidance of sitting directly on grass and leaf litter, prompt showering with soap and laundering of clothing after exposure, which removes unattached larvae, and vegetation management by mowing and clearing scrub around dwellings and campsites. Doxycycline prophylaxis has been used for short high-risk exposures in scrub typhus endemic areas. There is no licensed scrub typhus vaccine, and the extensive antigenic diversity of Orientia tsutsugamushi has repeatedly defeated vaccine development.",
  look: "Flea bites, which also cluster on the lower legs, mosquito bites, bed bug bites, scabies, which itches at night and burrows at characteristic sites, papular urticaria of any cause, contact dermatitis from plants, folliculitis, cercarial dermatitis in people who have been in fresh water, and seabather eruption. For the systemic disease, scrub typhus is confused with dengue, leptospirosis, murine typhus, typhoid, malaria and other acute undifferentiated fevers, and the eschar, when sought in the axilla, groin and under the breast, frequently resolves the question.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Only the six-legged larval stage is parasitic, it feeds on dissolved tissue through a stylostome rather than on blood, it does not burrow into the skin, and in Asia Leptotrombidium larvae transmit scrub typhus."],
    ["Memory trick", "Six legs and it is a chigger larva; eight legs and it is something else. It does not burrow, so nail varnish is pointless. Itch at the sock line and the waistband. Chigger in Asia equals scrub typhus and an eschar."],
    ["Classic setting", "A hiker or berry picker with intensely itchy clustered papules at the sock line and waistband appearing the evening after walking through long grass, or a farmer in rural Thailand, India or Korea with a week of fever, headache and a painless black eschar in the axilla."],
    ["Step focus", "Orientia tsutsugamushi is maintained transovarially in the mite, so the larva is infectious at its only blood-free meal, and the eschar at the bite site is present in roughly half of cases and is the most useful physical sign."]],
  worm: [["The mite that does not do what everyone says it does", "Folk belief, and a surprising amount of older medical writing, holds that the chigger burrows into the skin, lays eggs there, and has to be suffocated with nail varnish or dug out. None of that is true. The larva attaches at the surface, inserts its mouthparts, feeds on liquefied epidermal cells for a day or two and drops off, and by the time the itch is at its worst the mite is usually gone. The persistence of the burrowing myth is partly confusion with Tunga penetrans, the chigoe flea, whose similar common name genuinely does burrow."],
    ["The stylostome", "Rather than piercing a vessel, the trombiculid larva builds a feeding apparatus. Its saliva hardens into a hyaline tube that extends from the mouthparts down into the epidermis, through which successive doses of digestive enzymes are delivered and liquefied tissue withdrawn. The structure remains in the skin after the mite detaches and is visible on histology as a characteristic eosinophilic channel. It is both the mechanism of feeding and the explanation for the prolonged itch, since the host is reacting to a foreign protein plug left behind rather than to an insect still present."],
    ["Scrub typhus and the war in Burma", "Allied forces in the Southwest Pacific and Burma during the Second World War lost very large numbers of troops to scrub typhus, with some units suffering attack rates above fifty percent and case fatality in the tens of percent before antibiotics. Entomological teams identified Leptotrombidium larvae in the kunai grass of forest clearings, and the control measures that emerged, impregnating uniforms with dimethyl phthalate and later permethrin, clearing vegetation and avoiding mite islands, remain the basis of prevention. The disease shaped military medicine in the theatre as much as malaria did."],
    ["Mite islands and a disease that reappeared", "Trombiculid larvae are not evenly distributed; they occur in discrete patches a few metres across, called mite islands, where an infected rodent nest has seeded a focus, and transovarial maintenance keeps Orientia in that spot for generations. This is why two people walking the same path metres apart can have entirely different exposures. Scrub typhus was considered a rural curiosity after the 1950s and has since re-emerged as one of the commonest causes of acute undifferentiated fever across South and Southeast Asia, with a million or more cases a year estimated and foci now described in the Middle East, Chile and Africa."]] },

{ id: "ixodes-scapularis", k: "para", g: "Ectoparasites", nm: "Ixodes scapularis", also: "deer tick, blacklegged tick, Ixodes pacificus for the western blacklegged tick, Ixodes ricinus for the European sheep tick",
  cls: "Hard-bodied tick of the family Ixodidae with a dorsal scutum and anteriorly projecting mouthparts, a three-host life cycle over two to three years through larva, nymph and adult, each stage taking a single prolonged blood meal lasting days, the nymph about the size of a poppy seed and responsible for most human disease",
  plain: "The blacklegged deer tick, whose poppy-seed-sized nymph attaches for days unnoticed and delivers Lyme disease and several other infections.",
  live: "A three-host tick with different hosts at each stage. Larvae and nymphs feed chiefly on the white-footed mouse Peromyscus leucopus, along with chipmunks, shrews, voles and ground-feeding birds, which are the reservoirs of the pathogens, and adults feed mainly on white-tailed deer, which do not act as pathogen reservoirs but sustain the tick population and provide the mating site. The tick requires humid leaf litter and dies in dry conditions, so habitat is deciduous and mixed woodland, woodland edge, leaf litter, stone walls and brushy suburban gardens. Distribution covers the northeastern and upper midwestern United States and adjacent Canada with ongoing range expansion, Ixodes pacificus on the Pacific coast, and Ixodes ricinus across Europe.",
  spread: "Questing larvae and nymphs wait on low vegetation and leaf litter and attach to a passing host, then feed for three to five days. Human exposure is overwhelmingly from the nymph, which is about one millimetre across, quests in late spring and summer, and is small enough to go unnoticed for the days required for pathogen transmission, whereas the larger adult female, which quests in autumn and spring, is usually found and removed. Transmission of Borrelia burgdorferi generally requires attachment for thirty-six to forty-eight hours, since the spirochete has to switch surface proteins and migrate from the tick midgut to the salivary glands, while Anaplasma transmits faster and the Powassan virus group can transmit within fifteen minutes because the virus is already in the salivary glands.",
  causes: "The tick bite itself produces a small erythematous papule with mild itching, and a larger local hypersensitivity reaction in some people. Tick paralysis is not a feature of this species. The clinical weight is in what it transmits, and this tick carries more human pathogens than any other in North America: Borrelia burgdorferi and Borrelia mayonii causing Lyme disease, Anaplasma phagocytophilum causing human granulocytic anaplasmosis, Babesia microti causing babesiosis, Borrelia miyamotoi causing a relapsing febrile illness, Ehrlichia muris eauclairensis, and the deer tick virus lineage of Powassan virus causing a severe encephalitis with substantial mortality and lasting neurological deficit. Coinfection is common, with Lyme disease and anaplasmosis or babesiosis together producing a more severe and prolonged illness. Alpha-gal syndrome, the delayed mammalian meat allergy, is associated chiefly with Amblyomma americanum rather than with this tick.",
  mech: "The tick cuts the skin with chelicerae, inserts a barbed hypostome and, in this genus, secretes a cement cone that anchors it firmly, then feeds slowly over days, alternating between salivation and ingestion. Tick saliva is a pharmacological arsenal: anticoagulants, platelet aggregation inhibitors, vasodilators, complement inhibitors, histamine-binding proteins, and immunomodulators that suppress local dendritic cell and T-cell function, which together prevent the host from noticing or rejecting the tick and simultaneously create a locally immunosuppressed site that favours pathogen establishment. This is the reason a pathogen delivered in tick saliva is more readily transmitted than the same organism injected by needle. For Borrelia burgdorferi, spirochetes in the unfed midgut express OspA, binding them to the gut wall, and the blood meal triggers a switch to OspC with migration to the salivary glands over a day or two, which is the mechanistic basis of the attachment-time threshold and of the value of early tick removal.",
  lab: "Identification of the tick is morphological and matters because the species determines which infections are plausible: Ixodes has a hard dorsal scutum without ornamentation, anterior mouthparts, an inornate scutum, and in the adult female a red-brown body with a dark scutum. Species, stage and estimated attachment duration from the degree of engorgement inform management. Testing the removed tick for pathogens is generally discouraged in clinical management because it neither confirms nor excludes human infection, although it is used in surveillance. Diagnosis of the transmitted infections follows their own routes: two-tier serology for Lyme disease, which is often negative in the first weeks so that erythema migrans in an endemic area is diagnosed clinically, blood smear and polymerase chain reaction with morulae in neutrophils for anaplasmosis, blood smear and polymerase chain reaction for Babesia with intraerythrocytic ring and Maltese cross forms, and cerebrospinal fluid serology and molecular testing for Powassan virus.",
  tx: "Removal of the attached tick with fine-tipped forceps grasping close to the skin and steady upward traction, followed by cleaning of the site, and any retained mouthparts are treated as a foreign body rather than as ongoing infection risk. Burning, petrolatum occlusion and twisting are counterproductive because they prolong attachment and may provoke regurgitation. A single dose of doxycycline within seventy-two hours of removal is used as prophylaxis after a bite judged high risk by species, attachment duration and local Lyme incidence. Established infections are treated specifically: doxycycline, amoxicillin or cefuroxime for Lyme disease, doxycycline for anaplasmosis and for Borrelia miyamotoi, atovaquone with azithromycin or clindamycin with quinine for babesiosis, and supportive care for Powassan virus, for which no antiviral exists.",
  alt: "Permethrin-treated clothing and gear, which kills ticks on contact, and repellents containing DEET, picaridin, IR3535 or oil of lemon eucalyptus for skin. For Lyme prophylaxis where doxycycline is unsuitable, observation with clear instructions about the appearance of a rash is the usual course. Doxycycline covers both Borrelia burgdorferi and the coinfecting Anaplasma, which is one reason it is preferred when both are plausible.",
  res: "Acaricide resistance is not a significant clinical issue for this species in the way it is for livestock ticks, since there is no mass chemical control of it. Permethrin remains effective on clothing. For the transmitted pathogens, Borrelia burgdorferi has no meaningful acquired antibiotic resistance and persistent symptoms after treatment are not explained by resistance, while reduced azithromycin and atovaquone susceptibility has been documented in Babesia microti in immunocompromised patients on prolonged therapy, with resistance mutations identified in the cytochrome b gene.",
  prev: "Avoidance of leaf litter, long grass and brush margins in season, permethrin treatment of clothing and footwear, repellent on exposed skin, light-colored clothing with trousers tucked in, and daily full-body tick checks with particular attention to the scalp, behind the ears, axillae, groin, waistband and behind the knees, since removal within twenty-four hours usually prevents Lyme transmission. Showering after exposure and tumble-drying clothing on high heat remove unattached ticks. Landscape measures include clearing leaf litter, creating wood chip or gravel barriers, fencing deer and in some settings treating deer or rodents with acaricide through bait boxes or feeding stations. The LYMErix human vaccine was licensed in 1998 and withdrawn in 2002, and a new OspA-based vaccine candidate and an anti-tick saliva approach are in late-stage development.",
  look: "Dermacentor variabilis, the American dog tick, which is larger with an ornamented scutum and transmits Rocky Mountain spotted fever and tularemia. Amblyomma americanum, the lone star tick, with a single white dorsal spot in the female, which transmits ehrlichiosis, Heartland virus and is the principal cause of alpha-gal syndrome, and which causes southern tick-associated rash illness that mimics erythema migrans. Rhipicephalus sanguineus, the brown dog tick. For the bite reaction, a spider bite, folliculitis, an embedded foreign body, a skin tag, a seborrheic keratosis or a nipple of accessory tissue, all of which have been removed under the impression of being ticks, and conversely an engorged tick has been mistaken for a melanocytic lesion.",
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The nymph of Ixodes scapularis, about the size of a poppy seed and active in late spring and summer, transmits most human Lyme disease, and Borrelia burgdorferi transmission generally requires thirty-six to forty-eight hours of attachment."],
    ["Memory trick", "The mouse is the reservoir, the deer feeds the adults and keeps the tick going. Small nymph equals big problem, because nobody sees it. Find it and remove it the same day and Lyme is unlikely."],
    ["Classic setting", "A hiker or suburban gardener in Connecticut, New York, Wisconsin or Minnesota in June with an expanding annular rash on the thigh or trunk, who does not recall a tick bite because the nymph was never seen."],
    ["Step focus", "One tick can deliver several pathogens, so Lyme disease with high fever, severe headache, leukopenia and thrombocytopenia suggests coinfection with Anaplasma or Babesia, and doxycycline covers the former but not the latter."]],
  worm: [["A tick with its own literature", "Ixodes scapularis is probably the most intensively studied tick in the world, with a sequenced genome, decades of field ecology and an entire research community built around the Lyme disease it transmits. Much of the modern understanding of tick-borne disease ecology, including the dilution effect, the role of acorn crops and small mammal abundance in predicting infection risk, and the concept of a bridging vector, was developed on this species in the woodlands of Connecticut, New York and Wisconsin. The taxonomy was itself contested for years, with the northern populations described as Ixodes dammini before being merged back into Ixodes scapularis in 1993."],
    ["Acorns predict Lyme disease two years later", "Richard Ostfeld and colleagues documented a chain that runs from oak mast to human infection. A heavy acorn year feeds a large white-footed mouse population the following summer, those mice feed and infect a large cohort of larval ticks, and those ticks emerge as infected nymphs the summer after that. Forecasting human risk from acorn counts two years earlier worked well enough to be published and tested. It is one of the more striking demonstrations that vector-borne disease risk is an ecological output rather than a property of the pathogen."],
    ["Why saliva matters more than the bite", "Tick saliva contains hundreds of proteins that suppress coagulation, complement, histamine signalling, chemotaxis and local T-cell responses, and the tick changes the composition of that saliva through its multi-day feed. Pathogens exploit the resulting immunosuppressed feeding site, and Borrelia burgdorferi delivered in saliva establishes far more readily than the same number of organisms injected in buffer. This insight reoriented vaccine thinking: candidate anti-tick vaccines aim at salivary proteins so that the tick fails to feed and no pathogen is delivered, which would protect against every infection the tick carries at once."],
    ["The range is still moving", "Reconstructions from museum and survey records show this tick expanding north and west from refugia in the northeast and upper midwest over the past several decades, with established populations now in southern Canada and in counties that had none a generation ago. Milder winters, reforestation of former farmland, suburban development at the woodland edge and recovery of deer populations from near-extirpation in the early twentieth century all contribute. The Lyme disease map has followed, and the expansion is a standard worked example in discussions of climate and land use driving vector distribution."]] }
);

;
